window.COURSE = {"lessons": [{"id": "00-how-sql-thinks", "title": "How SQL thinks", "minutes": 25, "summary": "One idea explains most early SQL errors: you write a query in one order, and the database runs it in another.", "lesson": "", "date": "", "interview": false, "part": "", "blocks": [{"type": "md", "text": "## A query describes a result\n\nIn R you write *steps*: take this, filter it, then summarise. In SQL you write *one statement* describing the result you want, and the database decides how to compute it. That's what \"declarative\" means, and it has practical consequences.\n\n```sql\nSELECT product, COUNT(*) AS n_loans\nFROM loans\nWHERE originated_date >= DATE '2025-01-01'\nGROUP BY product\nORDER BY n_loans DESC\n```\n\nEvery query is built from the same clauses, always written in this order:\n\n```sqlstatic\nSELECT    -- which columns to return (and calculations)\nFROM      -- which table(s)\nWHERE     -- which rows to keep\nGROUP BY  -- how to collapse rows into groups\nHAVING    -- which groups to keep\nORDER BY  -- how to sort the result\nLIMIT     -- how many rows to return\n```\n\nOnly `SELECT` and `FROM` are required. The rest are optional, but when present they **must** appear in this order.\n\n## The order it's written is not the order it runs\n\nThe database evaluates the clauses in a different order:\n\n| Step | Clause | What happens |\n|---|---|---|\n| 1 | `FROM` / `JOIN` | assemble the rows |\n| 2 | `WHERE` | drop rows that fail the condition |\n| 3 | `GROUP BY` | collapse rows into groups |\n| 4 | `HAVING` | drop groups that fail the condition |\n| 5 | `SELECT` | compute output columns and name them |\n| 6 | `ORDER BY` | sort |\n| 7 | `LIMIT` | keep the first *n* |\n\nRead a query starting from `FROM`, not from `SELECT`. That habit alone will make other people's SQL easier to follow."}, {"type": "rlens", "title": "", "text": "Your dplyr pipeline is already written in run order. The query above is:\n\n```r\nloans |>\n  filter(originated_date >= as.Date(\"2025-01-01\")) |>\n  group_by(product) |>\n  summarise(n_loans = n()) |>\n  arrange(desc(n_loans))\n```\n\nSQL puts the `summarise()` part (`SELECT`) at the top and everything else below it. When you translate, write the `FROM` and `WHERE` first, then go back up and fill in `SELECT`."}, {"type": "md", "text": "The order explains rules that otherwise seem arbitrary:\n\n- **A column alias made in `SELECT` doesn't exist yet in `WHERE`**, because `WHERE` runs first.\n- **It does exist in `ORDER BY`**, which runs after `SELECT`.\n- **You can't filter on `COUNT(*)` in `WHERE`.** Groups don't exist yet; that's what `HAVING` is for."}, {"type": "predict", "id": "l0-alias", "sql": "SELECT loan_id, principal * apr AS yearly_interest\nFROM loans\nWHERE yearly_interest > 1000", "explain": "`WHERE` runs before `SELECT`, so the alias isn't defined yet: PostgreSQL, MySQL, SQL Server and BigQuery all reject it. A few engines are lenient: DuckDB (this course's engine) and Snowflake accept it, so running it here works. **Don't rely on that in an interview.** Repeat the expression instead:\n\n```sqlstatic\nWHERE principal * apr > 1000\n```\nOr compute it in a subquery or CTE first (lesson 5).", "options": [{"text": "It returns loans with more than 1,000 of yearly interest", "correct": false}, {"text": "It fails: `yearly_interest` doesn't exist yet when `WHERE` runs", "correct": true}, {"text": "It returns every loan, because the alias is ignored", "correct": false}, {"text": "It returns no rows", "correct": false}], "errors": false, "prompt": "In PostgreSQL, MySQL or SQL Server, what happens when you run this?"}, {"type": "md", "text": "## Tables have no row order\n\nA data frame has row 1, row 2, row 3. A SQL table doesn't. Without `ORDER BY`, the database may return rows in any order, and the order can change between runs. If order matters, say so. If you use `LIMIT`, you almost always need `ORDER BY` too, or \"the top 10\" is just \"some 10\".\n\n## Grain: what is one row?\n\nThe most useful question in SQL, and the one interviewers are really testing, is **what does one row represent?**\n\n- `loans`: one row per loan.\n- `installments`: one row per *scheduled payment*. A 12-month loan has 12 rows.\n- `payments`: one row per payment *attempt*, including failures and (as you'll discover) some duplicates.\n\nThe result of a query has a grain too. `GROUP BY product` produces one row per product. A join between loans and installments produces one row per installment, not per loan. Most wrong answers in SQL interviews are grain mistakes: counting installments when you meant loans, or summing a loan's principal once per payment."}, {"type": "interview", "title": "", "text": "Say the grain out loud before you write anything: \"So I want one row per merchant, with the number of loans and the total principal.\" It catches mistakes early and shows the interviewer how you think."}, {"type": "predict", "id": "l0-grain", "sql": "SELECT borrower_id, COUNT(*) AS n_loans\nFROM loans\nGROUP BY borrower_id", "explain": "`FROM loans` means only borrowers who appear in `loans` can show up. Borrowers with no loans are invisible, because they aren't in the table being grouped. To include them you'd start from `borrowers` and join (lesson 3).", "options": [{"text": "One row per loan", "correct": false}, {"text": "One row per borrower in the `borrowers` table", "correct": false}, {"text": "One row per borrower who has at least one loan", "correct": true}, {"text": "One row per person (de-duplicating people with two accounts)", "correct": false}], "errors": false, "prompt": "What is the grain of this query's result?"}, {"type": "md", "text": "## Your first queries\n\nThe exercises below check your answer against a reference result. Click **Check answer** (or press <kbd>Ctrl</kbd>+<kbd>Shift</kbd>+<kbd>Enter</kbd>). Use **Run** freely while you work things out."}, {"type": "exercise", "id": "l0-first", "kind": "write", "ordered": false, "digits": 2, "prompt": "List the merchants onboarded on or after 1 January 2025. Return `merchant_name`, `category` and `onboarded_date`.\n\nWrite date literals as `DATE '2025-01-01'`.", "starter": "", "solution": "SELECT merchant_name, category, onboarded_date\nFROM merchants\nWHERE onboarded_date >= DATE '2025-01-01'", "alts": [], "hints": ["Start with `FROM merchants`, then a `WHERE` on `onboarded_date`."], "explain": ""}, {"type": "exercise", "id": "l0-translate", "kind": "write", "ordered": true, "digits": 2, "prompt": "Translate this dplyr pipeline into SQL:\n\n```r\nloans |>\n  filter(product == \"monthly\") |>\n  select(loan_id, principal, term_months) |>\n  arrange(desc(principal)) |>\n  head(5)\n```\n\nStrings in SQL use **single quotes**: `'monthly'`. Double quotes mean an identifier (a column or table name) in standard SQL.", "starter": "", "solution": "SELECT loan_id, principal, term_months\nFROM loans\nWHERE product = 'monthly'\nORDER BY principal DESC\nLIMIT 5", "alts": [], "hints": ["`filter()` → `WHERE`, `select()` → `SELECT`, `arrange(desc())` → `ORDER BY ... DESC`, `head(5)` → `LIMIT 5`.", "Equality in SQL is a single `=`, not `==`."], "explain": ""}, {"type": "dialect", "title": "", "text": "`LIMIT 5` works in PostgreSQL, MySQL, Snowflake, BigQuery, DuckDB and SQLite. SQL Server uses `SELECT TOP 5 ...`. The standard (and Oracle 12c+) form is `FETCH FIRST 5 ROWS ONLY` after `ORDER BY`."}, {"type": "md", "text": "## Recap\n\n- SQL is written `SELECT → FROM → WHERE → GROUP BY → HAVING → ORDER BY → LIMIT` but runs `FROM → WHERE → GROUP BY → HAVING → SELECT → ORDER BY → LIMIT`.\n- Read queries from `FROM` down.\n- Tables have no row order; use `ORDER BY` whenever order matters.\n- Before writing a query, state its grain: what one row of the result represents."}]}, {"id": "01-filtering-and-null", "title": "Filtering, CASE and NULL", "minutes": 35, "summary": "A quick refresher on SELECT and WHERE, then the part that trips everyone up — how NULL behaves.", "lesson": "", "date": "", "interview": false, "part": "", "blocks": [{"type": "md", "text": "## SELECT and WHERE, briskly\n\nYou remember this part, so here's the whole toolkit in one place:\n\n```sql\nSELECT\n  loan_id,\n  principal,\n  principal * apr AS yearly_interest,   -- calculated column with an alias\n  UPPER(product) AS product_label\nFROM loans\nWHERE product = 'monthly'\n  AND principal BETWEEN 1000 AND 2000   -- inclusive at both ends\n  AND status IN ('late', 'charged_off')\nORDER BY principal DESC\n```\n\n| Operator | Means | Note |\n|---|---|---|\n| `=`, `<>` | equal, not equal | `!=` also works almost everywhere |\n| `IN ('a', 'b')` | any of these | |\n| `BETWEEN a AND b` | `>= a AND <= b` | **inclusive**, a trap with timestamps (lesson 8) |\n| `LIKE 'Blue%'` | pattern: `%` any text, `_` one character | case-sensitive in PostgreSQL, Snowflake and DuckDB; usually not in MySQL or SQL Server |\n| `IS NULL`, `IS NOT NULL` | missing / present | the *only* correct way to test for NULL |\n\n`SELECT DISTINCT` removes duplicate **rows** (all selected columns together), not duplicates in the first column."}, {"type": "predict", "id": "l1-distinct", "sql": "SELECT DISTINCT product, term_months\nFROM loans", "explain": "`DISTINCT` works on the combination of columns. For the purposes of `DISTINCT` and `GROUP BY`, all NULLs count as the same value, so Pay in 4 contributes a single `(pay_in_4, NULL)` row.", "options": [{"text": "2: one per product", "correct": false}, {"text": "4: one per term", "correct": false}, {"text": "5: four monthly terms plus one Pay in 4 row with NULL", "correct": true}, {"text": "6", "correct": false}], "errors": false, "prompt": "How many rows does this return? (Recall `term_months` is NULL for Pay in 4 loans; monthly loans have terms 3, 6, 12 or 24.)"}, {"type": "md", "text": "## AND before OR\n\n`AND` binds tighter than `OR`, just as `*` binds tighter than `+`. This is one of the most common silent bugs:\n\n```sqlstatic\nWHERE product = 'monthly' AND status = 'late' OR status = 'charged_off'\n-- means:\nWHERE (product = 'monthly' AND status = 'late') OR status = 'charged_off'\n```\n\nWhen you mix `AND` and `OR`, always use parentheses, or replace the `OR` with `IN`."}, {"type": "exercise", "id": "l1-precedence", "kind": "fix", "ordered": false, "digits": 2, "prompt": "We want **monthly** loans that are either late or charged off. This query runs, but returns the wrong rows. Fix it.", "starter": "SELECT loan_id, product, status\nFROM loans\nWHERE product = 'monthly' AND status = 'late' OR status = 'charged_off'", "solution": "SELECT loan_id, product, status\nFROM loans\nWHERE product = 'monthly'\n  AND status IN ('late', 'charged_off')", "alts": ["SELECT loan_id, product, status\nFROM loans\nWHERE product = 'monthly' AND (status = 'late' OR status = 'charged_off')"], "hints": ["Run the starter and look at the `product` column. Do you see any `pay_in_4` rows?", "Group the two status conditions with parentheses, or use `status IN (...)`."], "explain": ""}, {"type": "md", "text": "## CASE: if/else inside a query\n\n```sql\nSELECT\n  loan_id,\n  principal,\n  CASE\n    WHEN apr = 0 THEN 'interest-free'\n    WHEN apr < 0.20 THEN 'low APR'\n    ELSE 'high APR'\n  END AS apr_band\nFROM loans\nLIMIT 10\n```\n\nConditions are tested top to bottom and the first true one wins. If nothing matches and there's no `ELSE`, the result is NULL."}, {"type": "rlens", "title": "", "text": "`CASE WHEN` is `case_when()`, and it behaves the same way: first match wins, and unmatched rows get `NA`/NULL. The SQL spelling: each branch is `WHEN condition THEN value`, there's one optional `ELSE` instead of `.default = ` or `TRUE ~`, and the whole thing ends with `END`. Forgetting `END` is the most common syntax error."}, {"type": "exercise", "id": "l1-case", "kind": "write", "ordered": false, "digits": 2, "prompt": "For loans originated on 31 December 2025, return `loan_id`, `principal`, and a column `size_band` that is `'small'` below 250, `'medium'` from 250 up to (not including) 1000, and `'large'` from 1000.", "starter": "", "solution": "SELECT\n  loan_id,\n  principal,\n  CASE\n    WHEN principal < 250 THEN 'small'\n    WHEN principal < 1000 THEN 'medium'\n    ELSE 'large'\n  END AS size_band\nFROM loans\nWHERE originated_date = DATE '2025-12-31'", "alts": [], "hints": ["Because the first matching `WHEN` wins, the second branch only needs `principal < 1000`: anything below 250 was already caught."], "explain": ""}, {"type": "md", "text": "## NULL: the value that isn't one\n\nNULL means *unknown* or *missing*. SQL follows a simple rule that has surprising consequences:\n\n> **Any comparison with NULL is NULL**, not true or false. And `WHERE` keeps a row only when the condition is **TRUE**.\n\nSo SQL has three truth values: TRUE, FALSE and NULL (unknown).\n\n```sql\nSELECT\n  NULL = NULL   AS null_equals_null,\n  NULL <> 1     AS null_not_one,\n  NULL IS NULL  AS null_is_null,\n  1 + NULL      AS arithmetic_with_null\n```\n\nConsequences:\n\n- `WHERE annual_income = NULL` returns **nothing**, ever. Use `IS NULL`.\n- `WHERE payment_method <> 'ach'` silently drops rows where `payment_method` is NULL.\n- `WHERE NOT (condition)` also drops rows where the condition is NULL. `NOT NULL` is still NULL."}, {"type": "predict", "id": "l1-not-equal", "sql": "SELECT COUNT(*) AS n\nFROM payments\nWHERE payment_method <> 'ach'", "explain": "For the NULL rows, `NULL <> 'ach'` is NULL, not TRUE, so `WHERE` drops them. If \"not ACH\" should include unknown methods, say so explicitly:\n\n```sqlstatic\nWHERE payment_method <> 'ach' OR payment_method IS NULL\n```", "options": [{"text": "17,032", "correct": true}, {"text": "18,484", "correct": false}, {"text": "48,516", "correct": false}, {"text": "0", "correct": false}], "errors": false, "prompt": "`payments` has 30,032 `ach` rows, 17,032 `debit_card` rows and 1,452 rows where `payment_method` is NULL. What does this return?"}, {"type": "rlens", "title": "", "text": "Here, R and SQL actually agree: `NA != \"ach\"` is `NA`, and `filter()` drops rows where the condition is `NA`. Your instincts are right so far. The differences show up in aggregation (lesson 2) and joins (lesson 3)."}, {"type": "md", "text": "### COALESCE: fill in a default\n\n`COALESCE(a, b, c)` returns the first non-NULL argument. It's how you replace NULLs in output:\n\n```sql\nSELECT\n  COALESCE(payment_method, 'unknown') AS method,\n  amount\nFROM payments\nLIMIT 5\n```"}, {"type": "rlens", "title": "", "text": "`COALESCE(x, 'unknown')` is `coalesce(x, \"unknown\")` or `replace_na(x, \"unknown\")`. Same function, same name."}, {"type": "exercise", "id": "l1-missing-income", "kind": "fill", "ordered": false, "digits": 2, "prompt": "List borrower accounts in California (`state = 'CA'`) that have **no** reported income. Return `borrower_id` and `email`.", "starter": "SELECT borrower_id, email\nFROM borrowers\nWHERE state = 'CA'\n  AND annual_income ____", "solution": "SELECT borrower_id, email\nFROM borrowers\nWHERE state = 'CA'\n  AND annual_income IS NULL", "alts": [], "hints": ["`= NULL` never matches anything."], "explain": ""}, {"type": "exercise", "id": "l1-no-score", "kind": "write", "ordered": false, "digits": 2, "prompt": "Some applications were approved even though no credit file was found (`credit_score` is NULL). List those applications made on or after 1 December 2025: `application_id`, `applied_at` and `requested_amount`.", "starter": "", "solution": "SELECT application_id, applied_at, requested_amount\nFROM applications\nWHERE decision = 'approved'\n  AND credit_score IS NULL\n  AND applied_at >= DATE '2025-12-01'", "alts": [], "hints": ["Three conditions joined by `AND`: the decision, the missing score, and the date."], "explain": ""}, {"type": "md", "text": "## Sorting and NULLs\n\n`ORDER BY col DESC` sorts descending. Add more columns to break ties: `ORDER BY principal DESC, loan_id`. Where NULLs land depends on the database:"}, {"type": "dialect", "title": "", "text": "NULLs sort **last** in ascending order in PostgreSQL, DuckDB, Snowflake and Oracle, and **first** in MySQL, SQL Server, SQLite and BigQuery. Write `ORDER BY x NULLS LAST` where it's supported (not MySQL or SQL Server) when it matters, or sort on `x IS NULL` first."}, {"type": "interview", "title": "", "text": "When a question says \"top 10\", ask what should happen with ties, and add a tie-breaker (usually the ID) so the result is deterministic. Interviewers notice."}, {"type": "md", "text": "## Recap\n\n- `AND` binds before `OR`: parenthesize or use `IN`.\n- `CASE WHEN ... THEN ... ELSE ... END`: first match wins, no match gives NULL.\n- Comparisons with NULL are NULL, and `WHERE` keeps only TRUE. Use `IS NULL` / `IS NOT NULL`.\n- `<>` and `NOT` silently drop NULL rows.\n- `COALESCE` replaces NULL with a default."}]}, {"id": "02-aggregation-and-grain", "title": "Aggregation and grain", "minutes": 45, "summary": "GROUP BY, the three kinds of COUNT, rates done right, and how NULLs quietly change your averages.", "lesson": "", "date": "", "interview": false, "part": "", "blocks": [{"type": "md", "text": "## Collapsing rows\n\nAn aggregate function turns many rows into one value. Without `GROUP BY`, the whole table collapses into a single row:\n\n```sql\nSELECT\n  COUNT(*)        AS n_loans,\n  SUM(principal)  AS total_principal,\n  AVG(principal)  AS avg_principal,\n  MIN(originated_date) AS first_loan,\n  MAX(originated_date) AS last_loan\nFROM loans\n```\n\nWith `GROUP BY`, you get one row per group:\n\n```sql\nSELECT\n  product,\n  COUNT(*)                 AS n_loans,\n  ROUND(AVG(principal), 2) AS avg_principal\nFROM loans\nGROUP BY product\n```\n\n**The rule:** every column in `SELECT` must either appear in `GROUP BY` or be inside an aggregate function. `SELECT product, status, COUNT(*) ... GROUP BY product` is an error, because there's no single `status` for the whole `pay_in_4` group."}, {"type": "rlens", "title": "", "text": "`GROUP BY` + aggregates is `group_by() |> summarise()`. Three differences:\n\n- SQL has no `.by =` shortcut, and **you list the grouping columns twice**: once in `SELECT` (to see them) and once in `GROUP BY` (to group by them).\n- A grouped `summarise()` drops the grouping one level at a time. SQL has no leftover grouping: the result is a plain table.\n- `group_by() |> mutate()` (keep every row, add a group statistic) is **not** `GROUP BY`. That's a window function (lesson 6)."}, {"type": "md", "text": "## Three kinds of COUNT\n\n| Expression | Counts |\n|---|---|\n| `COUNT(*)` | rows |\n| `COUNT(col)` | rows where `col` is **not NULL** |\n| `COUNT(DISTINCT col)` | distinct non-NULL values of `col` |"}, {"type": "predict", "id": "l2-counts", "sql": "SELECT\n  COUNT(*)                    AS a,\n  COUNT(term_months)          AS b,\n  COUNT(DISTINCT term_months) AS c\nFROM loans", "explain": "`COUNT(term_months)` skips NULLs, and so does `COUNT(DISTINCT ...)`. Pay in 4 loans don't contribute a \"NULL term\" to the distinct count. Compare with `SELECT DISTINCT term_months`, which *does* return a NULL row.", "options": [{"text": "8,887 · 8,887 · 5", "correct": false}, {"text": "8,887 · 5,481 · 4", "correct": true}, {"text": "8,887 · 5,481 · 5", "correct": false}, {"text": "5,481 · 5,481 · 4", "correct": false}], "errors": false, "prompt": "There are 8,887 loans, 3,406 of them Pay in 4, where `term_months` is NULL. What does this return?"}, {"type": "rlens", "title": "", "text": "- `COUNT(*)` is `n()`.\n- `COUNT(x)` is `sum(!is.na(x))`.\n- `COUNT(DISTINCT x)` is `n_distinct(x, na.rm = TRUE)`. Careful: plain `n_distinct(x)` **counts NA as a value**, so it would give 5 here."}, {"type": "md", "text": "## Aggregates skip NULLs\n\n`SUM`, `AVG`, `MIN` and `MAX` ignore NULLs. That's convenient and dangerous, because it happens silently.\n\n```sql\nSELECT\n  AVG(annual_income)              AS avg_of_reported,\n  AVG(COALESCE(annual_income, 0)) AS avg_treating_missing_as_zero,\n  COUNT(*) - COUNT(annual_income) AS n_missing\nFROM borrowers\n```\n\nNeither is \"the\" right answer. The first is the average of people who reported an income; the second pretends non-reporters earn nothing. Pick deliberately, and say which in an interview."}, {"type": "rlens", "title": "", "text": "This is a real difference. In R, `mean(x)` returns `NA` if anything is missing, and you opt in to skipping with `na.rm = TRUE`. **In SQL, `na.rm = TRUE` is always on and can't be turned off.** If NULLs matter, you have to count them yourself (`COUNT(*) - COUNT(x)`)."}, {"type": "exercise", "id": "l2-avg-income", "kind": "fix", "ordered": false, "digits": 0, "prompt": "We want the **average reported income** by state, ignoring borrowers who didn't report one. The query runs, but the averages are too low. Fix it.", "starter": "SELECT\n  state,\n  AVG(COALESCE(annual_income, 0)) AS avg_income\nFROM borrowers\nGROUP BY state", "solution": "SELECT\n  state,\n  AVG(annual_income) AS avg_income\nFROM borrowers\nGROUP BY state", "alts": [], "hints": ["What does `COALESCE(annual_income, 0)` do to someone with no reported income, and what does `AVG` do with a NULL?"], "explain": ""}, {"type": "md", "text": "## WHERE vs HAVING\n\n- `WHERE` filters **rows**, before grouping.\n- `HAVING` filters **groups**, after grouping, so it can use aggregates.\n\n```sql\nSELECT state, COUNT(*) AS n_accounts\nFROM borrowers\nWHERE created_at >= DATE '2025-01-01'   -- rows: accounts opened in 2025\nGROUP BY state\nHAVING COUNT(*) >= 100                  -- groups: states with 100+ of them\nORDER BY n_accounts DESC\n```"}, {"type": "rlens", "title": "", "text": "`WHERE` is a `filter()` *before* `summarise()`; `HAVING` is a `filter()` *after* it."}, {"type": "exercise", "id": "l2-having", "kind": "fix", "ordered": false, "digits": 2, "prompt": "Find states with more than 300 borrower accounts. Return `state` and `n_accounts`. This one doesn't even run. Fix it.", "starter": "SELECT state, COUNT(*) AS n_accounts\nFROM borrowers\nWHERE COUNT(*) > 300\nGROUP BY state", "solution": "SELECT state, COUNT(*) AS n_accounts\nFROM borrowers\nGROUP BY state\nHAVING COUNT(*) > 300", "alts": [], "hints": ["Aggregates can't appear in `WHERE`, because groups don't exist yet when `WHERE` runs."], "explain": ""}, {"type": "md", "text": "## Conditional aggregation\n\nThis is the most useful pattern in analytics SQL: put a `CASE` *inside* an aggregate to count or sum only some rows.\n\n```sql\nSELECT\n  product,\n  COUNT(*) AS n_loans,\n  SUM(CASE WHEN status = 'charged_off' THEN 1 ELSE 0 END) AS n_charged_off,\n  AVG(CASE WHEN status = 'charged_off' THEN 1.0 ELSE 0 END) AS charge_off_rate\nFROM loans\nGROUP BY product\n```\n\n- `SUM(CASE WHEN cond THEN 1 ELSE 0 END)` counts rows meeting a condition.\n- `AVG(CASE WHEN cond THEN 1.0 ELSE 0 END)` is the **share** of rows meeting it, because the mean of 0/1 values is a proportion.\n- `SUM(CASE WHEN cond THEN amount END)` sums only matching rows. The missing `ELSE` gives NULL, which `SUM` skips."}, {"type": "rlens", "title": "", "text": "In R you'd write `sum(status == \"charged_off\")` and `mean(status == \"charged_off\")`, because logicals coerce to 0/1. Some databases let you sum a boolean, but not all, so `CASE` is the portable spelling. Learn it until it's automatic."}, {"type": "exercise", "id": "l2-pivot", "kind": "write", "ordered": false, "digits": 2, "prompt": "For each product, count loans in each status. Return `product`, `n_current`, `n_late`, `n_paid_off`, `n_charged_off` (one row per product).", "starter": "", "solution": "SELECT\n  product,\n  SUM(CASE WHEN status = 'current' THEN 1 ELSE 0 END)     AS n_current,\n  SUM(CASE WHEN status = 'late' THEN 1 ELSE 0 END)        AS n_late,\n  SUM(CASE WHEN status = 'paid_off' THEN 1 ELSE 0 END)    AS n_paid_off,\n  SUM(CASE WHEN status = 'charged_off' THEN 1 ELSE 0 END) AS n_charged_off\nFROM loans\nGROUP BY product", "alts": [], "hints": ["One `SUM(CASE WHEN ... THEN 1 ELSE 0 END)` per status column, and `GROUP BY product`."], "explain": ""}, {"type": "md", "text": "This \"pivot with conditional aggregation\" works in every dialect. Some databases have a `PIVOT` keyword, but the syntax varies too much to rely on.\n\n## Rates: the integer division trap\n\nIn PostgreSQL and SQL Server, dividing two integers **truncates**: `7 / 2` is `3`, and `COUNT(*) / COUNT(*)`-style rates come out as 0 or 1. DuckDB (this course's engine), MySQL, Snowflake and BigQuery return a decimal, so the bug won't show up here, but it will in a Postgres interview.\n\nThe habits that make rates safe everywhere:\n\n```sqlstatic\n1.0 * SUM(CASE WHEN status = 'charged_off' THEN 1 ELSE 0 END) / COUNT(*)  -- force decimals\nAVG(CASE WHEN status = 'charged_off' THEN 1.0 ELSE 0 END)                 -- or average a 0/1 flag\nx / NULLIF(y, 0)                                                         -- avoid divide-by-zero\n```\n\n`NULLIF(y, 0)` returns NULL when `y` is 0, so the division gives NULL instead of an error."}, {"type": "pitfall", "title": "Integer division", "text": "If a rate comes back as exactly 0 or 1 for every group, you've almost certainly divided integers. Multiply by `1.0` first."}, {"type": "exercise", "id": "l2-scored", "kind": "write", "ordered": false, "digits": 3, "prompt": "Do applicants without a credit file get approved less often? For applications, return `score_status` (`'scored'` when `credit_score` is present, `'no score'` when it's NULL), `n_applications`, and `approval_rate` (approved ÷ all, as a decimal like 0.734).", "starter": "", "solution": "SELECT\n  CASE WHEN credit_score IS NULL THEN 'no score' ELSE 'scored' END AS score_status,\n  COUNT(*) AS n_applications,\n  AVG(CASE WHEN decision = 'approved' THEN 1.0 ELSE 0 END) AS approval_rate\nFROM applications\nGROUP BY CASE WHEN credit_score IS NULL THEN 'no score' ELSE 'scored' END", "alts": [], "hints": ["You can `GROUP BY` an expression. Repeat the whole `CASE` in `GROUP BY`. (Many databases also accept `GROUP BY 1`, meaning \"the first selected column\", which is handy in interviews.)", "The rate is the average of a 0/1 flag: `AVG(CASE WHEN decision = 'approved' THEN 1.0 ELSE 0 END)`."], "explain": "Repeating the expression is the most portable form. `GROUP BY 1` works in PostgreSQL, MySQL, Snowflake, BigQuery and DuckDB, but not in SQL Server. Grouping by the alias (`GROUP BY score_status`) works in some engines but not all, so avoid relying on it."}, {"type": "dialect", "title": "", "text": "PostgreSQL and DuckDB also support `COUNT(*) FILTER (WHERE status = 'late')`. It's nice, but not portable: `CASE` works everywhere."}, {"type": "md", "text": "## Recap\n\n- Every selected column is either grouped or aggregated.\n- `COUNT(*)` counts rows, `COUNT(x)` non-NULL values, `COUNT(DISTINCT x)` distinct non-NULL values.\n- `SUM`/`AVG`/`MIN`/`MAX` silently skip NULLs. Decide whether that's what you want.\n- `WHERE` filters rows before grouping; `HAVING` filters groups after.\n- Conditional aggregation: `SUM(CASE WHEN ... THEN 1 ELSE 0 END)` to count, `AVG(CASE WHEN ... THEN 1.0 ELSE 0 END)` for rates.\n- Protect rates from integer division (`1.0 *`) and zero denominators (`NULLIF`)."}]}, {"id": "03-joins", "title": "Joins: syntax and types", "minutes": 55, "summary": "How to combine tables: INNER and LEFT joins, keys, the ON-versus-WHERE trap, and why NULL keys never match.", "lesson": "", "date": "", "interview": false, "part": "", "blocks": [{"type": "md", "text": "## Why joins exist\n\nEach table holds one kind of thing: loans, merchants, borrowers. Instead of copying the merchant's name onto every loan, `loans` stores a `merchant_id`, a **foreign key** that points to the merchant's row. A join follows that pointer.\n\n```sql\nSELECT\n  l.loan_id,\n  l.principal,\n  m.merchant_name,\n  m.category\nFROM loans l\nJOIN merchants m\n  ON m.merchant_id = l.merchant_id\nWHERE l.originated_date = DATE '2025-12-31'\n```\n\nAnatomy:\n\n- `FROM loans l` gives the table a short **alias**, `l`. Aliases make queries shorter, and they're required when the same column name exists in both tables.\n- `JOIN merchants m ON m.merchant_id = l.merchant_id` means: for each loan, find the merchant rows where this condition is true.\n- `l.loan_id`: prefix columns with the alias. Unprefixed `merchant_id` here would be an \"ambiguous column\" error, because it exists in both tables."}, {"type": "interview", "title": "", "text": "Prefix **every** column with its table alias once a query has a join, even when it isn't ambiguous. It reads better, it survives someone adding a column later, and interviewers read it as care."}, {"type": "md", "text": "## How to think about a join\n\nVenn diagrams are a popular way to explain joins, and they mislead: they suggest joins work on *sets of values*. A better mental model:\n\n> **For each row on the left, find every row on the right where the `ON` condition is true, and output one combined row per match.**\n\nThat model predicts everything:\n\n- A loan matches exactly one merchant → one output row per loan.\n- A loan matches 12 installments → **12 output rows** for that loan.\n- A row that matches nothing → dropped (INNER JOIN) or kept with NULLs (LEFT JOIN).\n\n## INNER JOIN and LEFT JOIN\n\n`JOIN` (short for `INNER JOIN`) keeps only rows that found at least one match. `LEFT JOIN` keeps **every** row from the left table; where there's no match, the right table's columns are NULL.\n\n```sql\n-- every borrower, with their loans if any\nSELECT\n  b.borrower_id,\n  b.created_at,\n  l.loan_id,\n  l.principal\nFROM borrowers b\nLEFT JOIN loans l\n  ON l.borrower_id = b.borrower_id\nORDER BY b.borrower_id DESC\nLIMIT 10\n```\n\nThe newest borrowers mostly haven't taken a loan yet, so you'll see NULLs in `loan_id` and `principal`."}, {"type": "predict", "id": "l3-left-count", "sql": "SELECT COUNT(*) AS n_rows\nFROM borrowers b\nLEFT JOIN loans l\n  ON l.borrower_id = b.borrower_id", "explain": "Each borrower with loans appears once per loan (8,887 rows in total), and each of the 1,412 borrowers without loans appears once with NULLs: 8,887 + 1,412 = 10,299. A LEFT JOIN never has *fewer* rows than its left table, but it can have many more.", "options": [{"text": "6,184: one per borrower", "correct": false}, {"text": "8,887: one per loan", "correct": false}, {"text": "10,299", "correct": true}, {"text": "54,957,208: every borrower paired with every loan", "correct": false}], "errors": false, "prompt": "There are 6,184 borrowers and 8,887 loans. 1,412 borrowers have no loans. How many rows does this return?"}, {"type": "rlens", "title": "", "text": "The joins map one to one:\n\n| dplyr | SQL |\n|---|---|\n| `inner_join(x, y, by = join_by(id))` | `FROM x JOIN y ON y.id = x.id` |\n| `left_join(x, y, by = ...)` | `FROM x LEFT JOIN y ON ...` |\n| `right_join()` | `RIGHT JOIN`: rare, just swap the tables and use LEFT |\n| `full_join()` | `FULL OUTER JOIN` |\n| `semi_join()` / `anti_join()` | `WHERE EXISTS` / `WHERE NOT EXISTS` (lesson 4) |\n\nTwo differences matter:\n\n- dplyr merges the key into one column and suffixes clashing names with `.x`/`.y`. SQL keeps both tables' columns side by side, and you pick them with the alias.\n- dplyr (≥ 1.1) **warns** about unexpected many-to-many matches. SQL never warns; it just multiplies rows."}, {"type": "exercise", "id": "l3-state-loans", "kind": "write", "ordered": false, "digits": 2, "prompt": "How many loans were made to borrowers in each state? Return `state` and `n_loans`.", "starter": "", "solution": "SELECT\n  b.state,\n  COUNT(*) AS n_loans\nFROM loans l\nJOIN borrowers b\n  ON b.borrower_id = l.borrower_id\nGROUP BY b.state", "alts": [], "hints": ["`state` lives on `borrowers`; loans live on `loans`. Join them on `borrower_id`, then group.", "Start from `loans`: the thing you're counting is the left table."], "explain": ""}, {"type": "md", "text": "## Counting with a LEFT JOIN\n\nA classic bug: after a LEFT JOIN, `COUNT(*)` counts the borrower's own row even when there's no loan, so borrowers with no loans show 1 instead of 0."}, {"type": "exercise", "id": "l3-count-zero", "kind": "fix", "ordered": false, "digits": 2, "prompt": "For every borrower account in Nevada (`state = 'NV'`), return `borrower_id` and `n_loans`, the number of loans they have, **including 0** for borrowers with none. The query below gives some borrowers the wrong count. Fix it.", "starter": "SELECT\n  b.borrower_id,\n  COUNT(*) AS n_loans\nFROM borrowers b\nLEFT JOIN loans l\n  ON l.borrower_id = b.borrower_id\nWHERE b.state = 'NV'\nGROUP BY b.borrower_id", "solution": "SELECT\n  b.borrower_id,\n  COUNT(l.loan_id) AS n_loans\nFROM borrowers b\nLEFT JOIN loans l\n  ON l.borrower_id = b.borrower_id\nWHERE b.state = 'NV'\nGROUP BY b.borrower_id", "alts": [], "hints": ["Run the starter and find a borrower with `n_loans = 1`. Do they really have a loan?", "Count a column from the *right* table. It's NULL when there was no match, and `COUNT(column)` skips NULLs."], "explain": ""}, {"type": "md", "text": "## ON vs WHERE in a LEFT JOIN\n\nWith an INNER JOIN, a condition behaves the same in `ON` or `WHERE`. With a LEFT JOIN it doesn't:\n\n- A condition in `ON` decides **which right-hand rows match**. Left rows are kept no matter what.\n- A condition in `WHERE` runs **after** the join and filters the combined rows. A `WHERE` on a right-table column throws away the NULL rows the LEFT JOIN just kept, **silently turning it into an INNER JOIN**.\n\n```sql\n-- every merchant, with its December 2025 loans (if any)\nSELECT\n  m.merchant_id,\n  m.merchant_name,\n  COUNT(l.loan_id) AS dec_2025_loans\nFROM merchants m\nLEFT JOIN loans l\n  ON l.merchant_id = m.merchant_id\n AND l.originated_date >= DATE '2025-12-01'   -- right-table filter goes in ON\nGROUP BY m.merchant_id, m.merchant_name\nORDER BY dec_2025_loans\nLIMIT 5\n```\n\nMove that date condition into a `WHERE` and the merchants with zero December loans vanish."}, {"type": "exercise", "id": "l3-on-where", "kind": "fix", "ordered": false, "digits": 2, "prompt": "For **every** merchant, return `merchant_id` and `n_charged_off`, its number of charged-off loans, **including merchants with 0**. Fix the query.", "starter": "SELECT\n  m.merchant_id,\n  COUNT(l.loan_id) AS n_charged_off\nFROM merchants m\nLEFT JOIN loans l\n  ON l.merchant_id = m.merchant_id\nWHERE l.status = 'charged_off'\nGROUP BY m.merchant_id", "solution": "SELECT\n  m.merchant_id,\n  COUNT(l.loan_id) AS n_charged_off\nFROM merchants m\nLEFT JOIN loans l\n  ON l.merchant_id = m.merchant_id\n AND l.status = 'charged_off'\nGROUP BY m.merchant_id", "alts": ["SELECT\n  m.merchant_id,\n  SUM(CASE WHEN l.status = 'charged_off' THEN 1 ELSE 0 END) AS n_charged_off\nFROM merchants m\nLEFT JOIN loans l\n  ON l.merchant_id = m.merchant_id\nGROUP BY m.merchant_id"], "hints": ["The answer should have 151 rows, one per merchant. How many does the starter return?", "Move the status condition from `WHERE` into the `ON` clause."], "explain": "Both versions keep every merchant. The first restricts which loans *match*; the second matches all loans and counts only the charged-off ones with conditional aggregation. Either is a good answer. Being able to explain why the `WHERE` version was wrong is the part interviewers listen for."}, {"type": "md", "text": "## NULL keys never match\n\nThe `ON` condition is a comparison, and `NULL = NULL` is NULL, not TRUE. So **rows with a NULL key never match anything**, not even another NULL."}, {"type": "predict", "id": "l3-null-keys", "sql": "WITH a AS (\n  SELECT 1 AS k UNION ALL SELECT NULL\n),\nb AS (\n  SELECT 1 AS k UNION ALL SELECT NULL\n)\nSELECT COUNT(*) AS n_rows\nFROM a\nJOIN b ON b.k = a.k", "explain": "Only `1 = 1` is TRUE. `NULL = NULL` is NULL, so the NULL rows don't pair up. With a LEFT JOIN, the NULL row from `a` would be kept with no match.", "options": [{"text": "1", "correct": true}, {"text": "2", "correct": false}, {"text": "4", "correct": false}, {"text": "0", "correct": false}], "errors": false, "prompt": "`WITH` just defines two tiny inline tables here (lesson 5 covers it). How many rows does the join return?"}, {"type": "rlens", "title": "", "text": "This is a real trap for R users: **dplyr joins match `NA` to `NA` by default** (`na_matches = \"na\"`). Join two data frames on a column with missing values and dplyr pairs the NAs up; SQL never does. If you really want NULLs to match, the condition is `ON a.k = b.k OR (a.k IS NULL AND b.k IS NULL)`, and that's rarely what you want."}, {"type": "md", "text": "## Joining more than two tables\n\nEach `JOIN` adds one table to the rows built so far. Chain them:"}, {"type": "exercise", "id": "l3-three-tables", "kind": "write", "ordered": false, "digits": 2, "prompt": "For loans to borrowers in California (`state = 'CA'`), return each merchant `category` (exactly as stored), with `n_loans` and `total_principal`.", "starter": "", "solution": "SELECT\n  m.category,\n  COUNT(*) AS n_loans,\n  SUM(l.principal) AS total_principal\nFROM loans l\nJOIN borrowers b\n  ON b.borrower_id = l.borrower_id\nJOIN merchants m\n  ON m.merchant_id = l.merchant_id\nWHERE b.state = 'CA'\nGROUP BY m.category", "alts": [], "hints": ["Start `FROM loans l`, join `borrowers b` for the state and `merchants m` for the category."], "explain": "You'll notice `Fashion` and `Fashion ` (trailing space), plus a NULL category. The data is messy on purpose. Cleaning it (`TRIM`, `UPPER`/`LOWER`, `COALESCE`) is a problem-bank exercise."}, {"type": "md", "text": "## Keys with more than one column\n\n`installments` is identified by **two** columns together: (`loan_id`, `installment_number`). Payments point to an installment the same way, so the join needs both:\n\n```sql\nSELECT\n  i.loan_id,\n  i.installment_number,\n  i.due_date,\n  i.amount_due,\n  p.paid_at,\n  p.amount,\n  p.status\nFROM installments i\nLEFT JOIN payments p\n  ON p.loan_id = i.loan_id\n AND p.installment_number = i.installment_number\nWHERE i.loan_id = 900194\nORDER BY i.installment_number, p.paid_at\n```\n\nForget the second condition and every payment matches *every* installment of the loan. That's the subject of the next lesson."}, {"type": "dialect", "title": "", "text": "`JOIN ... USING (loan_id)` is shorthand for equal-named keys in PostgreSQL, MySQL, Snowflake, BigQuery and DuckDB, but not SQL Server. `ON` works everywhere; prefer it under pressure."}, {"type": "md", "text": "## Recap\n\n- A join pairs each left row with **every** right row that satisfies `ON`.\n- `INNER JOIN` drops unmatched rows; `LEFT JOIN` keeps them with NULLs.\n- After a LEFT JOIN, count a right-table column, not `*`.\n- Right-table filters in a LEFT JOIN belong in `ON`. In `WHERE` they turn it into an inner join.\n- NULL keys never match, unlike dplyr's default.\n- Composite keys need every column in the `ON`."}]}, {"id": "04-fan-out-and-anti-joins", "title": "Joins: fan-out, semi and anti joins", "minutes": 50, "summary": "How joins multiply rows (and money), the aggregate-then-join fix, and how to ask \"has any\" or \"has none\" without a join.", "lesson": "", "date": "", "interview": false, "part": "", "blocks": [{"type": "md", "text": "## Cardinality: how many matches per row?\n\nBefore you join two tables, ask: **for one row on the left, how many rows on the right can match?**\n\n| Relationship | Example | Rows after joining |\n|---|---|---|\n| many → one | loans → merchants | same as `loans` |\n| one → many | loans → installments | one per installment |\n| many → many | payments → installments on `loan_id` only | can explode |\n\nJoining *to the one side* (looking up a merchant name) is safe: the grain doesn't change. Joining *to the many side* changes the grain, and every column from the \"one\" side gets **repeated** once per match."}, {"type": "predict", "id": "l4-installments", "sql": "SELECT COUNT(*) AS n_rows\nFROM loans l\nJOIN installments i\n  ON i.loan_id = l.loan_id", "explain": "Every installment belongs to exactly one loan, so the join produces one row per installment. The result's grain is now *installment*, even though you started `FROM loans`.", "options": [{"text": "8,887", "correct": false}, {"text": "66,511", "correct": true}, {"text": "8,887 × 66,511", "correct": false}, {"text": "Somewhere between them, it depends on the data", "correct": false}], "errors": false, "prompt": "`loans` has 8,887 rows. Each loan has 3 to 24 installments; `installments` has 66,511 rows. What does this return?"}, {"type": "md", "text": "## Fan-out: when joins inflate totals\n\nHere is the bug that sinks more SQL screens than any other. What's the total principal we've lent?\n\n```sql\nSELECT SUM(principal) AS total_principal\nFROM loans\n```\n\nNow the same total, but someone joined payments first, perhaps because they also wanted to sum collections:\n\n```sql\nSELECT\n  SUM(l.principal) AS total_principal,\n  SUM(p.amount)    AS total_paid\nFROM loans l\nJOIN payments p\n  ON p.loan_id = l.loan_id\n```\n\nPrincipal jumped from about \\$6.6M to \\$44M. Each loan's principal was added once **per payment row**. Nothing errored and no warning appeared. This is **fan-out**.\n\n**Diagnose it** by comparing row counts with distinct keys:\n\n```sql\nSELECT\n  COUNT(*)                  AS n_rows,\n  COUNT(DISTINCT l.loan_id) AS n_loans\nFROM loans l\nJOIN payments p\n  ON p.loan_id = l.loan_id\n```\n\nIf `n_rows` > `n_loans`, any loan-level column you `SUM` or `COUNT(*)` is inflated.\n\n### The fix: aggregate first, then join\n\nBring the many side **down to the grain you need** before joining. Here that means one row per loan:\n\n```sql\nWITH paid AS (           -- step 1: payments → one row per loan\n  SELECT\n    loan_id,\n    SUM(amount) AS total_paid\n  FROM payments\n  WHERE status = 'succeeded'\n  GROUP BY loan_id\n)\nSELECT                   -- step 2: now it's one-to-one\n  SUM(l.principal)  AS total_principal,\n  SUM(pd.total_paid) AS total_paid\nFROM loans l\nLEFT JOIN paid pd\n  ON pd.loan_id = l.loan_id\n```\n\n`WITH name AS (...)` defines a named intermediate result, a **CTE**, that the rest of the query can use like a table. Lesson 5 covers it properly; for now, read it as \"first compute this, then...\"."}, {"type": "pitfall", "title": "SUM(DISTINCT) is not a fix", "text": "`SUM(DISTINCT l.principal)` \"fixes\" the inflated total only by accident. It adds up distinct *values*, so two different loans that both happen to be \\$500.00 count once. `COUNT(DISTINCT loan_id)` is safe for counting; `SUM(DISTINCT ...)` almost never is. Aggregate first, then join."}, {"type": "rlens", "title": "", "text": "dplyr ≥ 1.1 would warn you here (\"Each row in `x` is expected to match at most 1 row in `y`\") if you declared `relationship = \"many-to-one\"`, and it warns automatically for many-to-many. SQL has no such guardrail. **You are the guardrail:** check `COUNT(*)` against `COUNT(DISTINCT key)` whenever a join touches a one-to-many relationship."}, {"type": "md", "text": "### Duplicates are fan-out's cousin\n\n`payments` has some rows loaded twice: same `payment_id`, same everything. Summing it double-counts those payments. `SELECT DISTINCT *` removes rows that are identical in every column:\n\n```sql\nSELECT\n  (SELECT COUNT(*) FROM payments)                          AS raw_rows,\n  (SELECT COUNT(*) FROM (SELECT DISTINCT * FROM payments) d) AS distinct_rows\n```\n\nA subquery in `FROM` (here `(SELECT DISTINCT * FROM payments) d`) works like a temporary table and needs an alias. Lesson 6 shows the more general dedup tool, `ROW_NUMBER()`."}, {"type": "exercise", "id": "l4-fanout", "kind": "fix", "ordered": false, "digits": 2, "prompt": "By product, we want `n_loans`, `total_principal` and `total_collected`: the sum of **successful** payments, counting each payment once, with loans that have no payments still counted. The starter has three separate problems. Find and fix all of them.", "starter": "SELECT\n  l.product,\n  COUNT(*)         AS n_loans,\n  SUM(l.principal) AS total_principal,\n  SUM(p.amount)    AS total_collected\nFROM loans l\nJOIN payments p\n  ON p.loan_id = l.loan_id\nWHERE p.status = 'succeeded'\nGROUP BY l.product", "solution": "WITH paid AS (\n  SELECT\n    loan_id,\n    SUM(amount) AS collected\n  FROM (SELECT DISTINCT * FROM payments) p\n  WHERE status = 'succeeded'\n  GROUP BY loan_id\n)\nSELECT\n  l.product,\n  COUNT(*)          AS n_loans,\n  SUM(l.principal)  AS total_principal,\n  SUM(pd.collected) AS total_collected\nFROM loans l\nLEFT JOIN paid pd\n  ON pd.loan_id = l.loan_id\nGROUP BY l.product", "alts": [], "hints": ["Problem 1: `COUNT(*)` and `SUM(l.principal)` are computed after joining to payments. What's the grain at that point?", "Problem 2: some payment rows are exact duplicates. Problem 3: an inner join drops loans that have no successful payments yet.", "Build a CTE that turns de-duplicated, successful payments into one row per loan, then `LEFT JOIN` it to `loans`."], "explain": "The three fixes: **aggregate before joining** (fan-out), **de-duplicate** (`SELECT DISTINCT *`), and **LEFT JOIN** so loans without payments still count. Each one changes the answer. In an interview, naming all three, even if you only had time to fix two, is a strong signal."}, {"type": "md", "text": "### Composite keys and many-to-many\n\nA join on part of a composite key creates a many-to-many match. If you join payments to installments on `loan_id` alone, every payment matches every installment of its loan."}, {"type": "exercise", "id": "l4-composite", "kind": "fix", "ordered": false, "digits": 2, "prompt": "For loan `900194`, return each installment's `installment_number`, `amount_due` and `amount_paid` (sum of successful payments, each counted once; 0 if none). The amounts come out far too big. Fix the join.", "starter": "SELECT\n  i.installment_number,\n  i.amount_due,\n  COALESCE(SUM(p.amount), 0) AS amount_paid\nFROM installments i\nLEFT JOIN (SELECT DISTINCT * FROM payments) p\n  ON p.loan_id = i.loan_id\n AND p.status = 'succeeded'\nWHERE i.loan_id = 900194\nGROUP BY i.installment_number, i.amount_due", "solution": "SELECT\n  i.installment_number,\n  i.amount_due,\n  COALESCE(SUM(p.amount), 0) AS amount_paid\nFROM installments i\nLEFT JOIN (SELECT DISTINCT * FROM payments) p\n  ON p.loan_id = i.loan_id\n AND p.installment_number = i.installment_number\n AND p.status = 'succeeded'\nWHERE i.loan_id = 900194\nGROUP BY i.installment_number, i.amount_due", "alts": [], "hints": ["What identifies an installment? One column isn't enough."], "explain": ""}, {"type": "md", "text": "## Semi joins: \"has at least one\"\n\nOften you don't want columns from the other table; you just want to know whether a match **exists**. A join would duplicate rows for every match. Use `EXISTS`:\n\n```sql\n-- merchants that have at least one loan over $5,000\nSELECT m.merchant_id, m.merchant_name\nFROM merchants m\nWHERE EXISTS (\n  SELECT 1\n  FROM loans l\n  WHERE l.merchant_id = m.merchant_id\n    AND l.principal > 5000\n)\n```\n\nThe subquery refers to `m` from the outer query: it's **correlated**. Read it as \"for each merchant, does at least one such loan exist?\" `SELECT 1` is a convention, since `EXISTS` only checks whether any row comes back.\n\n`IN` with a subquery does the same job:\n\n```sqlstatic\nWHERE m.merchant_id IN (SELECT l.merchant_id FROM loans l WHERE l.principal > 5000)\n```"}, {"type": "exercise", "id": "l4-semi", "kind": "write", "ordered": false, "digits": 2, "prompt": "List the merchants that have at least one **charged-off** loan: `merchant_id` and `merchant_name`, one row per merchant.", "starter": "", "solution": "SELECT m.merchant_id, m.merchant_name\nFROM merchants m\nWHERE EXISTS (\n  SELECT 1\n  FROM loans l\n  WHERE l.merchant_id = m.merchant_id\n    AND l.status = 'charged_off'\n)", "alts": ["SELECT m.merchant_id, m.merchant_name\nFROM merchants m\nWHERE m.merchant_id IN (\n  SELECT merchant_id FROM loans WHERE status = 'charged_off'\n)", "SELECT DISTINCT m.merchant_id, m.merchant_name\nFROM merchants m\nJOIN loans l\n  ON l.merchant_id = m.merchant_id\nWHERE l.status = 'charged_off'"], "hints": ["A plain join gives one row per charged-off loan. Either de-duplicate, or use `EXISTS`/`IN`, which never duplicates."], "explain": "All three work. `EXISTS` states the intent most directly and never fans out. `JOIN` + `DISTINCT` works but does extra work and hides the intent. If you reach for `DISTINCT` to fix a join, ask yourself whether you wanted a semi join."}, {"type": "md", "text": "## Anti joins: \"has none\"\n\n\"Customers who never...\", \"loans with no payment...\", \"merchants without...\" are anti joins, and interviews love them. Two reliable spellings:\n\n```sqlstatic\n-- 1. NOT EXISTS (preferred)\nSELECT b.borrower_id\nFROM borrowers b\nWHERE NOT EXISTS (\n  SELECT 1 FROM loans l WHERE l.borrower_id = b.borrower_id\n)\n\n-- 2. LEFT JOIN, keep the rows that found no match\nSELECT b.borrower_id\nFROM borrowers b\nLEFT JOIN loans l\n  ON l.borrower_id = b.borrower_id\nWHERE l.loan_id IS NULL\n```\n\nIn version 2, test a column that can't be NULL in a real match: the key itself is ideal."}, {"type": "pitfall", "title": "NOT IN and NULL", "text": "There is a third spelling, `WHERE x NOT IN (SELECT y FROM ...)`, and it has a trap. If the subquery returns **even one NULL**, `NOT IN` returns **no rows at all**. `x NOT IN (1, NULL)` means `x <> 1 AND x <> NULL`, and `x <> NULL` is never TRUE. Use `NOT EXISTS`."}, {"type": "predict", "id": "l4-not-in", "sql": "WITH t AS (SELECT 1 AS x UNION ALL SELECT 2 UNION ALL SELECT 3),\n     s AS (SELECT 1 AS y UNION ALL SELECT NULL)\nSELECT x\nFROM t\nWHERE x NOT IN (SELECT y FROM s)", "explain": "For `x = 2`: `2 <> 1` is TRUE, but `2 <> NULL` is NULL, and `TRUE AND NULL` is NULL, so the row is dropped. Every row fails the same way. `NOT EXISTS` returns 2 and 3 as you'd expect.", "options": [{"text": "2: the values 2 and 3", "correct": false}, {"text": "3", "correct": false}, {"text": "0", "correct": true}, {"text": "An error", "correct": false}], "errors": false, "prompt": "How many rows come back?"}, {"type": "exercise", "id": "l4-anti", "kind": "write", "ordered": false, "digits": 2, "prompt": "How many borrower accounts applied at least once but **never** got a loan? Return a single number, `n_borrowers`.", "starter": "", "solution": "SELECT COUNT(*) AS n_borrowers\nFROM borrowers b\nWHERE EXISTS (\n  SELECT 1 FROM applications a WHERE a.borrower_id = b.borrower_id\n)\nAND NOT EXISTS (\n  SELECT 1 FROM loans l WHERE l.borrower_id = b.borrower_id\n)", "alts": ["SELECT COUNT(DISTINCT a.borrower_id) AS n_borrowers\nFROM applications a\nLEFT JOIN loans l\n  ON l.borrower_id = a.borrower_id\nWHERE l.loan_id IS NULL"], "hints": ["Two conditions on each borrower: an application exists, and no loan exists."], "explain": ""}, {"type": "rlens", "title": "", "text": "`semi_join(x, y)` is `WHERE EXISTS`, and `anti_join(x, y)` is `WHERE NOT EXISTS`. Same idea, same guarantee: one output row per row of `x`, never duplicated."}, {"type": "md", "text": "## Recap\n\n- Before joining, know the cardinality. Joining to the many side changes the grain.\n- **Fan-out** inflates sums and counts of the one side. Check `COUNT(*)` vs `COUNT(DISTINCT key)`.\n- Fix fan-out by **aggregating the many side first**, then joining.\n- Join on **every** column of a composite key.\n- \"Has any\" → `EXISTS`; \"has none\" → `NOT EXISTS` (or `LEFT JOIN ... IS NULL`). Avoid `NOT IN` with subqueries."}]}, {"id": "05-ctes-and-subqueries", "title": "Building queries in steps", "minutes": 45, "summary": "CTEs and subqueries let you break a hard question into small, checkable steps, which is how to approach every interview problem.", "lesson": "", "date": "", "interview": false, "part": "", "blocks": [{"type": "md", "text": "## One question, several steps\n\nInterview questions are rarely one `SELECT`. \"What share of borrowers took a second loan within 90 days of their first?\" needs: find each borrower's loans in order → find the first and second → compare dates → compute a share.\n\nSQL's tool for this is the **common table expression (CTE)**: a named query defined at the top with `WITH`, then used like a table.\n\n```sql\nWITH loans_per_borrower AS (\n  SELECT\n    borrower_id,\n    COUNT(*) AS n_loans\n  FROM loans\n  GROUP BY borrower_id\n)\nSELECT\n  n_loans,\n  COUNT(*) AS n_borrowers\nFROM loans_per_borrower\nGROUP BY n_loans\nORDER BY n_loans\n```\n\nSeveral CTEs are separated by commas, and each can use the ones before it:\n\n```sqlstatic\nWITH step_one AS (\n  ...\n),\nstep_two AS (\n  SELECT ... FROM step_one ...\n)\nSELECT ... FROM step_two\n```"}, {"type": "rlens", "title": "", "text": "A CTE chain is what you'd write in R as intermediate objects:\n\n```r\nloans_per_borrower <- loans |> count(borrower_id, name = \"n_loans\")\nloans_per_borrower |> count(n_loans, name = \"n_borrowers\")\n```\n\nEach CTE is one of those assignments, scoped to the single query. The habit transfers directly: one CTE per idea, a descriptive name for each."}, {"type": "interview", "title": "", "text": "**Build CTE by CTE and run each one as you go.** Write the first CTE, finish with `SELECT * FROM step_one LIMIT 10`, look at the rows, then add the next. Out loud: \"First I'll get one row per borrower with their loan count, and check that looks right.\" This catches mistakes when they're cheap, and shows the interviewer a method rather than a guess."}, {"type": "exercise", "id": "l5-buckets", "kind": "write", "ordered": false, "digits": 2, "prompt": "Bucket borrower accounts by how many loans they have: `'1'`, `'2'`, or `'3+'`. Return `loan_bucket` and `n_borrowers`. Only borrowers with at least one loan count.", "starter": "", "solution": "WITH per_borrower AS (\n  SELECT borrower_id, COUNT(*) AS n_loans\n  FROM loans\n  GROUP BY borrower_id\n)\nSELECT\n  CASE\n    WHEN n_loans = 1 THEN '1'\n    WHEN n_loans = 2 THEN '2'\n    ELSE '3+'\n  END AS loan_bucket,\n  COUNT(*) AS n_borrowers\nFROM per_borrower\nGROUP BY CASE\n    WHEN n_loans = 1 THEN '1'\n    WHEN n_loans = 2 THEN '2'\n    ELSE '3+'\n  END", "alts": [], "hints": ["Step 1 (CTE): one row per borrower with `n_loans`. Step 2: `CASE` on `n_loans`, then group by that `CASE`."], "explain": ""}, {"type": "md", "text": "## Subqueries: the same idea, inline\n\nA subquery is a query in parentheses inside another. Where you put it determines what it must return:\n\n| Where | Must return | Example use |\n|---|---|---|\n| `FROM (...) alias` | a table | aggregate first, then use it |\n| `WHERE x IN (...)` | one column | membership test |\n| `WHERE EXISTS (...)` | anything | semi/anti join (lesson 4) |\n| `SELECT (...)` or `WHERE x > (...)` | **one value** (a *scalar* subquery) | compare to an overall total |\n\nA scalar subquery is perfect for \"compared to the overall average\":\n\n```sql\nSELECT loan_id, principal\nFROM loans\nWHERE principal > 5 * (SELECT AVG(principal) FROM loans)\nORDER BY principal DESC\n```\n\nCTEs and `FROM` subqueries are interchangeable. Prefer CTEs whenever there's more than one step: they read top to bottom, like a pipeline."}, {"type": "exercise", "id": "l5-share", "kind": "write", "ordered": false, "digits": 3, "prompt": "What share of total principal does each merchant `category` (exactly as stored) account for? Return `category` and `share_of_principal` as a decimal (e.g. 0.183).", "starter": "", "solution": "SELECT\n  m.category,\n  SUM(l.principal) / (SELECT SUM(principal) FROM loans) AS share_of_principal\nFROM loans l\nJOIN merchants m\n  ON m.merchant_id = l.merchant_id\nGROUP BY m.category", "alts": [], "hints": ["The numerator is a grouped `SUM`; the denominator is one number for the whole table: a scalar subquery."], "explain": "Lesson 6 shows a second way: `SUM(SUM(l.principal)) OVER ()`, a window over the grouped result. In PostgreSQL, if `principal` were an integer you'd need `1.0 *` to avoid integer division; here it's a decimal."}, {"type": "md", "text": "## Comparing each row to its group\n\n\"Loans that are unusually large *for their product*\" compares each row to a group statistic. Compute the group statistic in a CTE and join it back:"}, {"type": "exercise", "id": "l5-above-group", "kind": "write", "ordered": false, "digits": 2, "prompt": "Find loans whose principal is more than **three times the average principal of their product**. Return `loan_id`, `product` and `principal`.", "starter": "", "solution": "WITH product_avg AS (\n  SELECT product, AVG(principal) AS avg_principal\n  FROM loans\n  GROUP BY product\n)\nSELECT l.loan_id, l.product, l.principal\nFROM loans l\nJOIN product_avg pa\n  ON pa.product = l.product\nWHERE l.principal > 3 * pa.avg_principal", "alts": ["SELECT l.loan_id, l.product, l.principal\nFROM loans l\nWHERE l.principal > 3 * (\n  SELECT AVG(l2.principal)\n  FROM loans l2\n  WHERE l2.product = l.product\n)"], "hints": ["Step 1: average principal per product. Step 2: join it back to `loans` on `product` and filter."], "explain": "The second version uses a **correlated scalar subquery**: for each loan it computes the average of loans with the same product. It's correct and compact, but conceptually re-runs per row. The CTE version is easier to check step by step. Lesson 6's window functions give a third, often cleanest, option: `AVG(principal) OVER (PARTITION BY product)`."}, {"type": "md", "text": "## Aggregate-then-join, as a habit\n\nThe fix for fan-out from lesson 4 is the most common CTE pattern in analytics: **bring each table to the grain you need, then join**."}, {"type": "exercise", "id": "l5-collected", "kind": "fill", "ordered": false, "digits": 3, "prompt": "For each loan `status`, what fraction of principal has been collected so far? Fill in the CTE so it returns **one row per loan** with `collected`: the sum of successful, de-duplicated payments.", "starter": "WITH collected AS (\n  -- one row per loan_id with the amount collected\n  ____\n)\nSELECT\n  l.status,\n  SUM(COALESCE(c.collected, 0)) / SUM(l.principal) AS collected_ratio\nFROM loans l\nLEFT JOIN collected c\n  ON c.loan_id = l.loan_id\nGROUP BY l.status", "solution": "WITH collected AS (\n  SELECT loan_id, SUM(amount) AS collected\n  FROM (SELECT DISTINCT * FROM payments) p\n  WHERE status = 'succeeded'\n  GROUP BY loan_id\n)\nSELECT\n  l.status,\n  SUM(COALESCE(c.collected, 0)) / SUM(l.principal) AS collected_ratio\nFROM loans l\nLEFT JOIN collected c\n  ON c.loan_id = l.loan_id\nGROUP BY l.status", "alts": [], "hints": ["Inside the CTE: start `FROM (SELECT DISTINCT * FROM payments) p`, keep successful payments, group by `loan_id`."], "explain": "Paid-off loans come out above 1.0 because collections include interest. Charged-off loans show how much was recovered before default, typically well under half."}, {"type": "md", "text": "## Stacking results: UNION ALL\n\n`UNION ALL` stacks the rows of two queries with the same columns. `UNION` does the same **and removes duplicate rows**, which is slower and usually not what you want.\n\n```sql\nSELECT 'application' AS event, applied_at AS event_time\nFROM applications WHERE borrower_id = 200364\nUNION ALL\nSELECT 'loan', CAST(originated_date AS TIMESTAMP)\nFROM loans WHERE borrower_id = 200364\nORDER BY event_time\n```\n\nLook at the result: each loan sorts *before* the application it came from. Casting a date to a timestamp makes it midnight, earlier than the afternoon application. Mixing dates and timestamps is the subject of lesson 8."}, {"type": "predict", "id": "l5-union", "sql": "SELECT product FROM loans\nUNION\nSELECT product FROM loans", "explain": "`UNION` de-duplicates the *combined* result, so only the two distinct products survive. `UNION ALL` would return 17,774 rows. Default to `UNION ALL`, and only use `UNION` when you mean \"and remove duplicates\".", "options": [{"text": "17,774", "correct": false}, {"text": "8,887", "correct": false}, {"text": "2", "correct": true}, {"text": "4", "correct": false}], "errors": false, "prompt": "How many rows?"}, {"type": "rlens", "title": "", "text": "`UNION ALL` is `bind_rows()` (but columns are matched by **position**, not name). `UNION` is `bind_rows() |> distinct()`."}, {"type": "md", "text": "## Recap\n\n- `WITH a AS (...), b AS (...) SELECT ...`: one CTE per step, named for what it holds.\n- Build and run CTEs one at a time; check each before adding the next.\n- Scalar subqueries give you a single number (an overall total or average) to compare against.\n- Group statistics: compute them in a CTE and join back (or use a window function, next lesson).\n- `UNION ALL` stacks; `UNION` also de-duplicates."}]}, {"id": "06-window-functions-ranking", "title": "Window functions: ranking and dedup", "minutes": 50, "summary": "Compute group statistics without collapsing rows, then use ROW_NUMBER for the three classic patterns: top-N per group, first/latest per group, and de-duplication.", "lesson": "", "date": "", "interview": false, "part": "", "blocks": [{"type": "md", "text": "## Group statistics without collapsing\n\n`GROUP BY` turns many rows into one. Often you want to **keep every row** and attach something about its group, such as each loan's principal next to the average for its product:\n\n```sql\nSELECT\n  loan_id,\n  product,\n  principal,\n  AVG(principal) OVER (PARTITION BY product) AS product_avg,\n  principal / AVG(principal) OVER (PARTITION BY product) AS vs_product_avg\nFROM loans\nORDER BY loan_id\nLIMIT 10\n```\n\n`OVER (...)` turns an aggregate into a **window function**: compute it over a set of rows (the window), but return the result on every row instead of collapsing them.\n\n- `PARTITION BY product`: the window is \"all rows with the same product\". Leave it out and the window is the whole result.\n- `ORDER BY ...` inside `OVER`: orders rows within each partition, needed for ranking and running calculations."}, {"type": "rlens", "title": "", "text": "This is `group_by() |> mutate()`:\n\n```r\nloans |>\n  group_by(product) |>\n  mutate(product_avg = mean(principal),\n         vs_product_avg = principal / product_avg) |>\n  ungroup()\n```\n\n`PARTITION BY` is the `group_by()`, and the result keeps every row, exactly like `mutate()`. The difference: each window function carries its own partition, so one `SELECT` can mix windows partitioned different ways, plus a window with no partition at all."}, {"type": "md", "text": "## When windows are computed\n\nBack to the run order from lesson 0. Window functions are computed **in the `SELECT` step**, after `WHERE`, `GROUP BY` and `HAVING`. Two consequences:\n\n1. **The window only sees rows that survived `WHERE`.**\n2. **You can't filter on a window function in `WHERE`.** It doesn't exist yet. Compute it in a CTE, then filter outside."}, {"type": "predict", "id": "l6-where-first", "sql": "SELECT\n  loan_id,\n  product,\n  principal,\n  AVG(principal) OVER (PARTITION BY product) AS product_avg\nFROM loans\nWHERE originated_date = DATE '2025-12-31'", "explain": "`WHERE` runs first, so the window sees only that day's loans. If you need an all-time average next to a filtered set of rows, compute it in a CTE over the full table first, then filter.", "options": [{"text": "About $333, the average over all Pay in 4 loans", "correct": false}, {"text": "About $347, the average over Pay in 4 loans originated that day", "correct": true}, {"text": "Each loan's own principal", "correct": false}, {"text": "An error: windows can't be combined with WHERE", "correct": false}], "errors": false, "prompt": "Across *all* loans, the average Pay in 4 principal is about \\$333. On 31 December 2025, it was about \\$347. What does `product_avg` show for the Pay in 4 rows here?"}, {"type": "md", "text": "## Ranking: ROW_NUMBER, RANK, DENSE_RANK\n\nAll three number the rows of each partition in `ORDER BY` order. They differ only on ties:\n\n```sql\nWITH scores AS (\n  SELECT 'a' AS who, 100 AS score UNION ALL\n  SELECT 'b', 90 UNION ALL\n  SELECT 'c', 90 UNION ALL\n  SELECT 'd', 80\n)\nSELECT\n  who,\n  score,\n  ROW_NUMBER() OVER (ORDER BY score DESC) AS row_number,\n  RANK()       OVER (ORDER BY score DESC) AS rank,\n  DENSE_RANK() OVER (ORDER BY score DESC) AS dense_rank\nFROM scores\n```\n\n| Function | Ties get | After a tie | Use it for |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| `ROW_NUMBER()` | different numbers (arbitrary order!) | continues 1, 2, 3, 4 | picking exactly one row per group |\n| `RANK()` | the same number | skips: 1, 2, 2, **4** | competition ranking |\n| `DENSE_RANK()` | the same number | no gap: 1, 2, 2, **3** | \"top 3 distinct values\" |"}, {"type": "predict", "id": "l6-rank", "sql": "SELECT RANK() OVER (ORDER BY score DESC) AS rnk\nFROM (SELECT 100 AS score UNION ALL SELECT 90 UNION ALL SELECT 90 UNION ALL SELECT 80) s", "explain": "Two rows tie at rank 2, so `RANK` skips 3 and the next row is 4. `DENSE_RANK` would give 3.", "options": [{"text": "3", "correct": false}, {"text": "4", "correct": true}, {"text": "2", "correct": false}], "errors": false, "prompt": "With scores 100, 90, 90, 80, what is `RANK()` for the score of 80 (ordered high to low)?"}, {"type": "rlens", "title": "", "text": "`row_number()`, `min_rank()` and `dense_rank()` in dplyr are the same three functions (`min_rank` is SQL's `RANK`). One difference: dplyr's `row_number()` breaks ties by current row order, and SQL has no row order. **Add a tie-breaker to `ORDER BY`** (usually an ID) so `ROW_NUMBER` is deterministic."}, {"type": "md", "text": "## Pattern 1: first or latest row per group\n\nNumber the rows in each partition, then keep row 1. Because you can't filter a window in `WHERE`, this always takes a CTE:\n\n```sql\nWITH ranked AS (\n  SELECT\n    l.*,\n    ROW_NUMBER() OVER (\n      PARTITION BY borrower_id\n      ORDER BY originated_date DESC, loan_id DESC\n    ) AS rn\n  FROM loans l\n)\nSELECT borrower_id, loan_id, originated_date, principal\nFROM ranked\nWHERE rn = 1          -- each borrower's most recent loan\nORDER BY borrower_id\nLIMIT 10\n```"}, {"type": "exercise", "id": "l6-first-loan", "kind": "write", "ordered": false, "digits": 2, "prompt": "Return each borrower's **first** loan: `borrower_id`, `loan_id`, `originated_date`. If a borrower took two loans on the same first day, pick the lower `loan_id`.", "starter": "", "solution": "WITH ranked AS (\n  SELECT\n    borrower_id,\n    loan_id,\n    originated_date,\n    ROW_NUMBER() OVER (\n      PARTITION BY borrower_id\n      ORDER BY originated_date, loan_id\n    ) AS rn\n  FROM loans\n)\nSELECT borrower_id, loan_id, originated_date\nFROM ranked\nWHERE rn = 1", "alts": [], "hints": ["Same shape as the example above, but ascending order, so earliest first.", "The tie-breaker goes in the window's `ORDER BY`: `ORDER BY originated_date, loan_id`."], "explain": "Why not `MIN(originated_date)` with `GROUP BY borrower_id`? That gives the first *date*, but not which loan it was. Joining back on the date breaks when there are two loans that day. `ROW_NUMBER` returns the whole row, tie-break included."}, {"type": "exercise", "id": "l6-where-window", "kind": "fix", "ordered": false, "digits": 2, "prompt": "Return each borrower's **most recent** application: `borrower_id`, `application_id`, `applied_at`. This doesn't run. Fix it.", "starter": "SELECT\n  borrower_id,\n  application_id,\n  applied_at,\n  ROW_NUMBER() OVER (PARTITION BY borrower_id ORDER BY applied_at DESC) AS rn\nFROM applications\nWHERE rn = 1", "solution": "WITH ranked AS (\n  SELECT\n    borrower_id,\n    application_id,\n    applied_at,\n    ROW_NUMBER() OVER (PARTITION BY borrower_id ORDER BY applied_at DESC, application_id DESC) AS rn\n  FROM applications\n)\nSELECT borrower_id, application_id, applied_at\nFROM ranked\nWHERE rn = 1", "alts": [], "hints": ["`WHERE` runs before the window exists. Compute `rn` inside a CTE, then filter on it outside."], "explain": ""}, {"type": "dialect", "title": "", "text": "Snowflake, BigQuery, Databricks and DuckDB support `QUALIFY`, a `WHERE` for window functions, so no CTE is needed: `... QUALIFY ROW_NUMBER() OVER (...) = 1`. PostgreSQL, MySQL and SQL Server do **not**. Know it exists; default to the CTE version unless you're told the dialect."}, {"type": "md", "text": "## Pattern 2: top N per group\n\nThe same pattern with `rn <= N`:"}, {"type": "exercise", "id": "l6-top3", "kind": "write", "ordered": false, "digits": 2, "prompt": "For loans originated in 2025, find the **three largest loans in each product**: `product`, `loan_id`, `principal`. Break ties on `principal` with the lower `loan_id`.", "starter": "", "solution": "WITH ranked AS (\n  SELECT\n    product,\n    loan_id,\n    principal,\n    ROW_NUMBER() OVER (PARTITION BY product ORDER BY principal DESC, loan_id) AS rn\n  FROM loans\n  WHERE originated_date >= DATE '2025-01-01'\n)\nSELECT product, loan_id, principal\nFROM ranked\nWHERE rn <= 3", "alts": [], "hints": ["Filter to 2025 inside the CTE (before the window), partition by product, order by principal descending."], "explain": "`ROW_NUMBER` guarantees exactly three per product. If the question were \"the top three principal *amounts*, including ties\", you'd use `DENSE_RANK` and might get more than three rows. Asking which one the interviewer wants is a good clarifying question."}, {"type": "md", "text": "## Pattern 3: de-duplication\n\n`SELECT DISTINCT *` only removes rows that are identical in **every** column. Real duplicates are often messier: the same `payment_id` loaded twice with a different load timestamp, say. `ROW_NUMBER` handles both: partition by whatever *should* be unique, keep row 1.\n\n```sql\nWITH deduped AS (\n  SELECT\n    p.*,\n    ROW_NUMBER() OVER (PARTITION BY payment_id ORDER BY paid_at) AS rn\n  FROM payments p\n)\nSELECT COUNT(*) AS n_payments, COUNT(DISTINCT payment_id) AS n_ids\nFROM deduped\nWHERE rn = 1\n```"}, {"type": "exercise", "id": "l6-dedup", "kind": "write", "ordered": false, "digits": 2, "prompt": "What is the total amount of **successful** payments once duplicate `payment_id`s are removed? Return one number, `total_collected`.", "starter": "", "solution": "WITH deduped AS (\n  SELECT\n    p.*,\n    ROW_NUMBER() OVER (PARTITION BY payment_id ORDER BY paid_at) AS rn\n  FROM payments p\n)\nSELECT SUM(amount) AS total_collected\nFROM deduped\nWHERE rn = 1\n  AND status = 'succeeded'", "alts": ["SELECT SUM(amount) AS total_collected\nFROM (SELECT DISTINCT * FROM payments) p\nWHERE status = 'succeeded'"], "hints": ["Number rows within each `payment_id`, keep `rn = 1`, then sum successful amounts."], "explain": ""}, {"type": "interview", "title": "", "text": "When you de-duplicate, say what you're treating as the unique key and why: \"payment_id should be unique, so I'll keep one row per payment_id.\" If you can, show the evidence: `COUNT(*)` vs `COUNT(DISTINCT payment_id)`."}, {"type": "md", "text": "## Windows over grouped results\n\nA window can wrap an aggregate. This runs after `GROUP BY`, so it sees one row per group:\n\n```sql\nSELECT\n  product,\n  SUM(principal) AS total_principal,\n  SUM(principal) / SUM(SUM(principal)) OVER () AS share_of_total\nFROM loans\nGROUP BY product\n```\n\n`SUM(SUM(principal)) OVER ()` reads as: the sum, across all groups, of each group's sum. It looks strange the first time; it's the window equivalent of lesson 5's scalar subquery.\n\n## Recap\n\n- `fn() OVER (PARTITION BY ... ORDER BY ...)` computes per group **without collapsing rows**, like `group_by() |> mutate()`.\n- Windows run in the `SELECT` step: they see only rows that passed `WHERE`, and can't be filtered in `WHERE`, so use a CTE.\n- `ROW_NUMBER` (one per row), `RANK` (ties share, then skip), `DENSE_RANK` (ties share, no skip).\n- Always add a tie-breaker to the window's `ORDER BY`.\n- Three patterns: first/latest per group, top-N per group, de-duplication: all `ROW_NUMBER` + CTE + `WHERE rn ...`."}]}, {"id": "07-window-functions-sequences", "title": "Window functions: sequences and running totals", "minutes": 45, "summary": "LAG and LEAD for comparing a row to its neighbours, running totals, moving averages, and the window-frame default that catches people out.", "lesson": "", "date": "", "interview": false, "part": "", "blocks": [{"type": "md", "text": "## Looking at the previous row\n\n`LAG(x)` returns `x` from the previous row in the window's order, and `LEAD(x)` from the next. They're how you compute changes over time and gaps between events.\n\n```sql\nWITH monthly AS (\n  SELECT\n    EXTRACT(MONTH FROM originated_date) AS month,\n    COUNT(*) AS n_loans\n  FROM loans\n  WHERE originated_date >= DATE '2025-01-01'\n  GROUP BY EXTRACT(MONTH FROM originated_date)\n)\nSELECT\n  month,\n  n_loans,\n  LAG(n_loans) OVER (ORDER BY month) AS prev_month,\n  n_loans - LAG(n_loans) OVER (ORDER BY month) AS change\nFROM monthly\nORDER BY month\n```\n\nThe first row has no previous row, so `LAG` returns NULL, and so does the `change` calculated from it. `LAG(x, 2)` looks two rows back, and `LAG(x, 1, 0)` supplies a default instead of NULL."}, {"type": "rlens", "title": "", "text": "`lag()` and `lead()` in dplyr are the same idea. The important difference: **dplyr uses the data frame's current row order** unless you pass `order_by =`; **SQL requires the order in `OVER (ORDER BY ...)`**, because tables have no row order. Add `PARTITION BY` where you'd `group_by()`, for example per borrower."}, {"type": "exercise", "id": "l7-mom", "kind": "write", "ordered": true, "digits": 2, "prompt": "For 2025, return each month's loan count and the change versus the previous month: `month` (1–12), `n_loans`, `prev_n_loans`, `pct_change` (as a decimal, so 0.05 for +5%). Sort by month.", "starter": "", "solution": "WITH monthly AS (\n  SELECT\n    EXTRACT(MONTH FROM originated_date) AS month,\n    COUNT(*) AS n_loans\n  FROM loans\n  WHERE originated_date >= DATE '2025-01-01'\n  GROUP BY EXTRACT(MONTH FROM originated_date)\n)\nSELECT\n  month,\n  n_loans,\n  LAG(n_loans) OVER (ORDER BY month) AS prev_n_loans,\n  1.0 * (n_loans - LAG(n_loans) OVER (ORDER BY month))\n      / LAG(n_loans) OVER (ORDER BY month) AS pct_change\nFROM monthly\nORDER BY month", "alts": [], "hints": ["Aggregate to one row per month in a CTE, then apply `LAG` over that result.", "Percent change is `(this - previous) / previous`. Use `1.0 *` so it can't become integer division."], "explain": ""}, {"type": "md", "text": "## Gaps between events\n\nWith `PARTITION BY`, `LAG` compares each row to the previous row **for the same entity**:\n\n```sql\nSELECT\n  borrower_id,\n  loan_id,\n  originated_date,\n  LAG(originated_date) OVER (PARTITION BY borrower_id ORDER BY originated_date, loan_id) AS prev_loan_date\nFROM loans\nWHERE borrower_id IN (200364, 200147)\nORDER BY borrower_id, originated_date\n```\n\nSubtracting two dates gives the number of days between them in PostgreSQL and DuckDB (other dialects use `DATEDIFF`; see lesson 8)."}, {"type": "exercise", "id": "l7-gap", "kind": "write", "ordered": false, "digits": 1, "prompt": "Across all borrowers, what is the average number of days between one loan and the borrower's **next** loan? Return a single number, `avg_days_between`. Only consecutive pairs of loans count; a borrower's first loan has no gap.", "starter": "", "solution": "WITH gaps AS (\n  SELECT\n    originated_date\n      - LAG(originated_date) OVER (PARTITION BY borrower_id ORDER BY originated_date, loan_id)\n      AS days_since_prev\n  FROM loans\n)\nSELECT AVG(days_since_prev) AS avg_days_between\nFROM gaps", "alts": [], "hints": ["In a CTE, compute `originated_date - LAG(originated_date) OVER (PARTITION BY borrower_id ORDER BY ...)`. First loans get NULL.", "`AVG` skips NULLs, which is exactly what you want for first loans."], "explain": "Here aggregates skipping NULLs works in your favour: no `WHERE days_since_prev IS NOT NULL` needed, though writing it does no harm and makes the intent explicit."}, {"type": "md", "text": "## Running totals\n\nAn aggregate with `ORDER BY` inside `OVER` becomes **cumulative**: each row sees the rows from the start of its partition up to itself.\n\n```sql\nWITH monthly AS (\n  SELECT\n    EXTRACT(MONTH FROM originated_date) AS month,\n    SUM(principal) AS principal\n  FROM loans\n  WHERE originated_date >= DATE '2025-01-01'\n  GROUP BY EXTRACT(MONTH FROM originated_date)\n)\nSELECT\n  month,\n  principal,\n  SUM(principal) OVER (ORDER BY month) AS running_principal,\n  SUM(principal) OVER ()               AS year_total\nFROM monthly\nORDER BY month\n```"}, {"type": "rlens", "title": "", "text": "`SUM(x) OVER (ORDER BY t)` is `cumsum(x)` after `arrange(t)`. With `PARTITION BY` it's a grouped `cumsum`. `SUM(x) OVER ()` (empty window) is `sum(x)` repeated on every row."}, {"type": "exercise", "id": "l7-running", "kind": "write", "ordered": true, "digits": 2, "prompt": "For 2025, return `month` (1–12), `n_loans` that month, and `ytd_loans`: the cumulative number of loans so far in the year. Sort by month.", "starter": "", "solution": "WITH monthly AS (\n  SELECT\n    EXTRACT(MONTH FROM originated_date) AS month,\n    COUNT(*) AS n_loans\n  FROM loans\n  WHERE originated_date >= DATE '2025-01-01'\n  GROUP BY EXTRACT(MONTH FROM originated_date)\n)\nSELECT\n  month,\n  n_loans,\n  SUM(n_loans) OVER (ORDER BY month) AS ytd_loans\nFROM monthly\nORDER BY month", "alts": [], "hints": ["Aggregate per month first, then `SUM(n_loans) OVER (ORDER BY month)`."], "explain": ""}, {"type": "md", "text": "## Window frames, and the default that bites\n\nWhen a window has an `ORDER BY`, it also has a **frame**: which rows count as \"so far\". The default is\n\n```sqlstatic\nRANGE BETWEEN UNBOUNDED PRECEDING AND CURRENT ROW\n```\n\n`RANGE` means \"every row whose `ORDER BY` value is ≤ mine\", so **rows that tie on the order column are all included together**. A running total over data with repeated dates jumps by the whole day at once, and tied rows show the same total."}, {"type": "predict", "id": "l7-ties", "sql": "WITH t AS (\n  SELECT 1 AS d, 10 AS x UNION ALL\n  SELECT 2, 5 UNION ALL\n  SELECT 2, 5 UNION ALL\n  SELECT 3, 1\n)\nSELECT d, x, SUM(x) OVER (ORDER BY d) AS running\nFROM t", "explain": "With the default `RANGE` frame, both `d = 2` rows are peers and are included together: 10 + 5 + 5 = 20 on *both* rows. To accumulate strictly row by row, ask for `ROWS`: `SUM(x) OVER (ORDER BY d ROWS BETWEEN UNBOUNDED PRECEDING AND CURRENT ROW)`. Even then, the order *among* the tied rows is arbitrary, so add a tie-breaker.", "options": [{"text": "15", "correct": false}, {"text": "20", "correct": true}, {"text": "10", "correct": false}, {"text": "21", "correct": false}], "errors": false, "prompt": "What is `running` on the first row with `d = 2`?"}, {"type": "md", "text": "### Moving averages with ROWS\n\nFrames also give you moving windows:\n\n```sqlstatic\nAVG(n_loans) OVER (ORDER BY month ROWS BETWEEN 2 PRECEDING AND CURRENT ROW)\n```\n\nThat's a 3-row moving average: this row and the two before it. At the start of the data it simply averages fewer rows."}, {"type": "exercise", "id": "l7-moving", "kind": "write", "ordered": true, "digits": 2, "prompt": "For 2025, return `month`, `n_loans`, and `ma_3`: the average of this month's and the previous two months' loan counts (fewer at the start of the year). Sort by month.", "starter": "", "solution": "WITH monthly AS (\n  SELECT\n    EXTRACT(MONTH FROM originated_date) AS month,\n    COUNT(*) AS n_loans\n  FROM loans\n  WHERE originated_date >= DATE '2025-01-01'\n  GROUP BY EXTRACT(MONTH FROM originated_date)\n)\nSELECT\n  month,\n  n_loans,\n  AVG(n_loans) OVER (ORDER BY month ROWS BETWEEN 2 PRECEDING AND CURRENT ROW) AS ma_3\nFROM monthly\nORDER BY month", "alts": [], "hints": ["`ROWS BETWEEN 2 PRECEDING AND CURRENT ROW` inside the `OVER`, after the `ORDER BY`."], "explain": "`ROWS` counts *rows*, not months. If a month had no loans at all, it would be missing from `monthly`, and \"2 preceding\" would reach back three months. With sparse data, build a complete month list first (lesson 8)."}, {"type": "md", "text": "## Other useful window functions\n\n| Function | Returns |\n|---|---|\n| `FIRST_VALUE(x) OVER (...)` | `x` from the first row of the window |\n| `LAST_VALUE(x) OVER (...)` | **careful**: with the default frame, the \"last\" row is the current row. Add `ROWS BETWEEN UNBOUNDED PRECEDING AND UNBOUNDED FOLLOWING` |\n| `NTILE(4) OVER (ORDER BY x)` | quartile number 1–4 |\n| `COUNT(*) OVER (PARTITION BY g)` | group size on every row: handy for \"share of group\" |\n\n## Recap\n\n- `LAG`/`LEAD` compare a row to its neighbours; always give the window an `ORDER BY`, and partition per entity.\n- `SUM(x) OVER (ORDER BY t)` is a running total; `SUM(x) OVER ()` is the grand total on every row.\n- The default frame with `ORDER BY` is `RANGE ... CURRENT ROW`: tied rows are included together.\n- Use `ROWS BETWEEN n PRECEDING AND CURRENT ROW` for moving windows.\n- Aggregate to the right grain (often per month) in a CTE first, then apply the window."}]}, {"id": "08-dates-and-time", "title": "Dates and time", "minutes": 45, "summary": "Dates vs timestamps, safe date ranges, truncating to months, date arithmetic, and filling in missing months. This is where SQL dialects differ most.", "lesson": "", "date": "", "interview": false, "part": "", "blocks": [{"type": "dialect", "title": "Read this first", "text": "Date functions are the least portable part of SQL. This lesson uses PostgreSQL-style syntax, which DuckDB shares. The concepts are identical everywhere; only the spelling changes. Once you know your interview dialect, keep the [dialect sheet](#/ref/03-dialects) open next to this lesson."}, {"type": "md", "text": "## Dates and timestamps\n\n| Type | Example | In this data |\n|---|---|---|\n| `DATE` | `2025-03-14` | `originated_date`, `due_date`, `charged_off_date`, `onboarded_date` |\n| `TIMESTAMP` | `2025-03-14 18:22:05` | `applied_at`, `paid_at`, `created_at` |\n\nWrite literals as `DATE '2025-03-14'` and `TIMESTAMP '2025-03-14 18:22:05'`. Convert a timestamp to its date with `CAST(paid_at AS DATE)`.\n\nWhen you compare a timestamp with a date, the date is treated as **midnight at the start of that day**. That one fact causes the most common date bug in SQL.\n\n## Ranges: use half-open intervals\n\n\"Payments in January 2025.\" The tempting version:\n\n```sqlstatic\nWHERE paid_at BETWEEN DATE '2025-01-01' AND DATE '2025-01-31'\n```\n\n`DATE '2025-01-31'` means `2025-01-31 00:00:00`, so every payment made later on 31 January is excluded. The fix that works for both dates and timestamps, in every dialect: **from the start, up to but not including the next start**.\n\n```sqlstatic\nWHERE paid_at >= DATE '2025-01-01'\n  AND paid_at <  DATE '2025-02-01'\n```"}, {"type": "exercise", "id": "l8-half-open", "kind": "fix", "ordered": false, "digits": 2, "prompt": "Count the **successful** payment rows made in January 2025. Return `n_payments`. This undercounts. Fix it.", "starter": "SELECT COUNT(*) AS n_payments\nFROM payments\nWHERE status = 'succeeded'\n  AND paid_at BETWEEN DATE '2025-01-01' AND DATE '2025-01-31'", "solution": "SELECT COUNT(*) AS n_payments\nFROM payments\nWHERE status = 'succeeded'\n  AND paid_at >= DATE '2025-01-01'\n  AND paid_at < DATE '2025-02-01'", "alts": [], "hints": ["What time of day does `DATE '2025-01-31'` mean when it's compared with a timestamp?"], "explain": "`CAST(paid_at AS DATE) BETWEEN ...` also gives the right count, but wrapping the column in a function can stop a real database using an index on it. The half-open range is the professional habit."}, {"type": "md", "text": "## Pulling dates apart: EXTRACT\n\n```sql\nSELECT\n  originated_date,\n  EXTRACT(YEAR FROM originated_date)  AS yr,\n  EXTRACT(MONTH FROM originated_date) AS mon,\n  EXTRACT(DOW FROM originated_date)   AS day_of_week   -- 0 = Sunday here\nFROM loans\nLIMIT 5\n```"}, {"type": "pitfall", "title": "Day-of-week numbering", "text": "PostgreSQL and DuckDB number Sunday 0. MySQL's `DAYOFWEEK()` and SQL Server's `DATEPART(weekday, ...)` number Sunday **1**. BigQuery's `EXTRACT(DAYOFWEEK ...)` is also 1 = Sunday. Check before you write `= 0`."}, {"type": "predict", "id": "l8-month-merge", "sql": "SELECT EXTRACT(MONTH FROM originated_date) AS month, COUNT(*) AS n_loans\nFROM loans\nGROUP BY EXTRACT(MONTH FROM originated_date)", "explain": "`EXTRACT(MONTH ...)` is just the month number, so January 2023, 2024 and 2025 all land in group `1`. That's fine *within* one year (lesson 7 filtered to 2025 first). For a time series across years, **truncate** instead.", "options": [{"text": "36: one per month of each year", "correct": false}, {"text": "12", "correct": true}, {"text": "3", "correct": false}], "errors": false, "prompt": "Loans span 2023–2025. How many rows does this return?"}, {"type": "md", "text": "## Truncating to a period: DATE_TRUNC\n\n`DATE_TRUNC('month', d)` rounds a date or timestamp **down** to the first instant of its month, keeping the year. It's the standard way to build monthly time series:\n\n```sql\nSELECT\n  CAST(DATE_TRUNC('month', originated_date) AS DATE) AS month,\n  COUNT(*) AS n_loans\nFROM loans\nGROUP BY CAST(DATE_TRUNC('month', originated_date) AS DATE)\nORDER BY month\n```\n\nOther units: `'year'`, `'quarter'`, `'week'` (Monday-start in PostgreSQL), `'day'`. In PostgreSQL `DATE_TRUNC` returns a timestamp even for a date, hence the `CAST(... AS DATE)`."}, {"type": "rlens", "title": "", "text": "`DATE_TRUNC('month', d)` is `lubridate::floor_date(d, \"month\")`, and `EXTRACT(YEAR FROM d)` is `year(d)`. The R habit of making a `month` column with `floor_date()` and then `count(month)` translates directly."}, {"type": "exercise", "id": "l8-monthly", "kind": "write", "ordered": false, "digits": 2, "prompt": "For loans originated in 2024, return one row per month: `month` (the first day of the month), `n_loans` and `total_principal`.", "starter": "", "solution": "SELECT\n  CAST(DATE_TRUNC('month', originated_date) AS DATE) AS month,\n  COUNT(*) AS n_loans,\n  SUM(principal) AS total_principal\nFROM loans\nWHERE originated_date >= DATE '2024-01-01'\n  AND originated_date < DATE '2025-01-01'\nGROUP BY CAST(DATE_TRUNC('month', originated_date) AS DATE)", "alts": [], "hints": ["Filter to 2024 with a half-open range, then group by `DATE_TRUNC('month', originated_date)`."], "explain": ""}, {"type": "md", "text": "## Date arithmetic\n\n```sql\nSELECT\n  loan_id,\n  originated_date,\n  originated_date + INTERVAL '30 days'           AS plus_30_days,\n  originated_date + INTERVAL '1 month'           AS plus_1_month,\n  DATE '2025-12-31' - originated_date            AS days_on_book\nFROM loans\nWHERE loan_id = 900194\n```\n\n- `date + INTERVAL '...'` adds time. Month arithmetic clamps to month end (Jan 31 + 1 month = Feb 28/29).\n- `date - date` gives a number of **days** in PostgreSQL and DuckDB. Elsewhere it's a `DATEDIFF` function, with arguments in a different order per dialect.\n- Timestamp minus timestamp gives an *interval*, not a number. Cast to dates first if you want days.\n\n### Months between two dates\n\n\"Months on book\" (how old a loan was when something happened) is a core lending measure. The portable way to count calendar months between two dates:\n\n```sqlstatic\n(EXTRACT(YEAR FROM later) - EXTRACT(YEAR FROM earlier)) * 12\n  + (EXTRACT(MONTH FROM later) - EXTRACT(MONTH FROM earlier))\n```\n\nThis counts month *boundaries* crossed: 31 Jan → 1 Feb is 1. Dialects' `DATEDIFF(month, ...)` functions mostly do the same."}, {"type": "exercise", "id": "l8-mob", "kind": "write", "ordered": true, "digits": 2, "prompt": "For charged-off loans, how many months after origination did the charge-off happen? Return `months_on_book` and `n_loans`, sorted by `months_on_book`.", "starter": "", "solution": "SELECT\n  (EXTRACT(YEAR FROM charged_off_date) - EXTRACT(YEAR FROM originated_date)) * 12\n    + (EXTRACT(MONTH FROM charged_off_date) - EXTRACT(MONTH FROM originated_date)) AS months_on_book,\n  COUNT(*) AS n_loans\nFROM loans\nWHERE status = 'charged_off'\nGROUP BY\n  (EXTRACT(YEAR FROM charged_off_date) - EXTRACT(YEAR FROM originated_date)) * 12\n    + (EXTRACT(MONTH FROM charged_off_date) - EXTRACT(MONTH FROM originated_date))\nORDER BY months_on_book", "alts": [], "hints": ["Use the months-between formula with `charged_off_date` as the later date. Repeat it in `GROUP BY` (or use `GROUP BY 1`)."], "explain": "Nothing charges off before about month 4, because charge-off happens 120 days after the first missed payment. A metric like \"charged off within 3 months\" would be 0 by construction. Understanding *why* a number is what it is matters as much as the SQL."}, {"type": "md", "text": "### Joining on dates with an offset\n\nComparisons across tables work the same way. \"Payments made more than 30 days after the due date\":"}, {"type": "exercise", "id": "l8-days-late", "kind": "write", "ordered": false, "digits": 2, "prompt": "For **successful** payments made in Q4 2025 (October–December), what was the average number of days between the installment's due date and the payment date, by payment method? Return `payment_method` (show `'unknown'` for NULL) and `avg_days_late`. Count each payment once (remember the duplicates). Early payments count as negative.", "starter": "", "solution": "SELECT\n  COALESCE(p.payment_method, 'unknown') AS payment_method,\n  AVG(CAST(p.paid_at AS DATE) - i.due_date) AS avg_days_late\nFROM (SELECT DISTINCT * FROM payments) p\nJOIN installments i\n  ON i.loan_id = p.loan_id\n AND i.installment_number = p.installment_number\nWHERE p.status = 'succeeded'\n  AND p.paid_at >= DATE '2025-10-01'\n  AND p.paid_at < DATE '2026-01-01'\nGROUP BY COALESCE(p.payment_method, 'unknown')", "alts": [], "hints": ["Join payments to installments on **both** key columns to get each payment's `due_date`.", "Days late = `CAST(p.paid_at AS DATE) - i.due_date`. Cast the timestamp to a date first so you get a number of days, not an interval."], "explain": ""}, {"type": "md", "text": "## Filling gaps: a calendar to join against\n\n`GROUP BY month` only returns months that have data. A merchant with no loans in March simply has no March row, which breaks charts, `LAG` and moving averages. The fix is to generate every month and `LEFT JOIN` the data onto it:\n\n```sql\nWITH months AS (\n  SELECT CAST(m AS DATE) AS month\n  FROM generate_series(DATE '2025-01-01', DATE '2025-12-01', INTERVAL '1 month') AS t(m)\n)\nSELECT\n  mo.month,\n  COUNT(l.loan_id) AS n_loans\nFROM months mo\nLEFT JOIN loans l\n  ON CAST(DATE_TRUNC('month', l.originated_date) AS DATE) = mo.month\n AND l.merchant_id = 1054\nGROUP BY mo.month\nORDER BY mo.month\n```\n\nNote the merchant filter sits in the `ON`, for the same reason as in lesson 3."}, {"type": "dialect", "title": "Generating a series", "text": "`generate_series` exists in PostgreSQL and DuckDB. Snowflake uses `TABLE(GENERATOR(...))`, BigQuery `UNNEST(GENERATE_DATE_ARRAY(...))`, and SQL Server 2022 has `GENERATE_SERIES` for numbers only. A portable fallback: take `SELECT DISTINCT DATE_TRUNC('month', originated_date) FROM loans` as the calendar, since every month has *some* loan, or write a recursive CTE."}, {"type": "md", "text": "## Recap\n\n- `DATE` vs `TIMESTAMP`: comparing a timestamp to a date means \"midnight that day\".\n- Use half-open ranges, `>= start AND < next_start`, never `BETWEEN` with timestamps.\n- `EXTRACT(MONTH ...)` merges years; use `DATE_TRUNC('month', ...)` for time series.\n- `date - date` is days (PostgreSQL/DuckDB); months-between: the year×12 + month formula.\n- Generate a calendar and `LEFT JOIN` onto it to show periods with zero activity.\n- Date syntax is the most dialect-specific thing you'll write: check the dialect sheet."}]}, {"id": "09-lending-metrics", "title": "Lending metrics in SQL", "minutes": 60, "summary": "The handful of metrics a consumer-credit risk team lives on (funnel rates, days past due, charge-offs by vintage), and the SQL patterns behind each.", "lesson": "", "date": "", "interview": false, "part": "", "blocks": [{"type": "md", "text": "Interviewers for a risk and analytics team want to know that you can write the SQL *and* that you understand what the number means. This lesson pairs each metric with the pattern that computes it and the trap that makes it wrong.\n\n## The funnel: applications → approvals → loans\n\n| Metric | Definition | Grain to watch |\n|---|---|---|\n| **Approval rate** | approved ÷ applications | applications, borrowers, or people? |\n| **Take-up (conversion) rate** | loans ÷ approved applications | an approval that isn't used isn't a loan |\n\n`loans.application_id` links each loan back to its application, one-to-zero-or-one, so a `LEFT JOIN` from applications to loans can't fan out."}, {"type": "exercise", "id": "l9-funnel", "kind": "write", "ordered": false, "digits": 3, "prompt": "By year of application, return `app_year`, `n_applications`, `n_approved`, `n_loans`, `approval_rate` (approved ÷ applications) and `take_up_rate` (loans ÷ approved).", "starter": "", "solution": "SELECT\n  EXTRACT(YEAR FROM a.applied_at) AS app_year,\n  COUNT(*) AS n_applications,\n  SUM(CASE WHEN a.decision = 'approved' THEN 1 ELSE 0 END) AS n_approved,\n  COUNT(l.loan_id) AS n_loans,\n  AVG(CASE WHEN a.decision = 'approved' THEN 1.0 ELSE 0 END) AS approval_rate,\n  1.0 * COUNT(l.loan_id) / SUM(CASE WHEN a.decision = 'approved' THEN 1 ELSE 0 END) AS take_up_rate\nFROM applications a\nLEFT JOIN loans l\n  ON l.application_id = a.application_id\nGROUP BY EXTRACT(YEAR FROM a.applied_at)", "alts": [], "hints": ["Start from `applications`, `LEFT JOIN loans` on `application_id`, and group by the year.", "`COUNT(l.loan_id)` counts only applications that became loans. Use conditional aggregation for approvals."], "explain": ""}, {"type": "interview", "title": "The grain question", "text": "\"Approval rate\" sounds simple until you notice declined applicants often **retry within minutes** for less money. At the application grain, one person declined twice then approved once counts as a 33% approval rate; at the borrower-day grain, 100%. Neither is wrong, but you should ask which the team uses, or state the one you chose. Duplicate accounts (one person, two `borrower_id`s) push \"unique customers\" the same way."}, {"type": "md", "text": "### Approval rates by score band\n\nCredit decisions are analysed by **score band**. NULL scores are their own band, not something to drop:\n\n```sql\nSELECT\n  CASE\n    WHEN credit_score IS NULL THEN 'no score'\n    WHEN credit_score < 600 THEN '<600'\n    WHEN credit_score < 660 THEN '600-659'\n    WHEN credit_score < 720 THEN '660-719'\n    ELSE '720+'\n  END AS score_band,\n  SUM(CASE WHEN applied_at < DATE '2024-07-01' THEN 1 ELSE 0 END) AS apps_before,\n  AVG(CASE WHEN applied_at < DATE '2024-07-01'\n           THEN CASE WHEN decision = 'approved' THEN 1.0 ELSE 0 END END) AS approval_before,\n  SUM(CASE WHEN applied_at >= DATE '2024-07-01' THEN 1 ELSE 0 END) AS apps_after,\n  AVG(CASE WHEN applied_at >= DATE '2024-07-01'\n           THEN CASE WHEN decision = 'approved' THEN 1.0 ELSE 0 END END) AS approval_after\nFROM applications\nGROUP BY 1\nORDER BY 1\n```\n\nThe nested `CASE` produces 1/0 for rows in the period and NULL for rows outside it. `AVG` skips the NULLs, so each column is the approval rate *within* its period. Approval for the 600–659 band dropped sharply after the policy change on 2024-07-01.\n\n## Delinquency: days past due\n\nA loan is **delinquent** when a scheduled payment is overdue. **Days past due (DPD)** is measured from the **oldest unpaid installment**:\n\n> DPD as of date *D* = *D* − due date of the oldest installment that is due before *D* and not fully paid. 0 if there isn't one.\n\nLoans are then put in **buckets**: current, 1–29, 30–59, 60–89, 90+ DPD. \"30+ DPD rate\" (share of open balances or loans that are 30 or more days past due) is a standard early-warning metric.\n\nThe SQL needs three steps, and every lesson so far shows up:\n\n1. **Paid per installment**: de-duplicated, successful payments, summed per `(loan_id, installment_number)` (lessons 4 and 6).\n2. **Oldest unpaid installment per loan**: join to installments on both keys, keep those due before the snapshot and not fully paid, take `MIN(due_date)` (lessons 3 and 2).\n3. **DPD and bucket** for open loans: `LEFT JOIN` so loans with nothing overdue get DPD 0 (lessons 3 and 8)."}, {"type": "exercise", "id": "l9-dpd", "kind": "fill", "ordered": false, "digits": 2, "prompt": "As of 2025-12-31, count **open** loans (status `current` or `late`) in each DPD bucket: `'current'` (0), `'1-29'`, `'30-59'`, `'60-89'`, `'90+'`. Return `dpd_bucket` and `n_loans`. Steps 1 and 3 are written; fill in step 2.", "starter": "WITH paid AS (            -- step 1: amount paid per installment\n  SELECT loan_id, installment_number, SUM(amount) AS paid\n  FROM (SELECT DISTINCT * FROM payments) p\n  WHERE status = 'succeeded'\n  GROUP BY loan_id, installment_number\n),\noldest_unpaid AS (        -- step 2: one row per loan with an overdue installment\n  ____\n),\ndpd AS (                  -- step 3\n  SELECT\n    l.loan_id,\n    COALESCE(DATE '2025-12-31' - ou.oldest_due, 0) AS dpd\n  FROM loans l\n  LEFT JOIN oldest_unpaid ou\n    ON ou.loan_id = l.loan_id\n  WHERE l.status IN ('current', 'late')\n)\nSELECT\n  CASE\n    WHEN dpd = 0 THEN 'current'\n    WHEN dpd < 30 THEN '1-29'\n    WHEN dpd < 60 THEN '30-59'\n    WHEN dpd < 90 THEN '60-89'\n    ELSE '90+'\n  END AS dpd_bucket,\n  COUNT(*) AS n_loans\nFROM dpd\nGROUP BY 1", "solution": "WITH paid AS (\n  SELECT loan_id, installment_number, SUM(amount) AS paid\n  FROM (SELECT DISTINCT * FROM payments) p\n  WHERE status = 'succeeded'\n  GROUP BY loan_id, installment_number\n),\noldest_unpaid AS (\n  SELECT\n    i.loan_id,\n    MIN(i.due_date) AS oldest_due\n  FROM installments i\n  LEFT JOIN paid pd\n    ON pd.loan_id = i.loan_id\n   AND pd.installment_number = i.installment_number\n  WHERE i.due_date < DATE '2025-12-31'\n    AND COALESCE(pd.paid, 0) < i.amount_due\n  GROUP BY i.loan_id\n),\ndpd AS (\n  SELECT\n    l.loan_id,\n    COALESCE(DATE '2025-12-31' - ou.oldest_due, 0) AS dpd\n  FROM loans l\n  LEFT JOIN oldest_unpaid ou\n    ON ou.loan_id = l.loan_id\n  WHERE l.status IN ('current', 'late')\n)\nSELECT\n  CASE\n    WHEN dpd = 0 THEN 'current'\n    WHEN dpd < 30 THEN '1-29'\n    WHEN dpd < 60 THEN '30-59'\n    WHEN dpd < 90 THEN '60-89'\n    ELSE '90+'\n  END AS dpd_bucket,\n  COUNT(*) AS n_loans\nFROM dpd\nGROUP BY 1", "alts": [], "hints": ["Start `FROM installments i LEFT JOIN paid pd` on **both** key columns. Why LEFT? Installments with no payment at all must still count as unpaid.", "Keep installments due before the snapshot (`i.due_date < DATE '2025-12-31'`) where `COALESCE(pd.paid, 0) < i.amount_due`, then `MIN(i.due_date)` per loan."], "explain": "Three traps this avoids: **future installments** (due after the snapshot, so not overdue yet), **partial payments** (an installment with a payment isn't necessarily paid; compare the sum with `amount_due`), and **installments with no payments at all** (an inner join would hide exactly the loans you're looking for). As a check, the non-current buckets add up to the 249 loans with status `late`."}, {"type": "md", "text": "## Charge-offs and vintages\n\nA **charge-off** is the lender writing a loan off as a loss. Here that happens 120 days after the first missed payment. The headline risk metric is the **charge-off rate**: charged-off loans (or dollars) ÷ loans (or dollars) originated."}, {"type": "predict", "id": "l9-young", "sql": "SELECT\n  EXTRACT(YEAR FROM originated_date) AS orig_year,\n  COUNT(*) AS n_loans,\n  AVG(CASE WHEN status = 'charged_off' THEN 1.0 ELSE 0 END) AS charge_off_rate\nFROM loans\nGROUP BY 1\nORDER BY 1", "explain": "Charge-off takes at least ~4 months from origination, and most 2025 loans were made in the second half of the year. They haven't had time to go bad. The policy change helps too, but you can't measure it from this table, because the years are at different ages. This is the single most important idea in credit analytics: **only compare loans at the same age.**", "options": [{"text": "Credit policy tightened in July 2024, so 2025 borrowers are much safer", "correct": false}, {"text": "Most 2025 loans are too young to have charged off yet", "correct": true}, {"text": "2025 has more Pay in 4 loans, which never charge off", "correct": false}, {"text": "Duplicate payments make 2025 look better", "correct": false}], "errors": false, "prompt": "Charge-off rate by origination year comes out 2023: 6.5%, 2024: 4.7%, 2025: 1.1%. What's the main reason 2025 looks so good?"}, {"type": "md", "text": "### Vintage analysis: compare like with like\n\nA **vintage** is a cohort of loans originated in the same period (here, a quarter). To compare vintages fairly, measure every vintage over the **same window**: say, \"charged off within 12 months of origination\". Then include only vintages that have **had** 12 months: originated on or before 2024-12-31 for a 2025-12-31 snapshot."}, {"type": "exercise", "id": "l9-vintage", "kind": "write", "ordered": false, "digits": 3, "prompt": "By origination quarter, return `vintage` (first day of the quarter), `n_loans`, and `co_rate_12m`: the share of loans charged off **within 12 months of origination** (`charged_off_date < originated_date + INTERVAL '12 months'`). Include only vintages with a full 12 months of history.", "starter": "", "solution": "SELECT\n  CAST(DATE_TRUNC('quarter', originated_date) AS DATE) AS vintage,\n  COUNT(*) AS n_loans,\n  AVG(CASE WHEN charged_off_date < originated_date + INTERVAL '12 months'\n           THEN 1.0 ELSE 0 END) AS co_rate_12m\nFROM loans\nWHERE originated_date < DATE '2025-01-01'\nGROUP BY CAST(DATE_TRUNC('quarter', originated_date) AS DATE)", "alts": [], "hints": ["`DATE_TRUNC('quarter', ...)` gives the vintage. A loan with `charged_off_date` NULL makes the comparison NULL, so the `CASE` falls to `ELSE 0`, which is what you want.", "Full history: originated before 2025-01-01 means 12 months had passed by the snapshot."], "explain": "Now the policy change shows up: vintages from 2023 through Q2 2024 sit around 5–7%, and from Q3 2024 roughly 2–4%. The **vintage curve** extends this: for each vintage, the cumulative charge-off rate at 1, 2, 3, … months on book, plotted as one line per vintage. Each point uses only vintages old enough to have reached that age."}, {"type": "md", "text": "## Other metrics you should be able to define\n\n| Metric | Meaning | SQL shape |\n|---|---|---|\n| **First payment default (FPD)** | first installment still unpaid N days after it was due | installment 1, paid-per-installment CTE, date offset |\n| **Roll rate** | share of loans moving from one bucket to a worse one, month to month | DPD bucket at two snapshot dates, joined on `loan_id` |\n| **Loss rate ($)** | charged-off principal ÷ originated principal | conditional `SUM`, not `COUNT` |\n| **Collections rate** | collected ÷ scheduled, for installments already due | aggregate payments to installment grain, then compare |\n| **Repeat rate** | share of borrowers taking another loan within N days | `ROW_NUMBER`/`LAG` per borrower |"}, {"type": "interview", "title": "Count or dollars?", "text": "For any rate, ask: **by count or by dollars?** A 3% charge-off rate by loan count can be 5% by dollars if large loans default more. Risk teams usually care about dollars; product teams often count customers."}, {"type": "md", "text": "## Recap\n\n- Funnel rates: watch the grain (applications vs borrowers vs people) and say which you used.\n- Score bands: NULL is its own band.\n- DPD: oldest unpaid, already-due installment, comparing paid amounts with what was due. Exclude future installments, handle partial payments, keep installments with no payments.\n- Never compare loans of different ages. Use vintages with a fixed outcome window, and only mature vintages.\n- Always ask: by count or by dollars?"}]}, {"id": "10-interview-craft", "title": "The interview itself", "minutes": 35, "summary": "A repeatable method for any SQL question (clarify, plan, build, verify, explain) and what to say while you do it.", "lesson": "", "date": "", "interview": false, "part": "", "blocks": [{"type": "md", "text": "## What's being assessed\n\nA SQL screen for an analyst role tests three things, roughly in this order of weight:\n\n1. **Correctness under messiness**: do you notice the duplicate, the NULL, the fan-out, the timestamp?\n2. **Structured thinking**: can you break a vague business question into steps?\n3. **Fluency**: can you write the syntax without fighting it?\n\nYou can recover from forgotten syntax (\"in Postgres I'd use `DATE_TRUNC`; let me check the exact name here\"). It's much harder to recover from a confident wrong number. The method below is built around that.\n\n## The loop\n\n### 1. Clarify (1–2 minutes)\n\nRestate the question in your own words, then ask about what's ambiguous. For this kind of data:\n\n- **As of when?** Snapshot date, and whether \"in 2025\" means originated, applied, or paid in 2025.\n- **Which rows count?** Successful payments only? Open loans only? Exclude charged-off?\n- **Count or dollars?** Loans, borrowers, or people (duplicate accounts)?\n- **Ties and ordering?** \"Top 3\" with ties: exactly 3, or all tied?\n- **NULLs?** No credit score: exclude, or its own band?\n- **Duplicates?** \"I noticed payment_id isn't unique. Should I de-duplicate?\"\n\nIf the interviewer says \"your call\", **state your assumption out loud** and carry on. That's a full-credit answer.\n\n### 2. Plan the grain (1 minute)\n\nSay what one row of the final answer is, then sketch the steps as comments before writing code:\n\n```sqlstatic\n-- final grain: one row per origination quarter\n-- 1. loans with their quarter and a 12-month charge-off flag\n-- 2. aggregate per quarter\n-- 3. keep quarters with 12 months of history\n```\n\nThe comments become your CTE names.\n\n### 3. Build step by step\n\nWrite the first CTE, run it with `SELECT * FROM step1 LIMIT 10`, look at it, then add the next. Narrate briefly: \"This gives me one row per loan with the amount collected. Let me check a loan I know.\"\n\n### 4. Verify\n\nBefore you announce a number, spend a minute trying to break it:\n\n| Check | How |\n|---|---|\n| Grain | `COUNT(*)` vs `COUNT(DISTINCT key)` at each step |\n| Fan-out | does a total match the same total from the base table? |\n| Lost rows | did an inner join or a `WHERE` on a LEFT-joined column drop anything? |\n| Spot-check | pick one ID and verify it by hand: `WHERE loan_id = ...` |\n| Smell test | is a rate between 0 and 1? Do categories add up to the total? |\n\n### 5. Explain\n\nGive the answer, then one sentence of meaning and one of caveats: \"2023 vintages charge off at about 6% within a year, versus 3% after the July 2024 policy change. Caveat: I counted loans, not dollars, and the latest vintages aren't mature yet.\""}, {"type": "predict", "id": "l10-smell", "sql": "SELECT m.category, SUM(l.principal) AS principal\nFROM loans l\nJOIN merchants m ON m.merchant_id = l.merchant_id\nJOIN payments p ON p.loan_id = l.loan_id\nGROUP BY m.category", "explain": "The question doesn't need `payments` at all. Someone added the join for a different column and left it in. Loans → merchants is many-to-one (safe); loans → payments is one-to-many (fan-out). Reconciling a total against a number you trust is the fastest way to catch it.", "options": [{"text": "Duplicate merchants", "correct": false}, {"text": "The join to payments fans out each loan once per payment", "correct": true}, {"text": "NULL categories are being double counted", "correct": false}, {"text": "Interest is included in principal", "correct": false}], "errors": false, "prompt": "You've written a query for \"total principal originated by merchant category\" and the grand total across categories is \\$44M. You know from earlier that the loans table sums to about \\$6.6M. What's the most likely cause?"}, {"type": "md", "text": "## Talking while you type\n\nSilence makes interviewers nervous; constant narration makes it hard to think. A useful rhythm: **say the plan, type quietly, say what you're checking.**\n\n> \"So we want one row per borrower whose first loan was Pay in 4. I'll number each borrower's loans by date, take the first, and use `LEAD` to get the next loan date. Then the share whose next loan came within 90 days. Let me first make sure the numbering looks right… yes, borrower 200364 has four loans in date order.\"\n\n## When you're stuck\n\n- **Shrink the problem.** Solve it for one borrower or one month first.\n- **Write the version you can**, then improve it: \"This counts applications; if we want people I'd de-duplicate accounts by normalized email.\"\n- **Say what you'd do** if you can't remember syntax: \"I want the previous row's value per borrower, a `LAG` partitioned by borrower.\" The interviewer often fills in the syntax.\n- **Don't go silent for more than ~30 seconds.** Think out loud, even if it's messy.\n\n## Timed, unattended screens\n\nOn HackerRank-style tests with no interviewer:\n\n- Read **all** questions first; do the quickest ones first.\n- The checker usually compares exact output, so match the requested **column order, names, sort and rounding** precisely.\n- Test against edge cases yourself; you won't get partial credit for \"nearly\".\n\n## Capstone\n\nUse the full loop on this one. Clarify what you'd ask (the definitions below are the interviewer's answers), plan in comments, build CTE by CTE, verify, then check."}, {"type": "exercise", "id": "l10-capstone", "kind": "write", "ordered": false, "digits": 3, "prompt": "**Question:** Of borrowers whose **first** loan was Pay in 4, what share took **another loan within 90 days** of that first loan?\n\nDefinitions agreed with the interviewer:\n- Work at the `borrower_id` level (ignore duplicate accounts).\n- A borrower's first loan is the earliest `originated_date`, ties broken by lowest `loan_id`.\n- \"Another loan within 90 days\" means their next loan's `originated_date` is at most 90 days after the first loan's.\n- Only include borrowers whose first loan was originated on or before `2025-10-02`, so everyone had 90 days to repeat before the snapshot.\n\nReturn `n_borrowers`, `n_repeat_90d` and `repeat_rate`.", "starter": "", "solution": "WITH ordered AS (\n  SELECT\n    borrower_id,\n    loan_id,\n    product,\n    originated_date,\n    ROW_NUMBER() OVER (PARTITION BY borrower_id ORDER BY originated_date, loan_id) AS rn,\n    LEAD(originated_date) OVER (PARTITION BY borrower_id ORDER BY originated_date, loan_id) AS next_loan_date\n  FROM loans\n),\nfirst_loans AS (\n  SELECT *\n  FROM ordered\n  WHERE rn = 1\n    AND product = 'pay_in_4'\n    AND originated_date <= DATE '2025-10-02'\n)\nSELECT\n  COUNT(*) AS n_borrowers,\n  SUM(CASE WHEN next_loan_date - originated_date <= 90 THEN 1 ELSE 0 END) AS n_repeat_90d,\n  AVG(CASE WHEN next_loan_date - originated_date <= 90 THEN 1.0 ELSE 0 END) AS repeat_rate\nFROM first_loans", "alts": [], "hints": ["Step 1: for every loan, its position in the borrower's sequence (`ROW_NUMBER`) and the next loan's date (`LEAD`), with the same window ordering.", "Step 2: keep `rn = 1`, Pay in 4, and the date cutoff. **Filter after the window**, not before. Otherwise \"first loan\" would mean \"first Pay in 4 loan\".", "Step 3: a borrower with no next loan has `next_loan_date` NULL, so the comparison is NULL and the `CASE` falls to 0."], "explain": "The trap is in step 2: if you put `WHERE product = 'pay_in_4'` in the same query as the window, the window only sees Pay in 4 loans, and you'd measure \"first Pay in 4 loan\" and \"next Pay in 4 loan\", a different question. Windows see only the rows that survive `WHERE` (lesson 6)."}, {"type": "md", "text": "## Before your screen\n\n- Re-read the [pitfalls checklist](#/ref/04-pitfalls).\n- If you know the dialect, read its column in the [dialect sheet](#/ref/03-dialects) and write five date expressions from memory.\n- Take a [mock screen](#/mock) the day before, under time, and talk out loud even though nobody's listening."}]}], "drills": [{"id": "00-select-and-functions", "title": "SELECT, expressions and functions", "minutes": 30, "summary": "Calculated columns, rounding and casting, string functions, custom sort orders, paging, DISTINCT.", "lesson": ["00-how-sql-thinks", "01-filtering-and-null"], "date": "", "interview": false, "part": "", "blocks": [{"type": "md", "text": "These drills cover the everyday building blocks of `SELECT` and `ORDER BY`. Several functions here are spelled differently across dialects; the explanations say where.\n\n## Calculated columns"}, {"type": "exercise", "id": "s0-interest", "kind": "write", "ordered": false, "digits": 2, "prompt": "For loans originated on **31 December 2025**, return `loan_id`, `principal`, `apr`, and `yearly_interest`: principal × APR, **rounded to 2 decimals**.", "starter": "", "solution": "SELECT\n  loan_id,\n  principal,\n  apr,\n  ROUND(principal * apr, 2) AS yearly_interest\nFROM loans\nWHERE originated_date = DATE '2025-12-31'", "alts": [], "hints": [], "explain": ""}, {"type": "predict", "id": "s0-cast-round", "sql": "SELECT\n  CAST(3.7 AS INTEGER) AS cast_int,\n  FLOOR(3.7)           AS floored,\n  ROUND(3.7)           AS rounded", "explain": "In PostgreSQL, DuckDB, MySQL and Snowflake, casting a decimal to an integer **rounds**. In SQL Server it **truncates** (3). Don't rely on either: say what you mean with `ROUND`, `FLOOR` (down) or `CEILING` (up).", "options": [{"text": "3 · 3 · 4", "correct": false}, {"text": "4 · 3 · 4", "correct": true}, {"text": "4 · 4 · 4", "correct": false}], "errors": false, "prompt": "What does this return?"}, {"type": "md", "text": "## Strings"}, {"type": "exercise", "id": "s0-email-domain", "kind": "write", "ordered": false, "digits": 2, "prompt": "How many borrower accounts use each email **domain** (the part after `@`)? Normalize the domain to lower case with no stray spaces. Return `domain` and `n_accounts`.", "starter": "", "solution": "SELECT\n  LOWER(TRIM(SUBSTRING(email, POSITION('@' IN email) + 1))) AS domain,\n  COUNT(*) AS n_accounts\nFROM borrowers\nGROUP BY LOWER(TRIM(SUBSTRING(email, POSITION('@' IN email) + 1)))", "alts": ["SELECT\n  LOWER(TRIM(SPLIT_PART(email, '@', 2))) AS domain,\n  COUNT(*) AS n_accounts\nFROM borrowers\nGROUP BY LOWER(TRIM(SPLIT_PART(email, '@', 2)))"], "hints": ["`POSITION('@' IN email)` gives the position of the `@`; `SUBSTRING(email, start)` takes everything from `start` onwards."], "explain": "`SPLIT_PART(email, '@', 2)` is neater, but only PostgreSQL, Snowflake and DuckDB have it. MySQL uses `SUBSTRING_INDEX(email, '@', -1)`; SQL Server uses `CHARINDEX('@', email)` in place of `POSITION`. You'll also spot one odd domain: real data always has a few."}, {"type": "predict", "id": "s0-concat-null", "sql": "SELECT merchant_name, 'Category: ' || category AS label\nFROM merchants\nWHERE category IS NULL", "explain": "Concatenating anything with NULL gives NULL, like arithmetic. Wrap the column: `'Category: ' || COALESCE(category, 'unknown')`. `CONCAT()` behaves differently by dialect: PostgreSQL's skips NULLs, MySQL's returns NULL. `||` isn't string concatenation in MySQL or SQL Server at all.", "options": [{"text": "`Category: `", "correct": false}, {"text": "NULL", "correct": true}, {"text": "`Category: NULL`", "correct": false}], "errors": false, "prompt": "Two merchants have a NULL `category`. For those two, what is `label`?"}, {"type": "exercise", "id": "s0-label", "kind": "write", "ordered": false, "digits": 2, "prompt": "For merchants onboarded in **2025**, return `merchant_id` and `label` in the form `Summit Apparel (fashion)`: the name, then the cleaned category (lower case, trimmed) in brackets, with `unknown` for a missing category.", "starter": "", "solution": "SELECT\n  merchant_id,\n  merchant_name || ' (' || COALESCE(LOWER(TRIM(category)), 'unknown') || ')' AS label\nFROM merchants\nWHERE onboarded_date >= DATE '2025-01-01'", "alts": ["SELECT\n  merchant_id,\n  CONCAT(merchant_name, ' (', COALESCE(LOWER(TRIM(category)), 'unknown'), ')') AS label\nFROM merchants\nWHERE onboarded_date >= DATE '2025-01-01'"], "hints": [], "explain": ""}, {"type": "exercise", "id": "s0-length", "kind": "write", "ordered": false, "digits": 2, "prompt": "Which merchants have names **longer than 20 characters**? Return `merchant_name` and `name_length`.", "starter": "", "solution": "SELECT merchant_name, LENGTH(merchant_name) AS name_length\nFROM merchants\nWHERE LENGTH(merchant_name) > 20", "alts": [], "hints": [], "explain": "SQL Server calls it `LEN()`, and it ignores trailing spaces; elsewhere it's `LENGTH()`."}, {"type": "md", "text": "## Sorting and paging"}, {"type": "exercise", "id": "s0-order-case", "kind": "write", "ordered": true, "digits": 2, "prompt": "Count loans by status, listed in **lifecycle order**: `current`, `late`, `charged_off`, `paid_off`. Return `status` and `n_loans`.", "starter": "", "solution": "SELECT status, COUNT(*) AS n_loans\nFROM loans\nGROUP BY status\nORDER BY CASE status\n  WHEN 'current' THEN 1\n  WHEN 'late' THEN 2\n  WHEN 'charged_off' THEN 3\n  WHEN 'paid_off' THEN 4\nEND", "alts": [], "hints": ["You can `ORDER BY` an expression, including a `CASE` that maps each value to its position."], "explain": "`CASE status WHEN 'a' THEN ...` is the short form of `CASE WHEN status = 'a' THEN ...`. Handy when every branch tests the same column for equality."}, {"type": "exercise", "id": "s0-offset", "kind": "write", "ordered": true, "digits": 2, "prompt": "Return the **11th to 15th largest** loans by principal (ties broken by lower `loan_id`): `loan_id`, `principal`.", "starter": "", "solution": "SELECT loan_id, principal\nFROM loans\nORDER BY principal DESC, loan_id\nLIMIT 5 OFFSET 10", "alts": [], "hints": [], "explain": "`OFFSET 10` skips the first ten rows. SQL Server and the standard spell it `OFFSET 10 ROWS FETCH NEXT 5 ROWS ONLY`. For \"the Nth largest *per group*\", use `ROW_NUMBER()` instead."}, {"type": "md", "text": "## DISTINCT and top-N"}, {"type": "predict", "id": "d1-distinct", "sql": "SELECT DISTINCT payment_method\nFROM payments", "explain": "`ach`, `debit_card`, and NULL. `DISTINCT` (like `GROUP BY`) treats all NULLs as one value, unlike `=`, which never matches NULL. Yes, it's inconsistent; it's just how SQL works.", "options": [{"text": "2", "correct": false}, {"text": "3", "correct": true}, {"text": "48,516", "correct": false}], "errors": false, "prompt": "How many rows?"}, {"type": "exercise", "id": "d1-distinct-states", "kind": "write", "ordered": false, "digits": 2, "prompt": "Which states have **at least one** account with income above 250,000? Return `state`, one row per state.", "starter": "", "solution": "SELECT DISTINCT state\nFROM borrowers\nWHERE annual_income > 250000", "alts": ["SELECT state\nFROM borrowers\nWHERE annual_income > 250000\nGROUP BY state"], "hints": [], "explain": ""}, {"type": "exercise", "id": "d1-top-scores", "kind": "write", "ordered": true, "digits": 2, "prompt": "Return the **5 highest-scoring applications**: `application_id` and `credit_score`, highest first, ties broken by the lower `application_id`. Make it correct in any dialect.", "starter": "", "solution": "SELECT application_id, credit_score\nFROM applications\nWHERE credit_score IS NOT NULL\nORDER BY credit_score DESC, application_id\nLIMIT 5", "alts": [], "hints": ["Many applications share the top score. You need a tie-breaker.", "In PostgreSQL, `ORDER BY ... DESC` puts NULLs **first**, so without a filter your \"top 5\" would be five applications with no score."], "explain": "DuckDB happens to sort NULLs last in both directions, so you'd get the right answer here even without `IS NOT NULL`, but not in PostgreSQL or Oracle. Excluding NULLs explicitly makes the query mean the same thing everywhere."}]}, {"id": "01-filtering-and-null", "title": "WHERE, NULL and friends", "minutes": 30, "summary": "BETWEEN, IN, LIKE, AND/OR precedence, CASE, COALESCE, and how NULL behaves in all of them. Commit to each prediction before running anything.", "lesson": "01-filtering-and-null", "date": "", "interview": false, "part": "", "blocks": [{"type": "md", "text": "These drills go with lesson 1. They're short on purpose: the goal is to make the rules automatic. For every **Predict**, commit to an answer before you look.\n\n## Ranges and lists"}, {"type": "exercise", "id": "d1-between", "kind": "write", "ordered": false, "digits": 2, "prompt": "Return `loan_id` and `principal` for **monthly** loans with a **6-month** term and principal **from 500 to 1,000 inclusive**.", "starter": "", "solution": "SELECT loan_id, principal\nFROM loans\nWHERE product = 'monthly'\n  AND term_months = 6\n  AND principal BETWEEN 500 AND 1000", "alts": [], "hints": ["`BETWEEN a AND b` includes both ends."], "explain": ""}, {"type": "predict", "id": "d1-between-order", "sql": "SELECT COUNT(*) AS n\nFROM loans\nWHERE principal BETWEEN 200 AND 100", "explain": "`x BETWEEN 200 AND 100` means `x >= 200 AND x <= 100`, which no number satisfies. The low value must come first. No error, no warning: just an empty result.", "options": [{"text": "The number of loans between 100 and 200", "correct": false}, {"text": "0", "correct": true}, {"text": "An error", "correct": false}], "errors": false, "prompt": "What does this return?"}, {"type": "exercise", "id": "d1-in", "kind": "write", "ordered": false, "digits": 2, "prompt": "Return `borrower_id`, `state` and `annual_income` for accounts in **California, New York or Texas** with income **above 150,000**.", "starter": "", "solution": "SELECT borrower_id, state, annual_income\nFROM borrowers\nWHERE state IN ('CA', 'NY', 'TX')\n  AND annual_income > 150000", "alts": [], "hints": ["`IN ('CA', 'NY', 'TX')` instead of three `OR`s."], "explain": ""}, {"type": "exercise", "id": "d1-precedence", "kind": "fix", "ordered": false, "digits": 2, "prompt": "Accounts in California or New York earning **over 200,000**: `borrower_id`, `state`, `annual_income`. The query returns far too many rows. Fix it.", "starter": "SELECT borrower_id, state, annual_income\nFROM borrowers\nWHERE state = 'CA' OR state = 'NY' AND annual_income > 200000", "solution": "SELECT borrower_id, state, annual_income\nFROM borrowers\nWHERE state IN ('CA', 'NY')\n  AND annual_income > 200000", "alts": ["SELECT borrower_id, state, annual_income\nFROM borrowers\nWHERE (state = 'CA' OR state = 'NY')\n  AND annual_income > 200000"], "hints": ["`AND` is evaluated before `OR`. Which accounts does `state = 'CA'` let through on its own?"], "explain": ""}, {"type": "md", "text": "## Patterns"}, {"type": "exercise", "id": "d1-like-start", "kind": "write", "ordered": false, "digits": 2, "prompt": "Return `merchant_id` and `merchant_name` for merchants whose name **starts with** `Blue`.", "starter": "", "solution": "SELECT merchant_id, merchant_name\nFROM merchants\nWHERE merchant_name LIKE 'Blue%'", "alts": [], "hints": [], "explain": ""}, {"type": "predict", "id": "d1-like-case", "sql": "SELECT COUNT(*) AS n\nFROM merchants\nWHERE merchant_name LIKE 'blue%'", "explain": "`LIKE` is case-sensitive in PostgreSQL, DuckDB and Snowflake, so `'blue%'` doesn't match `'Blue ...'`. MySQL and SQL Server are usually case-insensitive and would return 10. Portable fix: `LOWER(merchant_name) LIKE 'blue%'`.", "options": [{"text": "10", "correct": false}, {"text": "0", "correct": true}], "errors": false, "prompt": "There are 10 merchants whose names start with `Blue`. What does this return here (DuckDB, like PostgreSQL)?"}, {"type": "exercise", "id": "d1-like-contains", "kind": "write", "ordered": false, "digits": 2, "prompt": "How many merchants have a `#` **anywhere** in their name? Return `n_merchants`.", "starter": "", "solution": "SELECT COUNT(*) AS n_merchants\nFROM merchants\nWHERE merchant_name LIKE '%#%'", "alts": [], "hints": ["`%` on both sides means \"anything before, anything after\"."], "explain": ""}, {"type": "exercise", "id": "d1-clean-match", "kind": "write", "ordered": false, "digits": 2, "prompt": "Find every **Electronics** merchant, however the category was typed (any case, stray spaces). Return `merchant_id`, `merchant_name`, `category`.", "starter": "", "solution": "SELECT merchant_id, merchant_name, category\nFROM merchants\nWHERE LOWER(TRIM(category)) = 'electronics'", "alts": [], "hints": ["Normalize the column, then compare to a normalized constant: `LOWER(TRIM(category)) = 'electronics'`."], "explain": ""}, {"type": "md", "text": "## NULL in WHERE"}, {"type": "predict", "id": "d1-or-null", "sql": "SELECT COUNT(*) AS n\nFROM borrowers\nWHERE annual_income > 100000\n   OR annual_income <= 100000", "explain": "For a NULL income both comparisons are NULL, and `NULL OR NULL` is NULL, so the row is dropped. A condition that looks like it covers \"everything\" still silently excludes NULLs.", "options": [{"text": "6,184: every number is either above or not above 100,000", "correct": false}, {"text": "5,619", "correct": true}, {"text": "565", "correct": false}, {"text": "0", "correct": false}], "errors": false, "prompt": "There are 6,184 borrower accounts; 565 have no income reported. How many rows does this count?"}, {"type": "predict", "id": "d1-not-null", "sql": "SELECT COUNT(*) AS n\nFROM borrowers\nWHERE NOT (annual_income > 100000)", "explain": "`NOT (NULL)` is still NULL, so the 565 unreported incomes are dropped too: 6,184 − 950 − 565 = 4,669. Negating a condition never brings NULL rows back.", "options": [{"text": "5,234 (6,184 − 950)", "correct": false}, {"text": "4,669", "correct": true}, {"text": "565", "correct": false}], "errors": false, "prompt": "950 accounts have income above 100,000, and 565 have no income reported, out of 6,184. How many does this count?"}, {"type": "exercise", "id": "d1-not-ach", "kind": "write", "ordered": false, "digits": 2, "prompt": "Count payment rows whose method is **anything other than ACH, including rows where the method is unknown**. Return `n_payments`.", "starter": "", "solution": "SELECT COUNT(*) AS n_payments\nFROM payments\nWHERE payment_method <> 'ach'\n   OR payment_method IS NULL", "alts": ["SELECT COUNT(*) AS n_payments\nFROM payments\nWHERE COALESCE(payment_method, 'unknown') <> 'ach'"], "hints": ["`payment_method <> 'ach'` alone drops the NULL rows. Add them back explicitly."], "explain": ""}, {"type": "exercise", "id": "d1-or-is-null", "kind": "fill", "ordered": false, "digits": 2, "prompt": "Applications with a credit score of **at least 700, or with no score at all**. Return `application_id` and `credit_score`.", "starter": "SELECT application_id, credit_score\nFROM applications\nWHERE credit_score >= 700\n  ____", "solution": "SELECT application_id, credit_score\nFROM applications\nWHERE credit_score >= 700\n   OR credit_score IS NULL", "alts": [], "hints": [], "explain": ""}, {"type": "predict", "id": "d1-neq-date", "sql": "SELECT COUNT(*) AS n\nFROM loans\nWHERE charged_off_date <> DATE '2025-01-01'", "explain": "Loans that were never charged off have NULL there, and `NULL <> date` is NULL. \"Not equal to X\" only matches rows that *have* a value. This is a classic way to lose most of a table.", "options": [{"text": "8,887", "correct": false}, {"text": "250", "correct": true}, {"text": "8,637", "correct": false}], "errors": false, "prompt": "8,887 loans; 250 have a `charged_off_date` (none on 1 January 2025). What does this return?"}, {"type": "predict", "id": "d1-not-in-null", "sql": "SELECT COUNT(*) AS n\nFROM applications\nWHERE credit_score NOT IN (700, NULL)", "explain": "`x NOT IN (700, NULL)` means `x <> 700 AND x <> NULL`. The second part is never TRUE, so no row qualifies. The same happens with `NOT IN (subquery)` when the subquery returns a NULL. Use `NOT EXISTS`, or filter NULLs out of the list.", "options": [{"text": "13,740 (everything except the 80)", "correct": false}, {"text": "12,962 (scored applications except the 80)", "correct": false}, {"text": "0", "correct": true}, {"text": "An error", "correct": false}], "errors": false, "prompt": "80 applications have a score of exactly 700. What does this return?"}, {"type": "exercise", "id": "d1-charged-june", "kind": "write", "ordered": false, "digits": 2, "prompt": "Which loans were charged off in **June 2025**? Return `loan_id` and `charged_off_date`.", "starter": "", "solution": "SELECT loan_id, charged_off_date\nFROM loans\nWHERE charged_off_date BETWEEN DATE '2025-06-01' AND DATE '2025-06-30'", "alts": ["SELECT loan_id, charged_off_date\nFROM loans\nWHERE charged_off_date >= DATE '2025-06-01'\n  AND charged_off_date < DATE '2025-07-01'"], "hints": [], "explain": "`BETWEEN` is safe here because `charged_off_date` is a **DATE**, with no time of day. On a timestamp column like `paid_at` it would miss most of 30 June (lesson 8). The half-open version works for both, which is why it's the safer habit."}, {"type": "md", "text": "## COALESCE"}, {"type": "predict", "id": "d1-coalesce-same", "sql": "SELECT\n  SUM(CASE WHEN annual_income > 50000 THEN 1 ELSE 0 END)              AS plain,\n  SUM(CASE WHEN COALESCE(annual_income, 0) > 50000 THEN 1 ELSE 0 END) AS coalesced\nFROM borrowers", "explain": "NULL fails the first test, and 0 fails the second, so the result is identical. `COALESCE` only matters when the default you choose would *pass* the condition (`COALESCE(annual_income, 0) < 50000` *would* differ), or when you display or aggregate the value. Think about what your default does, rather than adding `COALESCE` by reflex.", "options": [{"text": "Yes", "correct": true}, {"text": "No, the COALESCE version counts more", "correct": false}, {"text": "No, the COALESCE version counts fewer", "correct": false}], "errors": false, "prompt": "Do `WHERE annual_income > 50000` and `WHERE COALESCE(annual_income, 0) > 50000` keep the same number of accounts?"}, {"type": "exercise", "id": "d1-coalesce-types", "kind": "write", "ordered": false, "digits": 2, "prompt": "For loans originated on **31 December 2025**, return `loan_id`, `product`, and `term_label`: the term as text (e.g. `'12'`), or `'pay in 4'` when `term_months` is missing.", "starter": "", "solution": "SELECT\n  loan_id,\n  product,\n  COALESCE(CAST(term_months AS VARCHAR), 'pay in 4') AS term_label\nFROM loans\nWHERE originated_date = DATE '2025-12-31'", "alts": ["SELECT\n  loan_id,\n  product,\n  CASE WHEN term_months IS NULL THEN 'pay in 4' ELSE CAST(term_months AS VARCHAR) END AS term_label\nFROM loans\nWHERE originated_date = DATE '2025-12-31'"], "hints": ["Try `COALESCE(term_months, 'pay in 4')` first and read the error.", "All arguments to `COALESCE` must be the same type. Turn the number into text with `CAST(term_months AS VARCHAR)`."], "explain": "`COALESCE(integer, text)` fails in most databases: the result can only have one type. `CAST(... AS VARCHAR)` works almost everywhere (MySQL wants `CAST(... AS CHAR)`)."}, {"type": "md", "text": "## CASE and NULL"}, {"type": "exercise", "id": "d1-income-bands", "kind": "write", "ordered": false, "digits": 2, "prompt": "Count borrower accounts by income band: `'not reported'`, `'under 50k'` (below 50,000), `'50k-100k'` (50,000 up to but not including 100,000) and `'100k+'`. Return `income_band` and `n_accounts`.", "starter": "", "solution": "SELECT\n  CASE\n    WHEN annual_income IS NULL THEN 'not reported'\n    WHEN annual_income < 50000 THEN 'under 50k'\n    WHEN annual_income < 100000 THEN '50k-100k'\n    ELSE '100k+'\n  END AS income_band,\n  COUNT(*) AS n_accounts\nFROM borrowers\nGROUP BY 1", "alts": [], "hints": ["Put the `IS NULL` branch first. Otherwise NULLs fall through to the `ELSE`."], "explain": ""}, {"type": "exercise", "id": "d1-case-else", "kind": "fix", "ordered": false, "digits": 2, "prompt": "We want three groups: `'low'` (income below 40,000), `'not low'`, and `'unknown'`. Return `income_group` and `n_accounts`. The starter only shows two groups, and one of them is too big. Fix it.", "starter": "SELECT\n  CASE\n    WHEN annual_income < 40000 THEN 'low'\n    ELSE 'not low'\n  END AS income_group,\n  COUNT(*) AS n_accounts\nFROM borrowers\nGROUP BY 1", "solution": "SELECT\n  CASE\n    WHEN annual_income IS NULL THEN 'unknown'\n    WHEN annual_income < 40000 THEN 'low'\n    ELSE 'not low'\n  END AS income_group,\n  COUNT(*) AS n_accounts\nFROM borrowers\nGROUP BY 1", "alts": [], "hints": ["Where do the NULL incomes end up in the starter?"], "explain": ""}, {"type": "md", "text": "## Put it together"}, {"type": "exercise", "id": "d1-capstone", "kind": "write", "ordered": false, "digits": 2, "prompt": "Approved applications made **on or after 1 December 2025**, for a requested amount **from 1,000 to 2,000 inclusive**, where the credit score was **below 650 or missing**. Return `application_id`, `requested_amount`, `credit_score`.", "starter": "", "solution": "SELECT application_id, requested_amount, credit_score\nFROM applications\nWHERE decision = 'approved'\n  AND applied_at >= DATE '2025-12-01'\n  AND requested_amount BETWEEN 1000 AND 2000\n  AND (credit_score < 650 OR credit_score IS NULL)", "alts": [], "hints": ["Four conditions joined with `AND`. One of them is itself an `OR`. What does it need?"], "explain": "Without the parentheses, `... AND credit_score < 650 OR credit_score IS NULL` would also return every unscored application ever made, declined ones included."}]}, {"id": "02-aggregation", "title": "Aggregation and NULLs", "minutes": 30, "summary": "COUNT variants, aggregates over NULLs and empty sets, HAVING, conditional sums, and safe division.", "lesson": "02-aggregation-and-grain", "date": "", "interview": false, "part": "", "blocks": [{"type": "md", "text": "These drills go with lesson 2."}, {"type": "predict", "id": "d2-counts", "sql": "SELECT\n  COUNT(*)                       AS a,\n  COUNT(payment_method)          AS b,\n  COUNT(DISTINCT payment_method) AS c\nFROM payments", "explain": "`COUNT(col)` skips NULLs, and so does `COUNT(DISTINCT col)`, even though `SELECT DISTINCT payment_method` would show a NULL row. Three tools, three different answers.", "options": [{"text": "48,516 · 48,516 · 3", "correct": false}, {"text": "48,516 · 47,064 · 2", "correct": true}, {"text": "48,516 · 47,064 · 3", "correct": false}, {"text": "47,064 · 47,064 · 2", "correct": false}], "errors": false, "prompt": "There are 48,516 payment rows; 1,452 have no `payment_method`. What does this return?"}, {"type": "predict", "id": "d2-sum-empty", "sql": "SELECT SUM(principal) AS total\nFROM loans\nWHERE principal < 0", "explain": "An aggregate with no `GROUP BY` always returns exactly one row. `SUM`, `AVG`, `MIN` and `MAX` over zero rows give **NULL**; only `COUNT` gives 0. If a report should show 0, write `COALESCE(SUM(principal), 0)`.", "options": [{"text": "0", "correct": false}, {"text": "NULL", "correct": true}, {"text": "An error", "correct": false}, {"text": "No rows at all", "correct": false}], "errors": false, "prompt": "No loan has a negative principal. What does this return?"}, {"type": "predict", "id": "d2-avg-term", "sql": "SELECT AVG(term_months) AS avg_term\nFROM loans", "explain": "`AVG` silently drops NULLs, so this is the average term of monthly loans, even though the query reads like \"all loans\". If the question were \"average term across all loans, with Pay in 4 as 1.5 months\", you'd have to say so: `AVG(COALESCE(term_months, 1.5))`.", "options": [{"text": "About 9.6: the average over monthly loans only", "correct": true}, {"text": "About 6.0: Pay in 4 counted as 0", "correct": false}, {"text": "NULL, because some values are NULL", "correct": false}], "errors": false, "prompt": "`term_months` is 3, 6, 12 or 24 for monthly loans and NULL for the 3,406 Pay in 4 loans. Roughly what is this?"}, {"type": "exercise", "id": "d2-state-income", "kind": "write", "ordered": false, "digits": 0, "prompt": "For each state, return `n_accounts`, `n_reported` (accounts that reported an income) and `avg_income` (average of reported incomes).", "starter": "", "solution": "SELECT\n  state,\n  COUNT(*) AS n_accounts,\n  COUNT(annual_income) AS n_reported,\n  AVG(annual_income) AS avg_income\nFROM borrowers\nGROUP BY state", "alts": [], "hints": ["`COUNT(annual_income)` counts non-NULL values; `AVG` already ignores NULLs."], "explain": ""}, {"type": "exercise", "id": "d2-group-error", "kind": "fix", "ordered": false, "digits": 2, "prompt": "For each state: `n_accounts` and the highest income (`max_income`). The query doesn't run. Fix it.", "starter": "SELECT state, annual_income, COUNT(*) AS n_accounts\nFROM borrowers\nGROUP BY state", "solution": "SELECT state, COUNT(*) AS n_accounts, MAX(annual_income) AS max_income\nFROM borrowers\nGROUP BY state", "alts": [], "hints": ["Every selected column must be grouped or aggregated. Which aggregate gives \"highest\"?"], "explain": ""}, {"type": "exercise", "id": "d2-having", "kind": "write", "ordered": false, "digits": 2, "prompt": "Which merchants have **at least 50 loans** and **more than 100,000 in total principal**? Return `merchant_id`, `n_loans`, `total_principal`.", "starter": "", "solution": "SELECT\n  merchant_id,\n  COUNT(*) AS n_loans,\n  SUM(principal) AS total_principal\nFROM loans\nGROUP BY merchant_id\nHAVING COUNT(*) >= 50\n   AND SUM(principal) > 100000", "alts": [], "hints": ["Both conditions are about groups, so both go in `HAVING`."], "explain": ""}, {"type": "exercise", "id": "d2-success-fail", "kind": "write", "ordered": false, "digits": 2, "prompt": "For each payment method (show NULL as `'unknown'`), count distinct successful and distinct failed payments. Return `payment_method`, `n_succeeded`, `n_failed`.", "starter": "", "solution": "SELECT\n  COALESCE(payment_method, 'unknown') AS payment_method,\n  COUNT(DISTINCT CASE WHEN status = 'succeeded' THEN payment_id END) AS n_succeeded,\n  COUNT(DISTINCT CASE WHEN status = 'failed' THEN payment_id END) AS n_failed\nFROM payments\nGROUP BY COALESCE(payment_method, 'unknown')", "alts": ["SELECT\n  COALESCE(payment_method, 'unknown') AS payment_method,\n  SUM(CASE WHEN status = 'succeeded' THEN 1 ELSE 0 END) AS n_succeeded,\n  SUM(CASE WHEN status = 'failed' THEN 1 ELSE 0 END) AS n_failed\nFROM (SELECT DISTINCT * FROM payments) p\nGROUP BY COALESCE(payment_method, 'unknown')"], "hints": ["Either de-duplicate first and use `SUM(CASE ...)`, or use `COUNT(DISTINCT CASE WHEN ... THEN payment_id END)`. The `CASE` with no `ELSE` gives NULL, which `COUNT` skips."], "explain": ""}, {"type": "exercise", "id": "d2-zero-not-null", "kind": "write", "ordered": false, "digits": 2, "prompt": "For **every** borrower state, the total principal of **charged-off** loans: `state`, `charged_off_principal`. Show **0**, not NULL, for any state with none.", "starter": "", "solution": "SELECT\n  b.state,\n  SUM(CASE WHEN l.status = 'charged_off' THEN l.principal ELSE 0 END) AS charged_off_principal\nFROM loans l\nJOIN borrowers b\n  ON b.borrower_id = l.borrower_id\nGROUP BY b.state", "alts": ["SELECT\n  b.state,\n  COALESCE(SUM(CASE WHEN l.status = 'charged_off' THEN l.principal END), 0) AS charged_off_principal\nFROM loans l\nJOIN borrowers b\n  ON b.borrower_id = l.borrower_id\nGROUP BY b.state"], "hints": ["Filtering `WHERE status = 'charged_off'` would drop states with none entirely. Use a conditional sum instead."], "explain": "`ELSE 0` makes the sum 0 when there's nothing to add. Without it, the sum over only NULLs is NULL."}, {"type": "exercise", "id": "d2-nullif", "kind": "write", "ordered": false, "digits": 3, "prompt": "For each merchant: `merchant_id`, `n_late`, `n_charged_off`, and `co_per_late`: charged-off loans divided by late loans. Return NULL for `co_per_late` when a merchant has no late loans, rather than an error.", "starter": "", "solution": "SELECT\n  merchant_id,\n  SUM(CASE WHEN status = 'late' THEN 1 ELSE 0 END) AS n_late,\n  SUM(CASE WHEN status = 'charged_off' THEN 1 ELSE 0 END) AS n_charged_off,\n  1.0 * SUM(CASE WHEN status = 'charged_off' THEN 1 ELSE 0 END)\n      / NULLIF(SUM(CASE WHEN status = 'late' THEN 1 ELSE 0 END), 0) AS co_per_late\nFROM loans\nGROUP BY merchant_id", "alts": [], "hints": ["`x / NULLIF(y, 0)`: when `y` is 0 the denominator becomes NULL, and so does the result."], "explain": "Without `NULLIF`, DuckDB quietly returns `inf` (or `NaN` for 0 ÷ 0), and PostgreSQL, SQL Server and Snowflake raise a division-by-zero error. Neither is what you want in a report. `NULLIF` makes the result NULL everywhere. The `1.0 *` guards against integer division."}, {"type": "exercise", "id": "d2-min-max", "kind": "write", "ordered": false, "digits": 2, "prompt": "For each product: the first and last origination dates and the largest principal. Return `product`, `first_loan`, `last_loan`, `max_principal`.", "starter": "", "solution": "SELECT\n  product,\n  MIN(originated_date) AS first_loan,\n  MAX(originated_date) AS last_loan,\n  MAX(principal) AS max_principal\nFROM loans\nGROUP BY product", "alts": [], "hints": [], "explain": ""}, {"type": "exercise", "id": "d2-two-cols", "kind": "write", "ordered": false, "digits": 2, "prompt": "Loans and total principal by **product and status** together: `product`, `status`, `n_loans`, `total_principal`.", "starter": "", "solution": "SELECT product, status, COUNT(*) AS n_loans, SUM(principal) AS total_principal\nFROM loans\nGROUP BY product, status", "alts": [], "hints": [], "explain": "One row per *combination* that exists. Combinations with no loans don't appear; for a full grid you'd need a cross join (joins drills)."}, {"type": "exercise", "id": "d2-per-borrower", "kind": "write", "ordered": false, "digits": 2, "prompt": "For each borrower state: `n_loans`, `n_borrowers` (distinct borrower accounts with a loan), and `loans_per_borrower`.", "starter": "", "solution": "SELECT\n  b.state,\n  COUNT(*) AS n_loans,\n  COUNT(DISTINCT l.borrower_id) AS n_borrowers,\n  1.0 * COUNT(*) / COUNT(DISTINCT l.borrower_id) AS loans_per_borrower\nFROM loans l\nJOIN borrowers b\n  ON b.borrower_id = l.borrower_id\nGROUP BY b.state", "alts": [], "hints": [], "explain": ""}, {"type": "exercise", "id": "d2-having-distinct", "kind": "write", "ordered": false, "digits": 2, "prompt": "Which merchants have lent to **at least 100 different borrower accounts**? Return `merchant_id` and `n_borrowers`.", "starter": "", "solution": "SELECT merchant_id, COUNT(DISTINCT borrower_id) AS n_borrowers\nFROM loans\nGROUP BY merchant_id\nHAVING COUNT(DISTINCT borrower_id) >= 100", "alts": [], "hints": [], "explain": ""}, {"type": "exercise", "id": "d2-median", "kind": "write", "ordered": false, "digits": 2, "prompt": "What is the **median** principal for each product? Return `product` and `median_principal`.", "starter": "", "solution": "SELECT\n  product,\n  PERCENTILE_CONT(0.5) WITHIN GROUP (ORDER BY principal) AS median_principal\nFROM loans\nGROUP BY product", "alts": ["SELECT product, MEDIAN(principal) AS median_principal\nFROM loans\nGROUP BY product"], "hints": ["SQL has no `median()` in the standard. The standard spelling is `PERCENTILE_CONT(0.5) WITHIN GROUP (ORDER BY x)`."], "explain": "Medians are where dialects diverge most. `PERCENTILE_CONT(...) WITHIN GROUP` works in PostgreSQL, Snowflake, Oracle and DuckDB. Snowflake and DuckDB also have `MEDIAN()`. SQL Server only has it as a window function (`OVER (PARTITION BY ...)`), BigQuery uses `APPROX_QUANTILES`, and MySQL has none. If the dialect lacks it, say so and describe the `ROW_NUMBER`-and-count approach."}, {"type": "predict", "id": "d2-where-vs-case", "sql": "SELECT\n  (SELECT AVG(principal) FROM loans WHERE status = 'charged_off')           AS a,\n  AVG(CASE WHEN status = 'charged_off' THEN principal END)                  AS b,\n  AVG(CASE WHEN status = 'charged_off' THEN principal ELSE 0 END)           AS c\nFROM loans", "explain": "Without `ELSE`, non-matching rows become NULL and `AVG` skips them, so (b) averages only charged-off loans, like the `WHERE` in (a). `ELSE 0` puts every other loan into the denominator as a zero, giving charged-off principal per loan overall. Both are legitimate numbers; just know which one you're computing.", "options": [{"text": "a and b", "correct": true}, {"text": "b and c", "correct": false}, {"text": "a and c", "correct": false}, {"text": "All three", "correct": false}], "errors": false, "prompt": "Which two of these return the same number?"}]}, {"id": "03-joins", "title": "Joins", "minutes": 30, "summary": "Join syntax until it's automatic, plus the traps — wrong keys, LEFT JOIN with WHERE, counting after a LEFT JOIN, fan-out in averages.", "lesson": ["03-joins", "04-fan-out-and-anti-joins"], "date": "", "interview": false, "part": "", "blocks": [{"type": "md", "text": "These drills go with lessons 3 and 4."}, {"type": "exercise", "id": "d3-three-way", "kind": "write", "ordered": false, "digits": 2, "prompt": "For loans with principal **above 5,000**, return `loan_id`, `principal`, the borrower's `state` and the `merchant_name`.", "starter": "", "solution": "SELECT l.loan_id, l.principal, b.state, m.merchant_name\nFROM loans l\nJOIN borrowers b\n  ON b.borrower_id = l.borrower_id\nJOIN merchants m\n  ON m.merchant_id = l.merchant_id\nWHERE l.principal > 5000", "alts": [], "hints": ["Start from `loans l`, then one `JOIN ... ON` per lookup table."], "explain": ""}, {"type": "predict", "id": "d3-inner-count", "sql": "SELECT COUNT(*) AS n\nFROM loans l\nJOIN applications a\n  ON a.application_id = l.application_id", "explain": "Each loan matches exactly one application (many-to-one from the loan's side), so the row count is unchanged. Applications that never became loans have nothing to match and don't appear.", "options": [{"text": "8,887", "correct": true}, {"text": "13,820", "correct": false}, {"text": "8,887 × 13,820", "correct": false}], "errors": false, "prompt": "Every loan came from exactly one application; there are 8,887 loans and 13,820 applications. How many rows?"}, {"type": "exercise", "id": "d3-wrong-key", "kind": "fix", "ordered": false, "digits": 2, "prompt": "For loans originated on **31 December 2025**, return `loan_id` and the `credit_score` from the application the loan came from. Too many rows come back. Fix the join.", "starter": "SELECT l.loan_id, a.credit_score\nFROM loans l\nJOIN applications a\n  ON a.borrower_id = l.borrower_id\nWHERE l.originated_date = DATE '2025-12-31'", "solution": "SELECT l.loan_id, a.credit_score\nFROM loans l\nJOIN applications a\n  ON a.application_id = l.application_id\nWHERE l.originated_date = DATE '2025-12-31'", "alts": [], "hints": ["Joining on `borrower_id` matches **every** application that borrower ever made. Which column links a loan to *its* application?"], "explain": "Joining on a column that's shared but isn't the real relationship is a common and silent bug: the query runs and the numbers look plausible. Ask \"what does one row on the other side represent, and how many will match?\""}, {"type": "exercise", "id": "d3-not-taken", "kind": "write", "ordered": false, "digits": 2, "prompt": "Approved applications that **never became a loan**, by year applied: `app_year`, `n_not_taken`.", "starter": "", "solution": "SELECT\n  EXTRACT(YEAR FROM a.applied_at) AS app_year,\n  COUNT(*) AS n_not_taken\nFROM applications a\nLEFT JOIN loans l\n  ON l.application_id = a.application_id\nWHERE a.decision = 'approved'\n  AND l.loan_id IS NULL\nGROUP BY EXTRACT(YEAR FROM a.applied_at)", "alts": ["SELECT\n  EXTRACT(YEAR FROM a.applied_at) AS app_year,\n  COUNT(*) AS n_not_taken\nFROM applications a\nWHERE a.decision = 'approved'\n  AND NOT EXISTS (SELECT 1 FROM loans l WHERE l.application_id = a.application_id)\nGROUP BY EXTRACT(YEAR FROM a.applied_at)"], "hints": ["An anti join: `LEFT JOIN loans` and keep rows where `l.loan_id IS NULL`, or use `NOT EXISTS`."], "explain": ""}, {"type": "exercise", "id": "d3-left-where", "kind": "fix", "ordered": false, "digits": 2, "prompt": "For **every** borrower account in Oregon (`'OR'`), the number of loans they took **in 2025**, with **0** for those who took none. Return `borrower_id`, `n_loans_2025`. Fix the query: borrowers with no 2025 loans are missing.", "starter": "SELECT b.borrower_id, COUNT(l.loan_id) AS n_loans_2025\nFROM borrowers b\nLEFT JOIN loans l\n  ON l.borrower_id = b.borrower_id\nWHERE b.state = 'OR'\n  AND l.originated_date >= DATE '2025-01-01'\nGROUP BY b.borrower_id", "solution": "SELECT b.borrower_id, COUNT(l.loan_id) AS n_loans_2025\nFROM borrowers b\nLEFT JOIN loans l\n  ON l.borrower_id = b.borrower_id\n AND l.originated_date >= DATE '2025-01-01'\nWHERE b.state = 'OR'\nGROUP BY b.borrower_id", "alts": [], "hints": ["One of the two `WHERE` conditions is about the left table and is fine there. The other is about the right table. Where does it belong?"], "explain": "`b.state = 'OR'` filters borrowers, the left table, so it stays in `WHERE`. The date condition filters loans, so it moves to `ON`: \"match only 2025 loans, but keep every borrower\"."}, {"type": "predict", "id": "d3-count-star", "sql": "SELECT m.merchant_id, COUNT(*) AS n, COUNT(l.loan_id) AS n_loans\nFROM merchants m\nLEFT JOIN loans l\n  ON l.merchant_id = m.merchant_id\nGROUP BY m.merchant_id\nORDER BY n_loans, m.merchant_id\nLIMIT 5", "explain": "The LEFT JOIN keeps the merchant as one row with NULL loan columns. `COUNT(*)` counts that row (1); `COUNT(l.loan_id)` skips the NULL (0).", "options": [{"text": "0", "correct": false}, {"text": "1", "correct": true}, {"text": "NULL", "correct": false}, {"text": "It doesn't appear", "correct": false}], "errors": false, "prompt": "Merchant 1002 has **no loans**. What does `n` show for it?"}, {"type": "exercise", "id": "d3-never", "kind": "write", "ordered": false, "digits": 2, "prompt": "Which merchants have **never** had a loan? Return `merchant_id`, `merchant_name`, `onboarded_date`.", "starter": "", "solution": "SELECT m.merchant_id, m.merchant_name, m.onboarded_date\nFROM merchants m\nWHERE NOT EXISTS (\n  SELECT 1 FROM loans l WHERE l.merchant_id = m.merchant_id\n)", "alts": ["SELECT m.merchant_id, m.merchant_name, m.onboarded_date\nFROM merchants m\nLEFT JOIN loans l\n  ON l.merchant_id = m.merchant_id\nWHERE l.loan_id IS NULL"], "hints": [], "explain": "Look at the onboarding dates: most of these merchants joined years ago, so they're **inactive**, not new. A quick check like that is how you tell the two apart, and explaining *why* a result looks the way it does is worth a sentence in an interview."}, {"type": "exercise", "id": "d3-semi", "kind": "write", "ordered": false, "digits": 2, "prompt": "Borrower accounts with income **above 100,000** that have **at least one loan currently late**: `borrower_id`, `annual_income`, one row each.", "starter": "", "solution": "SELECT b.borrower_id, b.annual_income\nFROM borrowers b\nWHERE b.annual_income > 100000\n  AND EXISTS (\n    SELECT 1 FROM loans l\n    WHERE l.borrower_id = b.borrower_id\n      AND l.status = 'late'\n  )", "alts": ["SELECT DISTINCT b.borrower_id, b.annual_income\nFROM borrowers b\nJOIN loans l\n  ON l.borrower_id = b.borrower_id\nWHERE b.annual_income > 100000\n  AND l.status = 'late'"], "hints": [], "explain": ""}, {"type": "exercise", "id": "d3-fanout-avg", "kind": "fix", "ordered": false, "digits": 2, "prompt": "Average loan principal by borrower state: `state`, `avg_principal`. Someone joined payments \"in case we need them later\". The averages are now wrong. Fix it.", "starter": "SELECT b.state, AVG(l.principal) AS avg_principal\nFROM loans l\nJOIN borrowers b\n  ON b.borrower_id = l.borrower_id\nJOIN payments p\n  ON p.loan_id = l.loan_id\nGROUP BY b.state", "solution": "SELECT b.state, AVG(l.principal) AS avg_principal\nFROM loans l\nJOIN borrowers b\n  ON b.borrower_id = l.borrower_id\nGROUP BY b.state", "alts": [], "hints": ["After the payments join, each loan appears once per payment. Which loans get more weight in the average? Which loans disappear entirely?"], "explain": "Fan-out doesn't only inflate sums: it **reweights averages** (12-month loans have more payments than Pay in 4 loans, so they count more) and an inner join **drops** loans with no payments. Remove joins you don't need."}, {"type": "exercise", "id": "d3-self", "kind": "write", "ordered": false, "digits": 2, "prompt": "List each pair of accounts that belong to the same person (same email after `LOWER(TRIM(...))`): `account_a`, `account_b`, with `account_a` the smaller `borrower_id`.", "starter": "", "solution": "SELECT a.borrower_id AS account_a, b.borrower_id AS account_b\nFROM borrowers a\nJOIN borrowers b\n  ON LOWER(TRIM(a.email)) = LOWER(TRIM(b.email))\n AND a.borrower_id < b.borrower_id", "alts": [], "hints": ["Join `borrowers` to itself with two aliases. The join condition can be an expression, not just a column."], "explain": "`a.borrower_id < b.borrower_id` does two jobs: it stops an account matching itself, and it keeps only one of (A, B) and (B, A)."}, {"type": "exercise", "id": "d3-cross", "kind": "write", "ordered": false, "digits": 2, "prompt": "For merchant `1054`, show loans per **month of 2025 and product**, with **every combination** present (12 months × 2 products = 24 rows) and 0 where there were none. Return `month` (first of month), `product`, `n_loans`.", "starter": "", "solution": "WITH months AS (\n  SELECT DISTINCT CAST(DATE_TRUNC('month', originated_date) AS DATE) AS month\n  FROM loans\n  WHERE originated_date >= DATE '2025-01-01'\n),\nproducts AS (\n  SELECT DISTINCT product FROM loans\n)\nSELECT\n  mo.month,\n  pr.product,\n  COUNT(l.loan_id) AS n_loans\nFROM months mo\nCROSS JOIN products pr\nLEFT JOIN loans l\n  ON CAST(DATE_TRUNC('month', l.originated_date) AS DATE) = mo.month\n AND l.product = pr.product\n AND l.merchant_id = 1054\nGROUP BY mo.month, pr.product", "alts": [], "hints": ["Build the grid first: every month × every product with `CROSS JOIN`. Then `LEFT JOIN` the merchant's loans onto it."], "explain": "`CROSS JOIN` pairs every row with every row, the one join that's *supposed* to multiply. It's the standard way to build a complete grid before filling in counts."}, {"type": "exercise", "id": "d3-full-outer", "kind": "write", "ordered": false, "digits": 2, "prompt": "For every merchant with loans in **2024 or 2025**, show `merchant_id`, `loans_2024` and `loans_2025`, using 0 for a year with none.", "starter": "", "solution": "WITH y24 AS (\n  SELECT merchant_id, COUNT(*) AS n\n  FROM loans\n  WHERE originated_date >= DATE '2024-01-01' AND originated_date < DATE '2025-01-01'\n  GROUP BY merchant_id\n),\ny25 AS (\n  SELECT merchant_id, COUNT(*) AS n\n  FROM loans\n  WHERE originated_date >= DATE '2025-01-01'\n  GROUP BY merchant_id\n)\nSELECT\n  COALESCE(y24.merchant_id, y25.merchant_id) AS merchant_id,\n  COALESCE(y24.n, 0) AS loans_2024,\n  COALESCE(y25.n, 0) AS loans_2025\nFROM y24\nFULL OUTER JOIN y25\n  ON y25.merchant_id = y24.merchant_id", "alts": ["SELECT\n  merchant_id,\n  SUM(CASE WHEN originated_date >= DATE '2024-01-01' AND originated_date < DATE '2025-01-01' THEN 1 ELSE 0 END) AS loans_2024,\n  SUM(CASE WHEN originated_date >= DATE '2025-01-01' THEN 1 ELSE 0 END) AS loans_2025\nFROM loans\nWHERE originated_date >= DATE '2024-01-01'\nGROUP BY merchant_id"], "hints": ["Some merchants only appear in one of the years. A `FULL OUTER JOIN` keeps unmatched rows from both sides; `COALESCE` picks whichever ID exists."], "explain": "The second version, conditional aggregation over one table, is simpler and avoids the join entirely. When both inputs come from the same table, that's usually the better answer. `FULL OUTER JOIN` earns its place when the two sides come from different tables (e.g. reconciling two systems). MySQL doesn't support it."}, {"type": "exercise", "id": "d3-range", "kind": "write", "ordered": false, "digits": 2, "prompt": "How many loans were taken by a borrower who had **another loan in the 30 days before** it (strictly earlier date, at most 30 days earlier)? Return `n_loans`.", "starter": "", "solution": "SELECT COUNT(*) AS n_loans\nFROM loans l\nWHERE EXISTS (\n  SELECT 1\n  FROM loans p\n  WHERE p.borrower_id = l.borrower_id\n    AND p.originated_date < l.originated_date\n    AND p.originated_date >= l.originated_date - 30\n)", "alts": ["SELECT COUNT(DISTINCT l.loan_id) AS n_loans\nFROM loans l\nJOIN loans p\n  ON p.borrower_id = l.borrower_id\n AND p.originated_date < l.originated_date\n AND p.originated_date >= l.originated_date - 30"], "hints": ["Join `loans` to itself on the borrower, with a **range** condition on the dates instead of equality."], "explain": "Join conditions don't have to be `=`. Ranges like this are called non-equi joins. The plain self-join version can match several earlier loans per loan, hence `COUNT(DISTINCT ...)`; `EXISTS` never duplicates. `date - 30` works in PostgreSQL and DuckDB; elsewhere use your dialect's `DATEADD`."}]}, {"id": "04-subqueries-and-sets", "title": "Subqueries, CTEs and set operations", "minutes": 30, "summary": "Scalar, IN, correlated and derived-table subqueries; multi-step CTEs; UNION ALL, UNION, INTERSECT and EXCEPT.", "lesson": "05-ctes-and-subqueries", "date": "", "interview": false, "part": "", "blocks": [{"type": "md", "text": "These drills go with lesson 5.\n\n## Subqueries"}, {"type": "exercise", "id": "s4-scalar", "kind": "write", "ordered": false, "digits": 3, "prompt": "Each product's share of **all loans** by count: `product`, `n_loans`, `share`. Use a scalar subquery for the total.", "starter": "", "solution": "SELECT\n  product,\n  COUNT(*) AS n_loans,\n  1.0 * COUNT(*) / (SELECT COUNT(*) FROM loans) AS share\nFROM loans\nGROUP BY product", "alts": [], "hints": [], "explain": ""}, {"type": "predict", "id": "s4-multi-row", "sql": "SELECT COUNT(*) AS n\nFROM loans\nWHERE principal > (SELECT principal FROM loans WHERE borrower_id = 200364)", "explain": "A subquery used as a single value must return one row. Decide what you mean: `> (SELECT MAX(principal) ...)` for \"larger than all of them\", or `> ANY (...)` / `> ALL (...)` where supported. (Older DuckDB versions silently picked an arbitrary row instead of failing, which is worse.)", "options": [{"text": "Compares against the first of the four loans", "correct": false}, {"text": "Compares against all four (principal larger than every one)", "correct": false}, {"text": "An error: the subquery must return exactly one value", "correct": true}], "errors": true, "prompt": "Borrower 200364 has four loans. What happens?"}, {"type": "exercise", "id": "s4-in", "kind": "write", "ordered": false, "digits": 2, "prompt": "How many **applications** came from borrower accounts that have **at least one charged-off loan**? Return `n_applications`.", "starter": "", "solution": "SELECT COUNT(*) AS n_applications\nFROM applications\nWHERE borrower_id IN (\n  SELECT borrower_id FROM loans WHERE status = 'charged_off'\n)", "alts": ["SELECT COUNT(*) AS n_applications\nFROM applications a\nWHERE EXISTS (\n  SELECT 1 FROM loans l\n  WHERE l.borrower_id = a.borrower_id\n    AND l.status = 'charged_off'\n)"], "hints": [], "explain": "A plain join to charged-off loans would count an application twice for a borrower with two charged-off loans. `IN` and `EXISTS` never duplicate."}, {"type": "exercise", "id": "s4-correlated", "kind": "write", "ordered": false, "digits": 2, "prompt": "For each merchant, its **largest loan(s)**: `merchant_id`, `loan_id`, `principal`. If two loans tie for the largest, return both.", "starter": "", "solution": "SELECT l.merchant_id, l.loan_id, l.principal\nFROM loans l\nWHERE l.principal = (\n  SELECT MAX(l2.principal)\n  FROM loans l2\n  WHERE l2.merchant_id = l.merchant_id\n)", "alts": ["WITH ranked AS (\n  SELECT merchant_id, loan_id, principal,\n    RANK() OVER (PARTITION BY merchant_id ORDER BY principal DESC) AS rnk\n  FROM loans\n)\nSELECT merchant_id, loan_id, principal\nFROM ranked\nWHERE rnk = 1"], "hints": ["A correlated subquery: for each loan, compute the max principal **of that loan's merchant** and keep the loan if it matches."], "explain": "\"Keep ties\" is `RANK()` (or the correlated `MAX`), not `ROW_NUMBER()`, which would pick one arbitrarily."}, {"type": "exercise", "id": "s4-derived", "kind": "write", "ordered": false, "digits": 2, "prompt": "Among borrower accounts with at least one loan, how many loans does a borrower have **on average**? Return `avg_loans`. Use a subquery in `FROM`.", "starter": "", "solution": "SELECT AVG(n_loans) AS avg_loans\nFROM (\n  SELECT borrower_id, COUNT(*) AS n_loans\n  FROM loans\n  GROUP BY borrower_id\n) per_borrower", "alts": [], "hints": [], "explain": "Aggregating twice (count per borrower, then average the counts) always needs two levels: a subquery or CTE. `AVG(COUNT(*))` is not allowed."}, {"type": "exercise", "id": "s4-chain", "kind": "write", "ordered": false, "digits": 2, "prompt": "Three steps: total principal per borrower account → bucket each borrower as `'under 1k'`, `'1k-5k'` (1,000 up to but not including 5,000) or `'5k+'` → per bucket, `n_borrowers` and `total_principal`. Return `bucket`, `n_borrowers`, `total_principal`.", "starter": "", "solution": "WITH per_borrower AS (\n  SELECT borrower_id, SUM(principal) AS total\n  FROM loans\n  GROUP BY borrower_id\n),\nbucketed AS (\n  SELECT\n    CASE\n      WHEN total < 1000 THEN 'under 1k'\n      WHEN total < 5000 THEN '1k-5k'\n      ELSE '5k+'\n    END AS bucket,\n    total\n  FROM per_borrower\n)\nSELECT bucket, COUNT(*) AS n_borrowers, SUM(total) AS total_principal\nFROM bucketed\nGROUP BY bucket", "alts": [], "hints": [], "explain": "Naming the bucket in its own CTE means the `CASE` is written once, and the final `GROUP BY` can use the plain column."}, {"type": "md", "text": "## Set operations"}, {"type": "predict", "id": "s4-union", "sql": "SELECT borrower_id FROM loans\nUNION\nSELECT borrower_id FROM applications", "explain": "`UNION` removes duplicates across the combined result, *including duplicates within each side*. `UNION ALL` would return all 22,707 rows.", "options": [{"text": "22,707: every loan row plus every application row", "correct": false}, {"text": "6,102", "correct": true}, {"text": "4,772: borrowers with loans", "correct": false}], "errors": false, "prompt": "6,102 distinct borrower accounts appear in `applications`, and every loan's borrower also has an application. How many rows?"}, {"type": "exercise", "id": "s4-union-all", "kind": "write", "ordered": false, "digits": 2, "prompt": "Build one timeline for borrower `200147`: every application, every loan origination, and every **successful** payment (each payment once). Return `event_type` (`'application'`, `'loan'`, `'payment'`) and `event_date` (a DATE).", "starter": "", "solution": "SELECT 'application' AS event_type, CAST(applied_at AS DATE) AS event_date\nFROM applications\nWHERE borrower_id = 200147\nUNION ALL\nSELECT 'loan', originated_date\nFROM loans\nWHERE borrower_id = 200147\nUNION ALL\nSELECT 'payment', CAST(p.paid_at AS DATE)\nFROM (SELECT DISTINCT * FROM payments) p\nJOIN loans l\n  ON l.loan_id = p.loan_id\nWHERE l.borrower_id = 200147\n  AND p.status = 'succeeded'", "alts": [], "hints": ["Three `SELECT`s with the same two columns, stacked with `UNION ALL`. Payments don't have a borrower ID, so join to loans."], "explain": "Columns are matched **by position**, and the names come from the first `SELECT`. Using `UNION` here would merge two payments made on the same day into one row: a real bug."}, {"type": "exercise", "id": "s4-intersect", "kind": "write", "ordered": false, "digits": 2, "prompt": "How many borrower accounts have taken **both** a Pay in 4 loan **and** a monthly loan? Return `n_borrowers`. Try `INTERSECT`.", "starter": "", "solution": "SELECT COUNT(*) AS n_borrowers\nFROM (\n  SELECT borrower_id FROM loans WHERE product = 'pay_in_4'\n  INTERSECT\n  SELECT borrower_id FROM loans WHERE product = 'monthly'\n) both_products", "alts": ["SELECT COUNT(*) AS n_borrowers\nFROM (\n  SELECT borrower_id\n  FROM loans\n  GROUP BY borrower_id\n  HAVING COUNT(DISTINCT product) = 2\n) both_products"], "hints": [], "explain": "The `HAVING COUNT(DISTINCT product) = 2` version works in every dialect and extends naturally to \"at least 3 of 5 categories\". `INTERSECT` is supported in PostgreSQL, SQL Server, Snowflake, Oracle and MySQL 8.0.31+; BigQuery requires `INTERSECT DISTINCT`."}, {"type": "exercise", "id": "s4-except", "kind": "write", "ordered": false, "digits": 2, "prompt": "How many borrower accounts **applied but never got a loan**? Use `EXCEPT`. Return `n_borrowers`.", "starter": "", "solution": "SELECT COUNT(*) AS n_borrowers\nFROM (\n  SELECT borrower_id FROM applications\n  EXCEPT\n  SELECT borrower_id FROM loans\n) never_borrowed", "alts": [], "hints": [], "explain": "Same answer as the `NOT EXISTS` version in lesson 4. `EXCEPT` compares whole rows and removes duplicates; Oracle spells it `MINUS`, BigQuery `EXCEPT DISTINCT`."}]}, {"id": "05-window-functions", "title": "Window functions", "minutes": 30, "summary": "Ranking and ties, top-N, dedup, LAG/LEAD, running totals, NTILE, FIRST_VALUE and LAST_VALUE, shares of a group.", "lesson": ["06-window-functions-ranking", "07-window-functions-sequences"], "date": "", "interview": false, "part": "", "blocks": [{"type": "md", "text": "These drills go with lessons 6 and 7 (the first one leans on lesson 5)."}, {"type": "exercise", "id": "d4-big-borrowers", "kind": "write", "ordered": false, "digits": 2, "prompt": "Which borrower accounts have total principal **more than five times** the average borrower's total principal? Return `borrower_id` and `total_principal`.", "starter": "", "solution": "WITH totals AS (\n  SELECT borrower_id, SUM(principal) AS total_principal\n  FROM loans\n  GROUP BY borrower_id\n)\nSELECT borrower_id, total_principal\nFROM totals\nWHERE total_principal > 5 * (SELECT AVG(total_principal) FROM totals)", "alts": [], "hints": ["Step 1: one row per borrower with their total. Step 2: compare each total with the average *of those totals*, not the average loan."], "explain": "\"Average borrower's total\" and \"average loan\" are different denominators. This is the same grain question as always: the average is taken over the rows of `totals`, one per borrower."}, {"type": "exercise", "id": "d4-rank-states", "kind": "write", "ordered": false, "digits": 2, "prompt": "Rank states by number of borrower accounts, most first, with tied states sharing a rank and no gaps. Return `state`, `n_accounts`, `state_rank`.", "starter": "", "solution": "SELECT\n  state,\n  COUNT(*) AS n_accounts,\n  DENSE_RANK() OVER (ORDER BY COUNT(*) DESC) AS state_rank\nFROM borrowers\nGROUP BY state", "alts": ["WITH counts AS (\n  SELECT state, COUNT(*) AS n_accounts\n  FROM borrowers\n  GROUP BY state\n)\nSELECT state, n_accounts, DENSE_RANK() OVER (ORDER BY n_accounts DESC) AS state_rank\nFROM counts"], "hints": ["\"Ties share, no gaps\" is `DENSE_RANK`. You can rank on `COUNT(*)` directly, because windows run after `GROUP BY`."], "explain": ""}, {"type": "exercise", "id": "d4-second", "kind": "write", "ordered": false, "digits": 2, "prompt": "For each product, the **second-largest** loan by principal (ties broken by the lower `loan_id`). Return `product`, `loan_id`, `principal`.", "starter": "", "solution": "WITH ranked AS (\n  SELECT\n    product, loan_id, principal,\n    ROW_NUMBER() OVER (PARTITION BY product ORDER BY principal DESC, loan_id) AS rn\n  FROM loans\n)\nSELECT product, loan_id, principal\nFROM ranked\nWHERE rn = 2", "alts": [], "hints": [], "explain": ""}, {"type": "predict", "id": "d4-count-over", "sql": "SELECT loan_id, COUNT(*) OVER () AS n\nFROM loans\nWHERE product = 'pay_in_4'\nLIMIT 3", "explain": "`OVER ()` is a window over every row that survived `WHERE`, which is all 3,406 Pay in 4 loans, not the whole table. `LIMIT` runs last, after the window is computed, so it doesn't shrink the count either.", "options": [{"text": "1", "correct": false}, {"text": "3", "correct": false}, {"text": "3,406", "correct": true}, {"text": "8,887", "correct": false}], "errors": false, "prompt": "There are 3,406 Pay in 4 loans. What is `n` on the first row?"}, {"type": "exercise", "id": "d4-lag-partition", "kind": "fix", "ordered": false, "digits": 2, "prompt": "For borrowers `200364` and `200147`, each loan's `days_since_prev`: days since **that borrower's** previous loan (NULL for their first). Return `borrower_id`, `loan_id`, `days_since_prev`. One value is wrong. Find it and fix the window.", "starter": "SELECT\n  borrower_id,\n  loan_id,\n  originated_date - LAG(originated_date) OVER (ORDER BY borrower_id, originated_date, loan_id) AS days_since_prev\nFROM loans\nWHERE borrower_id IN (200364, 200147)", "solution": "SELECT\n  borrower_id,\n  loan_id,\n  originated_date - LAG(originated_date) OVER (PARTITION BY borrower_id ORDER BY originated_date, loan_id) AS days_since_prev\nFROM loans\nWHERE borrower_id IN (200364, 200147)", "alts": [], "hints": ["Look at the first loan of the second borrower. What is it being compared with?"], "explain": "Without `PARTITION BY`, `LAG` walks straight from one borrower's last loan into the next borrower's first. Sorting by borrower *looks* right but doesn't reset the window."}, {"type": "exercise", "id": "d4-share", "kind": "write", "ordered": false, "digits": 3, "prompt": "For borrower `200364`, each loan's share of that borrower's total principal: `loan_id`, `principal`, `share`.", "starter": "", "solution": "SELECT\n  loan_id,\n  principal,\n  principal / SUM(principal) OVER () AS share\nFROM loans\nWHERE borrower_id = 200364", "alts": ["SELECT\n  loan_id,\n  principal,\n  principal / SUM(principal) OVER (PARTITION BY borrower_id) AS share\nFROM loans\nWHERE borrower_id = 200364"], "hints": [], "explain": "With one borrower, `OVER ()` and `OVER (PARTITION BY borrower_id)` agree. Remove the `WHERE` and only the partitioned version still gives per-borrower shares."}, {"type": "exercise", "id": "d4-last-per-day", "kind": "write", "ordered": false, "digits": 2, "prompt": "Declined applicants often retry the same day. Keep only each borrower's **last application per calendar day**, and count what's left. Return `n_applications`.", "starter": "", "solution": "WITH ranked AS (\n  SELECT\n    application_id,\n    ROW_NUMBER() OVER (\n      PARTITION BY borrower_id, CAST(applied_at AS DATE)\n      ORDER BY applied_at DESC, application_id DESC\n    ) AS rn\n  FROM applications\n)\nSELECT COUNT(*) AS n_applications\nFROM ranked\nWHERE rn = 1", "alts": ["SELECT COUNT(*) AS n_applications\nFROM (\n  SELECT DISTINCT borrower_id, CAST(applied_at AS DATE) AS applied_day\n  FROM applications\n) d"], "hints": ["Partition by two things: the borrower and the day (`CAST(applied_at AS DATE)`)."], "explain": "For a count alone, `COUNT(DISTINCT borrower, day)` gives the same number. The `ROW_NUMBER` version also keeps *which* application survived, which you'd need to compute a per-borrower-day approval rate."}, {"type": "exercise", "id": "d4-signups", "kind": "write", "ordered": true, "digits": 2, "prompt": "For 2025, new borrower accounts per month and the running total for the year: `month` (first day of the month), `n_new`, `running_total`. Sort by month.", "starter": "", "solution": "WITH monthly AS (\n  SELECT\n    CAST(DATE_TRUNC('month', created_at) AS DATE) AS month,\n    COUNT(*) AS n_new\n  FROM borrowers\n  WHERE created_at >= DATE '2025-01-01'\n  GROUP BY CAST(DATE_TRUNC('month', created_at) AS DATE)\n)\nSELECT month, n_new, SUM(n_new) OVER (ORDER BY month) AS running_total\nFROM monthly\nORDER BY month", "alts": [], "hints": [], "explain": ""}, {"type": "exercise", "id": "d4-top-ties", "kind": "write", "ordered": false, "digits": 2, "prompt": "The **top 3 merchants by number of loans in 2025, keeping ties** (if two merchants tie for third, return both). Return `merchant_id`, `n_loans`, `rnk`.", "starter": "", "solution": "WITH counts AS (\n  SELECT merchant_id, COUNT(*) AS n_loans\n  FROM loans\n  WHERE originated_date >= DATE '2025-01-01'\n  GROUP BY merchant_id\n),\nranked AS (\n  SELECT merchant_id, n_loans, RANK() OVER (ORDER BY n_loans DESC) AS rnk\n  FROM counts\n)\nSELECT merchant_id, n_loans, rnk\nFROM ranked\nWHERE rnk <= 3", "alts": [], "hints": [], "explain": "`RANK() <= 3` keeps ties and can return more than three rows; `ROW_NUMBER() <= 3` returns exactly three; `DENSE_RANK() <= 3` returns the top three *distinct counts*, which can be many more merchants. Which one is \"right\" is a clarifying question."}, {"type": "exercise", "id": "d4-ntile", "kind": "write", "ordered": false, "digits": 2, "prompt": "Split **monthly** loans into **quartiles** by principal (ties broken by `loan_id`). For each quartile: `quartile`, `n_loans`, `min_principal`, `max_principal`.", "starter": "", "solution": "WITH q AS (\n  SELECT principal, NTILE(4) OVER (ORDER BY principal, loan_id) AS quartile\n  FROM loans\n  WHERE product = 'monthly'\n)\nSELECT quartile, COUNT(*) AS n_loans, MIN(principal) AS min_principal, MAX(principal) AS max_principal\nFROM q\nGROUP BY quartile", "alts": [], "hints": [], "explain": "`NTILE(n)` deals rows into n buckets as evenly as possible. Without the `loan_id` tie-breaker, identical principals on a boundary can land in either bucket, and the answer changes from run to run."}, {"type": "predict", "id": "d4-last-value", "sql": "SELECT x, LAST_VALUE(x) OVER (ORDER BY x) AS lv\nFROM (SELECT 1 AS x UNION ALL SELECT 2 UNION ALL SELECT 3) t", "explain": "With an `ORDER BY`, the default frame ends at the **current row**, so the \"last value\" so far is the row itself. For the true last value write `LAST_VALUE(x) OVER (ORDER BY x ROWS BETWEEN UNBOUNDED PRECEDING AND UNBOUNDED FOLLOWING)`, or flip the order and use `FIRST_VALUE`.", "options": [{"text": "3", "correct": false}, {"text": "1", "correct": true}, {"text": "NULL", "correct": false}], "errors": false, "prompt": "What is `lv` on the row where `x = 1`?"}, {"type": "exercise", "id": "d4-first-value", "kind": "write", "ordered": false, "digits": 2, "prompt": "For **repeat** loans (a borrower's second loan or later), count by the product of the borrower's **first** loan and the product of this loan. Return `first_product`, `product`, `n_loans`.", "starter": "", "solution": "WITH seq AS (\n  SELECT\n    product,\n    ROW_NUMBER() OVER (PARTITION BY borrower_id ORDER BY originated_date, loan_id) AS rn,\n    FIRST_VALUE(product) OVER (PARTITION BY borrower_id ORDER BY originated_date, loan_id) AS first_product\n  FROM loans\n)\nSELECT first_product, product, COUNT(*) AS n_loans\nFROM seq\nWHERE rn > 1\nGROUP BY first_product, product", "alts": [], "hints": ["`FIRST_VALUE(product) OVER (PARTITION BY borrower_id ORDER BY ...)` puts the first loan's product on every one of the borrower's rows."], "explain": ""}, {"type": "exercise", "id": "d4-lead", "kind": "write", "ordered": false, "digits": 2, "prompt": "For borrower `200147`, each loan with the date of their **next** loan and the days until it: `loan_id`, `originated_date`, `next_loan_date`, `days_to_next`.", "starter": "", "solution": "SELECT\n  loan_id,\n  originated_date,\n  LEAD(originated_date) OVER (ORDER BY originated_date, loan_id) AS next_loan_date,\n  LEAD(originated_date) OVER (ORDER BY originated_date, loan_id) - originated_date AS days_to_next\nFROM loans\nWHERE borrower_id = 200147", "alts": [], "hints": [], "explain": "With a single borrower, no `PARTITION BY` is needed; drop the `WHERE` and you'd need `PARTITION BY borrower_id`. The last loan's `next_loan_date` is NULL."}, {"type": "exercise", "id": "d4-running-borrower", "kind": "write", "ordered": false, "digits": 2, "prompt": "For borrower `200364`, a running total of principal borrowed over time: `loan_id`, `originated_date`, `principal`, `cumulative_principal`.", "starter": "", "solution": "SELECT\n  loan_id,\n  originated_date,\n  principal,\n  SUM(principal) OVER (ORDER BY originated_date, loan_id) AS cumulative_principal\nFROM loans\nWHERE borrower_id = 200364", "alts": [], "hints": [], "explain": ""}, {"type": "exercise", "id": "d4-share-of-group", "kind": "write", "ordered": false, "digits": 3, "prompt": "Each merchant's share of its **cleaned** category's loans (lower case, trimmed; skip NULL categories): `merchant_id`, `category`, `n_loans`, `share_of_category`.", "starter": "", "solution": "SELECT\n  m.merchant_id,\n  LOWER(TRIM(m.category)) AS category,\n  COUNT(*) AS n_loans,\n  1.0 * COUNT(*) / SUM(COUNT(*)) OVER (PARTITION BY LOWER(TRIM(m.category))) AS share_of_category\nFROM loans l\nJOIN merchants m\n  ON m.merchant_id = l.merchant_id\nWHERE m.category IS NOT NULL\nGROUP BY m.merchant_id, LOWER(TRIM(m.category))", "alts": [], "hints": ["Group to one row per merchant, then divide by a window sum of those counts partitioned by category: `SUM(COUNT(*)) OVER (PARTITION BY ...)`."], "explain": ""}]}, {"id": "06-dates", "title": "Dates and timestamps", "minutes": 30, "summary": "Timestamp ranges and equality, truncation (month, quarter, week), extraction, date arithmetic, month ends, weekends.", "lesson": "08-dates-and-time", "date": "", "interview": false, "part": "", "blocks": [{"type": "md", "text": "These drills go with lesson 8."}, {"type": "exercise", "id": "d5-half-open", "kind": "fix", "ordered": false, "digits": 2, "prompt": "How many borrower accounts were created in **November 2025**? Return `n_accounts`. The count is too low. Fix it.", "starter": "SELECT COUNT(*) AS n_accounts\nFROM borrowers\nWHERE created_at BETWEEN DATE '2025-11-01' AND DATE '2025-11-30'", "solution": "SELECT COUNT(*) AS n_accounts\nFROM borrowers\nWHERE created_at >= DATE '2025-11-01'\n  AND created_at < DATE '2025-12-01'", "alts": [], "hints": ["`created_at` is a timestamp. What time of day does `DATE '2025-11-30'` stand for?"], "explain": ""}, {"type": "exercise", "id": "d5-hour", "kind": "write", "ordered": true, "digits": 2, "prompt": "Applications by **hour of day** (0–23) of `applied_at`: `hour`, `n_applications`, sorted by hour.", "starter": "", "solution": "SELECT\n  EXTRACT(HOUR FROM applied_at) AS hour,\n  COUNT(*) AS n_applications\nFROM applications\nGROUP BY EXTRACT(HOUR FROM applied_at)\nORDER BY hour", "alts": [], "hints": [], "explain": "With real data you'd also ask which **time zone** the timestamps are in. If they're UTC, US evening activity lands on the next calendar day, which shifts daily and hourly counts."}, {"type": "exercise", "id": "d5-quarter", "kind": "write", "ordered": false, "digits": 2, "prompt": "Loans originated in 2025, by quarter: `quarter` (first day of the quarter) and `n_loans`.", "starter": "", "solution": "SELECT\n  CAST(DATE_TRUNC('quarter', originated_date) AS DATE) AS quarter,\n  COUNT(*) AS n_loans\nFROM loans\nWHERE originated_date >= DATE '2025-01-01'\nGROUP BY CAST(DATE_TRUNC('quarter', originated_date) AS DATE)", "alts": [], "hints": [], "explain": ""}, {"type": "predict", "id": "d5-month-end", "sql": "SELECT CAST(DATE '2025-01-31' + INTERVAL '1 month' AS DATE) AS d", "explain": "Month arithmetic clamps to the last day of the target month. (R's `as.Date(\"2025-01-31\") + months(1)` gives `NA`; `%m+%` clamps like SQL does.) Payment schedules hit this every month for loans originated on the 29th–31st.", "options": [{"text": "2025-03-03", "correct": false}, {"text": "2025-02-28", "correct": true}, {"text": "2025-02-31", "correct": false}, {"text": "An error", "correct": false}], "errors": false, "prompt": "What date does this produce?"}, {"type": "exercise", "id": "d5-account-age", "kind": "write", "ordered": false, "digits": 1, "prompt": "Average account age in **days** as of 2025-12-31, by state: `state`, `avg_age_days`.", "starter": "", "solution": "SELECT\n  state,\n  AVG(DATE '2025-12-31' - CAST(created_at AS DATE)) AS avg_age_days\nFROM borrowers\nGROUP BY state", "alts": [], "hints": ["Cast the timestamp to a date first, so the subtraction gives a number of days rather than an interval."], "explain": ""}, {"type": "exercise", "id": "d5-last-30", "kind": "write", "ordered": false, "digits": 2, "prompt": "How many loans were originated in the **30 days ending on 2025-12-31** (2 December through 31 December)? Return `n_loans`. Use date arithmetic from the snapshot date rather than typing `2025-12-02`.", "starter": "", "solution": "SELECT COUNT(*) AS n_loans\nFROM loans\nWHERE originated_date > DATE '2025-12-31' - INTERVAL '30 days'", "alts": ["SELECT COUNT(*) AS n_loans\nFROM loans\nWHERE originated_date >= DATE '2025-12-02'"], "hints": ["`DATE '2025-12-31' - INTERVAL '30 days'` is 1 December. \"After 1 December\" starts on the 2nd."], "explain": "Off-by-one errors on windows like this are common. Say the boundary out loud (\"strictly after December 1st, so 30 days including the 31st\") and the interviewer can confirm."}, {"type": "exercise", "id": "d5-on-time", "kind": "write", "ordered": false, "digits": 3, "prompt": "For successful payments made in **2025**, what share were made **on or before** the installment's due date? Count each payment once. Return `on_time_share`.", "starter": "", "solution": "SELECT\n  AVG(CASE WHEN CAST(p.paid_at AS DATE) <= i.due_date THEN 1.0 ELSE 0 END) AS on_time_share\nFROM (SELECT DISTINCT * FROM payments) p\nJOIN installments i\n  ON i.loan_id = p.loan_id\n AND i.installment_number = p.installment_number\nWHERE p.status = 'succeeded'\n  AND p.paid_at >= DATE '2025-01-01'\n  AND p.paid_at < DATE '2026-01-01'", "alts": [], "hints": ["Join to installments on both key columns to get the due date, then compare the payment **date** with it."], "explain": ""}, {"type": "predict", "id": "d5-ts-equals", "sql": "SELECT COUNT(*) AS n\nFROM applications\nWHERE applied_at = DATE '2025-12-31'", "explain": "`applied_at` is a timestamp; the date becomes `2025-12-31 00:00:00`, and only an application made at exactly midnight would match. Use a range (`>= '2025-12-31' AND < '2026-01-01'`) or compare dates: `CAST(applied_at AS DATE) = DATE '2025-12-31'`.", "options": [{"text": "All applications made that day", "correct": false}, {"text": "0", "correct": true}, {"text": "An error", "correct": false}], "errors": false, "prompt": "Plenty of applications were made on 31 December 2025. What does this return?"}, {"type": "exercise", "id": "d5-week", "kind": "write", "ordered": true, "digits": 2, "prompt": "Loans per **week** in December 2025 (weeks starting Monday): `week_start` and `n_loans`, sorted by week.", "starter": "", "solution": "SELECT\n  CAST(DATE_TRUNC('week', originated_date) AS DATE) AS week_start,\n  COUNT(*) AS n_loans\nFROM loans\nWHERE originated_date >= DATE '2025-12-01'\nGROUP BY CAST(DATE_TRUNC('week', originated_date) AS DATE)\nORDER BY week_start", "alts": [], "hints": [], "explain": "The first \"week\" starts on Monday 1 December, and the last (29–31 December) is partial, so it looks low for that reason alone. Partial periods at either end of a range are a classic source of fake trends. Week start days vary by dialect: Monday in PostgreSQL, DuckDB and BigQuery's `ISOWEEK`; Sunday in some others."}, {"type": "exercise", "id": "d5-weekend", "kind": "write", "ordered": false, "digits": 3, "prompt": "What share of applications were made on a **weekend** (Saturday or Sunday)? Return `weekend_share`.", "starter": "", "solution": "SELECT\n  AVG(CASE WHEN EXTRACT(DOW FROM applied_at) IN (0, 6) THEN 1.0 ELSE 0 END) AS weekend_share\nFROM applications", "alts": [], "hints": ["With `EXTRACT(DOW ...)`, Sunday is 0 and Saturday is 6."], "explain": ""}, {"type": "exercise", "id": "d5-floor-zero", "kind": "write", "ordered": false, "digits": 2, "prompt": "For successful payments made in **December 2025** (each counted once), the average days late, treating **early payments as 0** rather than negative. Return `avg_days_late`.", "starter": "", "solution": "SELECT\n  AVG(GREATEST(CAST(p.paid_at AS DATE) - i.due_date, 0)) AS avg_days_late\nFROM (SELECT DISTINCT * FROM payments) p\nJOIN installments i\n  ON i.loan_id = p.loan_id\n AND i.installment_number = p.installment_number\nWHERE p.status = 'succeeded'\n  AND p.paid_at >= DATE '2025-12-01'\n  AND p.paid_at < DATE '2026-01-01'", "alts": ["SELECT\n  AVG(CASE WHEN CAST(p.paid_at AS DATE) > i.due_date THEN CAST(p.paid_at AS DATE) - i.due_date ELSE 0 END) AS avg_days_late\nFROM (SELECT DISTINCT * FROM payments) p\nJOIN installments i\n  ON i.loan_id = p.loan_id\n AND i.installment_number = p.installment_number\nWHERE p.status = 'succeeded'\n  AND p.paid_at >= DATE '2025-12-01'\n  AND p.paid_at < DATE '2026-01-01'"], "hints": [], "explain": "`GREATEST(x, 0)` floors a value at zero. It exists in PostgreSQL, MySQL, Snowflake, BigQuery and DuckDB (SQL Server only since 2022); the `CASE` version works everywhere."}, {"type": "exercise", "id": "d5-last-day", "kind": "write", "ordered": false, "digits": 2, "prompt": "How many installments fall due on the **last day of a month**? Return `n_installments`.", "starter": "", "solution": "SELECT COUNT(*) AS n_installments\nFROM installments\nWHERE due_date = CAST(DATE_TRUNC('month', due_date) + INTERVAL '1 month' - INTERVAL '1 day' AS DATE)", "alts": ["SELECT COUNT(*) AS n_installments\nFROM installments\nWHERE due_date = LAST_DAY(due_date)"], "hints": ["Last day of the month = first day of the month, plus one month, minus one day."], "explain": "`LAST_DAY()` exists in MySQL, Snowflake, Oracle and DuckDB (SQL Server: `EOMONTH()`); the truncate-add-subtract version works in PostgreSQL too."}]}, {"id": "07-classic-patterns", "title": "Classic interview patterns", "minutes": 30, "summary": "The recurring shapes of analyst SQL questions — streaks, retention, A-then-B, Nth event, histograms, pivots, consecutive events, year-over-year, concentration.", "lesson": ["09-lending-metrics", "10-interview-craft"], "date": "", "interview": false, "part": "", "blocks": [{"type": "md", "text": "These combine techniques from several lessons. Each one is a *pattern* you'll recognise in other questions. Read the explanation after solving, even if you got it right: the point is to name the pattern."}, {"type": "exercise", "id": "p-nth", "kind": "write", "ordered": false, "digits": 2, "prompt": "Each borrower account's **third** loan (by date, ties by `loan_id`): `borrower_id`, `loan_id`, `originated_date`. Borrowers with fewer than three loans don't appear.", "starter": "", "solution": "WITH seq AS (\n  SELECT\n    borrower_id, loan_id, originated_date,\n    ROW_NUMBER() OVER (PARTITION BY borrower_id ORDER BY originated_date, loan_id) AS rn\n  FROM loans\n)\nSELECT borrower_id, loan_id, originated_date\nFROM seq\nWHERE rn = 3", "alts": [], "hints": [], "explain": "**Pattern: Nth event per entity.** `ROW_NUMBER` per entity, filter `rn = N`. \"First\", \"latest\" and \"second purchase\" are all this shape."}, {"type": "exercise", "id": "p-a-then-b", "kind": "write", "ordered": false, "digits": 2, "prompt": "How many borrower accounts took a **Pay in 4 loan as their first loan** and **later took a monthly loan**? Return `n_borrowers`.", "starter": "", "solution": "WITH seq AS (\n  SELECT\n    borrower_id, product, originated_date, loan_id,\n    ROW_NUMBER() OVER (PARTITION BY borrower_id ORDER BY originated_date, loan_id) AS rn\n  FROM loans\n),\nfirsts AS (\n  SELECT borrower_id, originated_date, loan_id\n  FROM seq\n  WHERE rn = 1 AND product = 'pay_in_4'\n)\nSELECT COUNT(*) AS n_borrowers\nFROM firsts f\nWHERE EXISTS (\n  SELECT 1\n  FROM seq s\n  WHERE s.borrower_id = f.borrower_id\n    AND s.product = 'monthly'\n    AND s.rn > 1\n)", "alts": [], "hints": ["Step 1: find each borrower's first loan, keep the Pay in 4 ones. Step 2: does any later loan of theirs have product `monthly`?"], "explain": "**Pattern: did A, then B.** Find the A event per entity, then check for a B event after it (`EXISTS` with an ordering condition). Funnels (\"viewed, then applied, then took a loan\") chain this."}, {"type": "exercise", "id": "p-histogram", "kind": "write", "ordered": true, "digits": 2, "prompt": "A histogram of loan sizes in **500-wide buckets**, with everything from 5,000 up in one top bucket. Return `bucket_start` (0, 500, 1000, …, 5000) and `n_loans`, sorted.", "starter": "", "solution": "SELECT\n  LEAST(FLOOR(principal / 500) * 500, 5000) AS bucket_start,\n  COUNT(*) AS n_loans\nFROM loans\nGROUP BY LEAST(FLOOR(principal / 500) * 500, 5000)\nORDER BY bucket_start", "alts": ["SELECT\n  CASE WHEN principal >= 5000 THEN 5000 ELSE FLOOR(principal / 500) * 500 END AS bucket_start,\n  COUNT(*) AS n_loans\nFROM loans\nGROUP BY CASE WHEN principal >= 5000 THEN 5000 ELSE FLOOR(principal / 500) * 500 END\nORDER BY bucket_start"], "hints": [], "explain": "**Pattern: binning.** `FLOOR(x / width) * width` gives each value's bucket start. Cap the tail so a few huge values don't create dozens of near-empty buckets."}, {"type": "exercise", "id": "p-pivot", "kind": "write", "ordered": true, "digits": 2, "prompt": "For each month of 2025, loans by product **as columns**: `month` (first of month), `n_pay_in_4`, `n_monthly`. Sort by month.", "starter": "", "solution": "SELECT\n  CAST(DATE_TRUNC('month', originated_date) AS DATE) AS month,\n  SUM(CASE WHEN product = 'pay_in_4' THEN 1 ELSE 0 END) AS n_pay_in_4,\n  SUM(CASE WHEN product = 'monthly' THEN 1 ELSE 0 END) AS n_monthly\nFROM loans\nWHERE originated_date >= DATE '2025-01-01'\nGROUP BY CAST(DATE_TRUNC('month', originated_date) AS DATE)\nORDER BY month", "alts": [], "hints": [], "explain": "**Pattern: long to wide.** One conditional aggregate per output column. It's `pivot_wider()` written by hand, and it works in every dialect."}, {"type": "exercise", "id": "p-yoy", "kind": "write", "ordered": true, "digits": 3, "prompt": "Year-over-year: for each month number (1–12), loans in 2024, loans in 2025, and `yoy_growth` (2025 ÷ 2024 − 1). Return `month_num`, `loans_2024`, `loans_2025`, `yoy_growth`, sorted by month.", "starter": "", "solution": "SELECT\n  EXTRACT(MONTH FROM originated_date) AS month_num,\n  SUM(CASE WHEN EXTRACT(YEAR FROM originated_date) = 2024 THEN 1 ELSE 0 END) AS loans_2024,\n  SUM(CASE WHEN EXTRACT(YEAR FROM originated_date) = 2025 THEN 1 ELSE 0 END) AS loans_2025,\n  1.0 * SUM(CASE WHEN EXTRACT(YEAR FROM originated_date) = 2025 THEN 1 ELSE 0 END)\n      / SUM(CASE WHEN EXTRACT(YEAR FROM originated_date) = 2024 THEN 1 ELSE 0 END) - 1 AS yoy_growth\nFROM loans\nWHERE originated_date >= DATE '2024-01-01'\nGROUP BY EXTRACT(MONTH FROM originated_date)\nORDER BY month_num", "alts": [], "hints": [], "explain": "**Pattern: same period, different year.** Here `EXTRACT(MONTH ...)` merging years is exactly what you want, with the year split out into columns. Compare with `LAG(n, 12)` over a monthly series, which breaks if any month is missing."}, {"type": "exercise", "id": "p-retention", "kind": "write", "ordered": false, "digits": 3, "prompt": "For accounts created in 2025 **on or before 1 December** (so everyone had 30 days to act), by **quarter of account creation**: `quarter`, `n_accounts`, and `share_loan_30d`: the share that took their first loan **within 30 days** of creating the account.", "starter": "", "solution": "WITH first_loan AS (\n  SELECT borrower_id, MIN(originated_date) AS first_loan_date\n  FROM loans\n  GROUP BY borrower_id\n)\nSELECT\n  CAST(DATE_TRUNC('quarter', b.created_at) AS DATE) AS quarter,\n  COUNT(*) AS n_accounts,\n  AVG(CASE WHEN f.first_loan_date - CAST(b.created_at AS DATE) <= 30 THEN 1.0 ELSE 0 END) AS share_loan_30d\nFROM borrowers b\nLEFT JOIN first_loan f\n  ON f.borrower_id = b.borrower_id\nWHERE b.created_at >= DATE '2025-01-01'\n  AND b.created_at < DATE '2025-12-02'\nGROUP BY CAST(DATE_TRUNC('quarter', b.created_at) AS DATE)", "alts": [], "hints": ["Start from **borrowers** (the cohort), LEFT JOIN each one's first loan date, and average a \"within 30 days\" flag. Accounts with no loan get NULL, which the `CASE` turns into 0."], "explain": "**Pattern: cohort retention/activation.** Define the cohort, attach the first qualifying event, measure within a fixed window, and **exclude members who haven't had the full window yet**. That last step is the one people forget; it's the same maturity idea as vintages."}, {"type": "exercise", "id": "p-streak", "kind": "write", "ordered": false, "digits": 2, "prompt": "Some borrowers took loans in several **consecutive calendar months**. Find borrower accounts whose longest such run is **3 months or more**: `borrower_id`, `longest_streak`.", "starter": "", "solution": "WITH months AS (\n  SELECT DISTINCT\n    borrower_id,\n    EXTRACT(YEAR FROM originated_date) * 12 + EXTRACT(MONTH FROM originated_date) AS month_num\n  FROM loans\n),\ngrouped AS (\n  SELECT\n    borrower_id,\n    month_num,\n    month_num - ROW_NUMBER() OVER (PARTITION BY borrower_id ORDER BY month_num) AS grp\n  FROM months\n),\nstreaks AS (\n  SELECT borrower_id, grp, COUNT(*) AS streak_length\n  FROM grouped\n  GROUP BY borrower_id, grp\n)\nSELECT borrower_id, MAX(streak_length) AS longest_streak\nFROM streaks\nGROUP BY borrower_id\nHAVING MAX(streak_length) >= 3", "alts": [], "hints": ["Turn each month into a number (`year * 12 + month`), one row per borrower-month.", "For consecutive months, `month_num - ROW_NUMBER()` is **constant**. Group by it to find each run."], "explain": "**Pattern: gaps and islands.** Within a run of consecutive values, value minus row number stays the same, so it labels the \"island\". It answers \"longest streak\", \"consecutive days active\" and \"periods of continuous delinquency\". Well worth memorising."}, {"type": "exercise", "id": "p-consecutive", "kind": "write", "ordered": false, "digits": 2, "prompt": "How many loans have **two installments in a row** that were each paid **after** their due date? Treat an installment's paid date as the date of its **last** successful payment (each payment counted once), and ignore installments with no successful payment. Return `n_loans`.", "starter": "", "solution": "WITH paid AS (\n  SELECT loan_id, installment_number, MAX(CAST(paid_at AS DATE)) AS paid_date\n  FROM (SELECT DISTINCT * FROM payments) p\n  WHERE status = 'succeeded'\n  GROUP BY loan_id, installment_number\n),\nflags AS (\n  SELECT\n    i.loan_id,\n    i.installment_number,\n    CASE WHEN pd.paid_date > i.due_date THEN 1 ELSE 0 END AS late\n  FROM installments i\n  JOIN paid pd\n    ON pd.loan_id = i.loan_id\n   AND pd.installment_number = i.installment_number\n),\nseq AS (\n  SELECT\n    loan_id,\n    late,\n    LAG(late) OVER (PARTITION BY loan_id ORDER BY installment_number) AS prev_late\n  FROM flags\n)\nSELECT COUNT(DISTINCT loan_id) AS n_loans\nFROM seq\nWHERE late = 1 AND prev_late = 1", "alts": [], "hints": ["Step 1: a paid date per installment. Step 2: a late flag per installment. Step 3: `LAG` the flag within each loan and look for 1 followed by 1."], "explain": "**Pattern: consecutive events.** Flag each event, `LAG` the flag, and look for the combination. For runs longer than two, switch to gaps and islands."}, {"type": "exercise", "id": "p-pareto", "kind": "write", "ordered": false, "digits": 2, "prompt": "What is the **smallest number of merchants**, taking the biggest first, that together account for **at least half** of all principal lent? Return `n_merchants`.", "starter": "", "solution": "WITH totals AS (\n  SELECT merchant_id, SUM(principal) AS total\n  FROM loans\n  GROUP BY merchant_id\n),\nrunning AS (\n  SELECT\n    ROW_NUMBER() OVER (ORDER BY total DESC, merchant_id) AS rn,\n    SUM(total) OVER (ORDER BY total DESC, merchant_id ROWS BETWEEN UNBOUNDED PRECEDING AND CURRENT ROW) AS cum_total,\n    SUM(total) OVER () AS grand_total\n  FROM totals\n)\nSELECT MIN(rn) AS n_merchants\nFROM running\nWHERE cum_total >= 0.5 * grand_total", "alts": [], "hints": ["Rank merchants by total, compute a running total down that ranking, and find the first rank where it reaches half the grand total."], "explain": "**Pattern: concentration (Pareto).** A running share over a sorted list. The same shape answers \"what share of losses comes from the top 10% of merchants?\""}]}], "daily": [{"id": "2026-10-08", "title": "Daily set 1", "minutes": 30, "summary": "Five drills. Check each one, then copy your results at the bottom.", "lesson": "", "date": "2026-10-08", "interview": false, "part": "", "blocks": [{"type": "note", "title": "How daily sets work", "text": "Do the five drills (check each one as usual), then press **Copy results** at the bottom and send me the text, or a photo of it. I'll review your SQL, not just pass/fail, and the next set will repeat what you got right, re-test what you missed in a new form, and add something new.\n\nDon't reveal solutions unless you're truly stuck: the review is most useful when it sees your own attempt."}, {"type": "exercise", "id": "dy1-score-bands", "kind": "write", "ordered": false, "digits": 3, "prompt": "Approval rates by credit-score band. Bands: `'no score'` (no credit file), `'below 600'`, `'600-699'`, `'700+'`.\n\nReturn `score_band`, `n_applications` and `approval_rate` (share of applications approved, as a decimal).", "starter": "", "solution": "WITH banded AS (\n  SELECT\n    CASE\n      WHEN credit_score IS NULL THEN 'no score'\n      WHEN credit_score < 600 THEN 'below 600'\n      WHEN credit_score < 700 THEN '600-699'\n      ELSE '700+'\n    END AS score_band,\n    decision\n  FROM applications\n)\nSELECT\n  score_band,\n  COUNT(*) AS n_applications,\n  AVG(CASE WHEN decision = 'approved' THEN 1.0 ELSE 0 END) AS approval_rate\nFROM banded\nGROUP BY score_band", "alts": [], "hints": ["Put the `NULL` check **first**. `credit_score < 600` is unknown for a NULL score, so a NULL would fall through every `WHEN` to the `ELSE`.", "Approval rate is the mean of a dummy: `AVG(CASE WHEN decision = 'approved' THEN 1.0 ELSE 0 END)`."], "explain": "`CASE` takes the **first** `WHEN` that's true, so order matters: the `NULL` branch goes first, then the bands from low to high, with `ELSE` catching the rest. Labelling in a CTE and grouping on the label keeps the `CASE` written once. Note the 'no score' approval rate (about 25%): a missing value is information, not something to drop."}, {"type": "exercise", "id": "dy1-electronics-zero", "kind": "write", "ordered": false, "digits": 2, "prompt": "Every **Electronics** merchant with its number of loans originated in **2025**, including merchants with none. Category text is messy (case, trailing spaces).\n\nReturn `merchant_id`, `merchant_name`, `n_loans_2025`.", "starter": "", "solution": "WITH electronics_merchants AS (\n  SELECT merchant_id, merchant_name\n  FROM merchants\n  WHERE LOWER(TRIM(category)) = 'electronics'\n),\nloans_2025 AS (\n  SELECT merchant_id, COUNT(*) AS n_loans\n  FROM loans\n  WHERE originated_date >= DATE '2025-01-01'\n    AND originated_date < DATE '2026-01-01'\n  GROUP BY merchant_id\n)\nSELECT\n  electronics_merchants.merchant_id,\n  electronics_merchants.merchant_name,\n  COALESCE(loans_2025.n_loans, 0) AS n_loans_2025\nFROM electronics_merchants\nLEFT JOIN loans_2025\n  ON loans_2025.merchant_id = electronics_merchants.merchant_id", "alts": ["SELECT\n  merchants.merchant_id,\n  merchants.merchant_name,\n  COUNT(loans.loan_id) AS n_loans_2025\nFROM merchants\nLEFT JOIN loans\n  ON loans.merchant_id = merchants.merchant_id\n AND loans.originated_date >= DATE '2025-01-01'\n AND loans.originated_date < DATE '2026-01-01'\nWHERE LOWER(TRIM(merchants.category)) = 'electronics'\nGROUP BY merchants.merchant_id, merchants.merchant_name"], "hints": ["Start `FROM` the merchants (every one must appear), clean the category with `LOWER(TRIM(...))`, and count 2025 loans per merchant in their own CTE.", "After the `LEFT JOIN`, merchants with no 2025 loans have `NULL` counts. `COALESCE(..., 0)` turns them into zeros."], "explain": "Two of the 23 Electronics merchants had no 2025 loans; they're the test. Either pre-filter loans in a CTE and `LEFT JOIN` (then `COALESCE`), or put the date condition in the `ON` clause and count `loans.loan_id`, not `*`, so a merchant with no match counts 0. Putting the date filter in `WHERE` would silently drop those two merchants."}, {"type": "exercise", "id": "dy1-never-paid", "kind": "write", "ordered": false, "digits": 2, "prompt": "How many borrower accounts have **at least one loan** but have **never had a successful payment** on any of their loans?\n\nReturn `n_borrowers`.", "starter": "", "solution": "WITH paid_borrowers AS (\n  SELECT DISTINCT loans.borrower_id\n  FROM loans\n  JOIN payments ON payments.loan_id = loans.loan_id\n  WHERE payments.status = 'succeeded'\n)\nSELECT COUNT(DISTINCT loans.borrower_id) AS n_borrowers\nFROM loans\nWHERE NOT EXISTS (\n  SELECT 1\n  FROM paid_borrowers\n  WHERE paid_borrowers.borrower_id = loans.borrower_id\n)", "alts": [], "hints": ["Payments link to loans, and loans link to borrowers. First find the borrowers who **have** paid (a CTE), then keep the borrowers with loans who are **not** in it.", "`WHERE NOT EXISTS (SELECT 1 FROM paid_borrowers WHERE paid_borrowers.borrower_id = loans.borrower_id)`. Count `DISTINCT` borrowers, because a borrower can have several loans."], "explain": "Anti-join in two steps: define the \"has paid\" population, then `NOT EXISTS` against it. Only succeeded payments count as paying. `COUNT(DISTINCT ...)` matters because the outer query runs over loans, and one borrower can have several."}, {"type": "exercise", "id": "dy1-behind", "kind": "write", "ordered": false, "digits": 2, "prompt": "Monthly loans originated in **Q1 2025**: how many are **behind** at the snapshot, meaning less has been collected (successful payments) than was due on installments with `due_date` on or before `2025-12-31`? And by how much in total?\n\nReturn `n_loans_behind` and `total_shortfall`.", "starter": "", "solution": "WITH q1_monthly AS (\n  SELECT loan_id\n  FROM loans\n  WHERE product = 'monthly'\n    AND originated_date >= DATE '2025-01-01'\n    AND originated_date < DATE '2025-04-01'\n),\ndue AS (\n  SELECT loan_id, SUM(amount_due) AS total_due\n  FROM installments\n  WHERE due_date <= DATE '2025-12-31'\n  GROUP BY loan_id\n),\nclean_payments AS (\n  SELECT DISTINCT * FROM payments\n),\ncollected AS (\n  SELECT loan_id, SUM(amount) AS total_collected\n  FROM clean_payments\n  WHERE status = 'succeeded'\n  GROUP BY loan_id\n),\nper_loan AS (\n  SELECT\n    q1_monthly.loan_id,\n    due.total_due,\n    COALESCE(collected.total_collected, 0) AS total_collected\n  FROM q1_monthly\n  JOIN due ON due.loan_id = q1_monthly.loan_id\n  LEFT JOIN collected ON collected.loan_id = q1_monthly.loan_id\n)\nSELECT\n  COUNT(*) AS n_loans_behind,\n  SUM(total_due - total_collected) AS total_shortfall\nFROM per_loan\nWHERE total_collected < total_due", "alts": [], "hints": ["Two \"many\" tables hang off each loan: installments and payments. Joining both to loans at once multiplies rows (every installment × every payment). Aggregate **each one to one row per loan in its own CTE**, then join.", "Payments have exact duplicate rows and failed attempts: `SELECT DISTINCT *` first, then keep `status = 'succeeded'`. A loan with no successful payments needs `COALESCE(..., 0)` after the `LEFT JOIN`."], "explain": "The answer is 30 loans. Joining loans → installments → payments in one go and summing gives **242**: each payment is counted once per installment row and each installment once per payment row, so the totals are nonsense. One CTE per \"many\" table, each collapsed to the loan grain, then join: that's the rule."}, {"type": "exercise", "id": "dy1-gap-fix", "kind": "write", "ordered": false, "digits": 1, "prompt": "For each loan that is **not** a borrower account's first loan, find the number of days since that account's previous loan. Then summarise by the product of the later loan.\n\nReturn `product`, `n_repeat_loans` and `avg_days_since_previous`.", "starter": "", "solution": "WITH sequenced AS (\n  SELECT\n    borrower_id,\n    product,\n    originated_date,\n    LAG(originated_date) OVER (PARTITION BY borrower_id ORDER BY originated_date, loan_id) AS previous_loan_date\n  FROM loans\n)\nSELECT\n  product,\n  COUNT(*) AS n_repeat_loans,\n  AVG(originated_date - previous_loan_date) AS avg_days_since_previous\nFROM sequenced\nWHERE previous_loan_date IS NOT NULL\nGROUP BY product", "alts": [], "hints": ["For each loan you need the same account's previous loan date: a window function that looks one row back within each borrower.", "`LAG(originated_date) OVER (PARTITION BY borrower_id ORDER BY originated_date, loan_id)`. A first loan gets `NULL`, so filter those out. `date - date` gives days."], "explain": "`LAG` within each borrower, ordered by date, then filter out first loans (their `LAG` is `NULL`) and average the difference. The `ORDER BY` inside `OVER` is essential: without it \"previous\" is whatever order the engine reads the rows in, and the averages come out wildly wrong without any error. Add `loan_id` as a tie-breaker for two loans on the same day. In Postgres, as here, `date - date` is a whole number of days."}]}, {"id": "2026-10-09", "title": "Daily set 2", "minutes": 30, "summary": "Five questions. Check each one, then copy your results at the bottom.", "lesson": "", "date": "2026-10-09", "interview": true, "part": "", "blocks": [{"type": "note", "title": "How this round works", "text": "No hints and no solutions: treat it like the live round. If you make an assumption the question doesn't settle, write it as a comment at the top of your query (`-- assuming ...`)."}, {"type": "exercise", "id": "dy2-1", "kind": "write", "ordered": false, "digits": 3, "prompt": "For applications submitted during **2025**, compare borrowers who reported an income with those who didn't. A borrower didn't report income when `annual_income` is `NULL` on their account.\n\nReturn `income_reported` (`'yes'` or `'no'`), `n_applications` and `approval_rate` (share of applications approved, as a decimal).", "starter": "", "solution": "WITH applications_2025 AS (\n  SELECT\n    applications.decision,\n    CASE WHEN borrowers.annual_income IS NULL THEN 'no' ELSE 'yes' END AS income_reported\n  FROM applications\n  JOIN borrowers ON borrowers.borrower_id = applications.borrower_id\n  WHERE applications.applied_at >= TIMESTAMP '2025-01-01'\n    AND applications.applied_at < TIMESTAMP '2026-01-01'\n)\nSELECT\n  income_reported,\n  COUNT(*) AS n_applications,\n  AVG(CASE WHEN decision = 'approved' THEN 1.0 ELSE 0 END) AS approval_rate\nFROM applications_2025\nGROUP BY income_reported", "alts": [], "hints": [], "explain": ""}, {"type": "exercise", "id": "dy2-2", "kind": "write", "ordered": false, "digits": 3, "prompt": "For **Pay in 4** loans originated in **November 2025**, take every installment with a `due_date` on or before **2025-12-31**. An installment is **paid in full** when the successful payments applied to it add up to at least its `amount_due` (some installments were paid in more than one payment, and `payments` contains exact duplicate rows that should count once).\n\nReturn `n_installments_due` and `share_fully_paid`.", "starter": "", "solution": "WITH november_pay_in_4 AS (\n  SELECT loan_id\n  FROM loans\n  WHERE product = 'pay_in_4'\n    AND originated_date >= DATE '2025-11-01'\n    AND originated_date < DATE '2025-12-01'\n),\ninstallments_due AS (\n  SELECT installments.loan_id, installments.installment_number, installments.amount_due\n  FROM installments\n  JOIN november_pay_in_4 ON november_pay_in_4.loan_id = installments.loan_id\n  WHERE installments.due_date <= DATE '2025-12-31'\n),\nclean_payments AS (\n  SELECT DISTINCT * FROM payments\n),\npaid_per_installment AS (\n  SELECT loan_id, installment_number, SUM(amount) AS amount_paid\n  FROM clean_payments\n  WHERE status = 'succeeded'\n  GROUP BY loan_id, installment_number\n)\nSELECT\n  COUNT(*) AS n_installments_due,\n  AVG(CASE WHEN COALESCE(paid_per_installment.amount_paid, 0) >= installments_due.amount_due THEN 1.0 ELSE 0 END) AS share_fully_paid\nFROM installments_due\nLEFT JOIN paid_per_installment\n  ON paid_per_installment.loan_id = installments_due.loan_id\n AND paid_per_installment.installment_number = installments_due.installment_number", "alts": [], "hints": [], "explain": ""}, {"type": "exercise", "id": "dy2-3", "kind": "write", "ordered": false, "digits": 2, "prompt": "Average loan size by merchant category: the average `principal` **per loan** in each category. Report categories in lowercase with no stray spaces (e.g. `'home & furniture'`), and leave out merchants with no category.\n\nReturn `category`, `n_loans` and `avg_principal`.", "starter": "", "solution": "SELECT\n  LOWER(TRIM(merchants.category)) AS category,\n  COUNT(*) AS n_loans,\n  AVG(loans.principal) AS avg_principal\nFROM loans\nJOIN merchants ON merchants.merchant_id = loans.merchant_id\nWHERE merchants.category IS NOT NULL\nGROUP BY LOWER(TRIM(merchants.category))", "alts": [], "hints": [], "explain": ""}, {"type": "exercise", "id": "dy2-4", "kind": "write", "ordered": false, "digits": 3, "prompt": "A **repeat loan** is any loan that isn't a borrower account's first (work at the `borrower_id` level; don't merge duplicate people). For each repeat loan, check whether it was originated **30 days or fewer** after the same account's previous loan. If an account took two loans on the same day, the lower `loan_id` counts as earlier.\n\nBy product of the repeat loan, return `product`, `n_repeat_loans` and `share_within_30_days` (share of repeat loans, each loan counting once).", "starter": "", "solution": "WITH sequenced AS (\n  SELECT\n    product,\n    originated_date,\n    LAG(originated_date) OVER (PARTITION BY borrower_id ORDER BY originated_date, loan_id) AS previous_loan_date\n  FROM loans\n)\nSELECT\n  product,\n  COUNT(*) AS n_repeat_loans,\n  AVG(CASE WHEN originated_date - previous_loan_date <= 30 THEN 1.0 ELSE 0 END) AS share_within_30_days\nFROM sequenced\nWHERE previous_loan_date IS NOT NULL\nGROUP BY product", "alts": [], "hints": [], "explain": ""}, {"type": "exercise", "id": "dy2-5", "kind": "write", "ordered": true, "digits": 4, "prompt": "The five merchants with the highest **charge-off rate** (share of the merchant's loans with `status = 'charged_off'`, all time), among merchants with **at least 50 loans**.\n\nReturn `merchant_name`, `n_loans` and `charge_off_rate`, highest rate first.", "starter": "", "solution": "WITH merchant_loans AS (\n  SELECT\n    merchants.merchant_name,\n    COUNT(*) AS n_loans,\n    AVG(CASE WHEN loans.status = 'charged_off' THEN 1.0 ELSE 0 END) AS charge_off_rate\n  FROM loans\n  JOIN merchants ON merchants.merchant_id = loans.merchant_id\n  GROUP BY merchants.merchant_id, merchants.merchant_name\n  HAVING COUNT(*) >= 50\n)\nSELECT merchant_name, n_loans, charge_off_rate\nFROM merchant_loans\nORDER BY charge_off_rate DESC\nLIMIT 5", "alts": [], "hints": [], "explain": ""}]}], "credit": [{"id": "01-how-bnpl-makes-money", "title": "How a BNPL lender makes money", "minutes": 60, "summary": "Four lines decide whether a loan was worth making. Learn them once and every case question gets easier.", "lesson": "", "date": "", "interview": false, "part": "Core", "blocks": [{"type": "predict", "id": "c1-pretest", "sql": "", "explain": "Monthly loans earned roughly 7–8 cents per dollar lent; Pay in 4 earned close to nothing in 2023. The rest of this chapter shows why, and why a lender might keep offering Pay in 4 anyway.", "options": [{"text": "Pay in 4: merchants pay a higher fee on it", "correct": false}, {"text": "Monthly installment loans, by a wide margin", "correct": true}, {"text": "About the same: the market prices them to be equal", "correct": false}], "errors": false, "prompt": "**Guess before you read** (guessing first makes the answer stick). Per dollar lent, which product earned this lender more in 2023, after funding costs and credit losses?"}, {"type": "md", "text": "## The business in one paragraph\n\nA buy-now-pay-later lender gets paid twice: **by merchants**, who pay a fee because offering pay-over-time lifts their sales, and **by borrowers** on loans that carry interest. It pays to **fund** the loans (borrowed money isn't free) and it absorbs **losses** when borrowers don't pay. Almost every question you'll get in a risk or growth interview is a variation on balancing those four lines.\n\n| | Pay in 4 | Monthly, 0% APR | Monthly, interest-bearing |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Who pays the lender | merchant | merchant | borrower (and the merchant, a little) |\n| Length | 6 weeks | 3–12 months | 3–24 months |\n| Typical size here | about \\$330 | about \\$920 | about \\$1,000 |\n\nThis course's data has one more table for this chapter: `merchant_terms`, the fee each merchant pays as a share of the purchase. The rates are **illustrative**, set to be plausible, not taken from any real lender.\n\n```sql\nSELECT product, apr = 0 AS zero_apr, COUNT(*) AS n_loans, ROUND(AVG(principal)) AS avg_principal\nFROM loans\nGROUP BY product, apr = 0\nORDER BY product, zero_apr\n```\n\n## Unit economics: four lines per dollar lent\n\nLenders compare products and segments **per dollar of purchase volume** (GMV), usually in **basis points** (bps): 1 bp = 0.01%, so a 5% merchant fee is 500 bps.\n\n| Line | How it's estimated here |\n|---|---|\n| + Merchant fee | `principal × fee rate`. The 0% APR rate applies to Pay in 4 and promotional loans |\n| + Interest | scheduled payments minus principal (zero for 0% APR loans) |\n| − Funding cost | 6% a year on the average balance (about half the principal) for the loan's life |\n| − Credit losses | principal not recovered on loans that charged off |\n\nReal lenders also pay for payment processing, servicing, fraud and customer acquisition. They're left out here so the four big lines stay visible."}, {"type": "chart", "id": "c1-unit-economics", "caption": "Pay in 4's merchant fee (about 489 bps) was almost entirely eaten by credit losses (about 447 bps). Monthly loans earned interest on top of a smaller fee, so they absorbed similar losses and still made roughly 770 bps. These are 2023 loans: old enough that almost all of their losses have happened, and made before the July 2024 policy tightening.", "sql": "WITH loans_2023 AS (\n  SELECT\n    loans.loan_id, loans.product, loans.principal, loans.status, loans.n_installments,\n    CASE WHEN loans.apr = 0 THEN merchant_terms.fee_rate_zero_apr\n         ELSE merchant_terms.fee_rate_interest_bearing END AS fee_rate\n  FROM loans\n  JOIN merchant_terms ON merchant_terms.merchant_id = loans.merchant_id\n  WHERE loans.originated_date >= DATE '2023-01-01' AND loans.originated_date < DATE '2024-01-01'\n),\nscheduled AS (\n  SELECT loan_id, SUM(amount_due) AS total_scheduled FROM installments GROUP BY loan_id\n),\ncollected AS (\n  SELECT loan_id, SUM(amount) AS collected\n  FROM (SELECT DISTINCT * FROM payments) AS clean_payments\n  WHERE status = 'succeeded'\n  GROUP BY loan_id\n),\nper_loan AS (\n  SELECT\n    loans_2023.product,\n    loans_2023.principal,\n    loans_2023.principal * loans_2023.fee_rate AS merchant_fee,\n    scheduled.total_scheduled - loans_2023.principal AS interest,\n    0.06 * 0.5 * loans_2023.principal *\n      CASE WHEN loans_2023.product = 'pay_in_4' THEN 6.0 / 52 ELSE loans_2023.n_installments / 12.0 END AS funding_cost,\n    CASE WHEN loans_2023.status = 'charged_off'\n         THEN GREATEST(loans_2023.principal - COALESCE(collected.collected, 0), 0) ELSE 0 END AS credit_loss\n  FROM loans_2023\n  JOIN scheduled ON scheduled.loan_id = loans_2023.loan_id\n  LEFT JOIN collected ON collected.loan_id = loans_2023.loan_id\n),\nby_product AS (\n  SELECT\n    product,\n    10000 * SUM(merchant_fee) / SUM(principal) AS fee_bps,\n    10000 * SUM(interest) / SUM(principal) AS interest_bps,\n    10000 * SUM(funding_cost) / SUM(principal) AS funding_bps,\n    10000 * SUM(credit_loss) / SUM(principal) AS loss_bps\n  FROM per_loan\n  GROUP BY product\n)\nSELECT 1 AS sort_order, '+ merchant fee' AS line, product, fee_bps AS bps FROM by_product\nUNION ALL SELECT 2, '+ interest', product, interest_bps FROM by_product\nUNION ALL SELECT 3, '− funding', product, -funding_bps FROM by_product\nUNION ALL SELECT 4, '− credit losses', product, -loss_bps FROM by_product\nUNION ALL SELECT 5, '= margin', product, fee_bps + interest_bps - funding_bps - loss_bps FROM by_product\nORDER BY sort_order, product", "chart": "bar", "x": "line", "y": ["bps"], "series": "product", "percent": false, "title": "2023 loans: unit economics per dollar lent (bps of GMV)", "stacked": false}, {"type": "md", "text": "Two habits worth copying from that query: each \"many\" table (installments, payments) is collapsed to **one row per loan** before it touches `loans`, and every line is divided by the **same denominator** (principal) so the lines add up.\n\n## Why would a merchant pay for 0% APR?\n\nBecause the loan sells the product. A \\$1,200 bike becomes \"\\$100 a month, no interest\", more people buy it, and some buy a bigger one. The merchant pays for that lift with a higher fee: in this data the 0% APR fee is about **2.2 times** the interest-bearing fee for the same merchant. Only merchants marked `zero_apr_eligible` offer the promotion."}, {"type": "exercise", "id": "c1-fee-revenue", "kind": "write", "ordered": false, "digits": 1, "prompt": "Merchant fee revenue on loans originated in **2025**, by product. Use `fee_rate_zero_apr` for loans with `apr = 0` and `fee_rate_interest_bearing` otherwise.\n\nReturn `product`, `gmv` (total principal), `fee_revenue`, and `fee_bps` (fee revenue ÷ GMV × 10,000).", "starter": "", "solution": "WITH loans_2025 AS (\n  SELECT\n    loans.product,\n    loans.principal,\n    CASE WHEN loans.apr = 0 THEN merchant_terms.fee_rate_zero_apr\n         ELSE merchant_terms.fee_rate_interest_bearing END AS fee_rate\n  FROM loans\n  JOIN merchant_terms ON merchant_terms.merchant_id = loans.merchant_id\n  WHERE loans.originated_date >= DATE '2025-01-01'\n    AND loans.originated_date < DATE '2026-01-01'\n)\nSELECT\n  product,\n  SUM(principal) AS gmv,\n  SUM(principal * fee_rate) AS fee_revenue,\n  10000 * SUM(principal * fee_rate) / SUM(principal) AS fee_bps\nFROM loans_2025\nGROUP BY product", "alts": [], "hints": ["Join `loans` to `merchant_terms` on `merchant_id` (one row per merchant, so no fan-out), and pick the rate with `CASE WHEN loans.apr = 0 THEN ... ELSE ... END`.", "`fee_bps` is a ratio of sums: `10000 * SUM(principal * fee_rate) / SUM(principal)`. Don't average the rates: big loans should count more."], "explain": "Pay in 4 earns about 496 bps in fees and monthly loans about 262. That gap is the merchant paying for the 0% APR. Note `fee_bps` is a **dollar-weighted** rate (a ratio of sums), not an average of merchant rates: a \\$2,000 loan contributes twenty times as much as a \\$100 one."}, {"type": "md", "text": "## Duration: the line that isn't on the chart\n\nPay in 4's funding cost is tiny (about 35 bps) because the money comes back in six weeks. That has a second consequence: the same dollar can be lent **about eight times a year**. A loan that makes 50 bps per six-week cycle makes roughly 400 bps per year on the capital it uses; a 12-month loan that makes 400 bps over its life ties the money up for the whole year.\n\nSo compare products **per dollar-year of capital**, not just per dollar lent. That is also why short-duration lenders can react fast: if a cohort goes bad, the book turns over within weeks."}, {"type": "predict", "id": "c1-velocity", "sql": "", "explain": "Capital tied up for 6 weeks can be lent again about 8.7 times a year. That's the core economic argument for short-duration products, and the reason \"per dollar lent\" comparisons can mislead.", "options": [{"text": "Both about 100 bps: margin is margin", "correct": false}, {"text": "About 870 bps for the 6-week loan (100 × 52/6), about 100 bps for the 12-month loan", "correct": true}, {"text": "About 100 bps for the 6-week loan, 1,200 bps for the 12-month loan", "correct": false}], "errors": false, "prompt": "Two loans each earn a margin of 100 bps over their life. One lasts 6 weeks, one lasts 12 months. Roughly what does each earn per year on the capital it ties up?"}, {"type": "md", "text": "Longer monthly terms carry more interest, but also more funding cost and more time for things to go wrong."}, {"type": "exercise", "id": "c1-term-economics", "kind": "write", "ordered": false, "digits": 1, "prompt": "For loans originated **before July 2024**, by term: return `term` (the `term_months` value as text, or `'pay_in_4'`), `n_loans`, `interest_bps` (scheduled interest ÷ principal × 10,000) and `chargeoff_bps` (principal of charged-off loans ÷ total principal × 10,000).", "starter": "", "solution": "WITH scheduled AS (\n  SELECT loan_id, SUM(amount_due) AS total_scheduled\n  FROM installments\n  GROUP BY loan_id\n),\nearly_loans AS (\n  SELECT\n    COALESCE(CAST(loans.term_months AS VARCHAR), 'pay_in_4') AS term,\n    loans.principal,\n    loans.status,\n    scheduled.total_scheduled\n  FROM loans\n  JOIN scheduled ON scheduled.loan_id = loans.loan_id\n  WHERE loans.originated_date < DATE '2024-07-01'\n)\nSELECT\n  term,\n  COUNT(*) AS n_loans,\n  10000 * SUM(total_scheduled - principal) / SUM(principal) AS interest_bps,\n  10000 * SUM(CASE WHEN status = 'charged_off' THEN principal ELSE 0 END) / SUM(principal) AS chargeoff_bps\nFROM early_loans\nGROUP BY term", "alts": [], "hints": ["Collapse `installments` to one row per loan (`SUM(amount_due)`) in its own CTE before joining, or every loan's principal is counted once per installment.", "`COALESCE(CAST(term_months AS VARCHAR), 'pay_in_4')` labels the Pay in 4 loans, whose `term_months` is NULL."], "explain": "Interest climbs steeply with term (24-month loans schedule about 2,800 bps of interest), and so does the time at risk. Loss rates here don't line up neatly by term because they also depend on who takes each term. That's a selection effect you'll meet again in chapter 4."}, {"type": "md", "text": "## Credit is a dial\n\nApproving more applicants adds volume, so fees and interest grow, but each extra approval is a slightly riskier borrower than the last. The profitable policy keeps approving until the **next** loan's expected margin falls to zero, within the losses the company is willing to tolerate (its **risk appetite**). Chapter 4 turns this into numbers.\n\n## No late fees: a deliberate choice\n\nMany card issuers and some BNPL lenders earn meaningful revenue from **late fees**. Some BNPL lenders charge **none at all**, and make that a selling point: the price is disclosed up front and never goes up. This data has no fee table, so it models a no-late-fee lender.\n\nWhat that does to the business:\n\n- **Revenue only comes from the \"good\" path**: merchant fees, interest, and (with a card product) interchange. A lender with no penalty revenue makes money only when customers pay as agreed, so its incentives line up with the customer's.\n- **Underwriting carries all the weight.** There's no fee income to offset losses on stretched borrowers, so every loss shows up in full. Approval and limit decisions, at the point of sale, are the main lever.\n- **Collections works differently.** No fee to threaten or waive, so the tools are reminders, payment-date changes, plans, and stopping further credit until the account is current.\n- **It's a regulatory and reputational asset.** Late-fee practices draw scrutiny; not charging them removes a class of complaints."}, {"type": "predict", "id": "c1-no-late-fee", "sql": "", "explain": "The business model rules out penalty pricing, and existing loans' terms are fixed. The lever is who gets new credit, and how much: underwriting at checkout.", "options": [{"text": "Introduce a late fee to deter missed payments", "correct": false}, {"text": "Tighten approvals or limits for the segments where delinquency is rising", "correct": true}, {"text": "Raise the APR on existing loans", "correct": false}], "errors": false, "prompt": "A no-late-fee lender sees delinquency rise. Which lever is most direct?"}, {"type": "md", "text": "## The margin metric lenders report: revenue less transaction costs\n\nListed BNPL lenders report a margin close to this chapter's four lines, under a name like **revenue less transaction costs**, as a percentage of GMV:\n\n| Revenue | Transaction costs |\n|---|---|\n| merchant fees | **provision for credit losses** (expected lifetime losses, booked at origination) |\n| interest income | **funding costs** (interest on the debt that funds loans held) |\n| card interchange, if there's a card | **processing and servicing** (card networks, bank transfers, customer service) |\n| gain on sale of loans, servicing income | **losses on loans sold below par**, or on commitments to buy back loans |\n\nIt's the **contribution margin** of lending: what's left to pay for people, technology and marketing. Listed lenders have reported it at roughly **3–4% of GMV**, with credit losses and processing usually the two largest costs.\n\nTwo differences from this chapter's version. **Provision versus realised loss:** the reported metric books expected losses at origination (CECL), so a growth spurt raises costs before any loan is late. **Processing:** our four lines left it out, and it matters most for small loans, because there's a cost **per payment**, not just per dollar."}, {"type": "exercise", "id": "c1-rltc", "kind": "write", "ordered": false, "digits": 0, "prompt": "Add processing to the 2023 unit economics. Assume processing costs **0.3% of principal plus \\$0.30 per successful payment** (deduplicate payments first). For loans originated in 2023, return `product`, `processing_bps` and `rltc_bps` (merchant fee + interest − funding − credit losses − processing, per dollar of principal, in bps). Use the same fee, interest, funding and loss definitions as the chart above.", "starter": "", "solution": "WITH loans_2023 AS (\n  SELECT\n    loans.loan_id, loans.product, loans.principal, loans.status, loans.n_installments,\n    CASE WHEN loans.apr = 0 THEN merchant_terms.fee_rate_zero_apr\n         ELSE merchant_terms.fee_rate_interest_bearing END AS fee_rate\n  FROM loans\n  JOIN merchant_terms ON merchant_terms.merchant_id = loans.merchant_id\n  WHERE loans.originated_date >= DATE '2023-01-01' AND loans.originated_date < DATE '2024-01-01'\n),\nscheduled AS (\n  SELECT loan_id, SUM(amount_due) AS total_scheduled FROM installments GROUP BY loan_id\n),\nclean_payments AS (\n  SELECT DISTINCT * FROM payments\n),\nsucceeded AS (\n  SELECT loan_id, SUM(amount) AS collected, COUNT(*) AS n_payments\n  FROM clean_payments\n  WHERE status = 'succeeded'\n  GROUP BY loan_id\n),\nper_loan AS (\n  SELECT\n    loans_2023.product,\n    loans_2023.principal,\n    loans_2023.principal * loans_2023.fee_rate AS merchant_fee,\n    scheduled.total_scheduled - loans_2023.principal AS interest,\n    0.06 * 0.5 * loans_2023.principal *\n      CASE WHEN loans_2023.product = 'pay_in_4' THEN 6.0 / 52 ELSE loans_2023.n_installments / 12.0 END AS funding_cost,\n    CASE WHEN loans_2023.status = 'charged_off'\n         THEN GREATEST(loans_2023.principal - COALESCE(succeeded.collected, 0), 0) ELSE 0 END AS credit_loss,\n    0.003 * loans_2023.principal + 0.30 * COALESCE(succeeded.n_payments, 0) AS processing_cost\n  FROM loans_2023\n  JOIN scheduled ON scheduled.loan_id = loans_2023.loan_id\n  LEFT JOIN succeeded ON succeeded.loan_id = loans_2023.loan_id\n)\nSELECT\n  product,\n  10000 * SUM(processing_cost) / SUM(principal) AS processing_bps,\n  10000 * SUM(merchant_fee + interest - funding_cost - credit_loss - processing_cost) / SUM(principal) AS rltc_bps\nFROM per_loan\nGROUP BY product", "alts": [], "hints": ["Count successful payments per loan in the same CTE that sums the amount collected, then `COALESCE` the count to 0 after the LEFT JOIN (loans with no successful payment still exist)."], "explain": "Processing is about 60 bps for both products, but it tips **Pay in 4 from +7 to about −56 bps**, while monthly loans still make about 713. A \\$344 Pay in 4 loan pays the per-payment cost four times on a small balance. That's why small-ticket lending lives or dies on processing costs (debit versus bank transfer, failed-payment retries) as much as on credit."}, {"type": "md", "text": "## Where the money comes from\n\nA lender that kept every loan on its own balance sheet would need a lot of equity and could grow only as fast as its capital. Larger BNPL lenders fund loans three ways, and most use all three:\n\n| Channel | How it works | What the lender keeps |\n|---|---|---|\n| **Warehouse facilities** | banks lend against a pool of the lender's loans, short term; the bridge from origination to the other two | the loans, the credit risk, the interest margin |\n| **Securitisation (ABS)** | a pool of loans goes into a trust that sells bonds to investors; the lender usually holds the riskiest slice | a residual slice of the risk and return; servicing |\n| **Forward flow** | institutional buyers (insurers, pensions, asset managers) commit in advance to buy loans meeting agreed criteria, at agreed prices | a gain or loss on sale, and servicing fees; most of the credit risk moves to the buyer |\n\nForward flow is what makes a lender **capital-light**: loans are sold soon after origination, so the same equity supports far more volume. The price is that the lender gives up the loans' future interest and depends on buyers' appetite.\n\nWhy a risk analyst cares:\n\n- **Credit performance sets the cost of funding.** ABS investors and loan buyers price on loss curves by vintage (chapter 3). If losses rise, the next deal prices worse, which raises costs on **every future loan**, not just the bad ones.\n- **Eligibility criteria constrain policy.** Forward-flow agreements specify which loans qualify (scores, terms, sizes). A policy change can push loans outside the criteria, back onto the balance sheet.\n- **Capital markets can tighten fast.** In a stress, buyers pull back or demand discounts just when losses rise (chapter 9). Lenders keep spare funding capacity, and spread it across many partners, for that reason."}, {"type": "explain", "id": "c1-capital", "prompt": "A lender sells most of its loans through forward flow. Losses on recent vintages come in 30% above what the buyers were told to expect. Walk through what happens next, over the following year, and what you'd do as the risk analyst.", "model": "**What happens:** buyers re-price: they pay less for new loans (a lower gain on sale, or a loss), tighten eligibility, or cut their commitments. The lender holds more loans on its balance sheet, which uses warehouse capacity and equity and raises funding costs. If the miss persists, the next securitisation prices wider too. The cost lands on every future loan, so margin on new volume falls even where credit is fine.\n**What I'd do:** (1) diagnose the miss with the chapter 5 playbook: which vintages, segments and merchants; fraud or credit; cohort or period. (2) Fix underwriting where it's broken, and quantify the change. (3) Re-forecast losses on the sold and held vintages and share the updated curves with funding partners early, with the fix and the evidence, because surprise is what erodes buyer confidence. (4) Check new policy against the buyers' eligibility criteria before launch."}, {"type": "bridge", "title": "", "text": "Two links to what you already know. **Net interest margin:** interest-bearing monthly loans look like a bank product; Pay in 4 looks like a payments business that happens to take credit risk. **CECL:** a lender reserves the *lifetime* expected loss when it originates a loan, so fast growth depresses near-term reported earnings even when every loan is profitable. Short loans reduce that drag, because reserves are smaller and are released within weeks."}, {"type": "interview", "title": "", "text": "\"How does a BNPL lender make money?\" is a common opener. Answer with the four lines, then say which one moves most with credit decisions (losses) and which with commercial ones (merchant fees and mix). One sentence on duration shows you think about capital, not just margin."}, {"type": "explain", "id": "c1-merchant-fee", "prompt": "A large merchant offers a deal: cut its fee from **5% to 4%** and it will route **50% more** volume to you. What do you need to know before saying yes? Name the numbers and the break-even logic.", "model": "The fee cut takes 100 bps off every dollar from this merchant, including the volume we already have. The question is whether the extra volume makes up for it.\n\n1. **Current margin per dollar at this merchant** (fee + interest − funding − losses). Say it's 150 bps. After the cut it's 50 bps, so total margin falls unless volume **triples**: we need 1.5 / 0.5 = 3×, not 1.5×. The deal as offered loses money.\n2. **Is the extra volume incremental?** If it shifts customers who'd have used us anyway, or comes from the merchant's other channels, the uplift is smaller than 50%.\n3. **Risk of the new customers.** New-to-us borrowers usually default more than repeat ones, so the loss line on the new volume is likely worse than today's average.\n4. **Product mix.** More 0% APR volume means fee-only revenue; more interest-bearing volume changes the picture.\n5. **Strategic value** that isn't in the margin: exclusivity, a marquee brand that brings other merchants, data.\n\nCounter-offer: a tiered fee that only drops once volume passes a threshold, so we're paid for the incremental volume we actually get."}, {"type": "explain", "id": "c1-why-pay-in-4", "prompt": "2023 Pay in 4 loans barely broke even. Give two reasons a lender might keep offering Pay in 4 anyway, and one number you'd check before believing either reason.", "model": "1. **It's the front door.** Small, short, merchant-funded loans are how many customers first try the product. If Pay in 4 users go on to take profitable monthly loans or keep coming back, the right unit is the customer, not the loan. Check: the share of Pay in 4 first-timers who take a second loan, and the margin on those later loans.\n2. **The losses were fixable.** 2023 was before the July 2024 tightening. If tighter approval cut Pay in 4 losses substantially, the product's margin today may be fine. Check: Pay in 4 loss rates for vintages after July 2024, compared at the same age (chapter 3).\n\nA third reason is the merchant relationship: merchants want a pay-in-4 option at checkout, and losing it could cost the monthly-loan volume too."}, {"type": "cards", "cards": [{"q": "Name the four big lines of BNPL unit economics.", "a": "+ merchant fee, + interest, − funding cost, − credit losses (plus processing, servicing, fraud and acquisition costs that sit outside this data).", "id": "01-how-bnpl-makes-money:c1"}, {"q": "What is a basis point, and what is a 4.5% fee in bps?", "a": "0.01%. 4.5% = 450 bps.", "id": "01-how-bnpl-makes-money:c2"}, {"q": "Who pays for a 0% APR loan?", "a": "The merchant, through a higher fee. Here the 0% APR fee is about 2.2× the interest-bearing fee.", "id": "01-how-bnpl-makes-money:c3"}, {"q": "Why is Pay in 4 so cheap to fund?", "a": "It lasts 6 weeks with about half the principal outstanding on average: 6% × 0.5 × 6/52 ≈ 35 bps.", "id": "01-how-bnpl-makes-money:c4"}, {"q": "In the 2023 loans, what nearly wiped out Pay in 4's margin?", "a": "Credit losses (about 447 bps) against merchant fees of about 489 bps.", "id": "01-how-bnpl-makes-money:c5"}, {"q": "Why compare products per dollar-year of capital?", "a": "Short loans recycle capital many times a year (about 8.7× for 6 weeks), so margin per cycle × cycles per year is the fair comparison.", "id": "01-how-bnpl-makes-money:c6"}, {"q": "What rises as loan term gets longer?", "a": "Interest income, funding cost, and time at risk (more months for a borrower's situation to change).", "id": "01-how-bnpl-makes-money:c7"}, {"q": "How does CECL interact with growth?", "a": "Lifetime expected losses are reserved at origination, so fast growth depresses near-term earnings even if every loan is profitable.", "id": "01-how-bnpl-makes-money:c8"}, {"q": "What does \"credit is a dial\" mean?", "a": "Approval policy trades volume (revenue) against losses; keep approving until the marginal loan's expected margin is about zero, within risk appetite.", "id": "01-how-bnpl-makes-money:c9"}, {"q": "A merchant wants its fee cut from 5% to 4% for 50% more volume. What's the break-even logic?", "a": "With margin m bps today, new margin is m − 100 on all volume; volume must grow by a factor m / (m − 100). At m = 150 that's 3×, so +50% loses money.", "id": "01-how-bnpl-makes-money:c10"}, {"q": "Why does a no-late-fee lender rely so heavily on underwriting?", "a": "There's no penalty revenue to offset losses, so every loss lands in full; approval and limits at checkout are the main lever (and incentives align with customers paying as agreed).", "id": "01-how-bnpl-makes-money:c11"}, {"q": "Revenue less transaction costs: what are the transaction costs?", "a": "Provision for credit losses, funding costs, processing and servicing, and losses on loans sold below par (or purchase commitments).", "id": "01-how-bnpl-makes-money:c12"}, {"q": "Roughly what do listed BNPL lenders report revenue less transaction costs at?", "a": "About 3–4% of GMV.", "id": "01-how-bnpl-makes-money:c13"}, {"q": "In this data, what did processing do to 2023 Pay in 4?", "a": "About 60 bps of processing (0.3% plus \\$0.30 per payment) turned it from about +7 to about −56 bps; monthly stayed near +713.", "id": "01-how-bnpl-makes-money:c14"}, {"q": "Three funding channels for a BNPL lender?", "a": "Warehouse facilities (bridge), securitisation/ABS (bonds backed by loan pools; lender keeps a residual slice), forward flow (buyers commit to purchase qualifying loans; capital-light).", "id": "01-how-bnpl-makes-money:c15"}, {"q": "Why does credit performance matter to funding?", "a": "Buyers and ABS investors price on vintage loss curves; worse losses raise funding costs and cut appetite for every future loan.", "id": "01-how-bnpl-makes-money:c16"}]}]}, {"id": "02-delinquency-and-loss-metrics", "title": "Delinquency and loss metrics", "minutes": 60, "summary": "Stocks and flows. A delinquency rate tells you how full the bathtub is; roll and cure rates tell you why.", "lesson": "", "date": "", "interview": false, "part": "Core", "blocks": [{"type": "md", "text": "## Warm-up: from chapter 1\n\nAnswer from memory first. Retrieving an idea is what keeps it."}, {"type": "predict", "id": "c2-recall-1", "sql": "", "explain": "Fees of about 489 bps against losses of about 447 bps. Funding was only about 35 bps because the loan lasts six weeks.", "options": [{"text": "Funding cost", "correct": false}, {"text": "Credit losses", "correct": true}, {"text": "The merchant fee being too low", "correct": false}], "errors": false, "prompt": "In the 2023 loans, which line nearly wiped out Pay in 4's margin?"}, {"type": "predict", "id": "c2-recall-2", "sql": "", "explain": "The cut applies to every dollar, old and new. New margin 100 bps, so volume must rise by a factor of 200 / 100 = 2.", "options": [{"text": "50%", "correct": false}, {"text": "It must double (200 / 100 = 2×)", "correct": true}, {"text": "100 bps worth of volume", "correct": false}], "errors": false, "prompt": "A merchant asks you to cut its fee by 100 bps in exchange for more volume. Your current margin there is 200 bps. How much must volume grow for total margin to stay the same?"}, {"type": "md", "text": "## The life of a loan, as states\n\nEvery month, each open loan is in one state:\n\n**current → 1–29 → 30–59 → 60–89 → 90–119 → charged off**\n\nwith **paid off** as the happy exit and **recoveries** (money collected after charge-off) as a footnote. Here, as for most closed-end consumer loans, a loan is charged off **120 days** after the first missed payment. Credit cards usually wait 180.\n\n**Days past due (DPD)** counts from the **oldest** installment that is due and not fully paid. Miss March 1 and April 1, and on April 10 you're 40 days past due, not 10. A payment usually covers the oldest installment first, so one payment can move a loan from 40 DPD back to 10: it **rolls back** a bucket without being \"current\".\n\n## The snapshot table\n\nReconstructing DPD from installments and payments is fiddly (lesson 9 did it once), so lenders keep a **monthly snapshot**: one row per loan per month-end with the official state. This course's version is `loan_month`. Here's one loan's whole life:\n\n```sql\nSELECT as_of_date, balance, dpd, status\nFROM loan_month\nWHERE loan_id = 904006\nORDER BY as_of_date\n```\n\nA 6-month loan from February 2025: current for two month-ends, then 28, 59 and 89 days past due, then charged off at the end of July. Two features of the table matter for everything below:\n\n- **Grain:** one row per loan per month, so the key is `(loan_id, as_of_date)`, not `loan_id`.\n- **Rows stop** after the month a loan closes. The closing month itself is there, with `status` set to `paid_off` or `charged_off`.\n\n## The stock: delinquency rate\n\nThe headline number is the **30+ delinquency rate**: the share of open loans 30 or more days past due. 1–29 is mostly noise: payday landed on a Friday, a card expired. 30+ means a whole payment cycle was missed."}, {"type": "chart", "id": "c2-dq-rate", "caption": "Two weightings of the same stock. Watch the last two months: both jump, and the dollar line jumps much more. Hold that thought: **something happened in late 2025**, and the dollar gap is a clue that the newly delinquent loans are larger than usual. Chapter 5 investigates.", "sql": "SELECT\n  STRFTIME(as_of_date, '%Y-%m') AS month_end,\n  AVG(CASE WHEN dpd >= 30 THEN 1.0 ELSE 0 END) AS by_accounts,\n  SUM(CASE WHEN dpd >= 30 THEN balance ELSE 0 END) / SUM(balance) AS by_dollars\nFROM loan_month\nWHERE status = 'open'\n  AND as_of_date >= DATE '2024-01-01'\nGROUP BY STRFTIME(as_of_date, '%Y-%m')\nORDER BY month_end", "chart": "line", "x": "month_end", "y": ["by_accounts", "by_dollars"], "series": null, "percent": true, "title": "30+ delinquency rate, open loans", "stacked": false}, {"type": "md", "text": "**Accounts or dollars?** Dollars are what hit the P&L and what investors watch. Accounts are customers, and collections workload. When the two diverge, loan size and risk are moving together: a fraud ring buying \\$1,800 laptops moves dollars far more than accounts."}, {"type": "exercise", "id": "c2-dq-2025", "kind": "write", "ordered": false, "digits": 4, "prompt": "For each month-end in **2025**, the 30+ delinquency rate by **accounts** among open loans. Return `as_of_date`, `n_open` and `rate_30_plus`.", "starter": "", "solution": "SELECT\n  as_of_date,\n  COUNT(*) AS n_open,\n  AVG(CASE WHEN dpd >= 30 THEN 1.0 ELSE 0 END) AS rate_30_plus\nFROM loan_month\nWHERE status = 'open'\n  AND as_of_date >= DATE '2025-01-01'\nGROUP BY as_of_date", "alts": [], "hints": ["Keep only `status = 'open'` rows: the closing-month rows (`paid_off`, `charged_off`) aren't part of the open book.", "The rate is a mean of a dummy: `AVG(CASE WHEN dpd >= 30 THEN 1.0 ELSE 0 END)`."], "explain": "The rate sits around 2% for most of 2025, then jumps to about 3.5% in November. Note the denominator, `n_open`, also grows fast late in the year: holiday lending adds many brand-new loans that *can't* be 30+ yet. That growth pushes the rate **down**, which makes the November jump even more striking."}, {"type": "md", "text": "## The flows: roll rates and cure rates\n\nA delinquency rate is a **stock**. It rises because more loans are **falling in**, or because fewer are **climbing out**, and those have different causes and different fixes. So lenders track the flows:\n\n- **Roll rate:** of loans in a bucket last month, the share that moved one bucket worse this month (current → 1–29, 1–29 → 30–59, and so on).\n- **Cure rate:** of loans in a bucket last month, the share that are current again (or paid off) this month.\n\nThe denominator is defined by **last month's** state. \"This month's 30–59 count ÷ this month's 1–29 count\" is not a roll rate; it divides two stocks. You need each loan's own previous state, which is exactly what `LAG` gives you."}, {"type": "chart", "id": "c2-roll-rates", "caption": "About 5% of current loans slip into 1–29 each month, and about a fifth of those keep going to 30–59. November 2025 breaks the pattern: **43%** of the loans that were 1–29 in October rolled to 30–59. Something changed about who was falling behind.", "sql": "WITH bucketed AS (\n  SELECT\n    loan_id,\n    as_of_date,\n    CASE\n      WHEN status = 'charged_off' THEN 'charged_off'\n      WHEN status = 'paid_off' THEN 'paid_off'\n      WHEN dpd = 0 THEN 'current'\n      WHEN dpd < 30 THEN '1-29'\n      WHEN dpd < 60 THEN '30-59'\n      WHEN dpd < 90 THEN '60-89'\n      ELSE '90+'\n    END AS bucket\n  FROM loan_month\n),\nwith_previous AS (\n  SELECT\n    as_of_date,\n    bucket,\n    LAG(bucket) OVER (PARTITION BY loan_id ORDER BY as_of_date) AS previous_bucket\n  FROM bucketed\n)\nSELECT\n  STRFTIME(as_of_date, '%Y-%m') AS month_end,\n  AVG(CASE WHEN bucket = '1-29' THEN 1.0 ELSE 0 END) FILTER (WHERE previous_bucket = 'current') AS current_to_1_29,\n  AVG(CASE WHEN bucket = '30-59' THEN 1.0 ELSE 0 END) FILTER (WHERE previous_bucket = '1-29') AS from_1_29_to_30_59\nFROM with_previous\nWHERE as_of_date >= DATE '2024-07-01'\nGROUP BY STRFTIME(as_of_date, '%Y-%m')\nORDER BY month_end", "chart": "line", "x": "month_end", "y": ["current_to_1_29", "from_1_29_to_30_59"], "series": null, "percent": true, "title": "Monthly roll rates", "stacked": false}, {"type": "md", "text": "`AGG(...) FILTER (WHERE ...)` restricts one aggregate to some rows: it's PostgreSQL and DuckDB syntax for \"conditional aggregation with a different denominator per column\". Everywhere else, write `AVG(CASE WHEN previous_bucket = 'current' THEN CASE WHEN bucket = '1-29' THEN 1.0 ELSE 0 END END)`: the inner `CASE` is the dummy, and the outer one gives `NULL` (ignored by `AVG`) to rows outside the denominator."}, {"type": "exercise", "id": "c2-roll-2025", "kind": "write", "ordered": false, "digits": 3, "prompt": "The **1–29 → 30–59** roll rate for each month-end in **2025**. Buckets: `dpd = 0` current, `1–29`, `30–59`, `60–89`, `90+`; a loan whose status is `paid_off` or `charged_off` in a month did **not** roll to 30–59. Check that the previous row really is the previous month.\n\nReturn `as_of_date`, `n_prev_1_29` (loans that were 1–29 at the previous month-end) and `roll_rate`.", "starter": "", "solution": "WITH bucketed AS (\n  SELECT\n    loan_id,\n    as_of_date,\n    CASE\n      WHEN status <> 'open' THEN status\n      WHEN dpd = 0 THEN 'current'\n      WHEN dpd < 30 THEN '1-29'\n      WHEN dpd < 60 THEN '30-59'\n      WHEN dpd < 90 THEN '60-89'\n      ELSE '90+'\n    END AS bucket\n  FROM loan_month\n),\nwith_previous AS (\n  SELECT\n    loan_id,\n    as_of_date,\n    bucket,\n    LAG(bucket) OVER (PARTITION BY loan_id ORDER BY as_of_date) AS previous_bucket,\n    LAG(as_of_date) OVER (PARTITION BY loan_id ORDER BY as_of_date) AS previous_date\n  FROM bucketed\n)\nSELECT\n  as_of_date,\n  COUNT(*) AS n_prev_1_29,\n  AVG(CASE WHEN bucket = '30-59' THEN 1.0 ELSE 0 END) AS roll_rate\nFROM with_previous\nWHERE previous_bucket = '1-29'\n  AND previous_date = LAST_DAY(as_of_date - INTERVAL 1 MONTH)\n  AND as_of_date >= DATE '2025-01-01'\nGROUP BY as_of_date", "alts": [], "hints": ["Label every row with a bucket in one CTE. In the next, `LAG(bucket)` and `LAG(as_of_date)` over `PARTITION BY loan_id ORDER BY as_of_date`.", "Keep rows whose previous bucket was `'1-29'` (that's the denominator) and whose previous date is the prior month-end: `previous_date = LAST_DAY(as_of_date - INTERVAL 1 MONTH)`. Then the rate is the mean of \"now 30–59\"."], "explain": "Two design choices to say out loud in an interview. **Denominator by previous state:** the population is \"1–29 last month\", followed forward. **Adjacency check:** `LAG` returns the previous *row*, not the previous *month*; in this table there are no gaps, but in real snapshot tables there often are, and a silent two-month jump would be counted as a one-month roll. Closing rows are included in the denominator (and count as \"didn't roll\"), so loans that paid off don't vanish from it."}, {"type": "predict", "id": "c2-stock-vs-flow", "sql": "", "explain": "Inflows (rolls) are unchanged, so new loans aren't worse. The stock is rising because the outflow (cures) dried up. That points at the back of the pipe: collections strategy, a payment-processing problem for delinquent accounts, or borrowers' ability to pay (income shocks). Underwriting changes show up first in the **front** of the pipe.", "options": [{"text": "Underwriting got worse: riskier borrowers are coming in", "correct": false}, {"text": "Fewer delinquent borrowers are catching up: look at collections, payment capacity, or a change in hardship programs", "correct": true}, {"text": "It's seasonal and will reverse", "correct": false}], "errors": false, "prompt": "Over three months the 30+ rate rises from 2.0% to 2.6%. Current → 1–29 and 1–29 → 30–59 roll rates are flat. The cure rate out of 30–59 fell from 25% to 10%. What's the most likely story?"}, {"type": "md", "text": "## Chaining the flows: a quick loss forecast\n\nIf 4.7% of current loans roll to 1–29 each month, 20% of those roll to 30–59, then 66%, 92% and 100% down the ladder, the share of today's current loans that will charge off is roughly the product:\n\n**0.047 × 0.20 × 0.66 × 0.92 × 1.00 ≈ 0.58%**, reaching charge-off in about five months.\n\nThis is a **net flow** (or roll-rate) model, one of the oldest forecasting tools in consumer credit. It assumes the rates hold steady, and they don't: seasons, loan age and vintage all move them. But it turns \"delinquency is up\" into \"here's what that means for losses in five months\".\n\n```sql\nWITH bucketed AS (\n  SELECT loan_id, as_of_date,\n         CASE WHEN status <> 'open' THEN status WHEN dpd = 0 THEN 'current' WHEN dpd < 30 THEN '1-29'\n              WHEN dpd < 60 THEN '30-59' WHEN dpd < 90 THEN '60-89' ELSE '90+' END AS bucket\n  FROM loan_month\n),\nwith_previous AS (\n  SELECT as_of_date, bucket, LAG(bucket) OVER (PARTITION BY loan_id ORDER BY as_of_date) AS previous_bucket\n  FROM bucketed\n)\nSELECT\n  previous_bucket,\n  COUNT(*) AS n_loans,\n  AVG(CASE WHEN (previous_bucket, bucket) IN (('current', '1-29'), ('1-29', '30-59'), ('30-59', '60-89'),\n                                             ('60-89', '90+'), ('90+', 'charged_off')) THEN 1.0 ELSE 0 END) AS flow_rate\nFROM with_previous\nWHERE previous_bucket IN ('current', '1-29', '30-59', '60-89', '90+')\n  AND as_of_date BETWEEN DATE '2025-01-31' AND DATE '2025-10-31'\nGROUP BY previous_bucket\nORDER BY n_loans DESC\n```"}, {"type": "bridge", "title": "", "text": "Two old friends. The bucket-to-bucket table is a **Markov transition matrix**, and the net-flow model is the product along its sub-diagonal (with the usual stationarity caveat). And stocks versus flows is exactly the **unemployment** decomposition: the unemployment rate moves with the separation rate (inflow) and the job-finding rate (outflow). Delinquency is the same bathtub, with rolls in and cures out."}, {"type": "md", "text": "## Noise: how many loans is that rate based on?\n\nMonthly roll rates here rest on 50–150 loans. A rate of 22% on 88 loans has a standard error of about √(0.22 × 0.78 / 88) ≈ **4.4 points**, so 21% versus 24% is noise. November's 43% on 143 loans is about six standard errors above the usual 21% (√(0.21 × 0.79 / 143) ≈ 3.4 points): that's a real change. Always show the denominator next to a rate.\n\n## First-payment default\n\n**First-payment default (FPD)**: the borrower never makes the first scheduled payment (here, monthly loans whose first installment isn't paid in full within 30 days). It's the earliest credit signal you can get, about a month after origination, and it's also a classic **fraud** signal: someone who intended to pay almost always makes the first payment."}, {"type": "chart", "id": "c2-fpd", "caption": "FPD normally runs 1–2%. The September 2025 vintage is at **13%**. Chapters 5 and 6 find out why.", "sql": "WITH first_installment AS (\n  SELECT installments.loan_id, installments.due_date, installments.amount_due\n  FROM installments\n  JOIN loans ON loans.loan_id = installments.loan_id\n  WHERE loans.product = 'monthly'\n    AND installments.installment_number = 1\n),\npaid_within_30_days AS (\n  SELECT\n    first_installment.loan_id,\n    COALESCE(SUM(CASE WHEN CAST(clean_payments.paid_at AS DATE) <= first_installment.due_date + 30\n                      THEN clean_payments.amount END), 0) AS amount_paid\n  FROM first_installment\n  LEFT JOIN (SELECT DISTINCT * FROM payments WHERE status = 'succeeded') AS clean_payments\n    ON clean_payments.loan_id = first_installment.loan_id\n   AND clean_payments.installment_number = 1\n  GROUP BY first_installment.loan_id\n)\nSELECT\n  STRFTIME(loans.originated_date, '%Y-%m') AS vintage,\n  AVG(CASE WHEN paid_within_30_days.amount_paid < first_installment.amount_due THEN 1.0 ELSE 0 END) AS fpd_rate\nFROM loans\nJOIN first_installment ON first_installment.loan_id = loans.loan_id\nJOIN paid_within_30_days ON paid_within_30_days.loan_id = loans.loan_id\nWHERE loans.originated_date >= DATE '2024-07-01'\n  AND loans.originated_date < DATE '2025-11-01'\n  AND first_installment.due_date + 30 <= DATE '2025-12-31'\nGROUP BY STRFTIME(loans.originated_date, '%Y-%m')\nORDER BY vintage", "chart": "bar", "x": "vintage", "y": ["fpd_rate"], "series": null, "percent": true, "title": "First-payment default by origination month (monthly loans)", "stacked": false}, {"type": "md", "text": "Note the last filter: a loan whose first payment came due less than 30 days before the snapshot can't have an FPD outcome yet, so it's excluded rather than counted as \"paid\". Leaving immature loans in the denominator flatters every early metric.\n\n## Charge-offs and recoveries\n\nA **charge-off** is the accounting recognition that a loan won't be collected; the lender keeps trying to recover, or sells the debt for cents on the dollar. **Gross charge-offs − recoveries = net charge-offs (NCO)**. This data has no recoveries, so gross equals net here.\n\nTwo ways to express losses, and mixing them up is a classic error:\n- **Period rate:** charge-offs in a period ÷ average outstanding balance, often annualized. Banks quote this.\n- **Cohort (lifetime) rate:** cumulative charge-offs of loans made in a period ÷ what was originated. That's the vintage view in chapter 3, and the natural one for short loans."}, {"type": "interview", "title": "", "text": "When someone says \"delinquency went up\", ask three things before anything else: **which bucket** (30+? 60+?), **which weighting** (accounts or dollars), and **stock or flow** (is it the rate, or the roll rates?). Then say which flow you'd look at first and why. It shows you know the metric is a summary of moving parts."}, {"type": "explain", "id": "c2-dollars-vs-accounts", "prompt": "In the latest month, the 30+ rate by **accounts** rose 0.3 points, but by **dollars** it rose 3 points. What does that tell you, and what are the next two things you'd check?", "model": "The newly delinquent loans are much larger than the average open loan: the same handful of accounts carries a big share of the balance. Big tickets concentrated in a short window suggest a specific segment rather than a broad deterioration: a merchant selling expensive items, a new product or term, or fraud (fraudsters pick high-value, resellable goods).\n\nNext: (1) break the new 30+ loans down by merchant, product/term and origination month: is it one vintage and one place? (2) check first-payment default for those loans: if most never paid at all, it smells like fraud rather than credit, which changes the fix."}, {"type": "explain", "id": "c2-roll-definition", "prompt": "A colleague computes \"roll rate\" as this month's 30–59 count divided by this month's 1–29 count. In two or three sentences, explain to them why that's wrong and what to do instead.", "model": "That divides two stocks from the same month, so it mixes different loans: this month's 30–59 loans were last month's 1–29 loans, not this month's. A roll rate follows a population forward: take the loans that were 1–29 **last** month and ask what share are 30–59 **now**, using each loan's own previous state (a `LAG` over the loan's history, checking the previous row really is the previous month). Loans that paid off or charged off stay in that denominator."}, {"type": "cards", "cards": [{"q": "How is days past due (DPD) counted?", "a": "From the **oldest** installment that is due and not fully paid; a payment usually covers the oldest first, so a loan can roll back a bucket without becoming current.", "id": "02-delinquency-and-loss-metrics:c1"}, {"q": "What's the grain and key of a monthly loan snapshot table?", "a": "One row per loan per month-end; key (loan_id, as_of_date). Rows stop after the month a loan closes.", "id": "02-delinquency-and-loss-metrics:c2"}, {"q": "Why is 30+ the headline delinquency bucket, not 1+?", "a": "1–29 is mostly noise (timing, expired cards) and usually cures; 30+ means a full payment cycle was missed.", "id": "02-delinquency-and-loss-metrics:c3"}, {"q": "Accounts vs dollars: when do they diverge, and what does it mean?", "a": "When delinquent loans are bigger or smaller than average; a dollar spike with a small account move points to large tickets in a specific segment (or fraud).", "id": "02-delinquency-and-loss-metrics:c4"}, {"q": "Define a roll rate and its denominator.", "a": "Of loans in a bucket last month, the share that moved one bucket worse this month. The denominator is last month's bucket population, followed forward.", "id": "02-delinquency-and-loss-metrics:c5"}, {"q": "Delinquency rises but roll rates are flat. Where do you look?", "a": "Cure rates: fewer loans are climbing out, so look at collections, payment processing, hardship programs, or borrowers' ability to pay.", "id": "02-delinquency-and-loss-metrics:c6"}, {"q": "What is a net-flow model?", "a": "Multiply the monthly roll rates down the ladder (current → 1–29 → … → charge-off) to estimate the share of current loans that will charge off in about five months.", "id": "02-delinquency-and-loss-metrics:c7"}, {"q": "What's the standard error of a 22% rate on 88 loans, roughly?", "a": "√(0.22 × 0.78 / 88) ≈ 4.4 points; so show denominators and don't chase 2–3 point wiggles.", "id": "02-delinquency-and-loss-metrics:c8"}, {"q": "What is first-payment default, and why does it matter?", "a": "The borrower never makes the first payment. It's the earliest credit signal and a classic fraud signal.", "id": "02-delinquency-and-loss-metrics:c9"}, {"q": "Period loss rate vs cohort loss rate?", "a": "Period: charge-offs in a period ÷ average balance (often annualized). Cohort: cumulative charge-offs of a vintage ÷ amount originated. Don't compare one with the other.", "id": "02-delinquency-and-loss-metrics:c10"}]}]}, {"id": "03-vintages-and-seasoning", "title": "Vintages and seasoning", "minutes": 60, "summary": "Loans go bad on a schedule. Compare them at the same age, or growth will lie to you.", "lesson": "", "date": "", "interview": false, "part": "Core", "blocks": [{"type": "md", "text": "## Warm-up: chapters 1 and 2"}, {"type": "predict", "id": "c3-recall-1", "sql": "", "explain": "A flow follows a population forward from its **previous** state. Dividing two November stocks mixes different loans.", "options": [{"text": "Loans that are 30–59 in November", "correct": false}, {"text": "Loans that are 1–29 in November", "correct": false}, {"text": "Loans that were 1–29 at the end of October, followed into November", "correct": true}], "errors": false, "prompt": "A roll rate from 1–29 to 30–59 for November uses which denominator?"}, {"type": "predict", "id": "c3-recall-2", "sql": "", "explain": "Funding cost scales with balance × time. Same dollar, about one-eighth of the time.", "options": [{"text": "Pay in 4 loans are smaller", "correct": false}, {"text": "The money is out for about six weeks instead of a year", "correct": true}, {"text": "Merchants pay the funding cost", "correct": false}], "errors": false, "prompt": "Why is Pay in 4's funding cost so much smaller than a 12-month loan's, per dollar lent?"}, {"type": "md", "text": "## The problem with a portfolio number\n\nHere's a number that looks like great news: **charge-off rate by origination year**, all loans ever made.\n\n```sql\nSELECT\n  YEAR(originated_date) AS origination_year,\n  COUNT(*) AS n_loans,\n  AVG(CASE WHEN status = 'charged_off' THEN 1.0 ELSE 0 END) AS chargeoff_rate\nFROM loans\nGROUP BY YEAR(originated_date)\nORDER BY origination_year\n```\n\n6.5% for 2023, 4.7% for 2024, 1.1% for 2025. Did the lender get five times better? Partly, but mostly **2025 loans haven't had time to go bad**. A loan can't charge off until about four months after its first missed payment, and December 2025 loans haven't had a payment due yet. Comparing years like this compares old loans with young ones.\n\nThe fix is the **vintage**: group loans by when they were made, and compare them **at the same age**.\n\n## Age: months on book\n\n**Months on book (MOB)** is a loan's age in calendar months: the month-index trick from the SQL track.\n\n```sqlstatic\n(YEAR(as_of_date) * 12 + MONTH(as_of_date)) - (YEAR(originated_date) * 12 + MONTH(originated_date))\n```\n\nA January loan is MOB 3 at the end of April, whatever day in January it started, so a whole monthly vintage ages in step.\n\n## Seasoning: delinquency depends on age"}, {"type": "chart", "id": "c3-seasoning", "caption": "Delinquency is near zero at first (nothing has been due long enough), **peaks around MOB 3**, then falls as the worst loans charge off and leave and Pay in 4 loans finish. That rise-and-fall is **seasoning**. Two lessons: a young book looks healthier than it is, and the July 2024 tightening shows up as a lower curve at every age, not just a lower average. (The bump at MOB 12 is the few long loans still open at that age.)", "sql": "WITH aged AS (\n  SELECT\n    loan_month.dpd,\n    (YEAR(loan_month.as_of_date) * 12 + MONTH(loan_month.as_of_date))\n      - (YEAR(loans.originated_date) * 12 + MONTH(loans.originated_date)) AS mob,\n    CASE WHEN loans.originated_date < DATE '2024-07-01' THEN 'before July 2024' ELSE 'from July 2024' END AS policy\n  FROM loan_month\n  JOIN loans ON loans.loan_id = loan_month.loan_id\n  WHERE loan_month.status = 'open'\n)\nSELECT mob, policy, AVG(CASE WHEN dpd >= 30 THEN 1.0 ELSE 0 END) AS rate_30_plus\nFROM aged\nWHERE mob BETWEEN 1 AND 12\nGROUP BY mob, policy\nORDER BY mob, policy", "chart": "line", "x": "mob", "y": ["rate_30_plus"], "series": "policy", "percent": true, "title": "30+ delinquency of open loans, by months on book", "stacked": false}, {"type": "md", "text": "**The growth trap.** If a lender grows fast, most of its book is young, below the seasoning peak, so the portfolio delinquency rate falls even if every vintage is the same. When growth slows, the book ages and the rate rises with nothing wrong. Before you call a portfolio trend a credit trend, ask: **did the age mix change?**\n\n## Vintage curves\n\nThe standard picture: for each vintage, the **cumulative** share of loans charged off by each month on book, drawn only as far as that vintage has actually aged."}, {"type": "chart", "id": "c3-vintage-curves", "caption": "Every curve is flat until MOB 4 (charge-off needs about four months of non-payment), then climbs and flattens. The 2023 to mid-2024 vintages cluster at 4–5% by MOB 6 and 5–7% by MOB 12. From the July 2024 vintage the curves sit visibly lower: that's the policy change, now compared fairly, **age for age**.", "sql": "WITH loan_ages AS (\n  SELECT\n    STRFTIME(DATE_TRUNC('quarter', originated_date), '%Y-Q') || CAST(QUARTER(originated_date) AS VARCHAR) AS vintage,\n    YEAR(originated_date) * 12 + MONTH(originated_date) AS origination_month,\n    CASE WHEN charged_off_date IS NOT NULL\n         THEN (YEAR(charged_off_date) * 12 + MONTH(charged_off_date)) - (YEAR(originated_date) * 12 + MONTH(originated_date))\n    END AS chargeoff_mob\n  FROM loans\n  WHERE originated_date < DATE '2025-07-01'\n),\nages AS (\n  SELECT mob FROM range(1, 25) AS t(mob)\n),\ncurve AS (\n  SELECT\n    loan_ages.vintage,\n    ages.mob,\n    MAX(loan_ages.origination_month) AS last_origination_month,\n    AVG(CASE WHEN loan_ages.chargeoff_mob <= ages.mob THEN 1.0 ELSE 0 END) AS cum_chargeoff_rate\n  FROM loan_ages\n  CROSS JOIN ages\n  GROUP BY loan_ages.vintage, ages.mob\n)\nSELECT mob, vintage, cum_chargeoff_rate\nFROM curve\nWHERE last_origination_month + mob <= 2025 * 12 + 12\nORDER BY mob, vintage", "chart": "line", "x": "mob", "y": ["cum_chargeoff_rate"], "series": "vintage", "percent": true, "title": "Cumulative charge-off rate by quarterly vintage", "stacked": false}, {"type": "md", "text": "The `CROSS JOIN` to a list of ages builds one row per vintage per age, and the final `WHERE` keeps only ages that **every** loan in the vintage has reached by the end of 2025. Drawing a curve past a vintage's real age would silently flatten it: loans that \"haven't charged off yet\" would count as good."}, {"type": "exercise", "id": "c3-mob6", "kind": "write", "ordered": false, "digits": 4, "prompt": "The **MOB-6 cumulative charge-off rate** for each quarterly vintage: the share of the vintage's loans that charged off by the end of their 6th month on book. Include only vintages whose **every** loan has reached MOB 6 by 2025-12-31 (so through the second quarter of 2025).\n\nReturn `vintage` (first day of the quarter), `n_loans`, `co_rate_mob6`.", "starter": "", "solution": "WITH loan_ages AS (\n  SELECT\n    CAST(DATE_TRUNC('quarter', originated_date) AS DATE) AS vintage,\n    CASE WHEN charged_off_date IS NOT NULL\n         THEN (YEAR(charged_off_date) * 12 + MONTH(charged_off_date))\n            - (YEAR(originated_date) * 12 + MONTH(originated_date))\n    END AS chargeoff_mob\n  FROM loans\n  WHERE originated_date < DATE '2025-07-01'\n)\nSELECT\n  vintage,\n  COUNT(*) AS n_loans,\n  AVG(CASE WHEN chargeoff_mob <= 6 THEN 1.0 ELSE 0 END) AS co_rate_mob6\nFROM loan_ages\nGROUP BY vintage", "alts": [], "hints": ["Compute each loan's charge-off age in months with the month-index trick (NULL if it never charged off), and the vintage with `DATE_TRUNC('quarter', ...)`.", "`AVG(CASE WHEN chargeoff_mob <= 6 THEN 1.0 ELSE 0 END)`: a NULL charge-off age fails the test and counts as 0, which is what you want. Keep vintages through June 2025: a June loan reaches MOB 6 at the end of December."], "explain": "Pre-tightening vintages sit at 4–5% at MOB 6; the July 2024 vintage at 2.2%, and 2025 vintages at 1.5–2.5%. That's the honest improvement: real, but around **half**, not the five-fold improvement the naive yearly view suggested."}, {"type": "md", "text": "## Early reads for young vintages\n\nCharge-offs take four-plus months to appear, so for the newest vintages you need earlier signals: **first-payment default** (chapter 2), and **30+ delinquency at MOB 3**, the seasoning peak."}, {"type": "exercise", "id": "c3-early-read", "kind": "write", "ordered": false, "digits": 4, "prompt": "For each **monthly** vintage from January to September 2025: among the vintage's loans that are **open** at the end of their 3rd month on book, the share that are 30+ days past due.\n\nReturn `vintage` (as `'YYYY-MM'`), `n_open_mob3`, `rate_30_plus_mob3`.", "starter": "", "solution": "WITH aged AS (\n  SELECT\n    STRFTIME(loans.originated_date, '%Y-%m') AS vintage,\n    loan_month.dpd,\n    (YEAR(loan_month.as_of_date) * 12 + MONTH(loan_month.as_of_date))\n      - (YEAR(loans.originated_date) * 12 + MONTH(loans.originated_date)) AS mob\n  FROM loan_month\n  JOIN loans ON loans.loan_id = loan_month.loan_id\n  WHERE loan_month.status = 'open'\n    AND loans.originated_date >= DATE '2025-01-01'\n    AND loans.originated_date < DATE '2025-10-01'\n)\nSELECT\n  vintage,\n  COUNT(*) AS n_open_mob3,\n  AVG(CASE WHEN dpd >= 30 THEN 1.0 ELSE 0 END) AS rate_30_plus_mob3\nFROM aged\nWHERE mob = 3\nGROUP BY vintage", "alts": [], "hints": ["Join `loan_month` to `loans` to get each row's age, keep open rows, then keep `mob = 3`."], "explain": "Most 2025 vintages run 2–7% at MOB 3, which is noisy at 140–260 loans each. September 2025 is at **17%**, far outside that range. It's the same vintage whose first-payment default jumped in chapter 2. Two independent early signals pointing at one cohort is strong evidence of a **cohort** problem (something about who was lent to that month), not an economy-wide one."}, {"type": "md", "text": "## Reading a vintage triangle\n\nLay the data out with **vintages down the side** and **calendar months across the top**, each cell the vintage's delinquency at that time. Patterns tell you different stories:\n\n| Pattern | What it means | Typical causes |\n|---|---|---|\n| One **row** bad at every age | a **cohort** effect: who was lent to | policy or model change, a new merchant, a fraud attack |\n| One **column** bad across all ages | a **period** effect: something hit everyone at once | economy, a payment-processor outage, a collections change |\n| Every vintage bad at the **same age** | **seasoning**: normal | nothing (it's the curve's shape) |"}, {"type": "bridge", "title": "", "text": "This is the **age–period–cohort** problem: calendar time = origination date + age, so the three can't be separately identified from smooth trends without assumptions. In practice, credit problems usually show up as **breaks** (one vintage, one month), and breaks are identifiable. You'll also recognise the projection method below: it's an insurance **loss-development triangle** (chain ladder), and the lifetime number it produces is the **CECL** lifetime loss estimate, built bottom-up."}, {"type": "md", "text": "## Projecting a young vintage to its lifetime loss\n\nWhen a lender says recent vintages are \"tracking to\" some lifetime loss, it means this: take how much older vintages grew from age *a* to age *b*, and apply that growth to a young vintage's current value.\n\nIn this data, pre-tightening vintages' charge-offs grew by a factor of about **1.24** from MOB 6 to MOB 12. The first-quarter 2025 vintage is at 2.5% at MOB 6, so it's tracking to roughly 2.5% × 1.24 ≈ **3.1%** by MOB 12.\n\nTwo cautions an interviewer will like:\n- **Factors come from a different regime.** The July 2024 vintage grew only 1.07× from MOB 6 to 12 (2.2% to 2.4%). Tighter policy changed the curve's shape, so old factors may **overstate** new vintages' lifetime losses.\n- **The newest points are mostly forecast.** At MOB 3 there are no charge-offs yet; the \"projection\" is the underwriting model's prediction plus early delinquency. Actuarial practice blends the two, leaning on the prior while data is thin (in your language: Bayesian updating)."}, {"type": "interview", "title": "", "text": "If asked to compare two cohorts, say the magic words: **\"at the same months on book.\"** Then mention maturity (\"the newest vintages haven't reached peak delinquency\") and say how you'd get an early read (first-payment default, 30+ at MOB 3). That's the whole vintage toolkit in three sentences."}, {"type": "explain", "id": "c3-loosen", "prompt": "An executive sees \"charge-off rate: 6.5% for 2023 loans, 1.1% for 2025 loans\" and says the July 2024 tightening worked so well that you can now loosen policy. Respond in four or five sentences, using numbers from this chapter.", "model": "The 1.1% isn't comparable: 2025 loans are young, many haven't had a payment due long enough to charge off, and the yearly view mixes ages. Compared at the same age, the improvement is real but smaller: about 4–5% charged off by month 6 for pre-tightening vintages versus roughly 1.5–2.5% for 2025 vintages, so about half, not a fifth. Projecting with development factors puts the first-quarter 2025 vintage around 3% at month 12, and old factors may overstate it. So the tightening did work. Whether to loosen is a separate question: it depends on whether the loans we stopped making were profitable at the margin (chapter 4). I'd propose testing a modest loosening on a random slice and reading first-payment default and MOB-3 delinquency within a quarter."}, {"type": "explain", "id": "c3-triangle", "prompt": "Describe the pattern you'd expect in a vintage triangle (vintages down, calendar months across) if: (a) a fraudulent group targeted one merchant in September 2025; (b) a regional employer laid off thousands of customers in October 2025.", "model": "(a) A **cohort** effect: the September 2025 row is bad from its very first months (first-payment default, MOB-2/3 delinquency), while neighbouring vintages look normal. The October column isn't unusual for other vintages. Drilling into the row would concentrate it in one merchant.\n\n(b) A **period** effect: from October 2025 onward, delinquency rises in **many** vintages at once, along a column or diagonal, concentrated in the affected region (state) rather than any one vintage. Younger vintages show it as higher early delinquency, older ones as lower cure rates. Each pattern points at a different fix: (a) merchant controls and fraud rules, (b) regional credit tightening and hardship options."}, {"type": "cards", "cards": [{"q": "Why can't you compare charge-off rates by origination year across the whole history?", "a": "Younger loans haven't had time to go bad; the comparison mixes ages. Compare vintages at the same months on book.", "id": "03-vintages-and-seasoning:c1"}, {"q": "Define a vintage and months on book (MOB).", "a": "Vintage: loans grouped by origination period. MOB: calendar months since origination, (year×12 + month) − (year×12 + month).", "id": "03-vintages-and-seasoning:c2"}, {"q": "What is seasoning, and where does delinquency peak in this data?", "a": "The age pattern of delinquency: near zero at first, peaking around MOB 3, then falling as bad loans charge off and short loans finish.", "id": "03-vintages-and-seasoning:c3"}, {"q": "How does fast growth distort portfolio delinquency?", "a": "It makes the book young (below the seasoning peak), so the rate falls even if no vintage changed; slowing growth does the reverse.", "id": "03-vintages-and-seasoning:c4"}, {"q": "Why must a vintage curve stop at the vintage's actual age?", "a": "Beyond that age, loans that haven't charged off \"yet\" would be counted as good, flattening the curve.", "id": "03-vintages-and-seasoning:c5"}, {"q": "What does one bad row in a vintage triangle mean, versus one bad column?", "a": "Row: a cohort effect (who was lent to: policy, merchant, fraud). Column: a period effect (economy, processing outage, collections change).", "id": "03-vintages-and-seasoning:c6"}, {"q": "How do you project a young vintage to lifetime loss?", "a": "Apply development factors from older vintages (e.g. MOB 6 → 12 growth of about 1.24×) to its current value; it's an insurance-style chain ladder.", "id": "03-vintages-and-seasoning:c7"}, {"q": "What's the main risk in using old development factors?", "a": "The regime changed (e.g. tighter policy reshapes the curve); old factors can overstate or understate new vintages.", "id": "03-vintages-and-seasoning:c8"}, {"q": "Name two early-read metrics for vintages too young to have charge-offs.", "a": "First-payment default, and 30+ delinquency at MOB 3 (the seasoning peak).", "id": "03-vintages-and-seasoning:c9"}, {"q": "What three words should you say when comparing cohorts?", "a": "\"At the same months on book\" (then mention maturity and early reads).", "id": "03-vintages-and-seasoning:c10"}]}]}, {"id": "04-credit-strategy", "title": "Credit strategy: turning the dial", "minutes": 60, "summary": "Who to approve, for how much, on what terms. Risk appetite sets the limits; marginal analysis finds the best point inside them.", "lesson": "", "date": "", "interview": false, "part": "Core", "blocks": [{"type": "md", "text": "## Warm-up: chapters 1–3"}, {"type": "predict", "id": "c4-recall-1", "sql": "", "explain": "Loans go bad on a schedule (seasoning). Compare vintages age for age, and say how mature the youngest one is.", "options": [{"text": "over the same calendar year", "correct": false}, {"text": "at the same months on book", "correct": true}, {"text": "with the same number of loans", "correct": false}], "errors": false, "prompt": "Comparing two cohorts' charge-off rates fairly requires comparing them…"}, {"type": "predict", "id": "c4-recall-2", "sql": "", "explain": "Inflows unchanged, outflows down: the bathtub is filling because fewer loans climb out.", "options": [{"text": "Underwriting: the approval model", "correct": false}, {"text": "The back of the pipe: collections, payment processing, borrowers' capacity to pay", "correct": true}, {"text": "Merchant mix", "correct": false}], "errors": false, "prompt": "The 30+ rate is rising. Roll rates into delinquency are flat; cure rates out of 30–59 have fallen. Where do you look first?"}, {"type": "explain", "id": "c4-recall-3", "prompt": "From memory: write the four lines of unit economics, and say which one credit policy moves most directly.", "model": "Merchant fee + interest − funding cost − credit losses. Credit policy moves **losses** most directly, and **volume** (so total fees and interest) with them: approve more and both revenue and losses rise."}, {"type": "md", "text": "## Risk appetite: the fence around the field\n\n**Risk appetite** is how much risk, and what kinds, a company has decided to accept to earn its returns. At a bank it's a board-approved statement with measurable limits and early-warning triggers. At a consumer lender it typically includes:\n\n- **Loss limits**: e.g. target lifetime losses per vintage, by product.\n- **Funding constraints**: what securitisation investors and loan buyers will accept; they price or refuse loans whose losses are unpredictable.\n- **Capital and liquidity**: how much loss the balance sheet can absorb.\n- **Regulatory and reputational limits**: fair lending, affordability, how it looks.\n\nRisk appetite is a **constraint**, not a goal: zero losses means zero lending. Inside the fence, strategy is a **profit-maximisation** problem."}, {"type": "bridge", "title": "", "text": "At your bank this is the risk appetite statement your stress scenarios feed into: your scenarios test whether the firm stays inside the fence under stress. Credit strategy works **inside** the fence, choosing the best point. And the core logic is the oldest one in economics: keep approving while the **marginal** loan's expected profit is positive, MR = MC."}, {"type": "md", "text": "## The basic lever: the approval cutoff\n\nMost approval decisions start from a **risk score** (here, `credit_score` at application) and a **cutoff**: approve above it, decline below, with grey zones in between. This lender tightened on 1 July 2024. You can see it directly in the applications:"}, {"type": "chart", "id": "c4-approval-by-band", "caption": "The tightening raised the bar across the 580–679 range. Approval for scores 620–639 fell from 66% to 38%, and for 640–659 from 91% to 64%. Above 700, almost nothing changed.", "sql": "WITH banded AS (\n  SELECT\n    CAST(FLOOR(credit_score / 20) * 20 AS INTEGER) AS band_start,\n    CASE WHEN applied_at < TIMESTAMP '2024-07-01' THEN '1. before' ELSE '2. after' END AS policy,\n    decision\n  FROM applications\n  WHERE credit_score BETWEEN 560 AND 739\n)\nSELECT\n  CAST(band_start AS VARCHAR) || '–' || CAST(band_start + 19 AS VARCHAR) AS score_band,\n  policy,\n  AVG(CASE WHEN decision = 'approved' THEN 1.0 ELSE 0 END) AS approval_rate\nFROM banded\nGROUP BY band_start, policy\nORDER BY band_start, policy", "chart": "bar", "x": "score_band", "y": ["approval_rate"], "series": "policy", "percent": true, "title": "Approval rate by credit score band, before and after 1 July 2024", "stacked": false}, {"type": "md", "text": "`FLOOR(credit_score / 20) * 20` puts each score at the start of its 20-point band: 637 → 31.85 → 31 → 620. It's the \"divide, round down, multiply back\" binning trick, useful whenever bands are equal width.\n\n## Rank-ordering: does the score separate good from bad?\n\nA score is useful if risk falls steadily as the score rises. Look at what happened to loans made **before** the tightening, which are old enough for their outcomes to be known:"}, {"type": "exercise", "id": "c4-co-by-band", "kind": "write", "ordered": false, "digits": 3, "prompt": "For loans originated **before 1 July 2024**, the charge-off rate by 20-point band of the credit score at application, for scores **580 to 719**.\n\nReturn `band_start` (580, 600, …, 700), `n_loans` and `chargeoff_rate`.", "starter": "", "solution": "WITH scored_loans AS (\n  SELECT\n    CAST(FLOOR(applications.credit_score / 20) * 20 AS INTEGER) AS band_start,\n    loans.status\n  FROM loans\n  JOIN applications ON applications.application_id = loans.application_id\n  WHERE loans.originated_date < DATE '2024-07-01'\n    AND applications.credit_score BETWEEN 580 AND 719\n)\nSELECT\n  band_start,\n  COUNT(*) AS n_loans,\n  AVG(CASE WHEN status = 'charged_off' THEN 1.0 ELSE 0 END) AS chargeoff_rate\nFROM scored_loans\nGROUP BY band_start", "alts": [], "hints": ["The score lives on the application: join `loans` to `applications` on `application_id` (one application per loan, so no fan-out).", "Band with `CAST(FLOOR(credit_score / 20) * 20 AS INTEGER)`; the charge-off rate is a mean of a dummy on `status`."], "explain": "About 27% of loans scored 580–619 charged off, 13% at 620–639, 8% at 640–679, then 6%, 3% and below. The score **rank-orders** well, except that the two lowest bands are similar and both small (93 loans together). Remember these are only the applicants who were **approved**: chapter 8 comes back to what that does to the picture."}, {"type": "md", "text": "## Profit by band: the marginal loan\n\nRank-ordering isn't enough: the question is where approving **stops paying**. Put each band through chapter 1's four lines (merchant fee + interest − funding − losses), using the same pre-tightening loans:"}, {"type": "chart", "id": "c4-margin-by-band", "caption": "Bands 580–619 lost 700–800 bps on every dollar lent: approving them was a mistake. But **620–679 made 530–710 bps**, comparable to the best bands. The July 2024 tightening cut approvals in both groups: it removed the losses and also gave up profitable loans. That's the classic shape of an over-correction, and exactly what a strategy analyst should catch.", "sql": "WITH early_loans AS (\n  SELECT\n    loans.loan_id, loans.product, loans.principal, loans.status, loans.n_installments,\n    CAST(FLOOR(applications.credit_score / 20) * 20 AS INTEGER) AS band_start,\n    CASE WHEN loans.apr = 0 THEN merchant_terms.fee_rate_zero_apr\n         ELSE merchant_terms.fee_rate_interest_bearing END AS fee_rate\n  FROM loans\n  JOIN applications ON applications.application_id = loans.application_id\n  JOIN merchant_terms ON merchant_terms.merchant_id = loans.merchant_id\n  WHERE loans.originated_date < DATE '2024-07-01'\n    AND applications.credit_score BETWEEN 580 AND 779\n),\nscheduled AS (\n  SELECT loan_id, SUM(amount_due) AS total_scheduled FROM installments GROUP BY loan_id\n),\ncollected AS (\n  SELECT loan_id, SUM(amount) AS collected\n  FROM (SELECT DISTINCT * FROM payments) AS clean_payments\n  WHERE status = 'succeeded'\n  GROUP BY loan_id\n),\nper_loan AS (\n  SELECT\n    early_loans.band_start,\n    early_loans.principal,\n    early_loans.principal * early_loans.fee_rate\n      + scheduled.total_scheduled - early_loans.principal\n      - 0.06 * 0.5 * early_loans.principal *\n          CASE WHEN early_loans.product = 'pay_in_4' THEN 6.0 / 52 ELSE early_loans.n_installments / 12.0 END\n      - CASE WHEN early_loans.status = 'charged_off'\n             THEN GREATEST(early_loans.principal - COALESCE(collected.collected, 0), 0) ELSE 0 END AS margin\n  FROM early_loans\n  JOIN scheduled ON scheduled.loan_id = early_loans.loan_id\n  LEFT JOIN collected ON collected.loan_id = early_loans.loan_id\n)\nSELECT\n  CAST(band_start AS VARCHAR) || '–' || CAST(band_start + 19 AS VARCHAR) AS score_band,\n  10000 * SUM(margin) / SUM(principal) AS margin_bps\nFROM per_loan\nGROUP BY band_start\nORDER BY band_start", "chart": "bar", "x": "score_band", "y": ["margin_bps"], "series": null, "percent": false, "title": "Margin by score band, loans made before July 2024 (bps of principal)", "stacked": false}, {"type": "md", "text": "**Average versus marginal.** The whole approved book was profitable before the tightening, so the *average* said \"fine\". The *marginal* bands, the ones a cutoff decision actually moves, were losing money. Decisions live at the margin. The reverse error is just as common: an average that looks bad can hide a margin that's fine, and cutting it throws away good customers."}, {"type": "pitfall", "title": "Three reasons not to trust that chart blindly", "text": "1. **Selection.** These are the applicants who were approved. The 620–639 applicants approved before July 2024 were the best two-thirds of that band, so the third declined afterwards may be riskier than the band's history suggests.\n2. **Missing costs.** Processing, servicing, fraud and acquisition costs aren't in this data. A 530 bps margin might be 200 after them; a −700 is still negative.\n3. **Noise.** Some bands have under 100 loans. A rate of 13% on 158 loans has a standard error of about 2.7 points."}, {"type": "md", "text": "## Swap sets: who a policy change adds or removes\n\nWhen a policy or model changes, compare the old and new decisions applicant by applicant:\n\n- **Swap-outs:** approved by the old policy, declined by the new. You know how similar people performed, because the old policy approved them.\n- **Swap-ins:** declined by the old policy, approved by the new. You **don't** know how they perform: they were never approved. This is the **reject inference** problem.\n\nThe July 2024 tightening was almost all swap-outs, so its cost can be estimated from history: the 620–679 swap-outs were profitable, the 580–619 ones weren't. A loosening is all swap-ins, so it needs a test.\n\n## Levers beyond approve or decline\n\nApprove/decline is the bluntest tool. For a marginal applicant, a lender can also change:\n\n- **amount**: approve a lower limit (this data shows declined applicants retrying smaller amounts within minutes);\n- **term**: a shorter term means less time at risk and less exposure;\n- **down payment**: the borrower has skin in the game, and exposure falls;\n- **price**: a higher APR where regulation and competition allow;\n- **verification**: a step-up check (income, bank account link) instead of a decline.\n\nOften the best answer to \"should we approve this segment?\" is \"yes, with smaller amounts and shorter terms\".\n\n## Testing: how to learn about the people you don't approve\n\n- **Champion/challenger:** route a random share of applicants to a new policy and compare outcomes.\n- **Holdouts below the cutoff:** approve a small random slice of applicants you'd normally decline. You pay for information with expected losses, but without it your models only ever learn from people you already approve.\n- **Guardrails:** pre-agree the stop rule (e.g. \"if first-payment default in the test cell exceeds 3%, stop\").\n- **Read early:** short loans give answers fast. First-payment default in about a month, 30+ at MOB 3 in a quarter."}, {"type": "interview", "title": "", "text": "A strategy question (\"should we approve more of segment X?\") has a reliable shape: (1) what's the **marginal** economics of X, from history or a test; (2) is it **inside risk appetite** (losses, funding, regulation); (3) which **lever**: approve/decline, amount, term, price; (4) how to **test** it safely and what you'd **monitor**."}, {"type": "explain", "id": "c4-recommend", "prompt": "Using this chapter's numbers, write a short recommendation (5–6 sentences) on the July 2024 tightening: what it got right, what it cost, and what you'd do next.", "model": "The tightening was right to cut the 580–619 range: before July 2024 those loans charged off about 27% of the time and lost 700–800 bps per dollar lent. But it also cut approvals sharply in 620–679 (for example 66% to 38% at 620–639), where pre-tightening loans made roughly 530–710 bps after losses and funding, so we likely gave up profitable volume. Two caveats: the declined 620–679 applicants may be riskier than the ones we approved before (selection), and margins here exclude processing, servicing and fraud costs. I'd test a partial reversal: restore approvals for a random 20% of 620–679 applicants, perhaps with lower limits or shorter terms, with a guardrail on first-payment default. If the test cell's MOB-3 delinquency and projected losses stay within appetite after a quarter, roll it out."}, {"type": "explain", "id": "c4-test-design", "prompt": "Design the test from your recommendation: who's in it, what's compared, what you measure and when, and what would make you stop early.", "model": "**Population:** applicants scoring 620–679 who the current policy would decline. Randomly assign 20% to \"approve, with limits capped at the band median and terms of 6 months or less\" (test), 80% to the current policy (control).\n**Comparison:** test approvals versus the same band's history, and test versus control on volume and revenue (control approvals are zero for these applicants, so the incremental volume is the test's).\n**Metrics:** first-payment default after one cycle; 30+ at MOB 3; projected lifetime loss from development factors; margin per dollar; and fraud indicators (new accounts, high amounts).\n**Stop rule (agreed in advance):** stop if first-payment default exceeds about twice the band's historical rate, or if MOB-3 delinquency projects to losses above the product's loss target.\n**Size:** enough loans to tell 8% from 13% losses apart, a few hundred per cell, so a quarter or two at current volumes."}, {"type": "cards", "cards": [{"q": "What is risk appetite, and is it a goal or a constraint?", "a": "The amount and kinds of risk the company accepts to earn returns (loss limits, funding, capital, regulation, reputation). A constraint: strategy maximises profit inside it.", "id": "04-credit-strategy:c1"}, {"q": "What does \"rank-ordering\" mean for a score?", "a": "Risk falls steadily as the score rises, so higher bands have lower bad rates.", "id": "04-credit-strategy:c2"}, {"q": "Average vs marginal: which does a cutoff decision depend on?", "a": "Marginal: the bands the cutoff moves. A profitable average can hide loss-making marginal bands (and vice versa).", "id": "04-credit-strategy:c3"}, {"q": "What did the July 2024 tightening get right and wrong in this data?", "a": "Right: cut 580–619 (about 27% charge-off, roughly −750 bps). Wrong: also cut 620–679, which earned about +530 to +710 bps.", "id": "04-credit-strategy:c4"}, {"q": "Swap-ins vs swap-outs?", "a": "Swap-outs: approved before, declined now (performance known from history). Swap-ins: newly approved (performance unknown, so it's a reject-inference problem and needs a test).", "id": "04-credit-strategy:c5"}, {"q": "Name four levers besides approve/decline.", "a": "Amount/limit, term, down payment, price (APR), and verification steps (also product steering).", "id": "04-credit-strategy:c6"}, {"q": "Why approve a random slice below the cutoff?", "a": "To observe outcomes for people you'd normally decline; you pay for information with losses, otherwise models only learn from approved applicants.", "id": "04-credit-strategy:c7"}, {"q": "What's a guardrail in a credit test?", "a": "A pre-agreed stop rule, e.g. stop if first-payment default in the test cell exceeds a set threshold.", "id": "04-credit-strategy:c8"}, {"q": "Three caveats on a historical margin-by-band chart?", "a": "Selection (only approved applicants), missing costs (processing, servicing, fraud, acquisition), noise (small bands).", "id": "04-credit-strategy:c9"}, {"q": "The shape of an answer to \"should we approve more of segment X?\"", "a": "Marginal economics → inside risk appetite? → which lever → how to test safely and what to monitor.", "id": "04-credit-strategy:c10"}]}]}, {"id": "05-diagnosing-a-change", "title": "Diagnosing a change", "minutes": 60, "summary": "Delinquency jumped. Why, and what do we do?\" A playbook that works for losses, fraud and conversion alike, worked through on real numbers.", "lesson": "", "date": "", "interview": false, "part": "Core", "blocks": [{"type": "md", "text": "## Warm-up: chapters 2–4"}, {"type": "predict", "id": "c5-recall-1", "sql": "", "explain": "Dollar weighting amplifies large loans. A big dollar move with a small account move means a few large loans, often one segment (or fraud).", "options": [{"text": "A data error: the two should move together", "correct": false}, {"text": "The newly delinquent loans are much larger than average, so look for a specific segment of big tickets", "correct": true}, {"text": "Small loans are going bad", "correct": false}], "errors": false, "prompt": "The 30+ rate rose 0.3 points by accounts and 3 points by dollars. What does that suggest?"}, {"type": "predict", "id": "c5-recall-2", "sql": "", "explain": "A bad row means one vintage. Look for what changed at origination: policy, model, a merchant, a fraud attack.", "options": [{"text": "A cohort effect: something about who was lent to in that period", "correct": true}, {"text": "A period effect: the economy", "correct": false}, {"text": "Seasoning", "correct": false}], "errors": false, "prompt": "In a vintage triangle, one **row** is bad at every age while its neighbours look normal. What kind of effect is that?"}, {"type": "predict", "id": "c5-recall-3", "sql": "", "explain": "A cutoff moves the marginal bands. The average can be fine while the margin loses money, or the reverse.", "options": [{"text": "The average margin of the whole book", "correct": false}, {"text": "The margin of the bands near the cutoff (the marginal loans)", "correct": true}, {"text": "The best band's margin", "correct": false}], "errors": false, "prompt": "Before July 2024, loans scored 620–639 made about +530 bps and loans scored 580–619 lost about 750 bps. Which matters for setting the cutoff?"}, {"type": "md", "text": "## The playbook\n\nWhen a metric moves, work through these in order. Each step either explains the move or narrows where to look next.\n\n1. **Is it real?** Same definition and data pipeline? Compared with the same period last year (seasonality)? A one-off (a payment outage, a holiday)?\n2. **Did the denominator change?** Growth, age mix, a portfolio sale.\n3. **Mix or rate?** Did the book shift toward riskier segments, or did segments themselves get worse?\n4. **Cohort or period?** One vintage (row) or all vintages at once (column)?\n5. **Front or back of the pipe?** More loans falling in (rolls, first-payment default) or fewer climbing out (cures)?\n6. **Where is it concentrated?** Merchant, product, term, score band, channel, geography, new vs repeat customers.\n7. **Correlation or decision-grade evidence?** Would this finding change what we do for the *next* applicant?\n8. **Size it, recommend, monitor.** How much of the move is explained, what we do, and what would tell us we're wrong.\n\nThe rest of this chapter runs the playbook on a real move in this data.\n\n## The case: November 2025\n\n> \"30+ delinquency jumped from 2.0% in October to 3.5% in November. What happened, and what should we do?\"\n\n**1. Is it real?** The definition hasn't changed. Last year, October to November went 2.6% to 2.0%, so there's no seasonal jump to expect. December stayed at 3.4%, so it's not a one-month blip.\n\n**2. The denominator?** Open loans grew from 2,342 to 2,700 (holiday lending). Growth adds young loans that can't be 30+ yet, so it pushes the rate **down**. The deterioration is, if anything, understated.\n\n**3. Mix or rate?** Split the change by product with a **shift-share**. The overall rate is a weighted average of product rates, so the change splits into a **mix** effect (Δweight × old rate), a **rate** effect (old weight × Δrate), and a small **interaction** (Δweight × Δrate)."}, {"type": "exercise", "id": "c5-shift-share", "kind": "write", "ordered": false, "digits": 4, "prompt": "Shift-share of the 30+ rate (by accounts, open loans) between **2025-10-31** and **2025-11-30**, by `product`. A product's weight is its share of that month's open loans.\n\nReturn `product`, `mix_effect` ((weight Nov − weight Oct) × rate Oct) and `rate_effect` (weight Oct × (rate Nov − rate Oct)).", "starter": "", "solution": "WITH product_month AS (\n  SELECT\n    loan_month.as_of_date,\n    loans.product,\n    COUNT(*) AS n_open,\n    AVG(CASE WHEN loan_month.dpd >= 30 THEN 1.0 ELSE 0 END) AS rate_30_plus\n  FROM loan_month\n  JOIN loans ON loans.loan_id = loan_month.loan_id\n  WHERE loan_month.status = 'open'\n    AND loan_month.as_of_date IN (DATE '2025-10-31', DATE '2025-11-30')\n  GROUP BY loan_month.as_of_date, loans.product\n),\nwith_weight AS (\n  SELECT\n    as_of_date,\n    product,\n    rate_30_plus,\n    1.0 * n_open / SUM(n_open) OVER (PARTITION BY as_of_date) AS weight\n  FROM product_month\n),\nside_by_side AS (\n  SELECT\n    product,\n    MAX(CASE WHEN as_of_date = DATE '2025-10-31' THEN weight END) AS weight_oct,\n    MAX(CASE WHEN as_of_date = DATE '2025-10-31' THEN rate_30_plus END) AS rate_oct,\n    MAX(CASE WHEN as_of_date = DATE '2025-11-30' THEN weight END) AS weight_nov,\n    MAX(CASE WHEN as_of_date = DATE '2025-11-30' THEN rate_30_plus END) AS rate_nov\n  FROM with_weight\n  GROUP BY product\n)\nSELECT\n  product,\n  (weight_nov - weight_oct) * rate_oct AS mix_effect,\n  weight_oct * (rate_nov - rate_oct) AS rate_effect\nFROM side_by_side", "alts": [], "hints": ["Step 1: one row per month and product with the open count and the 30+ rate. Step 2: each product's weight within its month, `n_open / SUM(n_open) OVER (PARTITION BY as_of_date)`.", "Step 3: put October and November side by side, one row per product: `MAX(CASE WHEN as_of_date = DATE '2025-10-31' THEN weight END)` and the same for the other three numbers (a pivot). Then compute the two effects."], "explain": "Mix adds about **+20 bps**: the book shifted toward Pay in 4, whose open loans run a high 30+ rate (good Pay in 4 loans finish in six weeks, so the ones still open skew delinquent: another denominator effect). The **rate** effect within monthly loans is about **+150 bps**: monthly loans went from 1.2% to 2.9% 30+. So the story is \"monthly loans got worse\", not \"the mix moved\". Adding the effects across products (with the interaction, about −7 bps) reproduces the full +156 bps."}, {"type": "md", "text": "**4. Cohort or period?** Count the November 30+ loans by origination month:"}, {"type": "chart", "id": "c5-by-vintage", "caption": "In October, 30+ loans came from the usual mix of recent vintages. In November, **43** of them came from the September 2025 vintage, versus 3 a month earlier. One row of the triangle: a **cohort** effect.", "sql": "SELECT\n  STRFTIME(loans.originated_date, '%Y-%m') AS vintage,\n  STRFTIME(loan_month.as_of_date, '%Y-%m') AS month_end,\n  COUNT(*) AS loans_30_plus\nFROM loan_month\nJOIN loans ON loans.loan_id = loan_month.loan_id\nWHERE loan_month.status = 'open'\n  AND loan_month.dpd >= 30\n  AND loan_month.as_of_date IN (DATE '2025-10-31', DATE '2025-11-30')\n  AND loans.originated_date >= DATE '2025-05-01'\nGROUP BY STRFTIME(loans.originated_date, '%Y-%m'), STRFTIME(loan_month.as_of_date, '%Y-%m')\nORDER BY vintage, month_end", "chart": "bar", "x": "vintage", "y": ["loans_30_plus"], "series": "month_end", "percent": false, "title": "Loans 30+ days past due, by origination month", "stacked": false}, {"type": "md", "text": "**5. Front or back of the pipe?** The front. Chapter 2 showed the 1–29 → 30–59 roll rate jumping to 43% in November, and first-payment default for the September vintage at 13%: these borrowers stopped paying almost immediately. Cures didn't change.\n\n**6. Where is it concentrated?**"}, {"type": "exercise", "id": "c5-merchants", "kind": "write", "ordered": true, "digits": 2, "prompt": "At **2025-11-30**, which merchants have the most open loans that are 30+ days past due? Return the top 5: `merchant_id`, `merchant_name`, `onboarded_date`, `n_open` and `n_30_plus`, most 30+ loans first, breaking ties by the lower `merchant_id`.", "starter": "", "solution": "WITH november AS (\n  SELECT loans.merchant_id, loan_month.dpd\n  FROM loan_month\n  JOIN loans ON loans.loan_id = loan_month.loan_id\n  WHERE loan_month.status = 'open'\n    AND loan_month.as_of_date = DATE '2025-11-30'\n),\nby_merchant AS (\n  SELECT\n    merchant_id,\n    COUNT(*) AS n_open,\n    SUM(CASE WHEN dpd >= 30 THEN 1 ELSE 0 END) AS n_30_plus\n  FROM november\n  GROUP BY merchant_id\n)\nSELECT\n  by_merchant.merchant_id,\n  merchants.merchant_name,\n  merchants.onboarded_date,\n  by_merchant.n_open,\n  by_merchant.n_30_plus\nFROM by_merchant\nJOIN merchants ON merchants.merchant_id = by_merchant.merchant_id\nORDER BY by_merchant.n_30_plus DESC, by_merchant.merchant_id\nLIMIT 5", "alts": [], "hints": ["Aggregate November's open loans to one row per merchant first (count, and a conditional sum for 30+), then join `merchants` for the name and onboarding date. Several merchants tie further down, so the tie-breaker matters: `ORDER BY n_30_plus DESC, merchant_id`."], "explain": "One merchant, onboarded in **August 2025**, has 30 of its 54 open loans 30+ days past due. The next merchant has 5 of 172. A brand-new merchant with more than half its loans in early delinquency is a strong, specific lead."}, {"type": "md", "text": "**7. The red herring.** Cut by state and you'll find Florida's 30+ rate jumped from **1.2% to 10.4%**, with Georgia also up sharply. It's tempting to reach for a story: hurricane season, a local employer, a Florida slowdown. Before you do, ask whether the Florida increase is **the same loans** you already found. It is: 25 of Florida's 28 loans that are 30+ in November are at the August merchant, in accounts opened in the same week of September. Geography here is a **correlate** of the real cause, not the cause, and a regional credit tightening would punish thousands of good Florida customers for one merchant's problem.\n\nThat's the distinction in step 7. \"Florida is up\" is interesting. Decision-grade evidence answers a specific question: **for the applicants we'll see tomorrow, in this segment, is expected loss above what we accept?** For Florida in general, no. For this merchant, very possibly.\n\n**8. Size it.** Exclude the August merchant and the 30+ rate went from 2.0% to 2.5%: so about **110 of the 156 bps** is this one merchant, and about **45 bps** is a broader increase spread across the July–October 2025 vintages (the summer vintages haven't cleared while the autumn ones reach their seasoning peak). Worth watching, but not an emergency."}, {"type": "bridge", "title": "", "text": "Steps 3 and 4 are decompositions you know: **shift-share** (mix versus rate) is standard in labour and trade economics, and cohort-versus-period is the **age–period–cohort** structure from chapter 3. Step 7 is the identification question: does the variable we're conditioning on (state) *cause* the outcome, or does it just co-move with the true cause (one merchant's accounts)? The difference decides the policy."}, {"type": "md", "text": "## Credit or fraud?\n\nBorrowers who never make a first payment, concentrated at one new merchant, in accounts opened in the same week: that pattern has a name, and it isn't \"credit deterioration\". If it's **fraud**, the fix isn't a tighter score cutoff (these applicants scored fine) but identity checks, merchant controls and velocity rules. Chapter 6 picks up exactly here."}, {"type": "interview", "title": "", "text": "Narrate the playbook out loud even when you can't run the queries: \"First I'd check it's real, compare to last year, and rule out a definition change. Then: did the denominator move? Then mix versus rate, cohort versus period, front versus back of the pipe, and where it's concentrated.\" Interviewers score the **order** as much as the answer: it shows you won't chase the first correlation you find."}, {"type": "explain", "id": "c5-exec-summary", "prompt": "Write the three-to-five sentence summary you'd send the head of risk about November 2025: the headline, the cause as you understand it, how sure you are, and what you recommend. Use numbers.", "model": "30+ delinquency rose from 2.0% to 3.5% in November, about 156 bps, and held in December, so it's not seasonal and not a one-off. About 110 bps comes from one merchant onboarded in August 2025: 30 of its 54 open loans are 30+, nearly all from accounts opened in one week in September that never made a payment, a pattern that looks like fraud rather than credit deterioration. Excluding that merchant, delinquency rose a more modest 45 bps, spread across the July–October vintages, which we'll monitor. Recommended today: pause or cap new lending at the merchant pending review, send the affected accounts to fraud review, and add an alert on first-payment default by merchant for newly onboarded merchants. No broad credit tightening is warranted: the Florida increase is the same accounts, not a regional trend."}, {"type": "explain", "id": "c5-residual", "prompt": "After removing the August merchant, 30+ still rose about 45 bps. Using the playbook, list the next three things you'd check about that residual, in order, and why.", "model": "1. **Is it noise?** 45 bps on about 2,600 loans is roughly 12 extra delinquent loans; the standard error of a 2–2.5% rate on that base is about 0.3 points, so the move is borderline. Check whether December confirms it.\n2. **Cohort or period?** It's spread across the July–October vintages. If those vintages are worse at the same MOB than earlier 2025 vintages, look at what changed at origination (merchant mix, a model tweak, a marketing push). If instead all vintages ticked up, it's a period effect: look at the economy or collections.\n3. **Front or back of the pipe?** Rolls up means more loans falling in (an underwriting or early-repayment issue); cures down means collections or payment capacity. Each points to a different owner."}, {"type": "cards", "cards": [{"q": "What are the eight steps of the diagnosis playbook?", "a": "Real? → denominator? → mix vs rate → cohort vs period → front vs back of pipe → concentration → correlation vs decision-grade → size, recommend, monitor.", "id": "05-diagnosing-a-change:c1"}, {"q": "Shift-share: define the mix and rate effects.", "a": "Mix = Σ (Δweight × old rate); rate = Σ (old weight × Δrate); plus a small interaction Σ (Δweight × Δrate).", "id": "05-diagnosing-a-change:c2"}, {"q": "Why do open Pay in 4 loans show a high 30+ rate?", "a": "Good Pay in 4 loans finish in six weeks, so the ones still open skew delinquent. It's a denominator/survivorship effect.", "id": "05-diagnosing-a-change:c3"}, {"q": "How did the November 2025 jump split between mix and rate?", "a": "Mix about +20 bps (more Pay in 4); rate about +150 bps (monthly loans 1.2% → 2.9%).", "id": "05-diagnosing-a-change:c4"}, {"q": "How much of November's 156 bps came from one merchant?", "a": "About 110 bps; excluding it, 30+ rose about 45 bps (2.0% → 2.5%).", "id": "05-diagnosing-a-change:c5"}, {"q": "Why was Florida a red herring?", "a": "Its jump was the same loans (one merchant's accounts opened in one week); a regional tightening would punish many good customers for one merchant's problem.", "id": "05-diagnosing-a-change:c6"}, {"q": "What makes evidence \"decision-grade\"?", "a": "It answers whether expected loss for the next applicants in a specific segment exceeds what we accept, not just that a segment correlates with the move.", "id": "05-diagnosing-a-change:c7"}, {"q": "What pattern suggests fraud rather than credit deterioration?", "a": "No first payment, concentrated at one (new) merchant, accounts opened close together, often large tickets.", "id": "05-diagnosing-a-change:c8"}, {"q": "Why does growth push the delinquency rate down?", "a": "It adds young loans that can't yet be 30+ days past due, so the denominator grows faster than the numerator.", "id": "05-diagnosing-a-change:c9"}]}]}, {"id": "06-fraud", "title": "Fraud for analysts", "minutes": 45, "summary": "When the borrower never meant to pay. How fraud differs from credit risk, the signals that expose it, and the trade-off every rule makes.", "lesson": "", "date": "", "interview": false, "part": "Breadth", "blocks": [{"type": "md", "text": "## Warm-up: chapters 2, 4 and 5"}, {"type": "explain", "id": "c6-recall-1", "prompt": "From memory: list the eight steps of the diagnosis playbook from chapter 5, in order. (Short phrases are fine.)", "model": "Is it real? → did the denominator change? → mix or rate? → cohort or period? → front or back of the pipe? → where is it concentrated? → correlation or decision-grade evidence? → size it, recommend, monitor."}, {"type": "predict", "id": "c6-recall-2", "sql": "", "explain": "Someone who intended to pay almost always makes the first payment. Never paying at all is the fingerprint of an applicant who didn't intend to.", "options": [{"text": "High 90+ delinquency", "correct": false}, {"text": "First-payment default: the borrower never makes the first payment", "correct": true}, {"text": "Low cure rates", "correct": false}], "errors": false, "prompt": "Which is the classic early signal of fraud in a loan book?"}, {"type": "md", "text": "## Fraud is not bad credit\n\nA **credit loss** is a real customer who meant to pay and couldn't. A **fraud loss** is an applicant who never meant to pay, or wasn't who they said they were. The distinction matters because:\n\n- **The fixes differ.** Fraud is stopped by identity checks, velocity rules and merchant controls. A tighter score cutoff often misses it: fraudsters frequently have fine scores (the ring in this data scored 660–710).\n- **The accounting differs.** Many lenders book fraud losses separately from credit losses.\n- **Models get contaminated.** If fraud loans are labelled as credit defaults, the next credit model learns that whatever the fraudsters looked like is \"risky\", and stays blind to the real fraud pattern.\n\n| Type | What it looks like |\n|---|---|\n| **Third-party: stolen identity** | someone applies as a real person without their knowledge; the victim disputes the debt |\n| **Third-party: account takeover** | a criminal gets into a real customer's account and borrows |\n| **First-party: never-pay** | a real person borrows with no intention of repaying; never makes a payment |\n| **First-party: bust-out** | builds a good record, then borrows heavily and disappears; pays once or twice, then stops |\n| **Synthetic identity** | real and invented details stitched together into a \"person\" who builds credit, then busts out |\n| **Merchant fraud or collusion** | a merchant processes fake sales, or knowingly lets a ring through |\n\n## The signals\n\nFraud leaves traces in **identity** (new accounts, unusual email domains, details that don't fit together), **behaviour** (applying at 3 am, minutes after signing up, for amounts near the maximum, for resellable goods), **velocity** (many accounts or applications sharing a device, address or payment method), and **outcome** (first-payment default). Each is weak alone; together they're strong."}, {"type": "chart", "id": "c6-never-paid-weekly", "caption": "Normal weeks have 0–3 loans that never made a payment. The weeks of **8 and 15 September 2025** have 23 and 10. That's the September cluster from chapters 2–5, now seen week by week.", "sql": "WITH ever_paid AS (\n  SELECT DISTINCT loan_id FROM payments WHERE status = 'succeeded'\n),\nfirst_due AS (\n  SELECT loan_id, MIN(due_date) AS first_due_date FROM installments GROUP BY loan_id\n)\nSELECT\n  STRFTIME(DATE_TRUNC('week', loans.originated_date), '%m-%d') AS week,\n  CASE WHEN ever_paid.loan_id IS NULL THEN 'never paid' ELSE 'paid at least once' END AS outcome,\n  COUNT(*) AS n_loans\nFROM loans\nJOIN first_due ON first_due.loan_id = loans.loan_id\nLEFT JOIN ever_paid ON ever_paid.loan_id = loans.loan_id\nWHERE loans.product = 'monthly'\n  AND loans.originated_date BETWEEN DATE '2025-07-07' AND DATE '2025-10-26'\n  AND first_due.first_due_date <= DATE '2025-11-30'\nGROUP BY week, outcome\nORDER BY week, outcome DESC", "chart": "bar", "x": "week", "y": ["n_loans"], "series": "outcome", "percent": false, "title": "Monthly loans by origination week, and how many never made a payment", "stacked": true}, {"type": "md", "text": "The last filter matters: a loan whose first payment isn't due yet can't have \"never paid\", so it isn't counted either way."}, {"type": "exercise", "id": "c6-profile", "kind": "write", "ordered": false, "digits": 2, "prompt": "Profile the **monthly** loans originated in **September 2025**, split by whether the loan **never** had a successful payment. Return `never_paid` (true/false), `n_loans`, `median_hours_since_signup` (median hours from the borrower account's `created_at` to the loan's application `applied_at`), `share_overnight` (share of applications made before 6 am) and `avg_principal`.", "starter": "", "solution": "WITH ever_paid AS (\n  SELECT DISTINCT loan_id FROM payments WHERE status = 'succeeded'\n),\nseptember AS (\n  SELECT\n    loans.principal,\n    applications.applied_at,\n    borrowers.created_at,\n    ever_paid.loan_id IS NULL AS never_paid\n  FROM loans\n  JOIN applications ON applications.application_id = loans.application_id\n  JOIN borrowers ON borrowers.borrower_id = loans.borrower_id\n  LEFT JOIN ever_paid ON ever_paid.loan_id = loans.loan_id\n  WHERE loans.product = 'monthly'\n    AND loans.originated_date >= DATE '2025-09-01'\n    AND loans.originated_date < DATE '2025-10-01'\n)\nSELECT\n  never_paid,\n  COUNT(*) AS n_loans,\n  MEDIAN(DATE_DIFF('hour', created_at, applied_at)) AS median_hours_since_signup,\n  AVG(CASE WHEN HOUR(applied_at) < 6 THEN 1.0 ELSE 0 END) AS share_overnight,\n  AVG(principal) AS avg_principal\nFROM september\nGROUP BY never_paid", "alts": [], "hints": ["\"Never paid\" is an anti-join: build the set of loans with any successful payment (`SELECT DISTINCT loan_id ...`), `LEFT JOIN` it, and test `IS NULL`.", "`DATE_DIFF('hour', created_at, applied_at)` gives hours (in PostgreSQL: `EXTRACT(EPOCH FROM applied_at - created_at) / 3600`). `HOUR(applied_at) < 6` flags overnight applications."], "explain": "The never-paid loans applied a median of **0 hours** after signing up (the payers: about 8,000 hours, roughly a year), 88% of them overnight (payers: 2%), for about \\$1,500 versus \\$1,100. Brand-new accounts, odd hours, bigger tickets: the identity and behaviour signals line up. In a real investigation you'd add velocity (shared devices, addresses, emails), and here you'd find 36 accounts on one unusual email domain, mostly in Florida, all at the merchant from chapter 5."}, {"type": "md", "text": "## Rules and their trade-off\n\nSuppose you deploy a rule: **flag any loan where the account was opened less than 24 hours before the application.** Every rule produces a 2×2 table:\n\n| | never paid (bad) | paid (good) |\n|---|---|---|\n| **flagged** | caught | false positive: a good customer inconvenienced or declined |\n| **not flagged** | missed | fine |\n\n- **Precision**: of the flagged loans, the share that were bad.\n- **Recall** (catch rate): of the bad loans, the share the rule flagged.\n- **False positive rate**: of the good loans, the share flagged. Each one is a customer you annoyed, and lost revenue."}, {"type": "exercise", "id": "c6-rule", "kind": "write", "ordered": false, "digits": 2, "prompt": "Evaluate the rule \"account created **less than 24 hours** before `applied_at`\" on **monthly** loans originated in **2025** whose first installment was due **on or before 2025-11-30**. \"Bad\" = the loan never had a successful payment.\n\nReturn `flagged` (true/false), `n_loans` and `n_never_paid`.", "starter": "", "solution": "WITH ever_paid AS (\n  SELECT DISTINCT loan_id FROM payments WHERE status = 'succeeded'\n),\nfirst_due AS (\n  SELECT loan_id, MIN(due_date) AS first_due_date FROM installments GROUP BY loan_id\n),\nevaluated AS (\n  SELECT\n    DATE_DIFF('hour', borrowers.created_at, applications.applied_at) < 24 AS flagged,\n    ever_paid.loan_id IS NULL AS never_paid\n  FROM loans\n  JOIN applications ON applications.application_id = loans.application_id\n  JOIN borrowers ON borrowers.borrower_id = loans.borrower_id\n  JOIN first_due ON first_due.loan_id = loans.loan_id\n  LEFT JOIN ever_paid ON ever_paid.loan_id = loans.loan_id\n  WHERE loans.product = 'monthly'\n    AND loans.originated_date >= DATE '2025-01-01'\n    AND first_due.first_due_date <= DATE '2025-11-30'\n)\nSELECT\n  flagged,\n  COUNT(*) AS n_loans,\n  SUM(CASE WHEN never_paid THEN 1 ELSE 0 END) AS n_never_paid\nFROM evaluated\nGROUP BY flagged", "alts": [], "hints": ["Same \"never paid\" anti-join as before, plus the maturity filter on the first due date (collapse `installments` to one row per loan first)."], "explain": "Flagged: 44 loans, 30 never paid. Not flagged: 2,225 loans, 26 never paid. Precision 30 / 44 = **68%**; recall 30 / 56 = **54%**; false positive rate 14 / 2,213 = **0.6%**. Look closer at the 14 \"false positives\": 6 are ring accounts that paid once and then stopped (bust-outs). The rule caught them; the **label** (\"never paid\") missed them. Fraud labels are always imperfect and late, which makes rules look worse than they are."}, {"type": "predict", "id": "c6-base-rate", "sql": "", "explain": "Per 10,000 applications: 20 fraud, 18 caught; 9,980 good, about 100 wrongly flagged. Precision ≈ 18 / 118 ≈ 15%. With rare events, even a good classifier's flags are mostly false positives, which is why flags usually trigger **step-up verification** (prove who you are) rather than a hard decline.", "options": [{"text": "About 90%", "correct": false}, {"text": "About 50%", "correct": false}, {"text": "About 15%", "correct": true}], "errors": false, "prompt": "A fraud model catches 90% of fraud and wrongly flags only 1% of good applications. Fraud is 0.2% of applications. Of the applications it flags, roughly what share are actually fraud?"}, {"type": "md", "text": "## Measuring a fraud programme\n\n- **Fraud losses**, in bps of GMV, by type and by channel or merchant.\n- **Catch rate** (recall) and **false positive rate**, plus the **customer cost** of each false positive (lost sale, friction, complaints).\n- **Review capacity**: a rule that flags 2,000 cases a day is useless if the team can review 200.\n- **Time to detect**: the ring here was visible in first-payment default about a month after it started; a velocity rule could have caught it the same night.\n- **Label delay**: fraud is often confirmed weeks or months later (disputes, investigations), so recent rule performance is always provisional.\n\n## What to do about a ring\n\n**Today:** pause or cap new lending at the merchant; freeze the linked accounts and send them to review; block the shared attributes (email domain, devices).\n**This month:** add velocity rules (accounts per device or address per day), step-up verification for brand-new accounts requesting large amounts, lower first-loan limits for brand-new accounts, and a first-payment-default alert by merchant for newly onboarded merchants.\n**Ask about the merchant:** was it a victim, or complicit? Check refund patterns, the merchant's own onboarding documents, and whether the goods were delivered.\n**Clean the labels:** mark these loans as fraud so they're excluded from credit-model training, and don't report true identity-theft victims to credit bureaus."}, {"type": "bridge", "title": "", "text": "Every rule is a **hypothesis test** with Type I errors (false positives: good customers flagged) and Type II errors (missed fraud). Moving the threshold trades one for the other along an **ROC curve**. The base-rate example is just **Bayes' rule**: with rare events, a low false positive rate still produces mostly false alarms."}, {"type": "interview", "title": "", "text": "\"Is this fraud or credit?\" Answer with the signals: **timing** (no first payment → fraud; pays for months then stops → credit), **concentration** (one merchant, one week, shared attributes → fraud; broad → credit), **identity** (new accounts, odd details), and **the fix** you'd apply in each case. Then mention labels: you'd separate fraud from credit losses before training any model."}, {"type": "explain", "id": "c6-rules", "prompt": "Propose two rules (or controls) that would have stopped most of the September ring **without** declining many good customers, and say what each costs.", "model": "1. **Step-up verification for brand-new accounts requesting large amounts:** if the account is under 24 hours old and the amount exceeds \\$1,000, require an identity check (document or bank-account link) before approval. It would have flagged nearly all the ring's loans, and only a handful of legitimate ones a year (about 8 here), who'd face friction rather than a decline. Cost: some abandonment by real customers, plus vendor fees for verification.\n2. **Velocity and attribute rules:** cap new accounts per email domain, device or address per day, and alert on unusual concentrations (36 accounts on one rare domain in a week). Almost no good customer trips it. Cost: engineering, and fraudsters adapt (they'll rotate domains), so it needs maintenance.\n\nA third, cheap safeguard: a first-payment-default alert by merchant for merchants onboarded in the last 90 days, which would have flagged this merchant in October."}, {"type": "explain", "id": "c6-training", "prompt": "A colleague wants to train next year's credit model on all 2025 loans, using \"charged off or 90+ days past due\" as the bad outcome. What's wrong with that, and what would you do?", "model": "The ring's loans will become \"bad\" outcomes, so the model learns that their characteristics are high **credit** risk. But their scores were fine and their behaviour was fraud. The model gets a distorted signal (for example, penalising Florida or electronics), and it's still blind to the next ring, which will look different. Fraud-confirmed loans should be labelled separately and excluded from credit-model training (or modelled by a fraud model). I'd also check for other likely-fraud loans (never paid, brand-new accounts, shared attributes), and sanity-check that excluding them doesn't remove real credit defaults."}, {"type": "cards", "cards": [{"q": "Fraud loss vs credit loss: what's the difference?", "a": "Credit: a real customer who meant to pay and couldn't. Fraud: the applicant never meant to pay or wasn't who they claimed.", "id": "06-fraud:c1"}, {"q": "Why must fraud and credit losses be separated?", "a": "Different fixes (identity and velocity controls vs score cutoffs), different accounting, and fraud labels contaminate credit-model training.", "id": "06-fraud:c2"}, {"q": "First-party vs third-party fraud?", "a": "First-party: a real person who never intends to pay (never-pay, bust-out). Third-party: someone using another person's identity or account.", "id": "06-fraud:c3"}, {"q": "What is a bust-out?", "a": "Building a good record, then borrowing heavily and disappearing, often after one or two payments.", "id": "06-fraud:c4"}, {"q": "Four families of fraud signals?", "a": "Identity (new accounts, odd details), behaviour (overnight, minutes after signup, high amounts), velocity (shared device/address/email), outcome (first-payment default).", "id": "06-fraud:c5"}, {"q": "Define precision, recall and false positive rate for a rule.", "a": "Precision: bad ÷ flagged. Recall: flagged bad ÷ all bad. False positive rate: flagged good ÷ all good.", "id": "06-fraud:c6"}, {"q": "In this data, how did the \"account under 24 hours old\" rule do?", "a": "44 flagged, 30 never paid: precision 68%, recall 54%, false positive rate 0.6%; 6 of the \"false positives\" were bust-outs.", "id": "06-fraud:c7"}, {"q": "Why do fraud flags usually trigger step-up verification rather than a decline?", "a": "Base rates: fraud is rare, so even a good model's flags are mostly good customers (Bayes).", "id": "06-fraud:c8"}, {"q": "What would you do today about a fraud ring at one merchant?", "a": "Pause/cap the merchant, freeze and review the linked accounts, block shared attributes; then add velocity rules, step-up checks and a new-merchant FPD alert.", "id": "06-fraud:c9"}, {"q": "How do you answer \"is this fraud or credit?\"", "a": "Timing (no first payment → fraud), concentration (one merchant/week/shared attributes → fraud), identity signals, and the different fix each implies.", "id": "06-fraud:c10"}]}]}, {"id": "07-growth", "title": "Growth and the growth–risk trade-off", "minutes": 45, "summary": "Approval rate is a product lever as well as a risk lever. How growth teams measure the funnel, repeat use and lifetime value, and where they meet the risk team.", "lesson": "", "date": "", "interview": false, "part": "Breadth", "blocks": [{"type": "md", "text": "## Warm-up: chapters 1, 3 and 6"}, {"type": "predict", "id": "c7-recall-1", "sql": "", "explain": "Precision = bad ÷ flagged = 20 / 50. Recall = flagged bad ÷ all bad = 20 / 40.", "options": [{"text": "Precision 40%, recall 50%", "correct": true}, {"text": "Precision 50%, recall 40%", "correct": false}, {"text": "Precision 20%, recall 50%", "correct": false}], "errors": false, "prompt": "A rule flags 50 applications; 20 turn out to be fraud. Fraud overall was 40 applications. What are its precision and recall?"}, {"type": "predict", "id": "c7-recall-2", "sql": "", "explain": "Growth adds young loans that can't be delinquent yet. Only a same-age vintage comparison shows whether credit actually improved.", "options": [{"text": "Whether the merchant fees rose", "correct": false}, {"text": "Whether the book got younger: compare vintages at the same months on book", "correct": true}, {"text": "Whether payments are deduplicated", "correct": false}], "errors": false, "prompt": "The lender grew fast all year and the portfolio 30+ rate fell. What should you check before celebrating?"}, {"type": "md", "text": "## The funnel\n\nGrowth metrics follow the customer through the same tables you know:\n\n| Stage | Metric | Table |\n|---|---|---|\n| Shows up | applications (or checkout views → applications) | `applications` |\n| Is approved | **approval rate** | `applications.decision` |\n| Takes the loan | **conversion** (approved → booked) | `applications` → `loans` |\n| Spends | GMV, average order value | `loans.principal` |\n| Comes back | **repeat rate**, loans per customer, retention | `loans` by `borrower_id` |"}, {"type": "chart", "id": "c7-funnel", "caption": "Approval fell from about 79% in early 2024 to about 70% after the July 2024 tightening, and stayed there. Conversion (approved applicants who take the loan) holds steady at 87–90%. Applications kept growing, with big holiday spikes, so volume grew despite the lower approval rate.", "sql": "WITH funnel AS (\n  SELECT\n    STRFTIME(applications.applied_at, '%Y-%m') AS month,\n    applications.decision,\n    loans.loan_id IS NOT NULL AS booked\n  FROM applications\n  LEFT JOIN loans ON loans.application_id = applications.application_id\n  WHERE applications.applied_at >= TIMESTAMP '2024-01-01'\n)\nSELECT\n  month,\n  AVG(CASE WHEN decision = 'approved' THEN 1.0 ELSE 0 END) AS approval_rate,\n  AVG(CASE WHEN booked THEN 1.0 ELSE 0 END) FILTER (WHERE decision = 'approved') AS conversion\nFROM funnel\nGROUP BY month\nORDER BY month", "chart": "line", "x": "month", "y": ["approval_rate", "conversion"], "series": null, "percent": true, "title": "Approval rate and conversion, by application month", "stacked": false}, {"type": "md", "text": "**Approval rate is a growth lever.** Every point of approval is volume, and merchants judge a lender partly by how many of their customers it approves. That's where growth and risk teams meet: growth wants approval up; risk wants losses inside appetite. Chapter 4's margin-by-band analysis is the shared language: approve more **where the marginal loan is profitable**.\n\n## A decline isn't the end of the story\n\nMany declined applicants try again straight away, usually for less."}, {"type": "exercise", "id": "c7-retries", "kind": "write", "ordered": false, "digits": 3, "prompt": "For every **declined** application, look at the same borrower account's **next** application (if any). Return `n_declines`, `n_retried_30m` (next application within 30 minutes), `n_retry_approved` (retried within 30 minutes and approved) and `avg_amount_ratio` (next requested amount ÷ declined requested amount, among retries within 30 minutes).", "starter": "", "solution": "WITH sequenced AS (\n  SELECT\n    decision,\n    applied_at,\n    requested_amount,\n    LEAD(applied_at) OVER (PARTITION BY borrower_id ORDER BY applied_at) AS next_applied_at,\n    LEAD(decision) OVER (PARTITION BY borrower_id ORDER BY applied_at) AS next_decision,\n    LEAD(requested_amount) OVER (PARTITION BY borrower_id ORDER BY applied_at) AS next_amount\n  FROM applications\n),\ndeclines AS (\n  SELECT\n    *,\n    next_applied_at <= applied_at + INTERVAL 30 MINUTE AS retried_30m\n  FROM sequenced\n  WHERE decision = 'declined'\n)\nSELECT\n  COUNT(*) AS n_declines,\n  SUM(CASE WHEN retried_30m THEN 1 ELSE 0 END) AS n_retried_30m,\n  SUM(CASE WHEN retried_30m AND next_decision = 'approved' THEN 1 ELSE 0 END) AS n_retry_approved,\n  AVG(CASE WHEN retried_30m THEN next_amount / requested_amount END) AS avg_amount_ratio\nFROM declines", "alts": [], "hints": ["`LEAD(...) OVER (PARTITION BY borrower_id ORDER BY applied_at)` gives each application the same account's next application: its time, decision and amount.", "A declined application with no next application has a NULL `next_applied_at`, so the 30-minute test is NULL. `CASE WHEN retried_30m THEN ...` treats that as not retried. In PostgreSQL write `INTERVAL '30 minutes'`."], "explain": "Of 3,753 declines, 164 retried within 30 minutes (4.4%), 66 of those were approved (40%), at about **57%** of the original amount. Two lessons. **Measurement:** approval rate per application understates approval per customer, so define which one a dashboard shows. **Product:** if a smaller amount is approvable, offering it directly (a \"counter-offer\") recovers a sale at lower risk without making the customer guess. That's the \"amount\" lever from chapter 4."}, {"type": "md", "text": "## Repeat use and lifetime value\n\nA growth team's favourite question: **do customers come back?**"}, {"type": "exercise", "id": "c7-repeat", "kind": "write", "ordered": false, "digits": 3, "prompt": "For borrower accounts whose **first** loan was originated in **2024**, the share that took a **second** loan within **180 days** of the first, by the first loan's product. Order loans by `originated_date`, then `loan_id`.\n\nReturn `first_product`, `n_borrowers`, `repeat_180d`.", "starter": "", "solution": "WITH sequenced AS (\n  SELECT\n    borrower_id,\n    product,\n    originated_date,\n    ROW_NUMBER() OVER (PARTITION BY borrower_id ORDER BY originated_date, loan_id) AS loan_number\n  FROM loans\n),\nfirst_loans AS (\n  SELECT borrower_id, product AS first_product, originated_date AS first_date\n  FROM sequenced\n  WHERE loan_number = 1\n),\nsecond_loans AS (\n  SELECT borrower_id, originated_date AS second_date\n  FROM sequenced\n  WHERE loan_number = 2\n)\nSELECT\n  first_loans.first_product,\n  COUNT(*) AS n_borrowers,\n  AVG(CASE WHEN second_loans.second_date <= first_loans.first_date + INTERVAL 180 DAY THEN 1.0 ELSE 0 END) AS repeat_180d\nFROM first_loans\nLEFT JOIN second_loans ON second_loans.borrower_id = first_loans.borrower_id\nWHERE first_loans.first_date >= DATE '2024-01-01'\n  AND first_loans.first_date < DATE '2025-01-01'\nGROUP BY first_loans.first_product", "alts": [], "hints": ["Number each account's loans with `ROW_NUMBER()`; take loan 1 and loan 2 into separate CTEs; `LEFT JOIN` them so accounts with only one loan stay in the denominator."], "explain": "About a third of 2024 first-time borrowers came back within six months, and Pay in 4 first-timers didn't return noticeably more often than monthly ones. That's evidence **against** the simple \"Pay in 4 is the front door\" story from chapter 1, at least on this window. That's what checking a claim with data looks like. Note the `LEFT JOIN`: an inner join would keep only borrowers who came back, and the repeat rate would be 100%."}, {"type": "md", "text": "**Lifetime value (LTV)**, in its simplest form: margin per loan × expected number of loans per customer, discounted. A customer whose first loan barely breaks even can still be valuable if they come back. Two cautions:\n\n- **Selection.** Repeat customers look safer (here, repeat loans made before July 2024 charged off 5.3% of the time versus 6.7% for first loans). Part of that is that the riskiest first-time borrowers never become repeat borrowers. Don't attribute it to the product \"improving\" people.\n- **Survival.** LTV depends on how many customers are still active after 6, 12 or 24 months: a retention curve, which is the same idea as a vintage curve with \"came back\" instead of \"charged off\".\n\n## Experiments: incrementality and guardrails\n\nGrowth teams live on A/B tests, and lending adds two twists:\n\n- **Incrementality.** Did the change create the purchase, or would the customer have bought anyway (on a card, say)? Merchants pay for **incremental** sales, so lenders run holdout studies at merchants to measure uplift.\n- **Guardrails.** A test that raises approvals must also watch losses, which arrive late. Pre-agree the guardrail metrics (first-payment default, 30+ at MOB 3) and stop rules, as in chapter 4.\n\nAnd growth doesn't have to mean looser credit: new merchants, new channels (a card, an app, wallets), more use by existing customers and 0% APR promotions all add volume without changing who's approved. A good question in any interview: **\"how much of this growth is distribution, and how much is credit expansion?\"**"}, {"type": "bridge", "title": "", "text": "LTV is a **present value** of a stream of expected margins; retention curves are **survival** analysis. Incrementality is the core causal question (treatment effect versus selection), and merchant holdout studies are randomised experiments. Your econometrics is directly useful here, especially in spotting selection in \"repeat customers are better\" claims."}, {"type": "interview", "title": "", "text": "Growth case shape: (1) define the metric precisely (per application or per customer? GMV or loans?); (2) decompose it (traffic × approval × conversion × order value × repeat); (3) find which component moved; (4) for any proposal, name the **risk guardrail** and how you'd test for incrementality. Mentioning the guardrail unprompted is the signal that you can work with both teams."}, {"type": "explain", "id": "c7-loosen-for-growth", "prompt": "The growth team says: \"approval fell 9 points after July 2024, losses are clearly fine now, let's go back to the old policy.\" Frame the decision in five or six sentences, using numbers from chapters 3, 4 and 7.", "model": "The tightening cost about 9 points of approval, but volume kept growing on application growth, so the question isn't growth versus no growth, it's which marginal loans are worth making. \"Losses are fine now\" partly reflects young vintages; age for age, losses roughly halved (4–5% to about 2% at MOB 6), which is the tightening working. Fully reverting would bring back the 580–619 band, which lost around 750 bps per dollar, so that's a no. The 620–679 band was profitable before the change, and is where I'd look to recover approvals. I'd propose a randomised test there, possibly with counter-offers at lower amounts (40% of quick retries get approved at about 57% of the amount), with first-payment-default and MOB-3 guardrails, and measure incremental GMV and margin rather than approval rate alone."}, {"type": "explain", "id": "c7-counteroffer-test", "prompt": "Design an experiment to test **counter-offers**: when an application would be declined, offer a lower approvable amount instead. What's randomised, what you measure, and one way the results could fool you.", "model": "**Randomise** applicants who'd be declined (and for whom a lower amount passes policy) between control (decline as today) and treatment (counter-offer at the highest approvable amount). Randomise at the borrower level so retries don't cross groups.\n**Measure:** take-up of the counter-offer; incremental loans and GMV versus control (control borrowers who retry and get approved count too); first-payment default and MOB-3 delinquency on the counter-offered loans; complaints; and margin per applicant, not per loan.\n**How it could fool you:** control applicants already retry for smaller amounts on their own (about 4% within 30 minutes), so the true increment is smaller than \"counter-offers accepted\". Losses arrive late, so an early read can look rosy. And if counter-offers attract more applicants who game the system (apply high, accept lower), the population shifts over time."}, {"type": "cards", "cards": [{"q": "Name the funnel stages and their metrics.", "a": "Applications → approval rate → conversion (approved → booked) → GMV / order value → repeat rate and retention.", "id": "07-growth:c1"}, {"q": "Why is approval rate both a growth and a risk lever?", "a": "Each point adds volume (and merchants judge lenders by it), but marginal approvals carry higher losses; the shared language is marginal margin by segment.", "id": "07-growth:c2"}, {"q": "What happens after a decline in this data?", "a": "4.4% retry within 30 minutes; 40% of those are approved, at about 57% of the original amount.", "id": "07-growth:c3"}, {"q": "What's a counter-offer, and why use one?", "a": "Offering a lower approvable amount instead of declining: it recovers sales at lower risk, using the amount lever.", "id": "07-growth:c4"}, {"q": "Why must a repeat-rate query use a LEFT JOIN from first loans?", "a": "To keep one-loan customers in the denominator; an inner join keeps only returners (rate = 100%).", "id": "07-growth:c5"}, {"q": "Why do repeat customers look safer, and why be careful?", "a": "Selection: the riskiest first-time borrowers don't become repeat borrowers. Don't credit the product for it.", "id": "07-growth:c6"}, {"q": "Simplest LTV formula?", "a": "Margin per loan × expected loans per customer, discounted; it depends on a retention (survival) curve.", "id": "07-growth:c7"}, {"q": "What's incrementality?", "a": "Whether a change created sales that wouldn't have happened anyway; measured with holdouts or experiments.", "id": "07-growth:c8"}, {"q": "What guardrails belong in a growth test in lending?", "a": "Early loss metrics (first-payment default, 30+ at MOB 3) with pre-agreed stop rules, because charge-offs arrive late.", "id": "07-growth:c9"}, {"q": "What question separates good growth from risky growth?", "a": "How much comes from distribution (merchants, channels, usage) versus credit expansion (approving riskier people)?", "id": "07-growth:c10"}]}]}, {"id": "08-models-for-analysts", "title": "Models for analysts", "minutes": 50, "summary": "You won't build the credit model, but you'll be asked whether it works. Rank-ordering, KS, AUC, stability and calibration, all in SQL.", "lesson": "", "date": "", "interview": false, "part": "Breadth", "blocks": [{"type": "md", "text": "## Warm-up: chapters 3, 4 and 6"}, {"type": "predict", "id": "c8-recall-1", "sql": "", "explain": "Chapter 4: 580–619 lost roughly 700–800 bps; 620–679 made roughly 530–710 bps. The tightening cut both.", "options": [{"text": "Every band was profitable, so the tightening was pure caution", "correct": false}, {"text": "580–619 lost money on every dollar lent, while 620–679 was solidly profitable", "correct": true}, {"text": "Only scores above 700 were profitable", "correct": false}], "errors": false, "prompt": "Before the July 2024 tightening, which statement about score bands was true in this data?"}, {"type": "explain", "id": "c8-recall-2", "prompt": "From memory: define precision and recall for a fraud rule, and say why a rule with a 1% false positive rate can still produce mostly false alarms.", "model": "Precision: of the flagged cases, the share that are bad. Recall: of the bad cases, the share flagged. When the bad event is rare (say 0.2%), the 1% of good cases flagged vastly outnumber the bad ones caught, so most flags are good customers. That's the base rate (Bayes)."}, {"type": "explain", "id": "c8-recall-3", "prompt": "From memory: why can't you compare the cumulative loss rate of a 2023 vintage and a 2025 vintage as of today? What do you compare instead?", "model": "They've had different amounts of time to go bad: the 2023 vintage is fully seasoned, the 2025 one isn't. Compare at the same months on book (for example, charge-off rate at MOB 6), or use an early-read metric like 30+ at MOB 3."}, {"type": "md", "text": "## What the analyst's job is\n\nA credit model turns applicant data into a **score**, usually an estimated probability of going bad. Data scientists build it. Risk analysts are asked the questions around it:\n\n- **Does it rank?** Are higher-risk applicants actually scored worse?\n- **How well does it separate** good from bad? (KS, AUC, Gini)\n- **Is it stable?** Does today's population look like the one it was built on? (PSI)\n- **Is it calibrated?** When it says 5%, do 5% go bad?\n- **What should the cutoff be?** (chapter 4: that's an economics question, not a model question)\n\nAll five are SQL plus a little arithmetic. The model is a black box you test from the outside.\n\nThroughout this chapter: loans originated **before 1 July 2024** with a score, and \"bad\" = the loan's `status` is `'charged_off'`. That's 2,146 loans, 130 of them bad (6.1%).\n\n## Does it rank? Deciles\n\nSort loans by score, cut them into ten equal groups, and compute the bad rate in each. A good score gives a falling staircase."}, {"type": "chart", "id": "c8-deciles", "caption": "Decile 1 (scores 550–635) charges off 20% of the time; decile 10 (772+) not once. The staircase isn't perfect: decile 5 is worse than decile 4, and 9 worse than 8. With about 215 loans and only 3–21 bads in each middle decile, a bump of a few loans is noise. What you'd worry about is a **systematic** reversal, such as the top three deciles all worse than the middle.", "sql": "WITH scored AS (\n  SELECT\n    loans.loan_id,\n    applications.credit_score,\n    CASE WHEN loans.status = 'charged_off' THEN 1 ELSE 0 END AS bad\n  FROM loans\n  JOIN applications ON applications.application_id = loans.application_id\n  WHERE loans.originated_date < DATE '2024-07-01'\n    AND applications.credit_score IS NOT NULL\n),\ndeciles AS (\n  SELECT *, NTILE(10) OVER (ORDER BY credit_score, loan_id) AS decile\n  FROM scored\n)\nSELECT decile, AVG(bad) AS bad_rate\nFROM deciles\nGROUP BY decile\nORDER BY decile", "chart": "bar", "x": "decile", "y": ["bad_rate"], "series": null, "percent": true, "title": "Charge-off rate by score decile (1 = lowest scores), loans before July 2024", "stacked": false}, {"type": "exercise", "id": "c8-decile-table", "kind": "write", "ordered": false, "digits": 3, "prompt": "Build the decile table for loans originated **before 2024-07-01** with a non-null `credit_score`. Bad = `status = 'charged_off'`. Use `NTILE(10)` ordered by score (lowest score = decile 1; break ties by `loan_id`).\n\nReturn `decile`, `min_score`, `max_score`, `n_loans`, `n_bad` and `bad_rate`, ordered by `decile`.", "starter": "", "solution": "WITH scored AS (\n  SELECT\n    loans.loan_id,\n    applications.credit_score,\n    CASE WHEN loans.status = 'charged_off' THEN 1 ELSE 0 END AS bad\n  FROM loans\n  JOIN applications ON applications.application_id = loans.application_id\n  WHERE loans.originated_date < DATE '2024-07-01'\n    AND applications.credit_score IS NOT NULL\n),\ndeciles AS (\n  SELECT *, NTILE(10) OVER (ORDER BY credit_score, loan_id) AS decile\n  FROM scored\n)\nSELECT\n  decile,\n  MIN(credit_score) AS min_score,\n  MAX(credit_score) AS max_score,\n  COUNT(*) AS n_loans,\n  SUM(bad) AS n_bad,\n  AVG(bad) AS bad_rate\nFROM deciles\nGROUP BY decile\nORDER BY decile", "alts": [], "hints": ["`NTILE(10) OVER (ORDER BY credit_score, loan_id)` assigns each row a group from 1 to 10 with (nearly) equal counts. Compute it in its own CTE, then aggregate by it."], "explain": "The **null filter matters**: `NTILE` sorts nulls last in DuckDB, so without it the 56 unscored loans would land in decile 10 and give the best-scored group a bad rate it didn't earn. The tie-breaker matters too: many loans share a score, and without `loan_id` the cut between deciles is arbitrary, so results can change from run to run."}, {"type": "rlens", "title": "", "text": "`ntile()` in dplyr is the same function: `mutate(decile = ntile(credit_score, 10))`."}, {"type": "md", "text": "## How well does it separate? KS\n\nWalk up the score range from the lowest score. At each score, ask: what share of **all bads** have I passed, and what share of **all goods**? A score that separates well passes the bads quickly (they're concentrated at the bottom) and the goods slowly. **KS** is the largest gap between those two cumulative shares."}, {"type": "chart", "id": "c8-ks-curve", "caption": "The gap is widest at a score of about 694: by then the walk has passed 85% of all bads but only 46% of goods. KS = 0.38. As a rough guide, application scorecards usually land around 0.3–0.5; under 0.2 is weak, and over 0.6 is suspicious (check for a leaked outcome).", "sql": "WITH scored AS (\n  SELECT\n    applications.credit_score,\n    CASE WHEN loans.status = 'charged_off' THEN 1 ELSE 0 END AS bad\n  FROM loans\n  JOIN applications ON applications.application_id = loans.application_id\n  WHERE loans.originated_date < DATE '2024-07-01'\n    AND applications.credit_score IS NOT NULL\n),\nby_score AS (\n  SELECT credit_score, SUM(bad) AS n_bad, SUM(1 - bad) AS n_good\n  FROM scored\n  GROUP BY credit_score\n)\nSELECT\n  credit_score,\n  SUM(n_bad) OVER (ORDER BY credit_score) / SUM(n_bad) OVER () AS cum_bad,\n  SUM(n_good) OVER (ORDER BY credit_score) / SUM(n_good) OVER () AS cum_good\nFROM by_score\nORDER BY credit_score", "chart": "line", "x": "credit_score", "y": ["cum_bad", "cum_good"], "series": null, "percent": true, "title": "Cumulative share of bads and goods, walking up the score range", "stacked": false}, {"type": "exercise", "id": "c8-ks", "kind": "write", "ordered": false, "digits": 3, "prompt": "Compute KS for the same loans (before 2024-07-01, non-null score, bad = `charged_off`). Return one row: `ks`, the maximum over scores of (cumulative share of bads − cumulative share of goods), walking up from the lowest score.", "starter": "", "solution": "WITH scored AS (\n  SELECT\n    applications.credit_score,\n    CASE WHEN loans.status = 'charged_off' THEN 1 ELSE 0 END AS bad\n  FROM loans\n  JOIN applications ON applications.application_id = loans.application_id\n  WHERE loans.originated_date < DATE '2024-07-01'\n    AND applications.credit_score IS NOT NULL\n),\nby_score AS (\n  SELECT credit_score, SUM(bad) AS n_bad, SUM(1 - bad) AS n_good\n  FROM scored\n  GROUP BY credit_score\n),\ncumulative AS (\n  SELECT\n    credit_score,\n    SUM(n_bad) OVER (ORDER BY credit_score) / SUM(n_bad) OVER () AS cum_bad,\n    SUM(n_good) OVER (ORDER BY credit_score) / SUM(n_good) OVER () AS cum_good\n  FROM by_score\n)\nSELECT MAX(cum_bad - cum_good) AS ks\nFROM cumulative", "alts": [], "hints": ["Collapse to one row per score first (counts of bads and goods), so loans sharing a score move together.", "A running total is `SUM(x) OVER (ORDER BY credit_score)`; the grand total is `SUM(x) OVER ()`. Divide one by the other."], "explain": "KS = **0.383**. Collapsing to one row per score first is what makes ties behave: if you ran the window over loan rows, two loans with the same score would be split across two points of the curve."}, {"type": "md", "text": "## AUC and Gini\n\n**AUC** answers a cleaner question: pick one bad loan and one good loan at random; how often does the bad one have the **lower** score? 0.5 is a coin flip; 1.0 is perfect. **Gini** = 2 × AUC − 1, the same thing on a 0–1 scale; it's the number many credit teams quote.\n\nThe definition translates straight into SQL: join every bad to every good and average the comparisons (130 × 2,016 ≈ 262,000 pairs, which is fine)."}, {"type": "scratch", "id": "c8-auc", "prompt": "Run it. Ties count as half.", "starter": "WITH scored AS (\n  SELECT\n    applications.credit_score,\n    CASE WHEN loans.status = 'charged_off' THEN 1 ELSE 0 END AS bad\n  FROM loans\n  JOIN applications ON applications.application_id = loans.application_id\n  WHERE loans.originated_date < DATE '2024-07-01'\n    AND applications.credit_score IS NOT NULL\n),\nbad_loans AS (SELECT credit_score FROM scored WHERE bad = 1),\ngood_loans AS (SELECT credit_score FROM scored WHERE bad = 0)\nSELECT\n  AVG(CASE\n        WHEN bad_loans.credit_score < good_loans.credit_score THEN 1.0\n        WHEN bad_loans.credit_score = good_loans.credit_score THEN 0.5\n        ELSE 0\n      END) AS auc\nFROM bad_loans\nCROSS JOIN good_loans"}, {"type": "md", "text": "AUC = **0.75**, Gini = **0.51**. Respectable for a single bureau-style score. On millions of rows you'd use a rank formula instead of the pairwise join, but the definition is what you need in an interview."}, {"type": "pitfall", "title": "These measure ranking, not level", "text": "A model that predicts 50% for every bad loan and 40% for every good one has a perfect AUC and is useless for pricing or loss forecasting. Ranking (KS, AUC) and calibration (below) are separate tests, and a model can pass one and fail the other."}, {"type": "md", "text": "## Is it stable? PSI\n\nA model is built on one population and used on another. The **population stability index** compares the score distribution now (\"actual\") with the one at build time (\"expected\"):\n\nPSI = Σ over bins of (actual share − expected share) × ln(actual share ÷ expected share)\n\nRule of thumb: under 0.1 stable, 0.1–0.25 worth investigating, over 0.25 a significant shift. The same formula applied to a single input variable is often called a **CSI** (characteristic stability index)."}, {"type": "exercise", "id": "c8-psi", "kind": "write", "ordered": false, "digits": 4, "prompt": "Compute PSI for **applicant** credit scores, 2023 (expected) vs 2025 (actual), using these bands: under 620, 620–659, 660–699, 700–739, 740+. Use applications with a non-null score and `applied_at` in those years.\n\nReturn `score_band`, `expected_share`, `actual_share` and `psi_part` (that band's term), ordered by band.", "starter": "", "solution": "WITH banded AS (\n  SELECT\n    YEAR(applied_at) AS application_year,\n    CASE\n      WHEN credit_score < 620 THEN '1: under 620'\n      WHEN credit_score < 660 THEN '2: 620-659'\n      WHEN credit_score < 700 THEN '3: 660-699'\n      WHEN credit_score < 740 THEN '4: 700-739'\n      ELSE '5: 740+'\n    END AS score_band\n  FROM applications\n  WHERE credit_score IS NOT NULL\n    AND YEAR(applied_at) IN (2023, 2025)\n),\ncounts AS (\n  SELECT\n    score_band,\n    SUM(CASE WHEN application_year = 2023 THEN 1 ELSE 0 END) AS n_2023,\n    SUM(CASE WHEN application_year = 2025 THEN 1 ELSE 0 END) AS n_2025\n  FROM banded\n  GROUP BY score_band\n),\nshares AS (\n  SELECT\n    score_band,\n    n_2023 * 1.0 / SUM(n_2023) OVER () AS expected_share,\n    n_2025 * 1.0 / SUM(n_2025) OVER () AS actual_share\n  FROM counts\n)\nSELECT\n  score_band,\n  expected_share,\n  actual_share,\n  (actual_share - expected_share) * LN(actual_share / expected_share) AS psi_part\nFROM shares\nORDER BY score_band", "alts": [], "hints": ["Count each band in each year with conditional aggregation, then turn counts into shares with `count / SUM(count) OVER ()`.", "`LN()` is the natural log in both DuckDB and PostgreSQL. Watch integer division: multiply by `1.0` before dividing."], "explain": "The parts sum to a PSI of **0.005**: the applicants coming through the door barely changed. Now run the same query on **approved** applications only and you get **0.056**: the approved book shifted (the under-620 share fell from 4.9% to 2.0%), because policy changed, not because applicants did. Always ask *which* population a PSI is computed on."}, {"type": "md", "text": "## Is it calibrated?\n\nTreat the pre-tightening charge-off rate in each score band as the model's \"prediction\", then check it against loans made **after** the tightening (July–December 2024, which have had their full 12 months). Bad here = charged off within 12 months of origination, so both periods have the same window."}, {"type": "chart", "id": "c8-calibration", "caption": "The ranking holds in both periods, but above 640 the old rates **over-predict**: 680–719 was predicted at 4.0% and came in at 1.9%; 720+ at 1.2% versus 0.5%. The score bands barely moved (the applicant PSI was 0.005), yet the bad rate within each band fell. Something the score doesn't capture changed: who was approved within each band, the product and term mix, or the economy. Stable PSI does **not** mean stable performance.", "sql": "WITH outcomes AS (\n  SELECT\n    CASE WHEN loans.originated_date < DATE '2024-07-01' THEN 'before' ELSE 'after' END AS period,\n    CASE\n      WHEN applications.credit_score < 640 THEN '1: under 640'\n      WHEN applications.credit_score < 680 THEN '2: 640-679'\n      WHEN applications.credit_score < 720 THEN '3: 680-719'\n      ELSE '4: 720+'\n    END AS score_band,\n    CASE WHEN loans.charged_off_date < loans.originated_date + INTERVAL 12 MONTH THEN 1.0 ELSE 0 END AS bad_12m\n  FROM loans\n  JOIN applications ON applications.application_id = loans.application_id\n  WHERE applications.credit_score IS NOT NULL\n    AND loans.originated_date < DATE '2025-01-01'\n)\nSELECT\n  score_band,\n  AVG(CASE WHEN period = 'before' THEN bad_12m END) AS predicted,\n  AVG(CASE WHEN period = 'after' THEN bad_12m END) AS actual\nFROM outcomes\nGROUP BY score_band\nORDER BY score_band", "chart": "bar", "x": "score_band", "y": ["predicted", "actual"], "series": null, "percent": true, "title": "12-month charge-off by band: pre-tightening rate (predicted) vs Jul–Dec 2024 loans (actual)", "stacked": false}, {"type": "md", "text": "Why it matters: losses are forecast, loans priced and reserves set from the **level**. Here, a forecast built on the old rates would overstate losses for the post-tightening book, which looks prudent but makes good segments look unprofitable and gets them cut. Mis-calibration is fixed by **recalibrating** (re-fitting the mapping from score to probability on recent data), which is much cheaper than rebuilding the model."}, {"type": "explain", "id": "c8-calibration-why", "prompt": "The applicant score distribution was stable (PSI 0.005) but bad rates within each band fell after July 2024. Give three explanations you'd check, and the query or cut that would test each.", "model": "1. **Selection within band:** the tightening may have declined on other criteria too (income, requested amount, thin files), so approved borrowers in a band are better than before. Test: compare approved vs declined applicants within each band on income and requested amount, before and after.\n2. **Mix:** more monthly vs Pay in 4, shorter terms, or a different merchant mix. Test: the same calibration table split by product and term; a shift-share of the band's bad rate into mix and rate.\n3. **Period effect:** the environment improved (or collections got better), lowering bad rates for everyone. Test: plot 12-month bad rates by origination quarter within a fixed band; a level shift across all bands at once points to period, not policy.\n\nAnd a data check first: the same \"bad\" definition and the same 12-month window for both periods."}, {"type": "md", "text": "## Monitoring, in one table\n\n| Question | Metric | Typical alarm |\n|---|---|---|\n| Is the input population changing? | PSI on the score; CSI on key inputs | PSI over 0.1 |\n| Does it still rank? | KS or Gini on each new matured vintage | drop of several points |\n| Is the level right? | predicted vs actual by band | actual outside a tolerance band for two periods |\n| Are people overriding it? | override rate and override performance | rising overrides, or overrides doing worse |\n| Is it fair? | approval and pricing by protected-class proxy (chapter 10) | unexplained disparities |\n\nTwo traps: **label delay** (a score's newest vintages have no outcomes yet, so you monitor early-read metrics like first-payment default or 30+ at MOB 3), and **feedback loops** (you only observe outcomes for approved applicants, which is the reject-inference problem from chapter 4)."}, {"type": "bridge", "title": "", "text": "This is model risk management, and you already know it: the bank's **SR 11-7** framework (conceptual soundness, ongoing monitoring, outcomes analysis, effective challenge) applies to credit scores exactly as it does to CCAR models. KS and AUC are \"outcomes analysis\" for a classifier; PSI is the monitoring check that the development sample still represents the population. In interviews, say you've worked inside that framework and can be the effective challenger."}, {"type": "interview", "title": "", "text": "\"How would you know if our underwriting model is degrading?\" Answer in four layers: **stability** (PSI on scores and key inputs, monthly), **ranking** (KS or Gini on each matured vintage, plus early-read metrics on recent ones), **calibration** (predicted vs actual by band), and **business outcome** (approval rate, losses in bps against plan). Then say what you'd do on an alarm: diagnose with the chapter 5 playbook before anyone retrains."}, {"type": "explain", "id": "c8-ks-vs-calibration", "prompt": "A data scientist says: \"The new model's AUC is 0.78 versus 0.75 for the old one, so we should switch.\" What else do you want to know before agreeing?", "model": "- **Out-of-time validation:** was the 0.78 measured on a later period than the training data, not a random split of the same months?\n- **Calibration:** does it predict the right level by band? Pricing and loss forecasts depend on it.\n- **At the cutoff:** AUC is an average over all thresholds. What matters is separation near our approval cutoff: how many approvals swap in and out (a swap set), and the expected losses and revenue of each group.\n- **Stability:** PSI of its inputs; does it rely on volatile or easily gamed fields?\n- **Fairness and explainability:** can we produce adverse-action reasons, and does it create disparities by protected-class proxies?\n- **Labels:** are fraud losses excluded from the target?\n\nThen a **champion–challenger** test on a slice of traffic before a full switch."}, {"type": "cards", "cards": [{"q": "Five questions an analyst asks about a credit model?", "a": "Does it rank? How well does it separate (KS/AUC)? Is it stable (PSI)? Is it calibrated? Where should the cutoff be (an economics question)?", "id": "08-models-for-analysts:c1"}, {"q": "What does a decile table show, and what's the classic SQL?", "a": "Bad rate by score decile, ideally a falling staircase. NTILE(10) OVER (ORDER BY score, id), then aggregate by decile.", "id": "08-models-for-analysts:c2"}, {"q": "Why filter nulls before NTILE?", "a": "Nulls sort to one end and land in a decile they don't belong to (DuckDB puts them last, so in the top decile).", "id": "08-models-for-analysts:c3"}, {"q": "Define KS.", "a": "The maximum gap between the cumulative share of bads and of goods as you walk up the score range. In this data, 0.38.", "id": "08-models-for-analysts:c4"}, {"q": "Define AUC in words. What is Gini?", "a": "The probability that a random bad scores lower than a random good (ties count half). Gini = 2 × AUC − 1. Here AUC 0.75, Gini 0.51.", "id": "08-models-for-analysts:c5"}, {"q": "Write the PSI formula. Thresholds?", "a": "Σ (actual − expected) × ln(actual / expected) over bins. Under 0.1 stable, 0.1–0.25 investigate, over 0.25 significant.", "id": "08-models-for-analysts:c6"}, {"q": "Ranking vs calibration?", "a": "Ranking: are riskier people scored worse (KS, AUC)? Calibration: is the predicted level right? A model can pass one and fail the other.", "id": "08-models-for-analysts:c7"}, {"q": "In this data, what happened to calibration after July 2024?", "a": "Ranking held but the old band rates over-predicted (680–719: 4.0% predicted vs 1.9% actual), even though applicant PSI was only 0.005.", "id": "08-models-for-analysts:c8"}, {"q": "Fix for a well-ranking but mis-calibrated model?", "a": "Recalibrate the score-to-probability mapping on recent data; no full rebuild needed.", "id": "08-models-for-analysts:c9"}, {"q": "Two traps in model monitoring?", "a": "Label delay (use early-read metrics) and feedback loops (outcomes only exist for approved applicants: reject inference).", "id": "08-models-for-analysts:c10"}, {"q": "Bridge from bank model risk to fintech credit models?", "a": "SR 11-7: conceptual soundness, ongoing monitoring (PSI), outcomes analysis (KS/AUC, calibration), effective challenge.", "id": "08-models-for-analysts:c11"}]}]}, {"id": "09-macro-to-micro", "title": "Macro to micro", "minutes": 45, "summary": "Where your CCAR and CECL experience meets a loan-level book. Seasonality, exposure, a scenario run in SQL, and what a downturn does to a short-duration lender.", "lesson": "", "date": "", "interview": false, "part": "Breadth", "blocks": [{"type": "md", "text": "## Warm-up: chapters 1, 5 and 8"}, {"type": "explain", "id": "c9-recall-1", "prompt": "From memory: write chapter 1's unit-economics identity, and say which line moves most when interest rates rise by two points.", "model": "Margin = merchant fee + interest income − funding cost − credit losses (per dollar lent, in bps of GMV). A rate rise hits **funding cost** first: the lender borrows to fund the loans. Interest income on new loans can be repriced (within APR caps), but 0% APR loans earn none, so their margin is squeezed hardest."}, {"type": "predict", "id": "c9-recall-2", "sql": "", "explain": "Chapter 8: ranking held, calibration drifted. Recalibrate, and investigate selection, mix and period effects.", "options": [{"text": "The model is broken and must be rebuilt", "correct": false}, {"text": "A stable input distribution doesn't guarantee stable performance: something the score doesn't see changed", "correct": true}, {"text": "PSI was computed wrong", "correct": false}], "errors": false, "prompt": "Applicant PSI is 0.005, yet the bad rate within every score band fell after a policy change. What does this show?"}, {"type": "predict", "id": "c9-recall-3", "sql": "", "explain": "Chapter 5, step 2: growth dilutes delinquency. The reverse happens when growth stops: the rate rises as the book ages, with no change in credit quality.", "options": [{"text": "Raises it", "correct": false}, {"text": "Lowers it: new loans can't be 30 days late yet, so the denominator grows first", "correct": true}, {"text": "Nothing", "correct": false}], "errors": false, "prompt": "Open loans grew from 2,342 to 2,700 over a month of holiday lending. What does that do to the 30+ rate, all else equal?"}, {"type": "md", "text": "## Why macro looks different here\n\nYou know macro credit from the top down: unemployment and rates drive loss rates on large, long-lived portfolios. A BNPL book changes three things.\n\n1. **Short duration.** Pay in 4 is repaid in six weeks; in 2025 the median monthly loan here had a 6-month term. Almost the whole book turns over within a year, so the lender can **tighten in weeks** and the book reflects it within months. Losses respond to *policy* faster than to the economy, and the main macro risk is being slow to react.\n2. **Early signals.** Missed first payments, failed debits and retry patterns show stress within weeks, often before bureau-reported delinquency moves. These are the leading indicators to watch.\n3. **Holiday seasonality.** Volume, and then repayment stress, follow the retail calendar."}, {"type": "chart", "id": "c9-seasonality", "caption": "November and December run at **1.6 to 2 times** a typical month in every year. Those loans come due in January to March, when household budgets are tightest. In this data the open-loan 30+ rate fell to 2.0% in November–December 2024 (new loans can't be late yet) and rose to 2.4% in January–February 2025.", "sql": "SELECT\n  MONTH(originated_date) AS month,\n  CAST(YEAR(originated_date) AS VARCHAR) AS origination_year,\n  COUNT(*) AS n_loans\nFROM loans\nGROUP BY month, origination_year\nORDER BY month, origination_year", "chart": "bar", "x": "month", "y": ["n_loans"], "series": "origination_year", "percent": false, "title": "Loans originated by calendar month", "stacked": false}, {"type": "bridge", "title": "", "text": "**CECL makes the holiday surge expensive up front.** Under CECL the lender books the **lifetime** expected loss as an allowance when a loan is originated. A Q4 surge means a big Q4 reserve build, hitting earnings before a single loan is late, and then releases as the short loans pay off. Growth looks costly in the quarter it happens; a slowdown looks flattering. You can explain that dynamic better than most candidates, because you've produced CECL scenarios."}, {"type": "md", "text": "## Exposure: where is the book?\n\nExposure questions are snapshot questions: take the book **as of** a date and cut the balance by segment. That's what `loan_month` is for (one row per loan per month-end, with `balance` and `status`)."}, {"type": "exercise", "id": "c9-exposure", "kind": "write", "ordered": false, "digits": 4, "prompt": "Open balance by borrower state as of **2025-12-31** (`loan_month` rows with `status = 'open'`). Return `state`, `open_balance` and `share_of_balance` (the state's share of total open balance), the **top 5** states by balance, largest first.", "starter": "", "solution": "WITH open_book AS (\n  SELECT borrowers.state, loan_month.balance\n  FROM loan_month\n  JOIN loans ON loans.loan_id = loan_month.loan_id\n  JOIN borrowers ON borrowers.borrower_id = loans.borrower_id\n  WHERE loan_month.as_of_date = DATE '2025-12-31'\n    AND loan_month.status = 'open'\n),\nby_state AS (\n  SELECT state, SUM(balance) AS open_balance\n  FROM open_book\n  GROUP BY state\n)\nSELECT\n  state,\n  open_balance,\n  open_balance / SUM(open_balance) OVER () AS share_of_balance\nFROM by_state\nORDER BY open_balance DESC\nLIMIT 5", "alts": [], "hints": ["`loan_month.status` and `loans.status` both exist: qualify the column. The share is `open_balance / SUM(open_balance) OVER ()`, computed **after** grouping so the total covers every state, not just the top 5."], "explain": "California 16%, Texas 11.7%, Florida 11.4%, New York 8.7%, Pennsylvania 5.4%: about \\$2.27M open in total. Order matters: if you filtered to the top 5 *before* computing the total, the shares would sum to 100% and be wrong. Florida is worth a second look; chapter 5 found a concentration there."}, {"type": "md", "text": "## A scenario, loan by loan\n\nAt a bank you might project losses top-down: a model links the portfolio loss rate to unemployment, and the scenario's unemployment path gives the loss path. With loan-level data you can do it bottom-up instead:\n\n**expected loss = exposure × loss rate × scenario multiplier**, by segment, then summed.\n\nThe multipliers come from history (how much did each segment's losses rise in the last downturn?) or, without a downturn in your data, from judgement and external benchmarks. Lower-income borrowers usually get bigger multipliers, because unemployment hits them first and they have less buffer."}, {"type": "exercise", "id": "c9-stress", "kind": "write", "ordered": false, "digits": 0, "prompt": "Stress the open book as of **2025-12-31** by income band.\n\n- Income bands from `borrowers.annual_income`: `'0: missing'` (null), `'1: under 40k'`, `'2: 40-70k'`, `'3: 70-100k'`, `'4: 100k+'`.\n- Historical **loss rate** per band: dollars lost on charged-off loans (principal minus successful payments, floored at 0) ÷ principal, for loans originated **before 2024-07-01**. Deduplicate payments first.\n- Scenario multipliers: missing 1.7, under 40k 2.0, 40–70k 1.7, 70–100k 1.4, 100k+ 1.2.\n\nReturn `income_band`, `open_balance`, `loss_rate`, `baseline_loss` (balance × loss rate) and `stressed_loss` (× multiplier), ordered by band.", "starter": "", "solution": "WITH income_bands AS (\n  SELECT\n    borrower_id,\n    CASE\n      WHEN annual_income IS NULL THEN '0: missing'\n      WHEN annual_income < 40000 THEN '1: under 40k'\n      WHEN annual_income < 70000 THEN '2: 40-70k'\n      WHEN annual_income < 100000 THEN '3: 70-100k'\n      ELSE '4: 100k+'\n    END AS income_band\n  FROM borrowers\n),\ncollected AS (\n  SELECT loan_id, SUM(amount) AS collected\n  FROM (SELECT DISTINCT * FROM payments) AS clean_payments\n  WHERE status = 'succeeded'\n  GROUP BY loan_id\n),\nhistorical_loss AS (\n  SELECT\n    income_bands.income_band,\n    SUM(CASE WHEN loans.status = 'charged_off'\n             THEN GREATEST(loans.principal - COALESCE(collected.collected, 0), 0) ELSE 0 END)\n      / SUM(loans.principal) AS loss_rate\n  FROM loans\n  JOIN income_bands ON income_bands.borrower_id = loans.borrower_id\n  LEFT JOIN collected ON collected.loan_id = loans.loan_id\n  WHERE loans.originated_date < DATE '2024-07-01'\n  GROUP BY income_bands.income_band\n),\nexposure AS (\n  SELECT income_bands.income_band, SUM(loan_month.balance) AS open_balance\n  FROM loan_month\n  JOIN loans ON loans.loan_id = loan_month.loan_id\n  JOIN income_bands ON income_bands.borrower_id = loans.borrower_id\n  WHERE loan_month.as_of_date = DATE '2025-12-31'\n    AND loan_month.status = 'open'\n  GROUP BY income_bands.income_band\n),\nstress AS (\n  SELECT * FROM (VALUES\n    ('0: missing', 1.7), ('1: under 40k', 2.0), ('2: 40-70k', 1.7),\n    ('3: 70-100k', 1.4), ('4: 100k+', 1.2)\n  ) AS multipliers(income_band, multiplier)\n)\nSELECT\n  exposure.income_band,\n  exposure.open_balance,\n  historical_loss.loss_rate,\n  exposure.open_balance * historical_loss.loss_rate AS baseline_loss,\n  exposure.open_balance * historical_loss.loss_rate * stress.multiplier AS stressed_loss\nFROM exposure\nJOIN historical_loss ON historical_loss.income_band = exposure.income_band\nJOIN stress ON stress.income_band = exposure.income_band\nORDER BY exposure.income_band", "alts": [], "hints": ["Three separate CTEs, each one row per income band: historical loss rate, current exposure, and the multipliers. Then join them on the band.", "A small lookup table can be written inline: `SELECT * FROM (VALUES ('a', 1.7), ('b', 2.0)) AS multipliers(income_band, multiplier)`. This works in PostgreSQL too."], "explain": "Baseline expected loss is about **\\$90k** on \\$2.27M open (3.9%); stressed, about **\\$145k** (6.4%), 1.6 times baseline. More than half the increase comes from the 40–70k band: not because its multiplier is largest, but because it holds 36% of the balance **and** has the highest loss rate (5.0%). Notice the historical loss rates don't fall steadily with income (under-40k at 2.8% is lower than 40–70k at 5.0%), possibly because the lender already gives lower-income applicants smaller loans or declines more of them. Selection again: never assume a segment's risk from intuition when you have the data.\n\nSimplifications to say out loud: a lifetime loss rate applied to a remaining balance overstates losses on loans that are partly through their life, and these multipliers are assumptions. In a real exercise you'd also stress **volume** (spending falls) and **funding cost**."}, {"type": "scratch", "id": "c9-sensitivity", "prompt": "Change the multipliers in the stress exercise (paste your solution here) and see how sensitive the stressed loss is. Try a uniform 1.5 for every band: how close does it get to the segment-level answer? When would a uniform multiplier mislead you?", "starter": ""}, {"type": "md", "text": "## What a downturn does, channel by channel\n\n| Channel | What happens | Where it shows up first |\n|---|---|---|\n| **Unemployment** | more borrowers can't pay | first-payment default, failed debits, roll rates from current |\n| **Interest rates** | funding cost rises; 0% APR loans squeezed | margin per dollar (chapter 1), funding line |\n| **Consumer spending** | fewer purchases, smaller tickets | GMV, application volume, average order value |\n| **Merchant stress** | merchant failures, refunds, disputes | refund rate, merchant concentration |\n| **Payment stress** | more insufficient-funds failures; retries | payment failure rate by method (chapter 2) |\n| **Stacking** | borrowers juggle several BNPL loans, not all visible in bureau data | rising application velocity per borrower |\n\nAnd the lender's levers, fastest first: cutoffs and limits on new loans (days), shorter terms and larger down payments (weeks), merchant-level controls, and collections strategy for the existing book."}, {"type": "explain", "id": "c9-rates", "prompt": "Funding costs rise by two points (from 6% to 8% a year). Using chapter 1's numbers (monthly loans: funding about 285 bps of GMV at 6%), estimate the hit to the monthly product's margin, and say which loans suffer most and what you'd consider doing.", "model": "Funding cost scales with the rate: 285 × 8/6 ≈ 380 bps, so margin falls by about **95 bps** of GMV, from roughly 770 to roughly 680 for the monthly product. Longer terms suffer more (funding grows with term), and **0% APR loans suffer most** because they earn no interest to offset it; their margin is the merchant fee minus funding and losses. Options: raise merchant fees on 0% APR promotions (or shorten the promotional terms), reprice interest-bearing loans within APR caps, and shift mix toward short terms. I'd size each by merchant before acting, since fee changes risk volume (chapter 1's fee-negotiation logic)."}, {"type": "interview", "title": "", "text": "\"What would a recession do to our book, and what would you watch?\" Structure it: **channels** (unemployment → defaults; rates → funding; spending → volume; merchant stress), **why BNPL differs** (short duration lets us tighten fast; the risk is reacting late), **leading indicators** (first-payment default, failed debits, roll rates out of current, applicant mix, external data like initial jobless claims and credit card delinquency), and **levers** (cutoffs, limits, terms, merchant controls). Then offer to size it: exposure by segment × stressed loss rates."}, {"type": "bridge", "title": "", "text": "Your scenario work maps straight across: **scenario design** (a coherent macro story with severity) is what feeds the multipliers; **top-down vs bottom-up** is the satellite-model vs loan-level choice; **CECL** is lifetime expected loss at origination. What's new is speed: the book you'd stress is mostly gone in a year, so the useful horizon is months, not nine quarters, and the analysis feeds underwriting decisions rather than capital plans. Say that contrast in an interview; it shows you understand the business, not just the method."}, {"type": "explain", "id": "c9-early-warning", "prompt": "Design a one-page early-warning dashboard for a BNPL book in a weakening economy. List five metrics, how each is computed (one line), and a trigger for each.", "model": "1. **First-payment default rate by weekly vintage**: share of loans whose first installment is unpaid 10+ days after due. Trigger: 1.5× its 12-week average.\n2. **Roll rate current → 1–29 days**: loans current last month that are late this month ÷ loans current last month. Trigger: above last year's level for the same month two months running (seasonality).\n3. **Payment failure rate by method**: failed attempts ÷ attempts, ACH and debit separately. Trigger: up 2 points on the month.\n4. **30+ at MOB 3 for each monthly vintage**: early read on new cohorts. Trigger: outside the range of the last four vintages.\n5. **Applicant mix**: PSI on applicant score and income, plus approval rate. Trigger: PSI over 0.1, or approval rate moving 5 points without a policy change.\n\nEach metric gets an owner, and a playbook of what we do if it triggers."}, {"type": "cards", "cards": [{"q": "Three ways BNPL differs from a long-duration book in a downturn?", "a": "Short duration (the book turns over within a year, so you can tighten fast), early signals (first-payment default, failed debits), and holiday seasonality.", "id": "09-macro-to-micro:c1"}, {"q": "What does the holiday surge do to the 30+ rate, and then in Q1?", "a": "Nov–Dec: dilutes it (new loans can't be late). Jan–Mar: the holiday loans come due and the rate rises.", "id": "09-macro-to-micro:c2"}, {"q": "How does CECL interact with a Q4 origination surge?", "a": "Lifetime expected loss is booked at origination, so Q4 shows a large reserve build before any loan is late, then releases as short loans pay off.", "id": "09-macro-to-micro:c3"}, {"q": "Which table and filter give an exposure snapshot here?", "a": "loan_month at one as_of_date with loan_month.status = 'open'; sum balance by segment.", "id": "09-macro-to-micro:c4"}, {"q": "Bottom-up stressed loss formula?", "a": "Exposure × loss rate × scenario multiplier, by segment, then summed.", "id": "09-macro-to-micro:c5"}, {"q": "In the stress exercise, why did 40–70k drive most of the increase?", "a": "Largest share of balance (36%) and highest historical loss rate (5.0%), not the largest multiplier.", "id": "09-macro-to-micro:c6"}, {"q": "Why weren't loss rates monotonic in income here?", "a": "Probably selection: the lender already lends less, or less often, to lower incomes. Check before assuming.", "id": "09-macro-to-micro:c7"}, {"q": "Effect of +2 points on funding for monthly loans (285 bps at 6%)?", "a": "About +95 bps of funding cost; 0% APR and long-term loans hurt most.", "id": "09-macro-to-micro:c8"}, {"q": "Five channels of a downturn?", "a": "Unemployment (defaults), rates (funding), spending (volume), merchant stress (refunds, failures), payment stress (failed debits) — plus stacking.", "id": "09-macro-to-micro:c9"}, {"q": "Fastest lever in a downturn for a BNPL lender?", "a": "Cutoffs and limits on new loans: days to implement, and the book reflects them within months.", "id": "09-macro-to-micro:c10"}]}]}, {"id": "10-regulation-essentials", "title": "Regulation essentials", "minutes": 35, "summary": "The rules that shape every credit decision, at the depth an analyst needs: what each one requires, and where it touches your work.", "lesson": "", "date": "", "interview": false, "part": "Breadth", "blocks": [{"type": "md", "text": "## Warm-up: chapters 4, 6 and 8"}, {"type": "explain", "id": "c10-recall-1", "prompt": "From memory: what is a swap set, and why can a tightening be evaluated from history while a loosening needs a test?", "model": "Comparing old and new policy: **swap-outs** were approved before and are declined now; **swap-ins** the reverse. A tightening is all swap-outs, whose outcomes we've observed because they were approved before. A loosening approves people we've never lent to, so their outcomes are unknown (reject inference) and need a test."}, {"type": "predict", "id": "c10-recall-2", "sql": "", "explain": "Chapter 8: ranking (KS, AUC) and calibration are separate tests.", "options": [{"text": "They're 75% accurate", "correct": false}, {"text": "Nothing: AUC measures ranking, not the level of the predictions", "correct": true}, {"text": "They're well calibrated if AUC is above 0.7", "correct": false}], "errors": false, "prompt": "A model has AUC 0.75. What does that tell you about whether its predicted default rates are right?"}, {"type": "explain", "id": "c10-recall-3", "prompt": "From memory: why should loans from a fraud ring be excluded from credit-model training data?", "model": "They'd be labelled as credit defaults, so the model would learn that the ring's characteristics (state, merchant category, new accounts) mean high credit risk. That distorts the credit model, penalising legitimate customers who share those traits, and it still won't catch the next ring."}, {"type": "md", "text": "## Why an analyst needs this\n\nYou won't be the compliance officer. But every cutoff change, new model, new data source and collections strategy you propose will be reviewed against these rules, and in an interview, an answer that spots the regulatory angle unprompted stands out. The aim of this chapter is **recognition**: know which rule a proposal touches and what question compliance will ask."}, {"type": "pitfall", "title": "Laws change; this chapter is a map, not legal advice", "text": "Rules and enforcement priorities for BNPL moved several times in 2024–2025. Treat the specifics below as accurate to 2025 and check what's current before relying on them. In an interview it's fine to say \"my understanding as of last year was …\"."}, {"type": "md", "text": "## The map\n\n| Rule | What it requires, in one line | Where it touches analytics |\n|---|---|---|\n| **ECOA / Regulation B** | no discrimination on a **prohibited basis** in any part of a credit transaction; tell declined applicants the **specific principal reasons** | model features, cutoffs, fair lending tests, reason codes |\n| **FCRA** | a **permissible purpose** to pull credit reports; notices when a report drives an adverse decision; **accurate** reporting to the bureaus | bureau data in models, adverse action notices, furnishing data |\n| **TILA / Regulation Z** | standard cost disclosures (APR, finance charge) and billing-dispute rights, for credit that's covered | APR calculations, product design (four installments, no fee) |\n| **UDAAP** | no unfair, deceptive or abusive acts or practices | fees, marketing claims, collections, product design |\n| **Military Lending Act** | a 36% all-in cost cap and other protections for active-duty servicemembers and dependents | covered-borrower checks at application |\n| **Debt collection (FDCPA, Reg F) and state law** | limits on contact frequency, timing and conduct | collections strategy and contact policies |\n| **State licensing and law** | lending licenses, rate caps, BNPL-specific laws in some states | state-level product rules, licensing reports |\n\n### ECOA and fair lending\n\n**Prohibited bases** under ECOA: race, colour, religion, national origin, sex, marital status, age (if the applicant can legally contract), receipt of public assistance income, and having exercised consumer credit rights in good faith.\n\nTwo theories of discrimination:\n\n- **Disparate treatment**: treating applicants differently because of a prohibited basis, or a close proxy for one. Intent isn't required; using the variable is enough.\n- **Disparate impact**: a neutral-looking policy that falls more heavily on a protected group. It isn't automatically illegal: the questions are whether it serves a **legitimate business need** and whether a **less discriminatory alternative** would serve that need about as well. (Federal enforcement priorities on disparate impact shifted in 2025; state regulators and private litigation remain, and policies outlive administrations, so most lenders keep testing.)\n\nFor non-mortgage credit, lenders generally **can't collect** race or ethnicity, so fair lending testing estimates them with proxies (a common method combines surname and geography, known as BISG).\n\n**Adverse action notices.** A declined applicant must get the specific principal reasons (or be told how to ask for them), generally within 30 days. \"You didn't meet our internal standards\" isn't specific enough, and US regulators have said that using a complex or machine-learning model doesn't change that: if you can't explain the decline, you can't use the model. That's why credit models come with **reason codes**, the features that pushed a given applicant's score down the most."}, {"type": "exercise", "id": "c10-air", "kind": "write", "ordered": false, "digits": 3, "prompt": "A first-pass disparity screen compares approval rates across groups. Using 2025 applications (`applied_at` on or after 2025-01-01), compute the approval rate by income band (`'0: missing'`, `'1: under 40k'`, `'2: 40-70k'`, `'3: 70-100k'`, `'4: 100k+'`, from `borrowers.annual_income`), and the **adverse impact ratio**: each band's approval rate ÷ the highest band's approval rate.\n\nReturn `income_band`, `n_applications`, `approval_rate` and `adverse_impact_ratio`, ordered by band.", "starter": "", "solution": "WITH banded AS (\n  SELECT\n    applications.decision,\n    CASE\n      WHEN borrowers.annual_income IS NULL THEN '0: missing'\n      WHEN borrowers.annual_income < 40000 THEN '1: under 40k'\n      WHEN borrowers.annual_income < 70000 THEN '2: 40-70k'\n      WHEN borrowers.annual_income < 100000 THEN '3: 70-100k'\n      ELSE '4: 100k+'\n    END AS income_band\n  FROM applications\n  JOIN borrowers ON borrowers.borrower_id = applications.borrower_id\n  WHERE applications.applied_at >= DATE '2025-01-01'\n),\nrates AS (\n  SELECT\n    income_band,\n    COUNT(*) AS n_applications,\n    AVG(CASE WHEN decision = 'approved' THEN 1.0 ELSE 0 END) AS approval_rate\n  FROM banded\n  GROUP BY income_band\n)\nSELECT\n  income_band,\n  n_applications,\n  approval_rate,\n  approval_rate / MAX(approval_rate) OVER () AS adverse_impact_ratio\nFROM rates\nORDER BY income_band", "alts": [], "hints": ["Approval rate is the mean of a dummy. The highest rate across bands is `MAX(approval_rate) OVER ()`, computed after grouping."], "explain": "Approval runs from 69% to 72% in every band, so every ratio is above 0.94. A common rule of thumb (borrowed from employment law) flags ratios **below 0.8** for review. Three caveats you'd raise: income isn't itself a prohibited basis (though receipt of public assistance is, so income **source** matters); a real test uses protected-class estimates, not income; and a raw gap isn't the end of the analysis, you'd then ask whether it's explained by legitimate credit factors (for example, comparing approval within score bands) and whether a less discriminatory alternative exists."}, {"type": "explain", "id": "c10-florida", "prompt": "After chapter 6's fraud ring, a colleague proposes: \"Decline all applicants in Florida whose account is under 30 days old.\" What concerns would compliance raise, and what would you propose instead?", "model": "**Geography can be a proxy for race or national origin.** Blanket declines by state (or ZIP code) are the pattern of redlining, and they'd hit legitimate Florida customers who share nothing with the ring but their address. The rule is also broader than the evidence: 30 of the ring's 36 accounts were in Florida, but the ring's real signature was **behavioural** (brand-new accounts applying overnight within minutes of signup, one email domain, one merchant, large tickets). Instead: a rule on those behaviours, applied everywhere (step-up verification for brand-new accounts requesting large amounts; velocity limits per email domain, device and address), plus controls on the merchant. Document the business justification and test the rule's impact across groups before launch."}, {"type": "md", "text": "### FCRA\n\nPulling a credit report needs a permissible purpose (an application is one). When a report contributes to an adverse decision, the notice must name the bureau, give the **credit score used** and its key factors, and explain the right to a free report and to dispute. Lenders that **furnish** data to the bureaus must report it accurately and investigate disputes; that's why fraud victims' loans are kept off their credit files (chapter 6). BNPL lenders and the bureaus have been building BNPL reporting since about 2022, and score developers announced BNPL-aware score versions in 2025, but coverage is still uneven, so BNPL debt is partly invisible to other lenders (\"stacking\").\n\n### TILA and Regulation Z\n\nRegulation Z's cost disclosures and billing rights apply to credit with a **finance charge**, or payable in **more than four** installments. That definitional line is why the classic product is \"Pay in 4\" with no fee: it has historically sat outside Reg Z. Interest-bearing monthly loans are covered and come with APR disclosures.\n\nIn 2024 the CFPB issued an interpretive rule treating BNPL lenders as **card issuers** for parts of Reg Z, mainly **dispute and refund rights** and periodic statements. In 2025 it withdrew that rule and said it wouldn't prioritise enforcement. Many BNPL lenders offer dispute and refund handling anyway, because merchants and customers expect it.\n\n### UDAAP\n\nThe catch-all: unfair (substantial, unavoidable injury not outweighed by benefits), deceptive (misleading), or abusive (taking unreasonable advantage). It's how regulators reach practices no specific rule covers: confusing late-fee designs, misleading \"no interest\" marketing, aggressive collections, making it hard to cancel. For an analyst, the practical version: if a strategy makes money **because** customers misunderstand it, it's a UDAAP risk.\n\n### Who regulates a fintech lender?\n\nMany fintech lenders originate loans through a **partner bank**: the bank makes the loan, and the fintech markets, underwrites to agreed criteria, services, and often buys the receivables. It lets one product run nationally under the bank's charter, but brings **true lender** risk (a court or state deciding the fintech is the real lender, so state rate caps and licensing apply) and puts the fintech under the bank's **third-party risk management**: the bank's examiners effectively examine the fintech's controls. Fintech lenders also hold **state licenses** in many states, and some states have BNPL-specific laws (New York enacted one in 2025). Outside the US, the UK has been bringing BNPL under FCA regulation, scheduled for 2026."}, {"type": "bridge", "title": "", "text": "You've lived the partner-bank relationship from the other side. Bank model risk management, third-party risk reviews and examiner expectations are what a fintech's controls get measured against. In an interview, that's a credible asset: you can anticipate what a bank partner's model validation or compliance team will ask, and build analyses that pass the first time."}, {"type": "interview", "title": "", "text": "When a case question proposes a change to who gets credit or on what terms, add one sentence on compliance unprompted: \"Before launch, I'd check this with compliance for fair lending (does it act as a proxy for a protected basis, and is there a less discriminatory way to get the same risk reduction?), and make sure we can still give specific adverse action reasons.\" It signals you've worked in a regulated institution, without derailing the answer."}, {"type": "predict", "id": "c10-q1", "sql": "", "explain": "A feature that stands in for a prohibited basis creates disparate treatment risk regardless of predictive power. The same goes for features like ZIP code or certain shopping categories: test them, and drop or justify them.", "options": [{"text": "None, if it improves AUC", "correct": false}, {"text": "First names correlate with sex and national origin, so it's a likely proxy for prohibited bases", "correct": true}, {"text": "It breaks FCRA permissible purpose", "correct": false}], "errors": false, "prompt": "A new model uses the applicant's first name as a feature because it improves AUC. What's the problem?"}, {"type": "predict", "id": "c10-q2", "sql": "", "explain": "Reg Z covers credit with a finance charge or payable in more than four installments.", "options": [{"text": "Loans under \\$1,000", "correct": false}, {"text": "No finance charge and no more than four installments", "correct": true}, {"text": "Repayment within 90 days", "correct": false}], "errors": false, "prompt": "Which Pay in 4 design has historically kept it outside Regulation Z's disclosure rules?"}, {"type": "predict", "id": "c10-q3", "sql": "", "explain": "Reasons must be specific and reflect the actual drivers of the decision, which is why models need reason codes.", "options": [{"text": "\"Did not meet our credit standards\"", "correct": false}, {"text": "\"Credit score too low\"", "correct": false}, {"text": "The specific factors that drove the score, e.g. \"limited credit history; recent delinquencies\"", "correct": true}], "errors": false, "prompt": "An applicant is declined mainly because of a high model score for risk. Which adverse action reason is acceptable?"}, {"type": "predict", "id": "c10-q4", "sql": "", "explain": "Contact frequency is governed by collection rules (Reg F has a presumption around seven calls in seven days for third-party collectors), and abusive practices fall under UDAAP regardless of who collects.", "options": [{"text": "ECOA and FCRA", "correct": false}, {"text": "Debt collection limits (FDCPA and Regulation F for third-party collectors, state laws) and UDAAP", "correct": true}, {"text": "TILA", "correct": false}], "errors": false, "prompt": "A collections strategy calls customers six times a day because contact rates rise. Which rules are most relevant?"}, {"type": "explain", "id": "c10-new-data", "prompt": "Your team wants to add a new data source to underwriting: whether the applicant shops mostly at discount retailers (from their purchase history with us). Write the five questions you'd want answered before it goes live.", "model": "1. **Predictive value, out of time:** does it improve ranking and calibration on a later period, and at our cutoff (swap set), not just overall AUC?\n2. **Fair lending:** is it a proxy for a prohibited basis (race, national origin, age, public assistance)? Test its correlation with protected-class estimates and the approval impact across groups; is there a less discriminatory alternative with similar lift?\n3. **Explainability:** can we turn it into a specific, accurate adverse action reason that a customer would understand?\n4. **Fairness to customers (UDAAP):** would customers be surprised or harmed that shopping choices affect their credit? Is it disclosed in our privacy terms?\n5. **Data quality and stability:** coverage (thin-history customers have no purchase history), stability over time (PSI), and whether it can be gamed."}, {"type": "cards", "cards": [{"q": "ECOA prohibited bases?", "a": "Race, colour, religion, national origin, sex, marital status, age (with capacity to contract), receipt of public assistance, good-faith exercise of consumer credit rights.", "id": "10-regulation-essentials:c1"}, {"q": "Disparate treatment vs disparate impact?", "a": "Treatment: different treatment because of a prohibited basis or close proxy. Impact: a neutral policy that hits a group harder; defensible only with a business need and no less discriminatory alternative.", "id": "10-regulation-essentials:c2"}, {"q": "How do lenders test fair lending without race data?", "a": "Proxy estimates (e.g. BISG: surname plus geography), then compare outcomes, controlling for legitimate credit factors.", "id": "10-regulation-essentials:c3"}, {"q": "What must an adverse action notice give?", "a": "Specific principal reasons (or how to get them), generally within 30 days; if a credit report was used, the bureau, the score used and key factors, and dispute rights.", "id": "10-regulation-essentials:c4"}, {"q": "Does using a complex model change adverse action rules?", "a": "No: reasons must still be specific and accurate, hence reason codes.", "id": "10-regulation-essentials:c5"}, {"q": "Which credit falls under Regulation Z?", "a": "Credit with a finance charge, or payable in more than four installments. That's why fee-free Pay in 4 has historically sat outside it.", "id": "10-regulation-essentials:c6"}, {"q": "CFPB BNPL interpretive rule: what and status?", "a": "2024: treated BNPL lenders as card issuers for dispute/refund rights and statements. Withdrawn in 2025.", "id": "10-regulation-essentials:c7"}, {"q": "What is UDAAP, in an analyst's terms?", "a": "Unfair, deceptive or abusive practices; if a strategy earns money because customers misunderstand it, it's a risk.", "id": "10-regulation-essentials:c8"}, {"q": "Partner-bank model: benefit and two risks?", "a": "Benefit: national lending under the bank's charter. Risks: true-lender challenges; the bank's third-party risk oversight of the fintech.", "id": "10-regulation-essentials:c9"}, {"q": "Adverse impact ratio and the rule-of-thumb threshold?", "a": "A group's approval rate ÷ the highest group's; below 0.8 is flagged for review.", "id": "10-regulation-essentials:c10"}, {"q": "Why not decline by state after a fraud ring?", "a": "Geography can proxy for race/national origin (redlining risk); target the behavioural signals instead.", "id": "10-regulation-essentials:c11"}, {"q": "Military Lending Act in one line?", "a": "36% all-in cost cap and other protections for active-duty servicemembers and dependents; check covered status at application.", "id": "10-regulation-essentials:c12"}]}]}, {"id": "11-case-and-take-home-craft", "title": "Case and take-home craft", "minutes": 40, "summary": "Turning everything in chapters 1–10 into answers: a case structure that works with pen and paper, clarifying questions, an answer-first five-slide deck, using AI on a take-home without it using you, and the readout.", "lesson": "", "date": "", "interview": false, "part": "Interview", "blocks": [{"type": "md", "text": "## Warm-up: one question from each part"}, {"type": "explain", "id": "c11-recall-1", "prompt": "From memory, in one or two lines each:\n1. The unit-economics identity (chapter 1).\n2. Roll rate vs cure rate (chapter 2).\n3. Why compare vintages at the same MOB (chapter 3).\n4. Average vs marginal (chapter 4).\n5. The first two steps of the diagnosis playbook (chapter 5).", "model": "1. Margin = merchant fee + interest − funding cost − credit losses, per dollar lent (bps of GMV).\n2. Roll: share of loans in a bucket that move to a worse bucket next month. Cure: share that move back to current.\n3. Loans go bad over time, so older vintages always look worse at a calendar date; only same-age comparisons are fair.\n4. Cutoff decisions move the marginal loans near the cutoff; a profitable average can hide a loss-making margin.\n5. Is it real (definition, seasonality, one-off)? Did the denominator change (growth, age mix)?"}, {"type": "explain", "id": "c11-recall-2", "prompt": "From memory, in one or two lines each:\n1. Precision vs recall for a fraud rule (chapter 6).\n2. Why measure retries when reporting approval rate (chapter 7).\n3. Ranking vs calibration (chapter 8).\n4. Why a BNPL lender can react to a downturn faster than a mortgage lender (chapter 9).\n5. Disparate impact, and the two questions that decide if it's defensible (chapter 10).", "model": "1. Precision: bad ÷ flagged. Recall: flagged bad ÷ all bad.\n2. Declined applicants often retry with a smaller amount; counting applications instead of people (or ignoring retries) distorts approval and conversion.\n3. Ranking: are riskier people scored worse (KS, AUC)? Calibration: are predicted rates the right level?\n4. Short duration: the book turns over within a year, so tightening shows up in months.\n5. A neutral policy that falls harder on a protected group; defensible if it serves a legitimate business need and no less discriminatory alternative would serve it about as well."}, {"type": "md", "text": "## What the interviews are testing\n\nA typical risk analytics loop at a fintech lender looks like this:\n\n| Round | Format | What they're really asking |\n|---|---|---|\n| **Hiring-manager screen** | behavioural questions plus a live case; pen, paper, calculator or a spreadsheet; no SQL | Can you structure an ambiguous business question, reason with numbers, and land on a recommendation? |\n| **Take-home** | a real business question on a few synthetic datasets; deliver a short executive presentation; AI tools allowed | Can you find what's actually driving the numbers, check it, take a stance and present it so a busy reader acts on it? |\n| **Take-home presentation** | present to a team member, then Q&A, like a real executive readout | Do you understand your own work: its assumptions, its weak spots, what you'd do next? |\n| **Behavioural with a cross-functional partner** | someone from product, policy, finance or operations who'd work with you | Can non-analysts trust you, follow you, and work with you? |\n| **Deeper case** | like the hiring-manager case, more depth | Same as the first case, pushed further: second-order effects, trade-offs |\n| **Final with a VP** | behavioural plus a case; no SQL | Business judgement: do you focus on what matters, and decide with incomplete data? |\n\nAcross all of them, three things get scored: **strategic thinking** (connecting a number to a decision), **analytical rigour** (interrogating data, choosing the right method, thinking about risk and return like an owner) and **communication** (a crisp narrative, and presence with executives).\n\nThe live rounds are **pen-and-paper cases**, so the core skill is back-of-the-envelope maths out loud. The **Paper cases** page drills exactly that. SQL matters for the take-home, and in every case answer it helps to say what data you'd ask for.\n\n## A case structure that works live\n\nFive moves. Say them out loud; interviewers score the structure as much as the answer.\n\n1. **Goal.** Restate the question as a decision and a metric. \"So the decision is whether to raise limits for repeat customers, and we'd judge it on contribution margin after losses, over, say, the next year?\" Then ask two or three clarifying questions (below) **before** any analysis.\n2. **Frame.** Lay out the drivers before diving in. For most credit questions it's chapter 1's identity, multiplied by volume: **volume × (fee + interest − funding − losses)**. Say which lines the decision moves.\n3. **Evidence.** For each driver, say what data you'd pull and what you'd expect. Use rough numbers: \"Repeat customers charge off about 5% versus 7% for first loans, so there's room, but the marginal limit increase goes to people who wanted more credit, which is adverse selection.\" Ask for data if the interviewer has it.\n4. **Answer.** Commit to a recommendation, sized: \"I'd raise limits by 25% for repeat customers with two or more on-time loans, as a test on a random 20%.\" A conditional answer (\"if X, then Y\") is fine; no answer isn't.\n5. **Risks and monitoring.** What could make you wrong, and the metric and threshold that would tell you early. Add the compliance sentence when credit access changes (chapter 10).\n\n### Clarifying questions that earn their place\n\nA good clarifying question is one whose answer would **change what you do next**. Four families cover most cases:\n\n| Ask about | Example | Why it changes the answer |\n|---|---|---|\n| **Objective** | \"Are we optimising for profit, growth, or losses within a cap?\" | different objectives give different recommendations |\n| **Scope** | \"All products, or just monthly loans? New customers only?\" | narrows which drivers matter |\n| **Constraints** | \"Is there a loss rate we can't exceed? Over what time horizon?\" | sets the bar a proposal must clear |\n| **What changed or what's known** | \"Was there a policy, product or merchant change? What data do we have?\" | often the answer itself |\n\nSkip questions you could answer with a stated assumption (\"I'll assume a 6% funding cost; tell me if that's off\"). Assumptions keep you moving; too many questions read as stalling.\n\n### Maths out loud\n\nYou'll have a calculator, but the interviewer is listening to the reasoning:\n\n- **Round first.** \\$812M becomes \"about \\$800M\". Precision comes later, if at all.\n- **Say units every time.** \"200 bps of GMV\", \"per loan\", \"per year\". Most live-case errors are unit errors.\n- **Per dollar, then scale.** Work out margin per dollar (bps), then multiply by volume. It keeps lines comparable and makes the break-even obvious.\n- **Sanity-check out loud.** \"That's \\$40M on a \\$500M book, 8%: high for a loss rate, so let me check.\"\n- **Write the tree on paper** before computing, so you and the interviewer can point at it."}, {"type": "pitfall", "title": "The three common failures", "text": "**Boiling the ocean:** listing twelve analyses without saying which matters most. Prioritise: \"the one thing I'd check first is …\". **Never landing:** ending with \"it depends\". Pick, then caveat. **Average thinking:** judging a change by the whole book's average when the decision only touches the margin (chapter 4)."}, {"type": "md", "text": "### A worked example\n\n> \"Our approval rate fell from 79% to 70% over the past year. The growth team wants it back. What do you think?\"\n\n**Goal.** \"The decision is whether to loosen underwriting, judged on margin after losses, not on approval rate itself. Is the drop concentrated in a product or channel, and was there a policy change?\" (Yes: a tightening in July 2024.)\n\n**Frame.** \"Approval rate fell for two possible reasons: the applicant mix got riskier, or the policy got stricter. Here it's mostly policy. So the question becomes: were the loans we stopped making profitable?\"\n\n**Evidence.** \"Before the tightening, the 580–619 band lost 700–800 bps per dollar and the 620–679 band made 530–710 bps. The tightening cut both. So part of the decline was right and part gave away profitable loans. I'd want the swap set: how many approvals we lost in each band, and their historical margin. One caveat: the declined 620–679 applicants may be riskier than the ones we approved before.\"\n\n**Answer.** \"Don't restore the old policy. Restore approvals for 620–679 as a randomised test on about 20% of those applicants, perhaps with lower limits for first loans. Keep 580–619 declined.\"\n\n**Risks and monitoring.** \"Watch first-payment default and 30+ at MOB 3 for the test cell against control; stop if MOB-3 delinquency is more than 1.5 times control. And I'd run the change past compliance for fair lending, since it changes who gets credit.\"\n\nThat's about two minutes spoken. Notice what it **didn't** do: no list of every possible cause, and no answer of \"approval rate isn't the right metric\" without then answering the question."}, {"type": "explain", "id": "c11-case-practice", "prompt": "Your turn, using the five moves. Write your answer in under 200 words:\n\n\"A large electronics retailer will join us only if we cut their merchant fee from 5% to 3.5%. They'd bring about \\$50M a year of volume. Should we do it?\"", "model": "**Goal:** decide whether to accept, judged on annual margin from this merchant, including the risk.\n**Frame:** margin = volume × (fee + interest − funding − losses). The fee cut costs 150 bps on every dollar.\n**Evidence:** at 3.5%, is the margin still positive? Electronics is resellable, so fraud risk is higher (chapter 6); I'd use our other electronics merchants' loss and fraud rates, the product mix (0% APR promotions earn no interest), and terms. If losses plus funding are about 250 bps, a 350 bps fee leaves about 100 bps, plus interest on interest-bearing loans: roughly \\$0.5M+ a year. Also: strategic value (new customers who become repeat users across other merchants), and whether other merchants will ask for the same rate.\n**Answer:** accept if the projected margin is positive under conservative loss assumptions, with conditions: fee tiers tied to volume, a cap on 0% APR promotional terms, and tighter fraud controls at launch.\n**Risks/monitoring:** first-payment default and fraud rate in the first 90 days (chapter 6's new-merchant alert), and fee-concession requests from other merchants."}, {"type": "md", "text": "## The take-home\n\n### Before you write anything\n\n- **Read the brief twice.** Underline the decision and the audience. Most take-homes are judged on whether you answered the question asked.\n- **Look before you write.** Row counts, date ranges, nulls, duplicates and the grain of every table, before any analysis (you've practised this all along: baseline counts, dedupe in its own CTE).\n- **Data integrity first, and show it.** Surface the anomalies (duplicates, impossible values, gaps, a sudden cluster) and say how you handled each. It's explicitly part of what's assessed, and it's where hidden findings live.\n- **Segment.** The headline number is rarely the story; cut by product, vintage, score band, merchant, channel until you find what's actually driving it.\n- **Write down your assumptions as you go**, in a running list. They become your assumptions slide and your defence in the Q&A.\n\n### Using AI well\n\nThe take-home allows AI. Use it the way a strong analyst would use a fast junior colleague:\n\n- **Good uses:** drafting boilerplate queries and charts, translating between SQL dialects, suggesting checks you might have missed, tightening your prose, and critiquing your deck (\"what would a sceptical risk VP push back on?\").\n- **Your job, not the AI's:** choosing what to analyse, the definitions (what counts as \"bad\", which population, which window), the interpretation, and the recommendation.\n- **Verify every number** with a second, independent query (a reconciliation: totals match the source, shares sum to 100%, row counts make sense). AI-written SQL is often plausible and wrong in exactly the ways you've practised spotting: fan-out on joins, wrong grain, unpaid rows silently dropped, integer division.\n- **Be able to explain every line.** The reviewer will ask why you chose a definition or a filter. \"The AI wrote it\" is the worst answer in the room.\n- **Disclose how you used it**, briefly, if the brief asks or doesn't say. It reads as confidence.\n\n### The deck: five sharp slides\n\nFive sharp slides beat fifteen shallow ones. **Answer first**: a busy executive should get the recommendation from slide 1.\n\n1. **The answer.** The recommendation, its sized impact, and the main risk, in three lines. Nothing else.\n2. **What's driving the numbers.** Your most important segmented finding, one chart, titled with the finding.\n3. **The second finding** (or the evidence that rules out the obvious explanation).\n4. **The recommendation, sized**, with the trade-off stated plainly (what we give up, what we gain) and how you'd test or roll it out.\n5. **Risks, assumptions and next steps**: what you assumed, what would change the answer, what you'd monitor, what you'd do with more time.\n\n**Appendix slides**: data integrity checks and how you handled each anomaly, definitions, reconciliations, AI use. Not presented unless asked, but they win the Q&A.\n\nEvery chart title is a **finding** (\"Losses in the 620–679 band are half the 580–619 band\"), not a variable (\"Losses by band\"). One message per slide."}, {"type": "explain", "id": "c11-headline", "prompt": "Rewrite these three weak chart titles as finding-titles, using numbers from this course:\n1. \"30+ delinquency rate by month\"\n2. \"Charge-off rate by score decile\"\n3. \"Approval rate by score band, before and after July 2024\"", "model": "1. \"30+ delinquency jumped from 2.0% to 3.5% in November 2025; one new merchant explains most of it.\"\n2. \"The score ranks well: the bottom decile charges off at 20%, the top at 0%.\"\n3. \"The July 2024 tightening cut approvals in the profitable 620–679 range as well as the loss-making 580–619 range.\""}, {"type": "md", "text": "## The readout and the VP\n\n**Presenting your take-home.** Treat it as a real executive readout: about 10 minutes, answer first, then the evidence; stop and invite questions rather than reading slides. Expect: \"Why that definition?\", \"What if you'd excluded X?\", \"What's the weakest part of this analysis?\", \"What would you do with another week?\". Write those answers before the day. Naming your own weakest assumption first builds credibility; defending everything doesn't.\n\n**Behavioural with a cross-functional partner.** They're asking whether they'd want to work with you. Have stories where you translated analysis for non-analysts, disagreed with a partner and found a path, and changed your view because of someone else's input.\n\n**VP live case.** Expect a broader, more strategic question (\"Should we enter a new category?\", \"How should we think about growth versus losses next year?\"). The same five moves work; lean harder on **business judgement**: what matters to the company's economics, what you'd prioritise, what you'd **not** do. Short, numerate, decisive."}, {"type": "interview", "title": "", "text": "A useful phrase when you don't have the data: \"I don't know the number, but here's how I'd estimate it, and here's the decision it would change.\" Then do a quick, explicit estimate. Interviewers want to see you reason; a made-up precise number is worse than a transparent rough one."}, {"type": "bridge", "title": "", "text": "Your scenario-design job is already this skill set: turning an ambiguous question (\"what's a plausible severe downturn?\") into a structured, defensible answer for senior people and regulators, and defending it under challenge. In the behavioural round, use that explicitly: your stories about defending a scenario or leading the SAS to R migration are stories about structure, persuasion and judgement. The **Behavioural stories** page has a template."}, {"type": "md", "text": "## Behavioural answers\n\nUse **STAR**, with an extra L: **Situation** (one sentence), **Task** (your responsibility), **Action** (most of the time, and say \"I\", not \"we\"), **Result** (a number if you have one), **Learning** (what you'd do differently, or what you now do every time). Aim for two minutes.\n\nPrepare five or six stories that each cover several themes: influencing without authority, a hard call with incomplete data, a mistake and what you changed, disagreeing with a senior person, delivering under pressure, developing people. Then map common questions to stories, so no question finds you without one."}, {"type": "explain", "id": "c11-story", "prompt": "Draft one STAR-L story, in under 200 words, for: \"Tell me about a time you changed how your team worked.\" (Your SAS to R migration is the obvious candidate.) Then name two other questions the same story could answer.", "model": "**Situation:** our scenario models ran in SAS scripts that were hard to review, test or reproduce. **Task:** I led moving them to an R package with version control and automated testing. **Action:** I built the package structure and CI/CD pipeline in GitLab publishing to Artifactory, wrote the first modules as examples, paired with each economist to migrate their own code, and set review rules so nothing merged without tests. I made the case to management in terms of risk (reproducibility for model validation and regulators), not technology. **Result:** [your numbers: models migrated, run time, review time, validation findings]. **Learning:** adoption came from making the new way easier for each person, not from mandating it; I now start every change with the people who'll use it.\n\nAlso answers: \"Tell me about influencing without authority\" and \"Tell me about a project you're proud of.\" (Fill in the bracket with real numbers; specifics are what make it credible.)"}, {"type": "cards", "cards": [{"q": "The five moves of a live case?", "a": "Goal (decision + metric), Frame (drivers), Evidence (data and rough numbers per driver), Answer (commit, sized), Risks and monitoring.", "id": "11-case-and-take-home-craft:c1"}, {"q": "The default frame for a credit case?", "a": "Volume × (merchant fee + interest − funding − losses); say which lines the decision moves.", "id": "11-case-and-take-home-craft:c2"}, {"q": "Three common case failures?", "a": "Boiling the ocean, never landing, average thinking.", "id": "11-case-and-take-home-craft:c3"}, {"q": "The five-slide take-home deck?", "a": "1 the answer (recommendation, impact, risk); 2–3 the two key segmented findings; 4 the sized recommendation and trade-off; 5 risks, assumptions, next steps. Appendix: data integrity checks.", "id": "11-case-and-take-home-craft:c4"}, {"q": "What makes a clarifying question worth asking?", "a": "Its answer would change what you do next: objective, scope, constraints, what changed. Otherwise state an assumption and move on.", "id": "11-case-and-take-home-craft:c5"}, {"q": "Four habits for maths out loud?", "a": "Round first, say units, per dollar then scale, sanity-check out loud (and write the tree on paper).", "id": "11-case-and-take-home-craft:c6"}, {"q": "What's a finding-title?", "a": "A chart title that states the conclusion with a number, not the variable plotted.", "id": "11-case-and-take-home-craft:c7"}, {"q": "What should AI do on a take-home, and what stays yours?", "a": "AI: boilerplate code, dialect translation, suggested checks, prose, critique. Yours: questions, definitions, interpretation, recommendation, and verifying every number.", "id": "11-case-and-take-home-craft:c8"}, {"q": "Classic bugs to check in AI-written SQL?", "a": "Join fan-out, wrong grain, unpaid rows dropped by driving from the wrong table, integer division, dialect-only functions.", "id": "11-case-and-take-home-craft:c9"}, {"q": "How to handle \"what's the weakest part of your analysis?\"", "a": "Name it first, say how much it could change the answer, and what you'd do to fix it.", "id": "11-case-and-take-home-craft:c10"}, {"q": "STAR-L?", "a": "Situation, Task, Action (mostly, and \"I\"), Result (a number), Learning.", "id": "11-case-and-take-home-craft:c11"}, {"q": "What to say when you don't have a number in a case?", "a": "\"Here's how I'd estimate it, and the decision it would change\", then a transparent rough estimate.", "id": "11-case-and-take-home-craft:c12"}]}]}], "cases": [{"id": "01-was-the-tightening-worth-it", "title": "Was the tightening worth it?", "minutes": 40, "summary": "Size what the July 2024 policy change saved and what it cost, then recommend.", "lesson": "", "date": "", "interview": false, "part": "", "blocks": [{"type": "md", "text": "> **The question.** \"In July 2024 we tightened underwriting. A year on, the growth team says it cost us too much volume; the credit team says it saved us from losses. You've got the data. Who's right, and what should we do?\"\n\nWork in three passes: structure first (no data), then evidence (SQL), then a recommendation. Write each before opening the next model answer. Chapters 1 and 4 have the tools."}, {"type": "explain", "id": "case1-structure", "prompt": "**Pass 1: structure.** Before touching data: what's the decision, what metric decides it, and what three or four pieces of evidence would you pull? Write it as you'd say it to the interviewer.", "model": "**Decision:** keep the July 2024 policy, reverse it, or reverse part of it. **Metric:** total margin after losses and funding over a year (not approval rate, not loss rate alone).\n**Evidence:**\n1. **Where approvals fell**, by score band: approval rate before vs after on comparable applicants (the swap-outs).\n2. **What those loans earned before**: margin per loan by band, on pre-tightening loans old enough to have outcomes.\n3. **Size it:** swapped-out approvals × conversion × margin per loan, by band. Net = margin given up in good bands minus losses avoided in bad bands.\n4. **Caveats:** the swap-outs are the riskier end of each band; applicant mix may have shifted; margins here leave out processing, servicing and acquisition costs."}, {"type": "scratch", "id": "case1-sql", "prompt": "**Pass 2: evidence.** Build the sizing: for each score band, approval before (applications from 2023-07-01 to 2024-06-30) and after (2024-07-01 to 2025-06-30), the number of post-tightening applications, and margin per loan on loans originated before 2024-07-01 (chapter 1's four lines). Then swapped-out approvals × margin per loan.", "starter": "-- Step 1: approval rate by score band, before vs after.\n-- Bands: no score, under 580, 580-619, 620-679, 680-719, 720+\nWITH score_bands AS (\n  SELECT\n    application_id,\n    applied_at,\n    decision,\n    CASE\n      WHEN credit_score IS NULL THEN 'no score'\n      WHEN credit_score < 580 THEN 'under 580'\n      WHEN credit_score < 620 THEN '580-619'\n      WHEN credit_score < 680 THEN '620-679'\n      WHEN credit_score < 720 THEN '680-719'\n      ELSE '720+'\n    END AS score_band\n  FROM applications\n)\nSELECT\n  score_band,\n  AVG(CASE WHEN decision = 'approved' THEN 1.0 ELSE 0 END)\n    FILTER (WHERE applied_at >= DATE '2023-07-01' AND applied_at < DATE '2024-07-01') AS approval_before,\n  AVG(CASE WHEN decision = 'approved' THEN 1.0 ELSE 0 END)\n    FILTER (WHERE applied_at >= DATE '2024-07-01' AND applied_at < DATE '2025-07-01') AS approval_after,\n  COUNT(*) FILTER (WHERE applied_at >= DATE '2024-07-01' AND applied_at < DATE '2025-07-01') AS applications_after\nFROM score_bands\nGROUP BY score_band\nORDER BY score_band\n-- Step 2: add margin per loan by band (fee + interest - funding - losses), from chapter 1.\n-- Step 3: join, and compute applications_after * (approval_before - approval_after) * margin_per_loan."}, {"type": "explain", "id": "case1-findings", "prompt": "**Findings.** What did you find? Three or four bullets, each a number plus its \"so what\".", "model": "- **580–619:** approval fell from 36% to 13%, about 128 fewer approvals in the year. Those loans lost about \\$33 each before the change, so the tightening **avoided about \\$4k** of losses. Right call.\n- **620–679:** approval fell from 86% to 63%, about 390 fewer approvals. Those loans made about \\$47 each, so it **gave up about \\$18k** of margin. This is where the cost is.\n- **680–719** gave up about \\$2k more; no-score applicants about \\$0.5k saved.\n- **Net:** roughly \\$16k of margin given up on approvals, about \\$14k after conversion (88% of approvals became loans), against about \\$221k of margin on the loans actually made that year: **a cost of about 6%**. Caveat: the 620–679 swap-outs are concentrated at the bottom of the band, so the true cost is likely somewhat lower."}, {"type": "explain", "id": "case1-recommendation", "prompt": "**Recommendation.** Answer first, in under 120 words, as you'd say it to a VP. Include what you'd do, the risk, and how you'd monitor it.", "model": "Both teams are partly right: the tightening correctly cut 580–619, which lost money, but it also cut 620–679, which was profitable, and that cost us roughly 6% of a year's margin. I'd **keep 580–619 declined** and **test restoring 620–679**: approve a random 20% of currently declined 620–679 applicants, with a lower limit on first loans. Guardrails: stop if the test cell's first-payment default or 30+ at MOB 3 runs above 1.5 times the approved 620–679 control. If it holds for a quarter, roll it out. I'd also clear the test with compliance, since it changes who gets credit."}]}, {"id": "02-longer-terms", "title": "Should we push 24-month terms?", "minutes": 35, "summary": "The most profitable product per dollar, on the smallest sample. Read the numbers, then decide how much to trust them.", "lesson": "", "date": "", "interview": false, "part": "", "blocks": [{"type": "md", "text": "> **The question.** \"Product wants to show the 24-month option first at checkout for purchases over \\$1,000. Their pitch: it's our highest-margin term. Do you agree?\""}, {"type": "explain", "id": "case2-structure", "prompt": "**Pass 1: structure.** What would you need to believe for this to be a good idea, and what evidence would test each belief?", "model": "The proposal works if: (1) 24-month loans really are more profitable **per dollar and per year of capital**, (2) that holds for the customers who'd **switch** to 24 months because it's shown first (not just those who choose it today), and (3) the extra risk (longer time at risk, bigger tickets) and funding exposure are acceptable.\nEvidence: unit economics by term on **fully matured** loans; loss rates by term with sample sizes; who chooses 24 months today (score, ticket size); a per-year view (margin ÷ term), since 24-month loans tie up capital twice as long as 12-month ones; sensitivity to funding costs."}, {"type": "scratch", "id": "case2-sql", "prompt": "**Pass 2: evidence.** Unit economics by term for loans originated in **2023** (old enough that even 24-month loans have finished). Count loans and charge-offs too.", "starter": "WITH scheduled AS (\n  SELECT loan_id, SUM(amount_due) AS total_scheduled FROM installments GROUP BY loan_id\n),\ncollected AS (\n  SELECT loan_id, SUM(amount) AS collected\n  FROM (SELECT DISTINCT * FROM payments) AS clean_payments\n  WHERE status = 'succeeded'\n  GROUP BY loan_id\n),\nloans_2023 AS (\n  SELECT\n    COALESCE(CAST(loans.term_months AS VARCHAR), 'pay_in_4') AS term,\n    loans.principal,\n    loans.status,\n    CASE WHEN loans.apr = 0 THEN merchant_terms.fee_rate_zero_apr\n         ELSE merchant_terms.fee_rate_interest_bearing END * loans.principal AS merchant_fee,\n    scheduled.total_scheduled - loans.principal AS interest,\n    0.06 * 0.5 * loans.principal *\n      CASE WHEN loans.product = 'pay_in_4' THEN 6.0 / 52 ELSE loans.n_installments / 12.0 END AS funding_cost,\n    CASE WHEN loans.status = 'charged_off'\n         THEN GREATEST(loans.principal - COALESCE(collected.collected, 0), 0) ELSE 0 END AS credit_loss\n  FROM loans\n  JOIN merchant_terms ON merchant_terms.merchant_id = loans.merchant_id\n  JOIN scheduled ON scheduled.loan_id = loans.loan_id\n  LEFT JOIN collected ON collected.loan_id = loans.loan_id\n  WHERE loans.originated_date < DATE '2024-01-01'\n)\nSELECT\n  term,\n  COUNT(*) AS n_loans,\n  SUM(CASE WHEN status = 'charged_off' THEN 1 ELSE 0 END) AS n_charged_off,\n  10000 * SUM(merchant_fee + interest - funding_cost - credit_loss) / SUM(principal) AS margin_bps\nFROM loans_2023\nGROUP BY term\nORDER BY term"}, {"type": "explain", "id": "case2-findings", "prompt": "**Findings.** What do the numbers say, and how much do you trust them? Do the per-year arithmetic.", "model": "- **24-month loans made about 2,320 bps** per dollar in the 2023 vintage, versus about 665 for 12-month and about 200 for 3- and 6-month. Almost all of it is interest (about 2,770 bps); none were 0% APR.\n- **Per year of capital** the gap narrows: 2,320 ÷ 2 ≈ 1,160 bps a year versus 665 for 12-month. Still the best, but by less than the headline.\n- **The loss rate rests on 2 charge-offs out of 72 loans** (2.8%). A plausible range for the true rate runs from well under 1% to nearly 10%. If it were 8% like the 12-month loans, losses would rise from about 60 bps to roughly 170–370 bps (depending on how much of each loan is repaid before charge-off). The margin would still lead, but the gap would shrink. Adding the first half of 2024 (159 loans) gives 4.4%, but those loans haven't finished yet.\n- **Selection:** today's 24-month borrowers chose it. Customers nudged into it by default placement may differ (larger baskets, more stretched budgets)."}, {"type": "explain", "id": "case2-recommendation", "prompt": "**Recommendation**, answer first, under 120 words.", "model": "Yes, cautiously, as a test. 24-month loans look like our most profitable term even per year of capital, but the loss estimate rests on 72 loans, and the customers who'd switch because of placement aren't the ones choosing it today. I'd run a randomised test: show 24 months first for half of eligible checkouts over \\$1,000. Measure conversion, take-up, first-payment default and 30+ at MOB 3 against control, and project margin with a conservative loss assumption (12-month rates). I'd also cap 24-month exposure while losses are unproven and check the funding plan, since longer terms lock in today's funding cost. Expand if margin per checkout beats control after two quarters."}]}, {"id": "03-merchant-fee-cut", "title": "The fee cut", "minutes": 35, "summary": "Our largest merchant wants a lower fee in exchange for more volume. Find the break-even, then ask who the extra customers are.", "lesson": "", "date": "", "interview": false, "part": "", "blocks": [{"type": "md", "text": "> **The question.** \"Coastal Auto Care (merchant 1137), our largest merchant by 2025 volume, wants its merchant fee cut by one percentage point on every loan. In return they'll make us the default payment option at checkout, which they say will grow their volume with us by 30%. Should we take the deal?\""}, {"type": "explain", "id": "case3-structure", "prompt": "**Pass 1: structure.** What's the break-even logic, and what would make the deal better or worse than the break-even suggests?", "model": "**Decision:** accept, counter, or decline. **Metric:** annual margin from this merchant (fee + interest − funding − losses), plus any effect on other merchants.\n**Logic:** a 1-point cut removes 100 bps from every dollar. If current margin is M bps, volume must grow by M ÷ (M − 100) − 1 just to stand still.\n**What changes it:** whether the extra volume is **incremental** (new customers) or **cannibalised** (customers who'd have used us anyway); whether the new customers are **riskier** (default placement brings in people who didn't seek us out); the product mix (0% APR loans earn no interest); and **precedent**: other large merchants will ask for the same."}, {"type": "scratch", "id": "case3-sql", "prompt": "**Pass 2: evidence.** Unit economics for merchant 1137 on loans originated **before 2025** (2025 loans haven't had time to go bad). Then GMV for 2025.", "starter": "WITH scheduled AS (\n  SELECT loan_id, SUM(amount_due) AS total_scheduled FROM installments GROUP BY loan_id\n),\ncollected AS (\n  SELECT loan_id, SUM(amount) AS collected\n  FROM (SELECT DISTINCT * FROM payments) AS clean_payments\n  WHERE status = 'succeeded'\n  GROUP BY loan_id\n),\nmerchant_loans AS (\n  SELECT\n    YEAR(loans.originated_date) AS origination_year,\n    loans.principal,\n    CASE WHEN loans.apr = 0 THEN merchant_terms.fee_rate_zero_apr\n         ELSE merchant_terms.fee_rate_interest_bearing END * loans.principal AS merchant_fee,\n    scheduled.total_scheduled - loans.principal AS interest,\n    0.06 * 0.5 * loans.principal *\n      CASE WHEN loans.product = 'pay_in_4' THEN 6.0 / 52 ELSE loans.n_installments / 12.0 END AS funding_cost,\n    CASE WHEN loans.status = 'charged_off'\n         THEN GREATEST(loans.principal - COALESCE(collected.collected, 0), 0) ELSE 0 END AS credit_loss\n  FROM loans\n  JOIN merchant_terms ON merchant_terms.merchant_id = loans.merchant_id\n  JOIN scheduled ON scheduled.loan_id = loans.loan_id\n  LEFT JOIN collected ON collected.loan_id = loans.loan_id\n  WHERE loans.merchant_id = 1137\n)\nSELECT\n  origination_year,\n  COUNT(*) AS n_loans,\n  SUM(principal) AS gmv,\n  10000 * SUM(merchant_fee) / SUM(principal) AS fee_bps,\n  10000 * SUM(credit_loss) / SUM(principal) AS loss_bps,\n  10000 * SUM(merchant_fee + interest - funding_cost - credit_loss) / SUM(principal) AS margin_bps\nFROM merchant_loans\nGROUP BY origination_year\nORDER BY origination_year"}, {"type": "explain", "id": "case3-findings", "prompt": "**Findings.** Current margin, break-even growth, and what happens if the new customers are riskier.", "model": "- **Current margin:** 2023–2024 loans pooled (228 loans, \\$181k) made about **544 bps**: fee 282, interest 815, funding 237, losses 316. By year it swings a lot (about 200 bps in 2023, 690 in 2024), so I'd use the pooled figure.\n- **Break-even:** after a 100 bps cut, margin is 444 bps, so volume must grow **23%** (544 ÷ 444) to stand still. Their promised 30% leaves us about **6%** better off: on 2025's \\$235k of volume, roughly \\$0.8k a year. A thin gain.\n- **If the extra customers are riskier:** suppose the new 30% has double the losses (632 bps). Their margin is about 128 bps, and the total falls to about 482 bps-equivalent, **11% worse** than today. The deal flips on an assumption we can't yet check.\n- About half this merchant's volume is 0% APR, where the fee is the main income; the cut hurts most there."}, {"type": "explain", "id": "case3-recommendation", "prompt": "**Recommendation**, answer first, under 120 words.", "model": "Don't accept as offered; counter. At a 1-point cut we need 23% more volume just to break even, the promised 30% gains us only about 6%, and if the extra customers are even moderately riskier the deal loses money. I'd counter with a **tiered fee**: the cut applies only to volume above today's run-rate, so we pay for incremental volume rather than discounting what we already have. Add a 6-month review with access to their checkout data so we can measure incrementality, and keep risk controls on the new flow (first-payment default by week). Before agreeing to anything, I'd check whether other large merchants have most-favoured terms or would ask for the same."}]}, {"id": "04-repeat-customer-limits", "title": "Bigger loans for repeat customers", "minutes": 40, "summary": "Repeat borrowers look safer. Check whether that holds for the ones who'd take the bigger loan, and watch for a time-travel trap.", "lesson": "", "date": "", "interview": false, "part": "", "blocks": [{"type": "md", "text": "> **The question.** \"Growth wants to raise the maximum loan size by 50% for customers who've borrowed from us before. Repeat customers default less, so it should be safe. Agree?\""}, {"type": "explain", "id": "case4-structure", "prompt": "**Pass 1: structure.** What's the claim, what's the hidden assumption, and what would you check?", "model": "**Claim:** repeat customers default less. **Hidden assumption:** the repeat customers who'd use a higher limit (people who want to borrow more than before) are as safe as repeat customers in general. **Checks:**\n1. Loss rate by loan number (first, second, third…), on loans old enough to have outcomes.\n2. Among repeat loans, outcome by **size relative to the previous loan**: do borrowers who stepped up a lot do worse?\n3. **Eligibility rules:** what counts as \"borrowed before\"? Someone who charged off with us shouldn't qualify. Check whether we already lend again after a charge-off.\n4. Economics: larger loans earn more interest per loan; does margin per dollar hold up?"}, {"type": "scratch", "id": "case4-sql", "prompt": "**Pass 2: evidence.** Number each borrower's loans in order, and attach the previous loan's principal. Bad = charged off within 12 months of origination; use loans originated before 2025.", "starter": "WITH sequenced AS (\n  SELECT\n    loan_id,\n    borrower_id,\n    originated_date,\n    principal,\n    charged_off_date,\n    ROW_NUMBER() OVER (PARTITION BY borrower_id ORDER BY originated_date, loan_id) AS loan_number,\n    LAG(principal) OVER (PARTITION BY borrower_id ORDER BY originated_date, loan_id) AS previous_principal\n  FROM loans\n)\nSELECT\n  LEAST(loan_number, 4) AS loan_number_capped,\n  COUNT(*) AS n_loans,\n  AVG(CASE WHEN charged_off_date < originated_date + INTERVAL 12 MONTH THEN 1.0 ELSE 0 END) AS bad_rate_12m\nFROM sequenced\nWHERE originated_date < DATE '2025-01-01'\nGROUP BY loan_number_capped\nORDER BY loan_number_capped\n-- Next: among repeat loans, bucket principal / previous_principal and compare bad rates.\n-- Then: how many loans were made to borrowers who had ALREADY charged off with us\n-- (an earlier loan's charged_off_date before this loan's originated_date)?"}, {"type": "explain", "id": "case4-findings", "prompt": "**Findings.** Three or four bullets.", "model": "- **Repeat loans are safer:** 12-month bad rate 5.5% for first loans (2,254) versus 2.8% for second loans (975), 5.1% for third (335) and 2.8% for fourth or later (142). Lower on average, but not steadily falling, and the later groups are small.\n- **Stepping up:** among repeat loans, those 1.5–2.5 times the previous loan went bad at 5.2% (174 loans) versus 2.6–3.2% for smaller step-ups or smaller loans. 2.5 times or more was 3.0% (332). No clear pattern, and the groups are small, so the data can't confirm a +50% limit is safe for the people who'd use it.\n- **Margin per dollar rises with size** (more interest), from about 590 bps for smaller-than-before loans to about 980 bps for 2.5 times or more.\n- **A policy gap:** 150 loans went to 95 borrowers who had **already** charged off with us. Watch the trap here: `loans.status` is today's status, so a \"previous loan is charged off\" test also catches loans that charged off **after** the new loan was made. Use `charged_off_date < originated_date` to see what was known at the decision."}, {"type": "explain", "id": "case4-recommendation", "prompt": "**Recommendation**, answer first, under 120 words.", "model": "Partly agree. Repeat customers do default less (about 3–5% versus 5.5% for first loans), but the data can't tell us how the customers who'd use a 50% higher limit will perform, and modest step-ups already show a bump. I'd **test** it: raise the limit by 50% for a random half of repeat customers with at least one loan **paid off on time** and no charge-off ever, then compare 12-month losses, MOB-3 delinquency and margin per customer against the control half. Separately, and now: **stop re-lending to borrowers who've charged off with us** (150 loans so far), unless there's a deliberate re-entry policy with its own limits."}]}, {"id": "05-debit-vs-ach", "title": "Debit card as the default", "minutes": 35, "summary": "A payments proposal where the observational data can't answer the causal question. Know what to measure, and design the test.", "lesson": "", "date": "", "interview": false, "part": "", "blocks": [{"type": "md", "text": "> **The question.** \"The payments team wants to make debit card, not bank account (ACH), the default repayment method for new loans. Their reasons: card payments confirm instantly, so we learn about a failed payment the same day instead of days later. What do you think?\""}, {"type": "explain", "id": "case5-structure", "prompt": "**Pass 1: structure.** What are the benefits and costs, and why can't you just compare today's debit and ACH payers?", "model": "**Benefits:** faster knowledge of failures, so faster retries and earlier collections contact; possibly fewer failures if customers keep cards funded. **Costs:** card processing usually costs more per payment than ACH; cards expire and get replaced (failed payments from stale cards); some customers prefer bank payments and may drop off at checkout.\n**Why not compare today's payers:** customers chose their method. Debit payers may differ from ACH payers in ways that drive outcomes (selection), so the observed difference isn't the effect of switching someone. That needs a randomised test.\n**Measure:** first-attempt failure rate, recovery rate and time to recovery after a failure, loss rate, cost per payment, and checkout conversion."}, {"type": "scratch", "id": "case5-sql", "prompt": "**Pass 2: evidence.** For each installment, find the first payment attempt (method and status) and whether it was eventually paid. Use deduplicated payments, attempts before 2025-07-01, and leave out rows with no method.", "starter": "WITH clean_payments AS (\n  SELECT DISTINCT * FROM payments WHERE payment_method IS NOT NULL\n),\nfirst_attempt AS (\n  SELECT\n    loan_id,\n    installment_number,\n    ARG_MIN(payment_method, paid_at) AS first_method,\n    ARG_MIN(status, paid_at) AS first_status,\n    MIN(paid_at) AS first_attempt_at\n  FROM clean_payments\n  GROUP BY loan_id, installment_number\n),\neventual_success AS (\n  SELECT loan_id, installment_number, MIN(paid_at) AS succeeded_at\n  FROM clean_payments\n  WHERE status = 'succeeded'\n  GROUP BY loan_id, installment_number\n)\nSELECT\n  first_attempt.first_method,\n  COUNT(*) AS n_installments,\n  AVG(CASE WHEN first_attempt.first_status = 'failed' THEN 1.0 ELSE 0 END) AS first_attempt_failure_rate\nFROM first_attempt\nLEFT JOIN eventual_success\n  ON eventual_success.loan_id = first_attempt.loan_id\n AND eventual_success.installment_number = first_attempt.installment_number\nWHERE first_attempt.first_attempt_at < TIMESTAMP '2025-07-01'\nGROUP BY first_attempt.first_method\n-- Add: among first attempts that failed, the share eventually paid, and the median days to recovery.\n-- (ARG_MIN is DuckDB; in PostgreSQL use ROW_NUMBER() to pick the first attempt.)"}, {"type": "explain", "id": "case5-findings", "prompt": "**Findings.** What does the data show, and what can't it show?", "model": "- **First-attempt failure:** ACH 5.0% (17,819 installments), debit 5.6% (10,057). Similar, slightly worse for debit.\n- **Recovery after a failure:** 67% (ACH) and 69% (debit) were eventually paid, both with a median of 6 days. In this data, debit's \"instant\" advantage doesn't show up in recovery, though the data records attempts, not when we learned of the failure.\n- **Failures matter:** loans with any failed attempt charged off at 19–22%, versus under 1% for loans with none. A failed payment is one of the strongest early-warning signals we have, so learning about it sooner has real value.\n- **Data quality:** 3% of payment rows have no method recorded. I'd fix that before any test, or the test can't be read.\n- **What it can't show:** the effect of **switching** a customer, because method is self-selected."}, {"type": "explain", "id": "case5-recommendation", "prompt": "**Recommendation**, answer first, under 120 words.", "model": "Don't switch the default yet; test it. Today's debit and ACH payers fail and recover at similar rates, so the data gives no reason to expect a big improvement, and card processing costs more. But failed payments predict charge-off strongly, so faster failure signals could be worth it. I'd randomise the default method for new loans for a quarter and compare checkout conversion, first-attempt failure, recovery rate and time, 6-month loss rate and processing cost per loan. Switch only if the reduction in losses and collections cost beats the extra processing cost. In parallel, fix the missing payment-method field."}]}, {"id": "06-missing-income", "title": "Dropping the income question", "minutes": 35, "summary": "The data says missing-income borrowers aren't riskier. Decide whether that answers the question, and what else is at stake.", "lesson": "", "date": "", "interview": false, "part": "", "blocks": [{"type": "md", "text": "> **The question.** \"About 9% of our borrowers leave the income field blank, and the product team says the field causes checkout drop-off. They want to remove the income question entirely. Credit risk has to sign off. Do you?\""}, {"type": "explain", "id": "case6-structure", "prompt": "**Pass 1: structure.** What do you need to know: about risk, about what income is used for, and about the people who'd be affected?", "model": "**Risk:** do borrowers without stated income perform worse, controlling for score? **Uses of income:** underwriting rules, limit setting, ability-to-pay checks a bank partner or state law may require, model features, and adverse action reasons. **Population:** today's blank-income borrowers **chose** to skip the field. If it's removed for everyone, the affected group is everyone, including people who would have reported low income. **Benefit:** how much conversion would we gain? Need a measured number, not a claim."}, {"type": "scratch", "id": "case6-sql", "prompt": "**Pass 2: evidence.** Compare borrowers with and without stated income: approval rate, average score, and (on loans originated before 2024-07-01) charge-off rate, overall and within score bands.", "starter": "SELECT\n  borrowers.annual_income IS NULL AS missing_income,\n  COUNT(*) AS n_applications,\n  AVG(CASE WHEN applications.decision = 'approved' THEN 1.0 ELSE 0 END) AS approval_rate,\n  AVG(applications.credit_score) AS avg_score\nFROM applications\nJOIN borrowers ON borrowers.borrower_id = applications.borrower_id\nGROUP BY missing_income\n-- Next: charge-off rate on loans originated before 2024-07-01, by missing_income,\n-- then by missing_income within score bands (under 660, 660-719, 720+)."}, {"type": "explain", "id": "case6-findings", "prompt": "**Findings.** Three or four bullets.", "model": "- **Same applicants on paper:** 565 borrowers (9%) have no income. Their 1,301 applications had the same average score (683) and approval rate (73%) as everyone else's.\n- **Not riskier:** on loans before July 2024, 5.1% charged off (237 loans) versus 6.4% (1,965). Within score bands they're similar or slightly better (under 660: 10.7% vs 13.8%; 720+: 1.3% vs 1.4%). Small samples, but no sign of extra risk.\n- **But lower margin:** 555 bps versus 655, on smaller loans (\\$688 vs \\$744 average), so the income field may be doing some work on loan size or mix.\n- **Selection:** these people chose to leave it blank. That tells us little about people who'd have reported a low income if asked."}, {"type": "explain", "id": "case6-recommendation", "prompt": "**Recommendation**, answer first, under 120 words.", "model": "I'd sign off on making income **optional**, not on removing it, and test the conversion gain. Borrowers who skip it today don't perform worse once you control for score, so requiring it isn't buying us much risk protection at these loan sizes. But removing the question entirely changes who's affected, and income may be needed for ability-to-pay checks expected by our bank partner or state law, for higher limits, and for adverse action reasons. Plan: A/B test optional versus required, measuring conversion, loss rates and margin; keep income **required above a loan-size threshold**; and confirm the regulatory requirements with compliance first."}]}], "prep": [{"id": "00-credit-start", "title": "Credit analytics for risk interviews", "minutes": 30, "summary": "Eleven chapters, six case drills and a mock take-home, built to be remembered two weeks from now, not just understood today.", "lesson": "", "date": "", "interview": false, "part": "", "blocks": [{"type": "md", "text": "## How this track works\n\nEach chapter is a short textbook chapter on the same lending data you've been querying, with live charts you can edit, SQL exercises, and **written answers**: short explanations you type, then compare with a strong answer. Writing the explanation yourself is the point. Recognising a good answer feels like knowing it; producing one is what the interview tests.\n\nThree things keep it from fading:\n\n- **Warm-ups.** Every chapter opens with recall questions from earlier chapters. Answer before scrolling.\n- **Review cards.** Opening a chapter adds its cards to your review deck. They come back after 1, 3, 7, 14 and 30 days, and a miss sends a card back to the start. Five minutes a day keeps chapter 1 alive in week 2.\n- **Cases that mix everything.** The case drills don't say which chapter they need. Choosing the tool is part of the skill.\n\n## The daily routine (60–120 minutes)\n\n1. **Review** the cards that are due (5–10 minutes). Answer out loud before revealing.\n2. **One chapter or one case.** Do every exercise and written answer before opening the model answer.\n3. **Copy your answers** at the bottom of the page and send them to me. I'll review your reasoning, not just the numbers, and tell you what to fix.\n\n## Two weeks, about 30 hours\n\nThe live rounds (hiring manager, the deeper case, the VP) are **pen-and-paper cases plus behavioural questions, with no SQL**. So the plan front-loads the core chapters, the case structure and the **Paper cases**, then broadens. The take-home comes after the first round and is a five-slide presentation.\n\n| Days | Work | Hours |\n|---|---|---|\n| 1–2 | Chapters 1–2: how BNPL makes money; delinquency and loss metrics | 3 |\n| 3 | Chapter 3: vintages. Paper cases 1–2 | 2 |\n| 4 | Chapter 4: credit strategy. Case drill 1 | 2.5 |\n| 5 | Chapter 5: diagnosing a change. Paper case 3 | 2 |\n| 6 | Chapter 11: case and take-home craft. Behavioural stories: pitch plus three stories | 2.5 |\n| 7 | Chapter 6: fraud. Paper cases 4–5 | 2 |\n| 8 | Chapter 7: growth. Case drill 3. Paper case 6 | 2.5 |\n| 9 | Chapter 8: models. Finish the stories, including the cross-functional one | 2.5 |\n| 10 | Chapters 9–10: macro; regulation. Paper cases 7–8 | 2.5 |\n| 11 | Voice mock 1 (case). Case drills 2 and 4 | 2 |\n| 12 | Voice mock 2, both versions (behavioural, cross-functional). Company research and your questions for them | 2 |\n| 13 | Voice mock 3 (VP style). Redo your weakest paper case from scratch, out loud | 1.5 |\n| 14 | Review cards only, plus your \"tell me about yourself\" out loud. Rest | 0.5 |\n| Every day | Review cards (about 2.5 hours in total) | 2.5 |\n| After the first round | Mock take-home (4 hours, five slides), the readout voice mock, case drills 5–6 | 6 |\n\nDaily SQL sets drop to every other day: SQL isn't tested live, but you'll need it, fast and correct, for the take-home."}, {"type": "note", "title": "If you only have ten hours", "text": "Chapters 1, 2, 4, 5 and 11; paper cases 1–4; your pitch and three stories; one voice mock. That covers the core of what a hiring-manager case will touch."}, {"type": "md", "text": "## What's in the data that's new\n\nTwo tables were added for this track: `loan_month` (a month-end snapshot of every loan: balance, days past due, status) and `merchant_terms` (each merchant's fee rates). A few things in the data are there to be discovered, the way they would be in a real portfolio. If something looks odd, that's usually the point."}]}, {"id": "01-paper-cases", "title": "Paper cases", "minutes": 30, "summary": "Nine live-case drills with pen, paper and a calculator, no SQL. The format the live rounds actually use.", "lesson": "", "date": "", "interview": false, "part": "", "blocks": [{"type": "md", "text": "## How to use this page\n\nThe live rounds give you pen, paper, a calculator or a spreadsheet, and no SQL. These drills train that: the numbers are given, and the skill is turning them into a decision, out loud.\n\nFor each one, set a **10-minute timer**, then write:\n\n1. **Clarifying questions**: the one or two whose answers would change your approach (chapter 11).\n2. **Your frame** in one line.\n3. **The maths**, with units.\n4. **Your recommendation** and the main trade-off.\n\nSay it out loud as you go, as if the interviewer were listening. Then compare with the strong answer. The numbers are illustrative, chosen to be round; they are not from the course data."}, {"type": "explain", "id": "paper-1", "prompt": "**1. Is Pay in 4 profitable?** Average order \\$200. Merchant fee 4% of GMV. Credit losses 1.5% of GMV. Funding: 6% a year on a balance that averages half the loan, for the loan's six-week life. Payment processing 0.4% of GMV. What's the margin per order, and how should we think about it over a year?", "model": "**Clarify:** do losses include fraud? Any customer acquisition cost? (Assume no.)\n**Frame:** margin = fee − losses − funding − processing, per dollar of GMV.\n**Maths:** fee 400 bps; losses 150; funding 6% × ½ × 6/52 ≈ 0.35% = 35 bps; processing 40. Margin ≈ **175 bps**, so about **\\$3.50 per \\$200 order**.\n**Over a year:** the money comes back in six weeks, so the same dollar can be lent about 8 times a year: roughly 175 × 8 ≈ **1,400 bps a year** per dollar of lending capacity. That's why a thin-looking per-order margin can be a good business.\n**Trade-off:** it only works if losses stay near 1.5%: every 50 bps of extra loss removes 29% of the margin. The product is very sensitive to credit and fraud, much less to funding costs."}, {"type": "explain", "id": "paper-2", "prompt": "**2. Approve more?** We get 1M applications a year and approve 70%. Average loan \\$800. On the approved book, fee + interest − funding is 500 bps and losses are 200 bps, so margin is 300 bps. Growth proposes approving 5 more points of applicants (50,000 more loans a year). We expect those marginal loans to lose 600 bps. Should we?", "model": "**Clarify:** is the objective profit or growth? Do approved customers come back for more loans? (Assume 30% take one repeat loan a year at book margin.)\n**Frame:** compare the margin on the marginal loans, not the book average (average vs marginal).\n**Maths:** marginal loans make 500 − 600 = **−100 bps**. 50,000 × \\$800 = \\$40M × −1% = **−\\$400k** in year one. Repeat value: 30% × 50,000 = 15,000 repeat loans × \\$800 × 3% = **+\\$360k**. Net ≈ **−\\$40k**: roughly break-even.\n**Recommendation:** not as proposed, but it's close enough to be worth a **test**: approve the extra slice for a random part of traffic, with lower first-loan limits (which cuts the loss on first loans) and measure losses and repeat rates. If repeat behaviour is better than 30%, or lower limits bring losses below 500 bps, it pays.\n**Trade-off:** growth now versus losses now; the case hinges on lifetime value, which is the assumption to test."}, {"type": "explain", "id": "paper-3", "prompt": "**3. Delinquency went up: mix or rate?** Last quarter the book was 80% prime (30+ rate 1.0%) and 20% near-prime (5.0%). This quarter it's 70% prime (still 1.0%) and 30% near-prime (5.5%). Total 30+ went from 1.8% to 2.35%. How much is mix, how much is rate, and what do you tell the CRO?", "model": "**Clarify:** did the near-prime share grow because of a policy change or a channel/merchant shift?\n**Frame:** shift-share: change = mix effect + rate effect.\n**Maths:** old total 0.8 × 1.0 + 0.2 × 5.0 = 1.8%. New mix at old rates: 0.7 × 1.0 + 0.3 × 5.0 = 2.2%, so **mix ≈ +0.40 points**. Rate change at new mix: 0.3 × 0.5 = **+0.15 points**. Total +0.55 ✓.\n**To the CRO:** \"About three-quarters of the increase is because we're lending more to near-prime customers, which we chose; a quarter is near-prime performing slightly worse, which we didn't. The second part is the early warning: I'd find where the near-prime deterioration is concentrated before deciding whether to slow near-prime growth.\"\n**Trade-off:** mix-driven delinquency isn't a problem if near-prime is priced for it; check margin, not just delinquency."}, {"type": "explain", "id": "paper-4", "prompt": "**4. A fraud rule.** 100,000 applications a month; 0.5% are fraud (500), each costing about \\$1,000. A new rule flags 2% of applications (2,000) and catches 60% of the fraud (300). Flagged applicants go through identity verification costing \\$2 each; 20% of the genuine customers flagged abandon, and each lost customer is worth \\$40. Is the rule worth it? What if flagged applicants were declined instead?", "model": "**Clarify:** does verification stop all the fraud it catches? (Assume yes.) Is \\$40 lifetime or first-loan value?\n**Frame:** fraud prevented − lost good customers − verification cost.\n**Maths:** benefit 300 × \\$1,000 = **\\$300k**. Genuine customers flagged: 2,000 − 300 = 1,700; 20% abandon = 340 × \\$40 = **\\$13.6k**. Verification 2,000 × \\$2 = **\\$4k**. Net ≈ **+\\$282k a month**. Precision is only 15% (300 ÷ 2,000), which is normal for rare events.\n**If declined instead:** all 1,700 genuine customers are lost, 1,700 × \\$40 = \\$68k, so still positive (≈ \\$232k) but worse, plus complaints and fair-lending exposure. Step-up verification is the better design.\n**Watch:** fraudsters adapt, so track catch rate monthly; and check the rule doesn't fall disproportionately on any protected group."}, {"type": "explain", "id": "paper-5", "prompt": "**5. An early read on a vintage.** March's vintage shows 3.0% of loans 30+ days past due at month 3 on book, against 2.0% for the previous six vintages. Historically, lifetime loss runs about 2.5 times the MOB-3 30+ rate. March originated \\$50M. How big is this, and what do you do first?", "model": "**Clarify:** did anything change in March: policy, a merchant, a channel, a product? Is March seasonally different?\n**Frame:** excess early delinquency × the loss multiple × volume.\n**Maths:** (3.0% − 2.0%) × 2.5 = 2.5 points of extra lifetime loss × \\$50M ≈ **\\$1.25M** of extra expected loss, if the historical relationship holds.\n**First steps:** (1) is it real: same definition, any payment-processing outage? (2) where is it concentrated: merchant, score band, new vs repeat, first-payment default? A cluster at one merchant with no first payments points to fraud, not credit. (3) has April's vintage got the same signature? If it's ongoing, act on new originations now (the fastest lever); if it was a one-off, it's a loss estimate and a lesson."}, {"type": "explain", "id": "paper-6", "prompt": "**6. Pricing a 0% APR promotion.** A merchant wants to offer 12-month 0% APR on a \\$1,200 average purchase. Our funding cost is 6% a year on a balance that averages half the loan. Losses run 3% of principal at 12 months, 2.5% at 6 months. Servicing is \\$10 per loan. We want a 1% margin. What merchant fee do we need at 12 months and at 6? The merchant currently pays 5%. And what happens if funding costs rise to 8%?", "model": "**Frame:** with 0% APR, the merchant fee must cover funding + losses + servicing + target margin.\n**Maths, 12 months:** funding 6% × ½ × 1 year × \\$1,200 = \\$36; losses 3% = \\$36; servicing \\$10; margin 1% = \\$12. Total **\\$94 ≈ 7.8%** of the purchase.\n**6 months:** funding \\$18, losses \\$30, servicing \\$10, margin \\$12 = **\\$70 ≈ 5.8%**.\n**At 5% the merchant pays \\$60**, so 12 months loses money on every loan (short by \\$34), and even 6 months is short by \\$10.\n**If funding rises to 8%:** 12-month funding becomes \\$48, so the required fee rises by \\$12, **one percentage point**.\n**Recommendation:** offer 6-month 0% at about 6%, or 12-month at about 8%; or a 12-month interest-bearing option at the 5% fee. 0% promotions are a funding-rate bet, so build a rate clause into long contracts."}, {"type": "explain", "id": "paper-7", "prompt": "**7. A new vertical.** A dental chain with 400 clinics wants us to finance procedures. Each clinic does about 250 procedures a month over \\$1,000, averaging \\$2,500. We think 15% of those patients would use us. Expected margin is about 250 bps of GMV. How big is this, and what would make you hesitate?", "model": "**Clarify:** terms (longer terms for larger tickets?), who carries the risk if a procedure is disputed or the clinic closes, and exclusivity.\n**Maths:** 400 × 250 × 12 = 1.2M procedures a year × 15% = **180,000 loans** × \\$2,500 = **\\$450M GMV** × 2.5% ≈ **\\$11M a year** of margin.\n**Hesitations:** (1) **concentration**: one partner, so a dispute or the chain's failure hits hard; (2) **refund and dispute risk**: if a procedure goes badly, patients stop paying the loan; (3) **regulatory scrutiny**: medical financing draws extra consumer-protection attention; (4) the **15% take-up** is the swing assumption: at 5% it's about \\$3.75M.\n**Recommendation:** worth pursuing as a pilot in a few dozen clinics, with merchant-level loss and dispute monitoring, a contract that puts refund risk on the clinic, and a concentration limit."}, {"type": "explain", "id": "paper-8", "prompt": "**8. Is better collections worth it?** A \\$1B book. Each month 1.5% of current loans roll to 30 days past due; 40% of those roll to 60; 60% of those to 90; 80% of those charge off. A new collections approach (costing \\$2M a year more) would cut the 30→60 roll from 40% to 35%. Worth it?", "model": "**Frame:** net flow: the share of current balances that eventually charge off is the product of the roll rates.\n**Maths:** today 1.5% × 40% × 60% × 80% = **0.288% a month** (about 3.5% a year). With 35%: 1.5% × 35% × 60% × 80% = **0.252%**. Saving 0.036% a month × \\$1B ≈ **\\$360k a month ≈ \\$4.3M a year**, against \\$2M of cost: **net ≈ +\\$2.3M**.\n**Caveats:** this treats the book as steady; cured customers may re-default (so the saving is overstated); and aggressive collections carry UDAAP and complaint risk. **Recommendation:** run it as a champion–challenger test on a random half of 30-day delinquents for two months and measure the actual roll rate and re-default rate."}, {"type": "explain", "id": "paper-9", "prompt": "**9. Hold or sell?** We've just originated \\$100M of 12-month loans at 15% APR. Held to maturity: interest income is about 15% × ½ (average balance) = 7.5% of principal, funding costs 6% × ½ = 3%, and losses 3%. A forward-flow buyer offers to buy them at **101.5** (1.5% above par), and we keep servicing. Holding ties up equity of about 10% of the average balance. Hold or sell? What if the buyer offers **99.5**?", "model": "**Clarify:** how sure are we of the 3% loss estimate? Is our funding capacity or equity constrained?\n**Frame:** compare the value of holding (spread, at risk, using capital) with the sale price (certain, now, capital freed).\n**Maths:** hold: 7.5% − 3% − 3% = **1.5% ≈ \\$1.5M** over the year, uncertain, using about 10% × \\$50M = **\\$5M of equity** (a 30% return on it if losses come in as expected). Sell at 101.5: **\\$1.5M now**, certain, and the \\$5M of equity is free to support the next \\$100M of loans. Same expected money; selling wins on risk and on growth capacity.\n**At 99.5:** selling **loses \\$0.5M** versus an expected \\$1.5M from holding. Hold if we have the capital and trust the loss estimate; but if capital is the constraint, selling at a small loss may still beat turning away profitable volume.\n**The risk analyst's angle:** the buyer's price depends on our loss track record. Losses 1 point above expectations on this pool cost \\$1M if held, and lower the price on every future sale."}, {"type": "interview", "title": "", "text": "Every case on this page used the same three moves: **frame the margin per dollar, scale by volume, find the swing assumption**. When you're stuck in a live case, say the frame out loud and ask for the number you need: \"To size this I need the loss rate on these loans; do we have that, or shall I assume something?\""}]}, {"id": "02-mock-take-home", "title": "Mock take-home", "minutes": 30, "summary": "A realistic, time-boxed take-home on three tables. Do it in whatever tool you like, with AI if you want, then build a five-slide deck and present it.", "lesson": "", "date": "", "interview": false, "part": "", "blocks": [{"type": "md", "text": "## The brief\n\n> You've joined the Risk & Analytics team at a BNPL lender. Leadership is setting 2026 credit policy for the **monthly installment** product and wants an independent view.\n>\n> You have three datasets: **applications**, **loans** and **payments**. Answer: **How did the monthly product perform in 2025, what's driving it, and what one or two changes would you make to credit policy for 2026?** Size the impact of your recommendations.\n>\n> Deliverable: an executive presentation of **at most five slides**, plus appendix slides with your data checks, definitions and code. You'll present it in about 10 minutes, followed by Q&A. Time box: **4 hours** for the analysis and slides. You may use any tools, including AI assistants; say briefly how you used them.\n\n## Getting the data\n\nUse only the three tables named in the brief (the others exist in the course for other chapters; leaving them out mirrors the real exercise). Use the site's SQL runner, or download the tables as CSV from **Progress & backup → Download the data** and work in R or anything else. The tables are documented in **Reference → The data**.\n\n## Rules for yourself\n\n- **Start a timer.** Stop at 4 hours, even if it's not perfect. Running out of time is part of the exercise.\n- **First 30 minutes: data integrity.** Grain, row counts, date ranges, nulls, duplicates, impossible values, and anything that clusters oddly. Write down every anomaly and how you'll handle it; that list becomes an appendix slide.\n- **Then segment.** Don't stop at the headline: cut by vintage, term, score band, merchant and timing until you know what's actually driving the numbers.\n- **Keep an assumptions list** as you go.\n- **Reconcile every headline number** with a second query.\n- **Build the deck answer-first**, following chapter 11: slide 1 the answer; slides 2–3 the findings; slide 4 the sized recommendation and trade-off; slide 5 risks, assumptions and next steps. Finding-titles on every chart.\n- **Rehearse the readout out loud** with a timer: 10 minutes, then stop for questions.\n\n## Submit for review"}, {"type": "explain", "id": "prep-th-memo", "prompt": "Paste your deck as text: each slide's title and its key points (and the numbers on any chart).", "model": "A strong deck would, at minimum: define its population and \"bad\" outcome explicitly; compare vintages at the same months on book; notice and separate the **September 2025 fraud cluster** at one new merchant (and say it's fraud, not credit, so it argues for fraud controls, not a cutoff change); size the **620–679 opportunity** from the July 2024 tightening with a test-based recommendation; and mention at least one data-quality issue it handled (duplicate payments, missing payment methods, applications without a score). The recommendations would be sized (dollars or bps of margin), have a risk and a monitoring plan, and fit on five slides, each with one message. Optional extras that impress: the 24-month term economics with a small-sample caveat, re-lending after charge-off, and a compliance sentence on any change to who gets credit."}, {"type": "explain", "id": "prep-th-appendix", "prompt": "List the checks you ran (reconciliations, row counts, duplicates) and the assumptions you made.", "model": ""}, {"type": "explain", "id": "prep-th-defence", "prompt": "Before presenting: write your answers to these four questions. (1) What's the weakest part of your analysis? (2) Why did you define \"bad\" the way you did? (3) What would change your recommendation? (4) What would you do with another week?", "model": ""}]}, {"id": "03-voice-mocks", "title": "Voice mock prompts", "minutes": 30, "summary": "Paste one of these into a voice assistant (ChatGPT or Claude voice mode) on your phone, and practise out loud. Then send me the summary it gives you.", "lesson": "", "date": "", "interview": false, "part": "", "blocks": [{"type": "md", "text": "## How to use these\n\n1. Open a voice-capable assistant on your **personal** phone. Paste one prompt, then switch to voice.\n2. Treat it as real: no notes beyond a pen and paper. Speak your structure out loud.\n3. At the end, ask it for the written feedback summary the prompt requests, then paste that into chat with me, together with anything you felt stuck on.\n\nThese are for **practice only**. In the real interviews, AI tools aren't allowed in any live round.\n\nEach prompt uses a generic lender. If you want company-specific practice, add the company's name and products yourself in the first line.\n\n## Mock 1: hiring-manager case (30 minutes)\n\n```\nYou are a hiring manager for a Senior Analyst, Risk & Analytics role at a buy-now-pay-later lender (Pay in 4 and monthly installment loans, merchant-funded 0% APR promotions, a partner-bank model). Interview me with ONE business case for about 25 minutes, then 5 minutes of feedback. There is no SQL or coding: I have pen, paper and a calculator, so the case should test structure, back-of-the-envelope maths and unit economics.\n\nRules:\n- Pick one case at random from: approval rate fell and growth wants it back; a large merchant wants a lower fee; delinquency rose last month; should we launch longer terms; should repeat customers get higher limits; first-payment default spiked at one merchant.\n- Give me only the opening question. Expect me to ask clarifying questions first; answer them briefly. Reveal data only when I ask for something specific, and make the numbers realistic, round and internally consistent.\n- Be collaborative, like a colleague working the problem with me, but don't do the thinking for me.\n- Ask one question at a time. Push back on vague answers (\"how would you size that?\", \"what would change your mind?\"). Don't teach or hint while the case is running.\n- If I go quiet for long, ask \"what would you look at first?\"\n- At the end, give feedback in writing under these headings, scoring each 1 to 5: Clarifying questions (did they change my approach?), Structure, Use of numbers (units, sanity checks), Business judgement, Recommendation (did I commit and size it?), Risk and monitoring, Communication. Then list the three most important things to improve, with an example of a better sentence for each.\n```\n\n## Mock 2: behavioural (30 minutes)\n\nRun it twice: once as below, once replacing the first line's interviewer with \"a cross-functional partner (a product manager or credit policy lead) who would work closely with this analyst\", which shifts the questions toward collaboration and communicating with non-analysts.\n\n```\nYou are interviewing me for a Senior Analyst, Risk & Analytics role at a consumer lending fintech. I'm an economist moving from a large bank, where I lead stress-testing scenario design, manage two economists and led a migration from SAS to R with CI/CD. Ask me five behavioural questions, one at a time, chosen from: influencing without authority; a decision with incomplete data; a mistake and what I changed; disagreeing with a senior stakeholder; delivering under a tight deadline; developing someone on my team; why move from a bank to fintech; tell me about yourself.\n- After each answer, ask one probing follow-up (what exactly did YOU do, what was the measurable result, what would you do differently).\n- Don't give feedback until the end.\n- At the end, for each answer: did it follow STAR (situation, task, action, result) with a learning? Was it under about two minutes? Was the result specific? Score each 1 to 5, then give me the two answers most worth rewriting and why.\n```\n\n## Mock 3: VP-style live case (30 minutes)\n\n```\nYou are a VP of Risk & Analytics at a buy-now-pay-later lender, interviewing a senior analyst candidate in a final round. You care about business judgement more than technique. There's no SQL; I have pen, paper and a calculator. Ask me one broad strategic question, chosen from: how should we balance growth and credit losses next year; should we enter a new merchant category (e.g. travel or healthcare); what would a recession do to our book and what should we do now; how would you know if our underwriting model is degrading; we're asked to cut losses by 20% without cutting volume more than 5%, how.\n- Let me structure it, then challenge my priorities (\"if you could only do one thing?\", \"what wouldn't you do?\", \"how confident are you, and why?\").\n- Interrupt occasionally the way a busy executive would, and ask for the answer in one sentence at least once.\n- At the end, give written feedback: Did I answer the question asked? Was I decisive? Did I show I understood how the business makes money? Score each 1 to 5, and give me the one habit that would most improve my executive communication.\n```\n\n## Mock 4: take-home readout (after the mock take-home)\n\n```\nYou are a senior member of a Risk & Analytics team at a buy-now-pay-later lender, and I'm presenting my take-home to you as an executive readout. My slides are pasted below as text. First, let me present for up to 10 minutes without interrupting, then ask me questions for 15 minutes as an executive would: what decision should we make, how confident am I, my definitions and population choices, how I handled data anomalies, the weakest assumption, alternative explanations, how I validated the numbers, how I used AI, and what I'd do with more time. Challenge at least two conclusions directly. At the end, tell me in writing: was the answer clear from my first slide? Which answers were convincing, which were weak, and what should I have said instead?\n\n[paste slides here]\n```"}]}, {"id": "04-behavioural-stories", "title": "Behavioural stories", "minutes": 30, "summary": "Seven stories, written once in STAR-L form, that between them answer almost any behavioural question.", "lesson": "", "date": "", "interview": false, "part": "", "blocks": [{"type": "md", "text": "## How to build the bank\n\nWrite each story in **STAR-L**: Situation (one sentence), Task (your responsibility), Action (most of the words, and \"I\", not \"we\"), Result (a number if you have one), Learning (what you now do differently). Aim for about two minutes spoken, which is roughly 250 words.\n\nThen practise **saying** them, not reading them. The written version is the skeleton; the spoken one should sound like you.\n\nA good story bank has six or seven stories that each cover several themes. Suggested slots, given your background:\n\n| Story | Themes it can answer |\n|---|---|\n| 1. Leading the SAS to R migration | changing how a team works; influencing without authority; technical leadership; a project you're proud of |\n| 2. Defending a scenario under challenge | disagreeing with a senior stakeholder; communicating to executives or regulators; judgement |\n| 3. A call with incomplete data | ambiguity; prioritisation; decisiveness |\n| 4. A mistake or a miss | failure; ownership; what you changed afterwards |\n| 5. Developing someone on your team | management; coaching; delegation |\n| 6. Bringing in AI tools | initiative; learning fast; driving adoption; risk awareness |\n| 7. Working with a non-analyst partner | cross-functional collaboration; translating analysis; changing your view because of a partner's input |\n\nOne round is with a **cross-functional partner** (product, policy, finance or operations), so story 7 matters: they want to know whether they'd enjoy working with you and could act on what you tell them."}, {"type": "explain", "id": "beh-pitch", "prompt": "**Tell me about yourself** (about 90 seconds). Past → present → why this role. End on why risk analytics at a lender is the natural next step, and connect your macro/scenario work to loan-level credit decisions.", "model": ""}, {"type": "explain", "id": "beh-story-1", "prompt": "**Story 1: changing how a team works.** STAR-L.", "model": ""}, {"type": "explain", "id": "beh-story-2", "prompt": "**Story 2: defending your work to a senior or sceptical audience.** STAR-L.", "model": ""}, {"type": "explain", "id": "beh-story-3", "prompt": "**Story 3: a decision with incomplete data.** STAR-L.", "model": ""}, {"type": "explain", "id": "beh-story-4", "prompt": "**Story 4: a mistake, and what you changed.** STAR-L. Pick a real one with a real consequence; \"I worked too hard\" isn't a mistake.", "model": ""}, {"type": "explain", "id": "beh-story-5", "prompt": "**Story 5: developing someone on your team.** STAR-L.", "model": ""}, {"type": "explain", "id": "beh-story-6", "prompt": "**Story 6: bringing in something new (AI tools).** STAR-L.", "model": ""}, {"type": "explain", "id": "beh-story-7", "prompt": "**Story 7: working with a non-analyst partner.** STAR-L. Show how you made the analysis usable for them, and one point where their input changed your view.", "model": ""}, {"type": "explain", "id": "beh-company", "prompt": "**Know the company.** From its \"how it works\" page and recent news: in three bullets, how it makes money, one recent development, and why it interests you. Then one sentence linking it to your experience.", "model": ""}, {"type": "explain", "id": "beh-why", "prompt": "**Why move from a bank to a fintech lender, and why risk analytics?** Under 60 seconds. Positive about where you're going, not negative about where you are.", "model": ""}, {"type": "explain", "id": "beh-questions", "prompt": "**Your questions for them.** Write three, one per round type: one about the team's work (e.g. how analysts and strategy partner on policy changes), one about how they measure success in the first six months, one about the business (e.g. how the team thinks about growth versus losses this year).", "model": ""}, {"type": "interview", "title": "", "text": "Common follow-ups to rehearse for every story: \"What exactly did **you** do?\", \"What was the measurable result?\", \"What would you do differently?\", \"What did the other person think?\". If a story can't survive those four, pick a different one."}]}], "problems": [{"type": "problem", "id": "t1-01", "title": "Loans by status", "tier": 1, "minutes": 3, "topics": ["group by"], "ordered": true, "digits": 2, "prompt": "How many loans are in each status? Sort from the most common status to the least.", "columns": "`status`, `n_loans`", "clarify": "", "hints": [], "solution": "SELECT status, COUNT(*) AS n_loans\nFROM loans\nGROUP BY status\nORDER BY n_loans DESC", "alts": [], "explain": "Grain: one row per status. `ORDER BY` can use the `SELECT` alias because it runs after `SELECT`.", "starter": "", "kind": "write"}, {"type": "problem", "id": "t1-02", "title": "Ten largest loans of 2025", "tier": 1, "minutes": 4, "topics": ["order by", "dates"], "ordered": true, "digits": 2, "prompt": "List the 10 largest loans (by principal) originated in 2025, largest first. If two loans have the same principal, list the lower `loan_id` first.", "columns": "`loan_id`, `originated_date`, `principal`", "clarify": "", "hints": ["Filter with a half-open range: `>= DATE '2025-01-01' AND < DATE '2026-01-01'`.", "Sort by two columns, `principal DESC, loan_id`, then `LIMIT 10`."], "solution": "SELECT loan_id, originated_date, principal\nFROM loans\nWHERE originated_date >= DATE '2025-01-01'\n  AND originated_date < DATE '2026-01-01'\nORDER BY principal DESC, loan_id\nLIMIT 10", "alts": [], "explain": "The tie-breaker makes the result deterministic. Without it, two runs could return different rows at the boundary.", "starter": "", "kind": "write"}, {"type": "problem", "id": "t1-03", "title": "How much income is missing?", "tier": 1, "minutes": 4, "topics": ["null", "aggregation"], "ordered": false, "digits": 3, "prompt": "What share of borrower accounts didn't report an income? Return the total number of accounts, the number with no income, and the share as a decimal (e.g. 0.123).", "columns": "`n_accounts`, `n_missing_income`, `share_missing`", "clarify": "", "hints": ["`COUNT(*)` counts rows; `COUNT(annual_income)` counts non-NULL incomes.", "Watch out for integer division when computing the share."], "solution": "SELECT\n  COUNT(*) AS n_accounts,\n  COUNT(*) - COUNT(annual_income) AS n_missing_income,\n  1.0 * (COUNT(*) - COUNT(annual_income)) / COUNT(*) AS share_missing\nFROM borrowers", "alts": ["SELECT\n  COUNT(*) AS n_accounts,\n  SUM(CASE WHEN annual_income IS NULL THEN 1 ELSE 0 END) AS n_missing_income,\n  AVG(CASE WHEN annual_income IS NULL THEN 1.0 ELSE 0 END) AS share_missing\nFROM borrowers"], "explain": "`COUNT(*) - COUNT(col)` is a neat idiom for counting NULLs. In PostgreSQL the share needs `1.0 *`, because both counts are integers.", "starter": "", "kind": "write"}, {"type": "problem", "id": "t1-04", "title": "Product mix", "tier": 1, "minutes": 3, "topics": ["group by"], "ordered": false, "digits": 2, "prompt": "For each product, return the number of loans, total principal, and average principal (rounded to 2 decimals).", "columns": "`product`, `n_loans`, `total_principal`, `avg_principal`", "clarify": "", "hints": [], "solution": "SELECT\n  product,\n  COUNT(*) AS n_loans,\n  SUM(principal) AS total_principal,\n  ROUND(AVG(principal), 2) AS avg_principal\nFROM loans\nGROUP BY product", "alts": [], "explain": "", "starter": "", "kind": "write"}, {"type": "problem", "id": "t1-05", "title": "Successful payments by method", "tier": 1, "minutes": 5, "topics": ["null", "count distinct"], "ordered": false, "digits": 2, "prompt": "How many distinct successful payments were made with each payment method? Show missing methods as `'unknown'`. Remember that some payment rows were loaded twice.", "columns": "`payment_method`, `n_payments`", "clarify": "", "hints": ["`COALESCE(payment_method, 'unknown')` in both `SELECT` and `GROUP BY`.", "Count distinct `payment_id`s rather than rows."], "solution": "SELECT\n  COALESCE(payment_method, 'unknown') AS payment_method,\n  COUNT(DISTINCT payment_id) AS n_payments\nFROM payments\nWHERE status = 'succeeded'\nGROUP BY COALESCE(payment_method, 'unknown')", "alts": [], "explain": "`COUNT(DISTINCT payment_id)` handles the duplicate rows without a separate de-duplication step. That's fine for counting; for summing amounts you'd de-duplicate first.", "starter": "", "kind": "write"}, {"type": "problem", "id": "t1-06", "title": "Applications by score band", "tier": 1, "minutes": 5, "topics": ["case", "null"], "ordered": false, "digits": 2, "prompt": "Count applications by credit score band: `'<600'`, `'600-659'`, `'660-719'`, `'720+'`, and `'no score'` when the score is missing.", "columns": "`score_band`, `n_applications`", "clarify": "", "hints": ["Test for NULL first in the `CASE`. Otherwise a NULL score falls through to the `ELSE`."], "solution": "SELECT\n  CASE\n    WHEN credit_score IS NULL THEN 'no score'\n    WHEN credit_score < 600 THEN '<600'\n    WHEN credit_score < 660 THEN '600-659'\n    WHEN credit_score < 720 THEN '660-719'\n    ELSE '720+'\n  END AS score_band,\n  COUNT(*) AS n_applications\nFROM applications\nGROUP BY 1", "alts": [], "explain": "If the `CASE` ended in `ELSE '720+'` with no NULL branch, every unscored application would be labelled 720+. A silent and very wrong result.", "starter": "", "kind": "write"}, {"type": "problem", "id": "t1-07", "title": "States with the most accounts", "tier": 1, "minutes": 3, "topics": ["having"], "ordered": true, "digits": 2, "prompt": "Which states have more than 300 borrower accounts? Sort by number of accounts, highest first.", "columns": "`state`, `n_accounts`", "clarify": "", "hints": [], "solution": "SELECT state, COUNT(*) AS n_accounts\nFROM borrowers\nGROUP BY state\nHAVING COUNT(*) > 300\nORDER BY n_accounts DESC", "alts": [], "explain": "", "starter": "", "kind": "write"}, {"type": "problem", "id": "t1-08", "title": "Interest-free share by term", "tier": 1, "minutes": 5, "topics": ["conditional aggregation"], "ordered": false, "digits": 3, "prompt": "For **monthly** loans, what share are interest-free (APR of 0) at each term length?", "columns": "`term_months`, `n_loans`, `zero_apr_share`", "clarify": "", "hints": ["The share is the average of a 0/1 flag: `AVG(CASE WHEN apr = 0 THEN 1.0 ELSE 0 END)`."], "solution": "SELECT\n  term_months,\n  COUNT(*) AS n_loans,\n  AVG(CASE WHEN apr = 0 THEN 1.0 ELSE 0 END) AS zero_apr_share\nFROM loans\nWHERE product = 'monthly'\nGROUP BY term_months", "alts": [], "explain": "24-month loans are never interest-free here: 0% promotions only apply up to 12 months.", "starter": "", "kind": "write"}, {"type": "problem", "id": "t2-01", "title": "Principal by merchant category (cleaned)", "tier": 2, "minutes": 7, "topics": ["joins", "data cleaning"], "ordered": false, "digits": 2, "prompt": "Merchant categories are inconsistently typed: some are upper case, lower case, or have trailing spaces, and a couple are missing. Report total loans and principal by **cleaned** category: lower-case and trimmed, with missing categories shown as `'unknown'`.", "columns": "`category`, `n_loans`, `total_principal`", "clarify": "- *Should 'Home & Furniture' and 'HOME & FURNITURE' be one category?* Yes, that's the point of cleaning.\n- *What casing should the output use?* Lower case is fine.", "hints": ["Look at `SELECT DISTINCT category FROM merchants` first.", "`COALESCE(LOWER(TRIM(m.category)), 'unknown')`. The order of the functions doesn't matter much, but the `COALESCE` must be outermost."], "solution": "SELECT\n  COALESCE(LOWER(TRIM(m.category)), 'unknown') AS category,\n  COUNT(*) AS n_loans,\n  SUM(l.principal) AS total_principal\nFROM loans l\nJOIN merchants m\n  ON m.merchant_id = l.merchant_id\nGROUP BY COALESCE(LOWER(TRIM(m.category)), 'unknown')", "alts": [], "explain": "In an interview, cleaning inline like this is fine. In production you'd fix it upstream or keep a mapping table, and it's worth saying so.", "starter": "", "kind": "write"}, {"type": "problem", "id": "t2-02", "title": "Approval rate by score band", "tier": 2, "minutes": 6, "topics": ["case", "conditional aggregation"], "ordered": false, "digits": 3, "prompt": "What's the approval rate in each credit score band? Use the bands `'<600'`, `'600-659'`, `'660-719'`, `'720+'` and `'no score'`.", "columns": "`score_band`, `n_applications`, `approval_rate`", "clarify": "- *Application-level or borrower-level?* Application-level: every application counts, including quick retries.", "hints": ["Same `CASE` as the score band warm-up, plus `AVG(CASE WHEN decision = 'approved' THEN 1.0 ELSE 0 END)`."], "solution": "SELECT\n  CASE\n    WHEN credit_score IS NULL THEN 'no score'\n    WHEN credit_score < 600 THEN '<600'\n    WHEN credit_score < 660 THEN '600-659'\n    WHEN credit_score < 720 THEN '660-719'\n    ELSE '720+'\n  END AS score_band,\n  COUNT(*) AS n_applications,\n  AVG(CASE WHEN decision = 'approved' THEN 1.0 ELSE 0 END) AS approval_rate\nFROM applications\nGROUP BY 1", "alts": [], "explain": "", "starter": "", "kind": "write"}, {"type": "problem", "id": "t2-03", "title": "Average loan size by state", "tier": 2, "minutes": 5, "topics": ["joins"], "ordered": false, "digits": 2, "prompt": "For each borrower state, how many loans were made and what was the average principal?", "columns": "`state`, `n_loans`, `avg_principal`", "clarify": "", "hints": [], "solution": "SELECT\n  b.state,\n  COUNT(*) AS n_loans,\n  AVG(l.principal) AS avg_principal\nFROM loans l\nJOIN borrowers b\n  ON b.borrower_id = l.borrower_id\nGROUP BY b.state", "alts": [], "explain": "Loans → borrowers is many-to-one, so the join doesn't change the grain, and `COUNT(*)` still counts loans.", "starter": "", "kind": "write"}, {"type": "problem", "id": "t2-04", "title": "Merchants with no 2025 loans", "tier": 2, "minutes": 6, "topics": ["anti join", "dates"], "ordered": false, "digits": 2, "prompt": "Which merchants that were onboarded **before 2025** had **no loans originated in 2025**?", "columns": "`merchant_id`, `merchant_name`", "clarify": "", "hints": ["That's an anti join with a date condition inside it.", "`WHERE NOT EXISTS (SELECT 1 FROM loans l WHERE l.merchant_id = m.merchant_id AND l.originated_date >= DATE '2025-01-01')`."], "solution": "SELECT m.merchant_id, m.merchant_name\nFROM merchants m\nWHERE m.onboarded_date < DATE '2025-01-01'\n  AND NOT EXISTS (\n    SELECT 1\n    FROM loans l\n    WHERE l.merchant_id = m.merchant_id\n      AND l.originated_date >= DATE '2025-01-01'\n  )", "alts": ["SELECT m.merchant_id, m.merchant_name\nFROM merchants m\nLEFT JOIN loans l\n  ON l.merchant_id = m.merchant_id\n AND l.originated_date >= DATE '2025-01-01'\nWHERE m.onboarded_date < DATE '2025-01-01'\n  AND l.loan_id IS NULL"], "explain": "In the LEFT JOIN version, the 2025 condition **must** be in `ON`. In `WHERE` it would contradict `l.loan_id IS NULL` and return nothing.", "starter": "", "kind": "write"}, {"type": "problem", "id": "t2-05", "title": "Repeat borrowers", "tier": 2, "minutes": 6, "topics": ["cte", "conditional aggregation"], "ordered": false, "digits": 3, "prompt": "Among borrower accounts with at least one loan, what share have two or more loans?", "columns": "`n_borrowers`, `n_repeat`, `repeat_share`", "clarify": "- *Accounts or people?* Accounts (`borrower_id`). Ignore duplicate accounts for now.", "hints": ["First one row per borrower with their loan count (CTE), then aggregate that."], "solution": "WITH per_borrower AS (\n  SELECT borrower_id, COUNT(*) AS n_loans\n  FROM loans\n  GROUP BY borrower_id\n)\nSELECT\n  COUNT(*) AS n_borrowers,\n  SUM(CASE WHEN n_loans >= 2 THEN 1 ELSE 0 END) AS n_repeat,\n  AVG(CASE WHEN n_loans >= 2 THEN 1.0 ELSE 0 END) AS repeat_share\nFROM per_borrower", "alts": [], "explain": "", "starter": "", "kind": "write"}, {"type": "problem", "id": "t2-06", "title": "Lent vs collected by origination year", "tier": 2, "minutes": 10, "topics": ["fan-out", "cte", "duplicates"], "ordered": false, "digits": 2, "prompt": "For each origination year, return the total principal lent and the total amount collected so far from those loans: successful payments only, with each payment counted once.", "columns": "`orig_year`, `total_principal`, `total_collected`", "clarify": "- *Collected when?* Any time up to the snapshot. Group by when the **loan** was originated, not when the payment was made.", "hints": ["Joining `loans` to `payments` and summing `principal` will inflate it. Why?", "Aggregate de-duplicated, successful payments to one row per loan first, then join to `loans`."], "solution": "WITH collected AS (\n  SELECT loan_id, SUM(amount) AS collected\n  FROM (SELECT DISTINCT * FROM payments) p\n  WHERE status = 'succeeded'\n  GROUP BY loan_id\n)\nSELECT\n  EXTRACT(YEAR FROM l.originated_date) AS orig_year,\n  SUM(l.principal) AS total_principal,\n  SUM(c.collected) AS total_collected\nFROM loans l\nLEFT JOIN collected c\n  ON c.loan_id = l.loan_id\nGROUP BY EXTRACT(YEAR FROM l.originated_date)", "alts": [], "explain": "This is the classic fan-out question. Saying \"I'll aggregate payments to the loan grain first so principal isn't repeated\" is exactly what the interviewer wants to hear. 2023 and 2024 collected more than they lent (interest), while 2025 loans are still being repaid.", "starter": "", "kind": "write"}, {"type": "problem", "id": "t2-07", "title": "People with more than one account", "tier": 2, "minutes": 7, "topics": ["data cleaning", "having"], "ordered": false, "digits": 2, "prompt": "Some people opened a second borrower account with the same email typed differently (case or stray spaces). Find them: for each normalized email with more than one account, return the email, the number of accounts, and the lowest `borrower_id`.", "columns": "`email_normalized`, `n_accounts`, `first_borrower_id`", "clarify": "", "hints": ["Normalize with `LOWER(TRIM(email))`, group by it, keep groups with `COUNT(*) > 1`."], "solution": "SELECT\n  LOWER(TRIM(email)) AS email_normalized,\n  COUNT(*) AS n_accounts,\n  MIN(borrower_id) AS first_borrower_id\nFROM borrowers\nGROUP BY LOWER(TRIM(email))\nHAVING COUNT(*) > 1", "alts": [], "explain": "This is the input to any \"unique customers\" metric: map each `borrower_id` to the first account of its person, and count those.", "starter": "", "kind": "write"}, {"type": "problem", "id": "t2-08", "title": "Merchants with above-average loan sizes", "tier": 2, "minutes": 8, "topics": ["subquery", "having"], "ordered": false, "digits": 2, "prompt": "Which merchants with at least 20 loans have an average loan principal **above the overall average** loan principal?", "columns": "`merchant_id`, `merchant_name`, `n_loans`, `avg_principal`", "clarify": "- *Overall average of what?* Of all loans (not the average of merchant averages).", "hints": ["Group loans by merchant, keep groups with `COUNT(*) >= 20` and `AVG(principal) > (SELECT AVG(principal) FROM loans)`."], "solution": "SELECT\n  m.merchant_id,\n  m.merchant_name,\n  COUNT(*) AS n_loans,\n  AVG(l.principal) AS avg_principal\nFROM loans l\nJOIN merchants m\n  ON m.merchant_id = l.merchant_id\nGROUP BY m.merchant_id, m.merchant_name\nHAVING COUNT(*) >= 20\n   AND AVG(l.principal) > (SELECT AVG(principal) FROM loans)", "alts": [], "explain": "\"Average of all loans\" and \"average of merchant averages\" differ, because big merchants weigh more in the first. That's a good clarifying question to ask out loud.", "starter": "", "kind": "write"}, {"type": "problem", "id": "t2-09", "title": "Payment failure rate by method", "tier": 2, "minutes": 6, "topics": ["conditional aggregation", "duplicates"], "ordered": false, "digits": 3, "prompt": "What share of payment attempts fail, by payment method? Count each `payment_id` once. Show missing methods as `'unknown'`.", "columns": "`payment_method`, `n_attempts`, `failure_rate`", "clarify": "", "hints": [], "solution": "SELECT\n  COALESCE(payment_method, 'unknown') AS payment_method,\n  COUNT(*) AS n_attempts,\n  AVG(CASE WHEN status = 'failed' THEN 1.0 ELSE 0 END) AS failure_rate\nFROM (SELECT DISTINCT * FROM payments) p\nGROUP BY COALESCE(payment_method, 'unknown')", "alts": [], "explain": "", "starter": "", "kind": "write"}, {"type": "problem", "id": "t2-10", "title": "First installment never paid", "tier": 2, "minutes": 8, "topics": ["anti join", "composite key"], "ordered": false, "digits": 2, "prompt": "How many **monthly** loans have a first installment that was due before 2025-12-31 but has **no successful payment at all**?", "columns": "`n_loans`", "clarify": "", "hints": ["Start from `installments` where `installment_number = 1`, join `loans` to filter the product.", "Anti join to `payments` on **both** `loan_id` and `installment_number`, with `status = 'succeeded'` inside the subquery."], "solution": "SELECT COUNT(*) AS n_loans\nFROM installments i\nJOIN loans l\n  ON l.loan_id = i.loan_id\nWHERE l.product = 'monthly'\n  AND i.installment_number = 1\n  AND i.due_date < DATE '2025-12-31'\n  AND NOT EXISTS (\n    SELECT 1\n    FROM payments p\n    WHERE p.loan_id = i.loan_id\n      AND p.installment_number = i.installment_number\n      AND p.status = 'succeeded'\n  )", "alts": [], "explain": "The `status = 'succeeded'` condition must be inside the `NOT EXISTS`: \"no successful payment\" is not the same as \"no payment\" (many of these loans have failed attempts).", "starter": "", "kind": "write"}, {"type": "problem", "id": "t3-01", "title": "Most recent payment per loan", "tier": 3, "minutes": 8, "topics": ["row_number"], "ordered": false, "digits": 2, "prompt": "For loans originated in **November 2025**, return each loan's most recent **successful** payment: when it was made and how much. Loans with no successful payment can be left out.", "columns": "`loan_id`, `last_paid_at`, `amount`", "clarify": "", "hints": ["`ROW_NUMBER() OVER (PARTITION BY loan_id ORDER BY paid_at DESC)` in a CTE, then keep `rn = 1`.", "Filter to successful payments and November loans inside the CTE."], "solution": "WITH ranked AS (\n  SELECT\n    p.loan_id,\n    p.paid_at,\n    p.amount,\n    ROW_NUMBER() OVER (PARTITION BY p.loan_id ORDER BY p.paid_at DESC, p.payment_id DESC) AS rn\n  FROM payments p\n  JOIN loans l\n    ON l.loan_id = p.loan_id\n  WHERE p.status = 'succeeded'\n    AND l.originated_date >= DATE '2025-11-01'\n    AND l.originated_date < DATE '2025-12-01'\n)\nSELECT loan_id, paid_at AS last_paid_at, amount\nFROM ranked\nWHERE rn = 1", "alts": [], "explain": "`MAX(paid_at)` gives the time but not the amount. Getting the whole row is what `ROW_NUMBER` is for. Duplicate payment rows don't matter here, because they're identical and you keep only one.", "starter": "", "kind": "write"}, {"type": "problem", "id": "t3-02", "title": "Month-over-month origination growth", "tier": 3, "minutes": 10, "topics": ["lag", "date_trunc"], "ordered": true, "digits": 3, "prompt": "For every month from January 2024 to December 2025, return the number of loans originated and the percentage change from the previous month (as a decimal). Sort by month.", "columns": "`month` (first day of the month), `n_loans`, `pct_change`", "clarify": "- *What's January 2024's change?* Compared with December 2023, which is outside the requested range. Either NULL or using December 2023 is defensible; here, use December 2023 so January has a real value.", "hints": ["Compute monthly counts for **all** months in a CTE, apply `LAG`, and only then filter to 2024–2025, so January 2024 can see December 2023.", "Use `DATE_TRUNC('month', ...)`, not `EXTRACT(MONTH ...)`, which would merge years."], "solution": "WITH monthly AS (\n  SELECT\n    CAST(DATE_TRUNC('month', originated_date) AS DATE) AS month,\n    COUNT(*) AS n_loans\n  FROM loans\n  GROUP BY CAST(DATE_TRUNC('month', originated_date) AS DATE)\n),\nwith_change AS (\n  SELECT\n    month,\n    n_loans,\n    1.0 * (n_loans - LAG(n_loans) OVER (ORDER BY month))\n      / LAG(n_loans) OVER (ORDER BY month) AS pct_change\n  FROM monthly\n)\nSELECT month, n_loans, pct_change\nFROM with_change\nWHERE month >= DATE '2024-01-01'\nORDER BY month", "alts": [], "explain": "The window runs in the `SELECT` step, after `WHERE`. If you filtered to 2024+ in the same query, `LAG` for January 2024 would be NULL. Computing the window first and filtering afterwards is a pattern worth remembering.", "starter": "", "kind": "write"}, {"type": "problem", "id": "t3-03", "title": "Cumulative principal in 2025", "tier": 3, "minutes": 6, "topics": ["running total"], "ordered": true, "digits": 2, "prompt": "For 2025, return each month's principal originated and the cumulative principal for the year to date. Sort by month.", "columns": "`month` (first day of the month), `principal`, `cumulative_principal`", "clarify": "", "hints": [], "solution": "WITH monthly AS (\n  SELECT\n    CAST(DATE_TRUNC('month', originated_date) AS DATE) AS month,\n    SUM(principal) AS principal\n  FROM loans\n  WHERE originated_date >= DATE '2025-01-01'\n  GROUP BY CAST(DATE_TRUNC('month', originated_date) AS DATE)\n)\nSELECT\n  month,\n  principal,\n  SUM(principal) OVER (ORDER BY month) AS cumulative_principal\nFROM monthly\nORDER BY month", "alts": [], "explain": "", "starter": "", "kind": "write"}, {"type": "problem", "id": "t3-04", "title": "Time to second loan", "tier": 3, "minutes": 8, "topics": ["row_number", "lead", "dates"], "ordered": false, "digits": 1, "prompt": "For borrowers with at least two loans, what is the average number of days between their **first** and **second** loan?", "columns": "`n_borrowers`, `avg_days_to_second`", "clarify": "- *Same-day loans?* Order by `originated_date`, then `loan_id`. A second loan on the same day counts as 0 days.", "hints": ["Number each borrower's loans and get the next loan's date with `LEAD`, both over the same window.", "Keep `rn = 1` rows where the next date isn't NULL."], "solution": "WITH ordered AS (\n  SELECT\n    borrower_id,\n    originated_date,\n    ROW_NUMBER() OVER (PARTITION BY borrower_id ORDER BY originated_date, loan_id) AS rn,\n    LEAD(originated_date) OVER (PARTITION BY borrower_id ORDER BY originated_date, loan_id) AS next_date\n  FROM loans\n)\nSELECT\n  COUNT(*) AS n_borrowers,\n  AVG(next_date - originated_date) AS avg_days_to_second\nFROM ordered\nWHERE rn = 1\n  AND next_date IS NOT NULL", "alts": ["WITH ordered AS (\n  SELECT\n    borrower_id,\n    originated_date,\n    ROW_NUMBER() OVER (PARTITION BY borrower_id ORDER BY originated_date, loan_id) AS rn\n  FROM loans\n)\nSELECT\n  COUNT(*) AS n_borrowers,\n  AVG(s.originated_date - f.originated_date) AS avg_days_to_second\nFROM ordered f\nJOIN ordered s\n  ON s.borrower_id = f.borrower_id\n AND s.rn = 2\nWHERE f.rn = 1"], "explain": "The self-join version (first loan joined to second loan) is a perfectly good answer, and some people find it easier to reason about.", "starter": "", "kind": "write"}, {"type": "problem", "id": "t3-05", "title": "Top two merchants in each category", "tier": 3, "minutes": 10, "topics": ["row_number", "data cleaning"], "ordered": false, "digits": 2, "prompt": "Within each **cleaned** merchant category (lower-case, trimmed), find the two merchants with the highest total principal. Exclude merchants whose category is missing. Break ties with the lower `merchant_id`.", "columns": "`category`, `merchant_id`, `total_principal`", "clarify": "", "hints": ["Step 1: total principal per merchant with its cleaned category. Step 2: `ROW_NUMBER()` partitioned by category. Step 3: `rn <= 2`."], "solution": "WITH merchant_totals AS (\n  SELECT\n    LOWER(TRIM(m.category)) AS category,\n    m.merchant_id,\n    SUM(l.principal) AS total_principal\n  FROM loans l\n  JOIN merchants m\n    ON m.merchant_id = l.merchant_id\n  WHERE m.category IS NOT NULL\n  GROUP BY LOWER(TRIM(m.category)), m.merchant_id\n),\nranked AS (\n  SELECT\n    *,\n    ROW_NUMBER() OVER (PARTITION BY category ORDER BY total_principal DESC, merchant_id) AS rn\n  FROM merchant_totals\n)\nSELECT category, merchant_id, total_principal\nFROM ranked\nWHERE rn <= 2", "alts": [], "explain": "Aggregate first (one row per merchant), then rank. Ranking loans directly would give you the top two *loans* per category.", "starter": "", "kind": "write"}, {"type": "problem", "id": "t3-06", "title": "Applications by day of week", "tier": 3, "minutes": 6, "topics": ["extract", "dialects"], "ordered": true, "digits": 3, "prompt": "By day of the week of the application, return the number of applications and the approval rate. Use `EXTRACT(DOW ...)` numbering (0 = Sunday) and sort by it.", "columns": "`day_of_week`, `n_applications`, `approval_rate`", "clarify": "", "hints": [], "solution": "SELECT\n  EXTRACT(DOW FROM applied_at) AS day_of_week,\n  COUNT(*) AS n_applications,\n  AVG(CASE WHEN decision = 'approved' THEN 1.0 ELSE 0 END) AS approval_rate\nFROM applications\nGROUP BY EXTRACT(DOW FROM applied_at)\nORDER BY day_of_week", "alts": [], "explain": "Synthetic data has no weekday effect, so the rates are flat. In an interview, say how you'd check the result is plausible. Also say the numbering out loud: in MySQL, `DAYOFWEEK()` makes Sunday 1.", "starter": "", "kind": "write"}, {"type": "problem", "id": "t3-07", "title": "Category share of 2025 principal", "tier": 3, "minutes": 7, "topics": ["window over aggregate"], "ordered": false, "digits": 3, "prompt": "For loans originated in 2025, what share of total principal came from each **cleaned** merchant category (lower-case, trimmed, missing shown as `'unknown'`)? Use a window function rather than a subquery for the total.", "columns": "`category`, `principal`, `share`", "clarify": "", "hints": ["`SUM(SUM(l.principal)) OVER ()` is the grand total across all groups."], "solution": "SELECT\n  COALESCE(LOWER(TRIM(m.category)), 'unknown') AS category,\n  SUM(l.principal) AS principal,\n  SUM(l.principal) / SUM(SUM(l.principal)) OVER () AS share\nFROM loans l\nJOIN merchants m\n  ON m.merchant_id = l.merchant_id\nWHERE l.originated_date >= DATE '2025-01-01'\nGROUP BY COALESCE(LOWER(TRIM(m.category)), 'unknown')", "alts": [], "explain": "", "starter": "", "kind": "write"}, {"type": "problem", "id": "t3-08", "title": "Time from sign-up to first loan", "tier": 3, "minutes": 8, "topics": ["dates", "case", "min"], "ordered": false, "digits": 2, "prompt": "For borrower accounts that have taken at least one loan, how long after the account was created did they take their first loan? Bucket the days as `'same day'` (0), `'1-30'`, `'31-90'`, and `'91+'`, and count borrowers in each.", "columns": "`days_bucket`, `n_borrowers`", "clarify": "", "hints": ["First loan date per borrower: `MIN(originated_date)` grouped by `borrower_id`. You don't need the loan itself, just the date.", "`created_at` is a timestamp: `CAST(b.created_at AS DATE)` before subtracting."], "solution": "WITH first_loan AS (\n  SELECT borrower_id, MIN(originated_date) AS first_loan_date\n  FROM loans\n  GROUP BY borrower_id\n),\ndays AS (\n  SELECT f.first_loan_date - CAST(b.created_at AS DATE) AS days_to_first\n  FROM first_loan f\n  JOIN borrowers b\n    ON b.borrower_id = f.borrower_id\n)\nSELECT\n  CASE\n    WHEN days_to_first = 0 THEN 'same day'\n    WHEN days_to_first <= 30 THEN '1-30'\n    WHEN days_to_first <= 90 THEN '31-90'\n    ELSE '91+'\n  END AS days_bucket,\n  COUNT(*) AS n_borrowers\nFROM days\nGROUP BY 1", "alts": [], "explain": "Here `MIN()` is enough because you only need the date. When you need other columns from the first row, use `ROW_NUMBER`.", "starter": "", "kind": "write"}, {"type": "problem", "id": "t3-09", "title": "Retrying after a decline", "tier": 3, "minutes": 12, "topics": ["lead", "timestamps"], "ordered": false, "digits": 3, "prompt": "When an application is **declined**, how often does the same borrower submit **another application within 24 hours**, and how often is that next application approved?\n\nReturn the number of declined applications, the share followed by another application within 24 hours, and, among those retries, the share approved.", "columns": "`n_declined`, `retry_rate_24h`, `retry_approval_rate`", "clarify": "- *\"Next application\"?* The borrower's very next application by `applied_at`, regardless of merchant.\n- *24 hours exactly?* `next_applied_at <= applied_at + INTERVAL '24 hours'`.", "hints": ["`LEAD(applied_at)` and `LEAD(decision)` over each borrower's applications, ordered by time.", "Then filter to declined applications and use conditional aggregation. The approval rate's denominator is retries, not all declines."], "solution": "WITH seq AS (\n  SELECT\n    application_id,\n    applied_at,\n    decision,\n    LEAD(applied_at) OVER (PARTITION BY borrower_id ORDER BY applied_at, application_id) AS next_applied_at,\n    LEAD(decision)   OVER (PARTITION BY borrower_id ORDER BY applied_at, application_id) AS next_decision\n  FROM applications\n),\ndeclines AS (\n  SELECT\n    *,\n    CASE WHEN next_applied_at <= applied_at + INTERVAL '24 hours' THEN 1 ELSE 0 END AS retried\n  FROM seq\n  WHERE decision = 'declined'\n)\nSELECT\n  COUNT(*) AS n_declined,\n  AVG(1.0 * retried) AS retry_rate_24h,\n  AVG(CASE WHEN retried = 1 THEN CASE WHEN next_decision = 'approved' THEN 1.0 ELSE 0 END END) AS retry_approval_rate\nFROM declines", "alts": [], "explain": "Two windows, then the filter. As in other problems, the decline filter must come **after** `LEAD`, or the \"next application\" would be the next *declined* one. Business takeaway: about one in six declines is followed by a quick retry (usually for less money), and many of those retries are approved. That is why application-level approval rates understate approvals per customer.", "starter": "", "kind": "write"}, {"type": "problem", "id": "t4-01", "title": "30+ days past due by product", "tier": 4, "minutes": 18, "topics": ["dpd", "cte", "composite key"], "ordered": false, "digits": 3, "prompt": "As of 2025-12-31, for each product, how many loans are open, and what share of them are **30 or more days past due**?\n\nAn open loan has status `current` or `late`. Days past due = days between the snapshot and the due date of the loan's **oldest installment that was due before the snapshot and isn't fully paid**. An installment is fully paid when its successful, de-duplicated payments add up to at least `amount_due`.", "columns": "`product`, `n_open`, `n_30_plus`, `rate_30_plus`", "clarify": "- *By count or balance?* Count of loans.\n- *Partial payments?* Count toward the installment; it's paid only when the total reaches `amount_due`.", "hints": ["CTE 1: paid amount per `(loan_id, installment_number)` from de-duplicated, successful payments.", "CTE 2: per loan, `MIN(due_date)` of installments due before the snapshot whose paid amount (`COALESCE(..., 0)`) is below `amount_due`. LEFT JOIN from installments!", "Final: open loans LEFT JOIN CTE 2, DPD = snapshot − oldest due (0 if none), conditional aggregation by product."], "solution": "WITH paid AS (\n  SELECT loan_id, installment_number, SUM(amount) AS paid\n  FROM (SELECT DISTINCT * FROM payments) p\n  WHERE status = 'succeeded'\n  GROUP BY loan_id, installment_number\n),\noldest_unpaid AS (\n  SELECT i.loan_id, MIN(i.due_date) AS oldest_due\n  FROM installments i\n  LEFT JOIN paid pd\n    ON pd.loan_id = i.loan_id\n   AND pd.installment_number = i.installment_number\n  WHERE i.due_date < DATE '2025-12-31'\n    AND COALESCE(pd.paid, 0) < i.amount_due\n  GROUP BY i.loan_id\n),\nopen_loans AS (\n  SELECT\n    l.loan_id,\n    l.product,\n    COALESCE(DATE '2025-12-31' - ou.oldest_due, 0) AS dpd\n  FROM loans l\n  LEFT JOIN oldest_unpaid ou\n    ON ou.loan_id = l.loan_id\n  WHERE l.status IN ('current', 'late')\n)\nSELECT\n  product,\n  COUNT(*) AS n_open,\n  SUM(CASE WHEN dpd >= 30 THEN 1 ELSE 0 END) AS n_30_plus,\n  AVG(CASE WHEN dpd >= 30 THEN 1.0 ELSE 0 END) AS rate_30_plus\nFROM open_loans\nGROUP BY product", "alts": [], "explain": "Every piece of the course is in here: de-duplication, aggregate-then-join on a composite key, a LEFT JOIN that keeps installments with no payments, future installments excluded, `COALESCE` for loans with nothing overdue, and conditional aggregation. If time is short in an interview, write CTE 1 and 2, say what CTE 3 would do, and explain the traps.", "starter": "", "kind": "write"}, {"type": "problem", "id": "t4-02", "title": "Vintage charge-off curve", "tier": 4, "minutes": 15, "topics": ["vintage", "conditional aggregation", "dates"], "ordered": true, "digits": 3, "prompt": "Build a small vintage table. For each quarterly origination vintage with at least 12 months of history as of 2025-12-31, return the number of loans and the cumulative share charged off within **6**, **9** and **12** months of origination. Sort by vintage.", "columns": "`vintage` (first day of the quarter), `n_loans`, `co_6m`, `co_9m`, `co_12m`", "clarify": "- *\"Within 6 months\"?* `charged_off_date < originated_date + INTERVAL '6 months'`.\n- *Which vintages are mature?* Originated before 2025-01-01, so every loan has 12 months of history.", "hints": ["One row per vintage: `DATE_TRUNC('quarter', originated_date)`.", "Three `AVG(CASE WHEN charged_off_date < originated_date + INTERVAL '...' THEN 1.0 ELSE 0 END)` columns."], "solution": "SELECT\n  CAST(DATE_TRUNC('quarter', originated_date) AS DATE) AS vintage,\n  COUNT(*) AS n_loans,\n  AVG(CASE WHEN charged_off_date < originated_date + INTERVAL '6 months' THEN 1.0 ELSE 0 END) AS co_6m,\n  AVG(CASE WHEN charged_off_date < originated_date + INTERVAL '9 months' THEN 1.0 ELSE 0 END) AS co_9m,\n  AVG(CASE WHEN charged_off_date < originated_date + INTERVAL '12 months' THEN 1.0 ELSE 0 END) AS co_12m\nFROM loans\nWHERE originated_date < DATE '2025-01-01'\nGROUP BY CAST(DATE_TRUNC('quarter', originated_date) AS DATE)\nORDER BY vintage", "alts": [], "explain": "Read across a row to see a vintage's losses grow with age. Read down a column to compare vintages **at the same age**, the only fair comparison. The vintages after the July 2024 policy change sit visibly lower. To include younger vintages, you'd show each one only up to the age it has reached (NULL beyond it), which is how a vintage chart is built.", "starter": "", "kind": "write"}, {"type": "problem", "id": "t4-03", "title": "First payment default", "tier": 4, "minutes": 15, "topics": ["fpd", "dates", "composite key"], "ordered": true, "digits": 3, "prompt": "**First payment default (FPD30)**: a monthly loan's first installment is not fully paid within 30 days of its due date. Compute the FPD30 rate by origination quarter for monthly loans whose first installment was due **on or before 2025-12-01**, so all of them have had 30 days to pay. Sort by quarter.", "columns": "`vintage` (first day of the quarter), `n_loans`, `fpd30_rate`", "clarify": "- *Paid within 30 days?* Successful, de-duplicated payments on installment 1 with `CAST(paid_at AS DATE) <= due_date + 30` add up to at least `amount_due`.\n- *Partial payments?* They count toward the total.", "hints": ["Start from installment 1 of monthly loans with `due_date <= DATE '2025-12-01'`.", "Sum qualifying payments per loan in a CTE (joined on both keys, with the date condition), then LEFT JOIN and compare with `amount_due`."], "solution": "WITH first_inst AS (\n  SELECT i.loan_id, i.due_date, i.amount_due, l.originated_date\n  FROM installments i\n  JOIN loans l\n    ON l.loan_id = i.loan_id\n  WHERE l.product = 'monthly'\n    AND i.installment_number = 1\n    AND i.due_date <= DATE '2025-12-01'\n),\npaid_30 AS (\n  SELECT f.loan_id, SUM(p.amount) AS paid\n  FROM first_inst f\n  JOIN (SELECT DISTINCT * FROM payments) p\n    ON p.loan_id = f.loan_id\n   AND p.installment_number = 1\n  WHERE p.status = 'succeeded'\n    AND CAST(p.paid_at AS DATE) <= f.due_date + 30\n  GROUP BY f.loan_id\n)\nSELECT\n  CAST(DATE_TRUNC('quarter', f.originated_date) AS DATE) AS vintage,\n  COUNT(*) AS n_loans,\n  AVG(CASE WHEN COALESCE(pd.paid, 0) < f.amount_due THEN 1.0 ELSE 0 END) AS fpd30_rate\nFROM first_inst f\nLEFT JOIN paid_30 pd\n  ON pd.loan_id = f.loan_id\nGROUP BY CAST(DATE_TRUNC('quarter', f.originated_date) AS DATE)\nORDER BY vintage", "alts": [], "explain": "FPD is an early read on fraud and credit quality: you see it within weeks, where charge-offs take months. That's why it's used to monitor recent vintages. `due_date + 30` adds 30 days to a date in PostgreSQL and DuckDB; elsewhere use your dialect's `DATEADD`.", "starter": "", "kind": "write"}, {"type": "problem", "id": "t4-04", "title": "Dollar loss rate by score band", "tier": 4, "minutes": 12, "topics": ["joins", "conditional aggregation"], "ordered": false, "digits": 3, "prompt": "For loans originated in 2023 and 2024, compute the **dollar** loss rate by credit score band at application: charged-off principal ÷ total principal. Bands: `'<600'`, `'600-659'`, `'660-719'`, `'720+'`, `'no score'`.", "columns": "`score_band`, `n_loans`, `total_principal`, `charged_off_principal`, `loss_rate`", "clarify": "", "hints": ["The score lives on `applications`; join loans to applications on `application_id`.", "`SUM(CASE WHEN l.status = 'charged_off' THEN l.principal ELSE 0 END)`."], "solution": "SELECT\n  CASE\n    WHEN a.credit_score IS NULL THEN 'no score'\n    WHEN a.credit_score < 600 THEN '<600'\n    WHEN a.credit_score < 660 THEN '600-659'\n    WHEN a.credit_score < 720 THEN '660-719'\n    ELSE '720+'\n  END AS score_band,\n  COUNT(*) AS n_loans,\n  SUM(l.principal) AS total_principal,\n  SUM(CASE WHEN l.status = 'charged_off' THEN l.principal ELSE 0 END) AS charged_off_principal,\n  SUM(CASE WHEN l.status = 'charged_off' THEN l.principal ELSE 0 END) / SUM(l.principal) AS loss_rate\nFROM loans l\nJOIN applications a\n  ON a.application_id = l.application_id\nWHERE l.originated_date < DATE '2025-01-01'\nGROUP BY 1", "alts": [], "explain": "Using principal as the loss is a simplification (a real loss figure subtracts what was repaid before charge-off), and it's worth saying so. Restricting to 2023–2024 avoids comparing mature loans with very young ones.", "starter": "", "kind": "write"}, {"type": "problem", "id": "t4-05", "title": "Scheduled vs collected", "tier": 4, "minutes": 15, "topics": ["fan-out", "composite key", "dates"], "ordered": true, "digits": 3, "prompt": "For installments **due in 2025 before 2025-12-31**, by month due: how much was scheduled, how much of it has been collected (successful, de-duplicated payments applied to those installments, at any time up to the snapshot), and the collection rate. Sort by month.", "columns": "`due_month` (first day of the month), `scheduled`, `collected`, `collection_rate`", "clarify": "- *Can collected exceed scheduled for an installment?* Not in this data, but it's worth asking.", "hints": ["Aggregate payments to the installment grain **before** joining, or each installment's `amount_due` gets repeated once per payment.", "LEFT JOIN from installments so unpaid installments still count as scheduled."], "solution": "WITH paid AS (\n  SELECT loan_id, installment_number, SUM(amount) AS paid\n  FROM (SELECT DISTINCT * FROM payments) p\n  WHERE status = 'succeeded'\n  GROUP BY loan_id, installment_number\n)\nSELECT\n  CAST(DATE_TRUNC('month', i.due_date) AS DATE) AS due_month,\n  SUM(i.amount_due) AS scheduled,\n  SUM(COALESCE(pd.paid, 0)) AS collected,\n  SUM(COALESCE(pd.paid, 0)) / SUM(i.amount_due) AS collection_rate\nFROM installments i\nLEFT JOIN paid pd\n  ON pd.loan_id = i.loan_id\n AND pd.installment_number = i.installment_number\nWHERE i.due_date >= DATE '2025-01-01'\n  AND i.due_date < DATE '2025-12-31'\nGROUP BY CAST(DATE_TRUNC('month', i.due_date) AS DATE)\nORDER BY due_month", "alts": [], "explain": "December collects visibly less, partly because late payers haven't paid *yet*: the same maturity effect as with charge-offs. A collection rate for December is not comparable with one for June.", "starter": "", "kind": "write"}, {"type": "problem", "id": "t4-06", "title": "Do repeat loans perform better?", "tier": 4, "minutes": 15, "topics": ["row_number", "vintage"], "ordered": false, "digits": 3, "prompt": "Compare the 12-month charge-off rate of borrowers' **first** loans with their **repeat** loans (second and later), for loans originated before 2025-01-01. Charged off within 12 months means `charged_off_date < originated_date + INTERVAL '12 months'`.", "columns": "`loan_type` (`'first'` or `'repeat'`), `n_loans`, `co_rate_12m`", "clarify": "- *First loan ever, or first loan before 2025?* Ever: number each borrower's loans over their full history, **then** filter to pre-2025 originations.", "hints": ["`ROW_NUMBER() OVER (PARTITION BY borrower_id ORDER BY originated_date, loan_id)` over **all** loans in a CTE.", "Filter to `originated_date < DATE '2025-01-01'` outside the CTE."], "solution": "WITH numbered AS (\n  SELECT\n    l.*,\n    ROW_NUMBER() OVER (PARTITION BY borrower_id ORDER BY originated_date, loan_id) AS loan_seq\n  FROM loans l\n)\nSELECT\n  CASE WHEN loan_seq = 1 THEN 'first' ELSE 'repeat' END AS loan_type,\n  COUNT(*) AS n_loans,\n  AVG(CASE WHEN charged_off_date < originated_date + INTERVAL '12 months' THEN 1.0 ELSE 0 END) AS co_rate_12m\nFROM numbered\nWHERE originated_date < DATE '2025-01-01'\nGROUP BY CASE WHEN loan_seq = 1 THEN 'first' ELSE 'repeat' END", "alts": [], "explain": "Here the order doesn't actually change the answer: filtering to *before* 2025 keeps each borrower's earliest loans, so the numbering is the same either way. It **would** matter for a later window (say 2025 only). Thinking about it out loud is what counts. Duplicate accounts mean some \"first\" loans are really repeat customers. Mention it as a caveat.", "starter": "", "kind": "write"}, {"type": "problem", "id": "t4-07", "title": "Riskiest merchants", "tier": 4, "minutes": 12, "topics": ["having", "ranking"], "ordered": true, "digits": 3, "prompt": "Among merchants with **at least 40 loans** originated in 2023–2024, which five have the highest charge-off rate (share of those loans with status `charged_off`)? Break ties with the lower `merchant_id`. Sort from highest rate.", "columns": "`merchant_id`, `merchant_name`, `n_loans`, `co_rate`", "clarify": "- *Why a minimum of 40?* A merchant with 3 loans and 1 charge-off has a 33% rate that means nothing. The threshold keeps small samples out.", "hints": [], "solution": "SELECT\n  m.merchant_id,\n  m.merchant_name,\n  COUNT(*) AS n_loans,\n  AVG(CASE WHEN l.status = 'charged_off' THEN 1.0 ELSE 0 END) AS co_rate\nFROM loans l\nJOIN merchants m\n  ON m.merchant_id = l.merchant_id\nWHERE l.originated_date < DATE '2025-01-01'\nGROUP BY m.merchant_id, m.merchant_name\nHAVING COUNT(*) >= 40\nORDER BY co_rate DESC, m.merchant_id\nLIMIT 5", "alts": [], "explain": "Ranking rates without a volume floor is a classic analytics mistake; mention it even if the question already gives a threshold. Credibility-weighted rates are a step further if the interviewer asks.", "starter": "", "kind": "write"}, {"type": "problem", "id": "t4-08", "title": "Paid on time", "tier": 4, "minutes": 12, "topics": ["composite key", "dates", "fan-out"], "ordered": false, "digits": 3, "prompt": "Of installments due in **Q4 2025** (1 October to 30 December 2025), what share were paid in full **on or before their due date**? Show it by product.", "columns": "`product`, `n_installments`, `on_time_rate`", "clarify": "- *On or before the due date?* Successful, de-duplicated payments with `CAST(paid_at AS DATE) <= due_date` add up to at least `amount_due`. Later payments don't count toward \"on time\".", "hints": ["Aggregate qualifying payments per installment first: the date condition compares each payment with its installment's due date, so join installments in that CTE.", "Then LEFT JOIN that to the Q4 installments and compare with `amount_due`."], "solution": "WITH q4 AS (\n  SELECT i.loan_id, i.installment_number, i.due_date, i.amount_due, l.product\n  FROM installments i\n  JOIN loans l\n    ON l.loan_id = i.loan_id\n  WHERE i.due_date >= DATE '2025-10-01'\n    AND i.due_date < DATE '2025-12-31'\n),\npaid_on_time AS (\n  SELECT q.loan_id, q.installment_number, SUM(p.amount) AS paid\n  FROM q4 q\n  JOIN (SELECT DISTINCT * FROM payments) p\n    ON p.loan_id = q.loan_id\n   AND p.installment_number = q.installment_number\n  WHERE p.status = 'succeeded'\n    AND CAST(p.paid_at AS DATE) <= q.due_date\n  GROUP BY q.loan_id, q.installment_number\n)\nSELECT\n  q.product,\n  COUNT(*) AS n_installments,\n  AVG(CASE WHEN COALESCE(pt.paid, 0) >= q.amount_due THEN 1.0 ELSE 0 END) AS on_time_rate\nFROM q4 q\nLEFT JOIN paid_on_time pt\n  ON pt.loan_id = q.loan_id\n AND pt.installment_number = q.installment_number\nGROUP BY q.product", "alts": [], "explain": "Pay in 4's first installment is collected at checkout, so it's always on time. That flatters Pay in 4's rate. A careful analyst would mention it, or exclude installment 1 for a fairer comparison.", "starter": "", "kind": "write"}], "reference": [{"id": "00-start-here", "title": "Start here", "blocks": [{"type": "md", "text": "## How this course works\n\nYou already think in data frames. This course teaches SQL the way SQL wants to be learned. It leans on dplyr only where that genuinely speeds you up, and it says plainly where your R instincts will mislead you.\n\nEverything runs in this browser tab. A real SQL engine (DuckDB) holds a synthetic consumer-lending database (merchants, borrowers, applications, loans, payment schedules and payments) with the messiness interviewers like to test: duplicates, NULLs, many-to-one relationships, timestamps.\n\nTry it: edit the query below and press **Run** (or <kbd>Ctrl</kbd>+<kbd>Enter</kbd>).\n\n```sql\nSELECT loan_id, originated_date, product, principal, status\nFROM loans\nORDER BY principal DESC\nLIMIT 5\n```\n\nThe **Tables** button (top right) opens the schema whenever you need it, as you'd have it on an interview screen.\n\n## The path\n\n| Block | What | Time |\n|---|---|---|\n| Foundations | Lessons 0–2: how SQL thinks, filtering and NULL, aggregation | ~2 h |\n| Joins | Lessons 3–4: syntax, cardinality, fan-out, anti-joins | ~2 h |\n| Structure | Lesson 5: CTEs and subqueries | ~45 min |\n| Windows | Lessons 6–7: ranking, dedup, LAG/LEAD, running totals | ~1.5 h |\n| Time | Lesson 8: dates and timestamps | ~45 min |\n| Domain | Lesson 9: lending metrics | ~1 h |\n| Craft | Lesson 10: the interview itself | ~30 min |\n| Practice | Problem bank (mixed tiers) and 2 timed mock screens | 3–4 h |\n\nThat's roughly 12 hours. If time gets short, keep the mocks and cut Tier 4 problems, not the other way round.\n\nA good rhythm per session: one or two lessons, then three or four problem-bank questions from earlier tiers. Mixing old material with new is what makes it stick.\n\n## Kinds of exercise\n\nEach lesson moves from watching to doing:\n\n- **Predict**: read a query and say what it returns *before* running it. This is how you build an accurate mental model, and it's exactly what happens when an interviewer asks \"what does this return?\"\n- **Fix the bug**: a query that runs but is wrong. Most real-world SQL errors look like this.\n- **Fill in**: a partly written query. The structure is given; you supply the key clause.\n- **Write**: a blank editor and a question. This is the interview.\n\n**Check answer** (<kbd>Ctrl</kbd>+<kbd>Shift</kbd>+<kbd>Enter</kbd>) runs your query and compares the result with the reference answer:\n\n- Row order is ignored unless the question asks for a sort.\n- Column names don't need to match, but column order does unless you used the expected names.\n- Numbers are compared to 2 decimal places unless stated otherwise.\n\nWhen you're wrong it tells you *how*: too many rows (often a fan-out), too few (often a join or NULL), or different values.\n\n**Hints** come one at a time. Use them. Revealing the solution is fine too, but if you do, redo the exercise from a blank editor the next day. The Progress page keeps a list.\n\n## The R boxes\n\nBlue boxes headed *If you know dplyr* map SQL to what you already know. They show up when the comparison helps, and they also flag the places where the analogy breaks. Those are worth reading twice.\n\n## Before your recruiter call\n\nThings worth asking, because they change how you prepare:\n\n- **Which SQL dialect?** (PostgreSQL, MySQL, Snowflake, BigQuery, SQL Server…) The [dialect sheet](#/ref/03-dialects) covers what changes, which is mostly dates.\n- **Live with an interviewer, or timed and unattended?** Which platform (CoderPad, HackerRank, CodeSignal)?\n- **How long, and how many questions?**\n- **Can you look up syntax?** Is the schema provided?\n- **Pure SQL, or SQL plus interpreting the result?**"}]}, {"id": "01-the-data", "title": "The data", "blocks": [{"type": "md", "text": "A synthetic buy-now-pay-later lender. Customers apply for credit at checkout with a merchant. If approved, they take a loan: either **Pay in 4** (four interest-free payments every two weeks, the first at checkout) or a **monthly** installment loan of 3, 6, 12 or 24 months. Every loan has a payment schedule (`installments`) and a log of payment attempts (`payments`).\n\nThe snapshot date is **2025-12-31**. Nothing in the data happens after it, but schedules extend into the future.\n\n## How the tables connect\n\n```text\nmerchants (merchant_id)\n    │ 1\n    │ many\napplications (application_id) ── borrower_id ──▶ borrowers (borrower_id)\n    │ 1\n    │ 0 or 1\nloans (loan_id) ── borrower_id, merchant_id\n    │ 1\n    │ many\ninstallments (loan_id, installment_number)\n    │ 1\n    │ many\npayments (payment_id) ── loan_id, installment_number\n```\n\n## Tables\n\n**merchants**: one row per merchant.\n\n| column | notes |\n|---|---|\n| `merchant_id` | key |\n| `merchant_name` | |\n| `category` | Electronics, Fashion, Travel… **inconsistently cased, some trailing spaces, two NULLs** |\n| `onboarded_date` | |\n| `zero_apr_eligible` | merchant can offer 0% APR promotions |\n\n**borrowers**: one row per *account*.\n\n| column | notes |\n|---|---|\n| `borrower_id` | key |\n| `email` | **about 120 people opened a second account with the same email typed differently** (case, trailing space) |\n| `created_at` | timestamp |\n| `state` | two-letter code |\n| `annual_income` | self-reported, **NULL for ~9%** |\n\n**applications**: one row per credit application.\n\n| column | notes |\n|---|---|\n| `application_id` | key |\n| `borrower_id`, `merchant_id` | |\n| `applied_at` | timestamp |\n| `requested_amount` | |\n| `credit_score` | at time of application, **NULL when no credit file was found** |\n| `decision` | `approved` / `declined`. **Declined applicants often retry within minutes for a smaller amount.** |\n\nCredit policy tightened on **2024-07-01** (higher score cutoff).\n\n**loans**: one row per loan. About 88% of approved applications become loans.\n\n| column | notes |\n|---|---|\n| `loan_id` | key |\n| `application_id`, `borrower_id`, `merchant_id` | |\n| `originated_date` | date |\n| `product` | `pay_in_4` or `monthly` |\n| `term_months` | **NULL for pay_in_4** |\n| `n_installments` | 4 for pay_in_4, otherwise the term |\n| `principal`, `apr` | `apr` is 0 for pay_in_4 and promo loans |\n| `status` | as of the snapshot: `current`, `late`, `paid_off`, `charged_off` |\n| `charged_off_date` | set when charged off: 120 days after the first missed installment |\n\n**installments**: one row per scheduled payment. Key: (`loan_id`, `installment_number`).\n\n| column | notes |\n|---|---|\n| `due_date` | **includes future due dates** (after 2025-12-31) |\n| `amount_due` | |\n\n**payments**: one row per payment attempt. *Supposedly.*\n\n| column | notes |\n|---|---|\n| `payment_id` | **not unique: a pipeline replay loaded ~700 rows twice** |\n| `loan_id`, `installment_number` | which installment the payment was applied to |\n| `paid_at` | timestamp |\n| `amount` | **partial payments exist**: one installment can take two payments |\n| `status` | `succeeded` or `failed`. **Failed attempts are not cash.** |\n| `payment_method` | `ach`, `debit_card`, **or NULL** |\n\n**loan_month**: one row per loan per month-end while the loan is open, a \"snapshot\" table. Key: (`loan_id`, `as_of_date`). Derived from `installments` and successful, de-duplicated `payments`.\n\n| column | notes |\n|---|---|\n| `as_of_date` | the month-end date of the snapshot (last day of the month) |\n| `balance` | scheduled payments not yet made, as of that date (principal plus scheduled interest) |\n| `dpd` | days past due: days since the **oldest** installment that was due and still not fully paid; 0 if nothing is overdue |\n| `status` | `open`, or `paid_off` / `charged_off` in the month the loan closed. **A loan has no rows after it closes**, and none before its origination month. |\n\n**merchant_terms**: one row per merchant, the fee the lender charges the merchant on each loan, as a share of the purchase. **Illustrative numbers for exercises, not real market rates.**\n\n| column | notes |\n|---|---|\n| `fee_rate_zero_apr` | fee on 0% APR loans (all Pay in 4, plus monthly promotions): the merchant pays for the promotion |\n| `fee_rate_interest_bearing` | fee on interest-bearing loans: lower, because the borrower pays interest |\n\n## Things a careful analyst notices"}, {"type": "interview", "title": "", "text": "Interviewers often hand you a schema like this and watch whether you ask about these before writing SQL. Saying \"I'd check whether payment_id is unique first\" earns credit even if you don't have time to do it."}, {"type": "md", "text": "1. `payments` has exact duplicate rows. Sum it naively and you overstate collections.\n2. `payments` includes failed attempts. Filter `status = 'succeeded'` for cash.\n3. Joining `loans` to `payments` or `installments` multiplies loan rows. Aggregate first, then join.\n4. One person can have two `borrower_id`s. \"Unique customers\" is not `COUNT(DISTINCT borrower_id)` without thought.\n5. `credit_score`, `annual_income`, `term_months`, `category`, `payment_method` all have NULLs, for different reasons.\n6. `paid_at`, `applied_at` and `created_at` are timestamps. `<= DATE '2025-01-31'` misses most of January 31st.\n7. Recent loans haven't had time to go bad. Compare loans of the same age.\n\n```sql\n-- Check row counts against distinct keys\nSELECT COUNT(*) AS payment_rows, COUNT(DISTINCT payment_id) AS distinct_payment_ids\nFROM payments\n```"}]}, {"id": "02-dplyr-to-sql", "title": "dplyr → SQL phrasebook", "blocks": [{"type": "md", "text": "A lookup table for when you know what you'd write in R. The SQL column is portable unless marked.\n\n## Verbs\n\n| dplyr | SQL |\n|---|---|\n| `filter(x > 1, y == \"a\")` | `WHERE x > 1 AND y = 'a'` |\n| `filter(is.na(x))` | `WHERE x IS NULL` |\n| `filter(x %in% c(\"a\", \"b\"))` | `WHERE x IN ('a', 'b')` |\n| `filter(between(x, 1, 5))` | `WHERE x BETWEEN 1 AND 5` |\n| `filter(str_detect(x, \"^Blue\"))` | `WHERE x LIKE 'Blue%'` |\n| `select(a, b)` | `SELECT a, b` |\n| `select(new = old)` / `rename()` | `SELECT old AS new` |\n| `mutate(z = x * y)` | `SELECT *, x * y AS z` |\n| `arrange(desc(x), y)` | `ORDER BY x DESC, y` |\n| `head(10)` / `slice_head(n = 10)` | `LIMIT 10` (after `ORDER BY`) |\n| `distinct(a, b)` | `SELECT DISTINCT a, b` |\n| `count(a)` | `SELECT a, COUNT(*) FROM t GROUP BY a` |\n| `group_by(a) \\|> summarise(n = n(), s = sum(x))` | `SELECT a, COUNT(*) AS n, SUM(x) AS s FROM t GROUP BY a` |\n| `summarise(...) \\|> filter(n > 5)` | `... GROUP BY a HAVING COUNT(*) > 5` |\n| `group_by(a) \\|> mutate(s = sum(x))` | `SUM(x) OVER (PARTITION BY a)` |\n| `bind_rows(x, y)` | `SELECT ... UNION ALL SELECT ...` (matched by position) |\n\n## Functions\n\n| R | SQL | Watch out |\n|---|---|---|\n| `n()` | `COUNT(*)` | |\n| `sum(!is.na(x))` | `COUNT(x)` | |\n| `n_distinct(x, na.rm = TRUE)` | `COUNT(DISTINCT x)` | plain `n_distinct()` counts NA |\n| `mean(x, na.rm = TRUE)` | `AVG(x)` | SQL always skips NULLs |\n| `mean(x == \"a\")` | `AVG(CASE WHEN x = 'a' THEN 1.0 ELSE 0 END)` | |\n| `sum(x == \"a\")` | `SUM(CASE WHEN x = 'a' THEN 1 ELSE 0 END)` | |\n| `if_else(c, a, b)` | `CASE WHEN c THEN a ELSE b END` | |\n| `case_when(c1 ~ a, c2 ~ b, .default = d)` | `CASE WHEN c1 THEN a WHEN c2 THEN b ELSE d END` | |\n| `coalesce(x, 0)` / `replace_na()` | `COALESCE(x, 0)` | |\n| `na_if(x, 0)` | `NULLIF(x, 0)` | |\n| `round(x, 2)` | `ROUND(x, 2)` | |\n| `x %/% y` | `FLOOR(x / y)` | `/` on integers truncates in Postgres/SQL Server |\n| `paste0(a, b)` | `a \\|\\| b` or `CONCAT(a, b)` | `\\|\\|` isn't string concat in MySQL or SQL Server |\n| `str_to_lower()`, `str_trim()` | `LOWER()`, `TRIM()` | |\n| `row_number()` | `ROW_NUMBER() OVER (...)` | SQL needs an explicit `ORDER BY` |\n| `min_rank()` / `dense_rank()` | `RANK()` / `DENSE_RANK()` | |\n| `lag(x)` / `lead(x)` | `LAG(x) OVER (ORDER BY ...)` | |\n| `cumsum(x)` | `SUM(x) OVER (ORDER BY ...)` | ties share a value (RANGE frame) |\n| `floor_date(d, \"month\")` | `DATE_TRUNC('month', d)` | dialect-specific |\n| `year(d)` | `EXTRACT(YEAR FROM d)` | |\n| `d + days(30)` | `d + INTERVAL '30 days'` | dialect-specific |\n| `as.numeric(d2 - d1)` | `d2 - d1` | Postgres/DuckDB; `DATEDIFF` elsewhere |\n\n## Joins\n\n| dplyr | SQL |\n|---|---|\n| `inner_join(x, y, by = join_by(id))` | `FROM x JOIN y ON y.id = x.id` |\n| `left_join(x, y, by = join_by(id))` | `FROM x LEFT JOIN y ON y.id = x.id` |\n| `full_join()` | `FULL OUTER JOIN` |\n| `semi_join(x, y, by = ...)` | `WHERE EXISTS (SELECT 1 FROM y WHERE y.id = x.id)` |\n| `anti_join(x, y, by = ...)` | `WHERE NOT EXISTS (...)` |\n| `join_by(a, b)` (two keys) | `ON y.a = x.a AND y.b = x.b` |\n| `join_by(id, date >= start)` | `ON y.id = x.id AND x.date >= y.start` |\n\n## Where the analogy breaks\n\n- **Order:** dplyr runs in written order; SQL runs `FROM → WHERE → GROUP BY → HAVING → SELECT → ORDER BY → LIMIT`.\n- **Row order:** data frames have it; tables don't. Window functions and `LIMIT` need explicit `ORDER BY`.\n- **NA in joins:** dplyr matches NA to NA by default; SQL never matches NULL keys.\n- **na.rm:** SQL aggregates always skip NULLs, silently.\n- **Many-to-many joins:** dplyr warns; SQL multiplies rows without a word.\n- **Grouping:** SQL's `GROUP BY` collapses rows and leaves no grouping behind; `group_by() |> mutate()` is a window function."}]}, {"id": "03-dialects", "title": "Dialect sheet", "blocks": [{"type": "md", "text": "This course runs on **DuckDB**, which follows **PostgreSQL** closely (plus `QUALIFY`), so the PostgreSQL column is what you've been practising. Once you know your interview dialect, read down its column and rewrite a few of the lesson queries in it.\n\n## Dates and times\n\n| Task | PostgreSQL | MySQL | Snowflake | BigQuery | SQL Server |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| Date literal | `DATE '2025-01-31'` | `DATE '2025-01-31'` | `DATE '2025-01-31'` | `DATE '2025-01-31'` | `CAST('2025-01-31' AS DATE)` |\n| Today | `CURRENT_DATE` | `CURRENT_DATE` | `CURRENT_DATE` | `CURRENT_DATE()` | `CAST(GETDATE() AS DATE)` |\n| Timestamp → date | `CAST(ts AS DATE)` | `DATE(ts)` | `CAST(ts AS DATE)` | `DATE(ts)` | `CAST(ts AS DATE)` |\n| First of month | `DATE_TRUNC('month', d)` ¹ | `DATE_FORMAT(d, '%Y-%m-01')` ² | `DATE_TRUNC('month', d)` | `DATE_TRUNC(d, MONTH)` | `DATETRUNC(month, d)` ³ |\n| Year | `EXTRACT(YEAR FROM d)` | `YEAR(d)` | `YEAR(d)` | `EXTRACT(YEAR FROM d)` | `YEAR(d)` |\n| Day of week | `EXTRACT(DOW FROM d)`, 0 = Sun | `DAYOFWEEK(d)`, **1** = Sun | `DAYOFWEEK(d)`, 0 = Sun ⁴ | `EXTRACT(DAYOFWEEK FROM d)`, **1** = Sun | `DATEPART(weekday, d)`, **1** = Sun ⁴ |\n| Add 30 days | `d + INTERVAL '30 days'` | `DATE_ADD(d, INTERVAL 30 DAY)` | `DATEADD(day, 30, d)` | `DATE_ADD(d, INTERVAL 30 DAY)` | `DATEADD(day, 30, d)` |\n| Add 1 month | `d + INTERVAL '1 month'` | `DATE_ADD(d, INTERVAL 1 MONTH)` | `DATEADD(month, 1, d)` | `DATE_ADD(d, INTERVAL 1 MONTH)` | `DATEADD(month, 1, d)` |\n| Days between | `d2 - d1` | `DATEDIFF(d2, d1)` | `DATEDIFF(day, d1, d2)` | `DATE_DIFF(d2, d1, DAY)` | `DATEDIFF(day, d1, d2)` |\n| Months between | year×12 + month formula ⁵ | `TIMESTAMPDIFF(MONTH, d1, d2)` ⁶ | `DATEDIFF(month, d1, d2)` | `DATE_DIFF(d2, d1, MONTH)` | `DATEDIFF(month, d1, d2)` |\n| Format as text | `TO_CHAR(d, 'YYYY-MM')` | `DATE_FORMAT(d, '%Y-%m')` | `TO_CHAR(d, 'YYYY-MM')` | `FORMAT_DATE('%Y-%m', d)` | `FORMAT(d, 'yyyy-MM')` |\n| Calendar of months | `generate_series(a, b, INTERVAL '1 month')` | recursive CTE | `GENERATOR` + `DATEADD` | `UNNEST(GENERATE_DATE_ARRAY(a, b, INTERVAL 1 MONTH))` | recursive CTE |\n\n1. Returns a **timestamp** in PostgreSQL; wrap in `CAST(... AS DATE)`.\n2. Returns text. For a real date: `DATE_SUB(d, INTERVAL DAY(d) - 1 DAY)`, or `CAST(DATE_FORMAT(d, '%Y-%m-01') AS DATE)`.\n3. SQL Server 2022+. Older: `DATEFROMPARTS(YEAR(d), MONTH(d), 1)`.\n4. Depends on a session setting (`WEEK_START` in Snowflake, `DATEFIRST` in SQL Server); these are the defaults.\n5. `(EXTRACT(YEAR FROM d2) - EXTRACT(YEAR FROM d1)) * 12 + EXTRACT(MONTH FROM d2) - EXTRACT(MONTH FROM d1)` counts month boundaries, like `DATEDIFF(month, ...)` elsewhere.\n6. Counts **complete** months: Jan 31 → Feb 1 is 0, where `DATEDIFF(month, ...)` in Snowflake/SQL Server gives 1.\n\n**Argument order is the trap.** `DATEDIFF(d2, d1)` in MySQL vs `DATEDIFF(day, d1, d2)` in Snowflake and SQL Server vs `DATE_DIFF(d2, d1, DAY)` in BigQuery. Say it out loud as you type: \"unit, from, to\".\n\n## Everything else\n\n| Task | PostgreSQL | MySQL | Snowflake | BigQuery | SQL Server |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| First n rows | `LIMIT n` | `LIMIT n` | `LIMIT n` | `LIMIT n` | `SELECT TOP n ...` |\n| `7 / 2` (integers) | **3** | 3.5000 | 3.5 | 3.5 | **3** |\n| String concat | `a \\|\\| b` | `CONCAT(a, b)` | `a \\|\\| b` | `CONCAT(a, b)` or `\\|\\|` | `a + b` or `CONCAT(a, b)` |\n| Case-insensitive match | `ILIKE` | `LIKE` (case-insensitive by default) | `ILIKE` | `LOWER(x) LIKE ...` | `LIKE` (case-insensitive by default) |\n| Filter on a window | CTE | CTE | `QUALIFY` | `QUALIFY` | CTE |\n| NULLs in `ORDER BY x` | last | first | last | first | first |\n| `GROUP BY 1` | yes | yes | yes | yes | **no** |\n| `FULL OUTER JOIN` | yes | **no** (UNION a LEFT and a RIGHT) | yes | yes | yes |\n| Cast that returns NULL on failure | none built in | none built in | `TRY_CAST` | `SAFE_CAST` | `TRY_CAST` |\n| Quote an identifier | `\"col\"` | `` `col` `` | `\"col\"` | `` `col` `` | `[col]` |\n| Boolean in `SELECT` | `x > 1 AS flag` | returns 1/0 | `x > 1 AS flag` | `x > 1 AS flag` | **not allowed**: use `CASE` |\n\n## The portable subset\n\nIf you don't know the dialect, this works nearly everywhere:\n\n- `CASE WHEN`, `COALESCE`, `NULLIF`, `CAST(x AS ...)`\n- `COUNT`/`SUM`/`AVG` with `CASE` inside, rather than `FILTER` or boolean sums\n- `ROW_NUMBER() OVER (...)` in a CTE, then `WHERE rn = 1`, rather than `QUALIFY`\n- `JOIN ... ON`, rather than `USING`\n- `1.0 * a / b` for rates\n- `EXTRACT(YEAR FROM d)`, `EXTRACT(MONTH FROM d)`\n- Half-open date ranges with `>=` and `<`"}]}, {"id": "04-pitfalls", "title": "Pitfalls checklist", "blocks": [{"type": "md", "text": "Read this the night before. Each item links back to the lesson that covers it.\n\n## Before you write\n\n- [ ] **What's one row of the answer?** Say the grain out loud. ([lesson 0](#/lesson/00-how-sql-thinks))\n- [ ] **Which rows count?** Status filters, successful payments only, open loans only, and the date range: originated, applied or paid?\n- [ ] **Count or dollars? Loans, accounts or people?**\n- [ ] **Ties?** Exactly N rows, or all tied rows?\n- [ ] **Is the key really unique?** `COUNT(*)` vs `COUNT(DISTINCT id)`.\n\n## While you write\n\n**NULL** ([lesson 1](#/lesson/01-filtering-and-null), [lesson 2](#/lesson/02-aggregation-and-grain))\n\n- [ ] `= NULL` never matches. Use `IS NULL`.\n- [ ] `<>`, `NOT` and `NOT IN` silently drop NULL rows.\n- [ ] `AVG`, `SUM` and `COUNT(col)` skip NULLs. Is that what you want?\n- [ ] `NOT IN (subquery)` returns nothing if the subquery has a NULL. Use `NOT EXISTS`.\n- [ ] NULL keys never join (unlike dplyr).\n\n**Joins** ([lesson 3](#/lesson/03-joins), [lesson 4](#/lesson/04-fan-out-and-anti-joins))\n\n- [ ] Joining to a \"many\" table repeats the \"one\" side. Sums of the one side are inflated: **aggregate first, then join**.\n- [ ] Composite keys need **every** column in `ON`.\n- [ ] A `WHERE` on a LEFT-joined table's column turns it into an INNER JOIN. Put it in `ON`.\n- [ ] After a LEFT JOIN, `COUNT(right.id)`, not `COUNT(*)`.\n- [ ] Reaching for `DISTINCT` to fix a join? You probably wanted `EXISTS`.\n\n**Aggregation** ([lesson 2](#/lesson/02-aggregation-and-grain))\n\n- [ ] Every selected column is grouped or aggregated.\n- [ ] Rates: `1.0 *` to avoid integer division, `NULLIF(denominator, 0)` to avoid divide-by-zero.\n- [ ] `WHERE` filters rows before grouping; `HAVING` filters groups after.\n\n**Windows** ([lesson 6](#/lesson/06-window-functions-ranking), [lesson 7](#/lesson/07-window-functions-sequences))\n\n- [ ] Can't filter a window in `WHERE`: use a CTE (or `QUALIFY` where supported).\n- [ ] Windows see only rows that passed `WHERE`. Filter *after* the window when the window needs all rows.\n- [ ] Add a tie-breaker to every window `ORDER BY`.\n- [ ] Running totals with tied order values jump together (default `RANGE` frame).\n- [ ] `LAST_VALUE` needs an explicit full frame.\n\n**Dates** ([lesson 8](#/lesson/08-dates-and-time))\n\n- [ ] Timestamps vs dates: use `>= start AND < next_start`, never `BETWEEN` on a timestamp.\n- [ ] `EXTRACT(MONTH ...)` merges years; use `DATE_TRUNC` for time series.\n- [ ] Months with no data are missing rows, not zeros: join to a calendar.\n- [ ] Future dates (schedules) aren't \"missed\" yet.\n- [ ] Recent cohorts haven't had time to go bad: compare at equal age. ([lesson 9](#/lesson/09-lending-metrics))\n\n**Data quality in this dataset**\n\n- [ ] `payments`: duplicate rows, failed attempts, partial payments.\n- [ ] `borrowers`: one person can have two accounts.\n- [ ] `merchants.category`: inconsistent case and spaces, some NULLs.\n- [ ] `term_months`: NULL for Pay in 4.\n\n## Before you answer\n\n- [ ] Does a total reconcile with the base table?\n- [ ] Spot-check one ID by hand.\n- [ ] Rates between 0 and 1? Groups add up to the whole?\n- [ ] Columns, names, sort order and rounding exactly as asked.\n- [ ] State your assumptions and one caveat."}]}, {"id": "05-coverage", "title": "Coverage checklist", "blocks": [{"type": "md", "text": "Everything an analyst SQL screen is likely to test, and where this course teaches (**L**) and drills (**D**) it. Problem-bank questions (**P**) combine several skills at once. Use it to spot gaps: if a row feels shaky, do its drills again from a blank editor.\n\n| Skill | Taught | Practised |\n|---|---|---|\n| **Query basics** | | |\n| Clause order vs. run order; grain | [L0](#/lesson/00-how-sql-thinks) | throughout |\n| Calculated columns, aliases, `ROUND`, `CAST` | [L1](#/lesson/01-filtering-and-null) | [D: SELECT & functions](#/drill/00-select-and-functions) |\n| String functions: `LOWER`, `TRIM`, `SUBSTRING`, `POSITION`, `LENGTH`, `\\|\\|` / `CONCAT` | [D: SELECT & functions](#/drill/00-select-and-functions) | P: t2-01, t2-07 |\n| `ORDER BY` (expressions, `CASE`, tie-breakers, NULL placement), `LIMIT`/`OFFSET` | [L1](#/lesson/01-filtering-and-null) | [D: SELECT & functions](#/drill/00-select-and-functions), P: t1-02 |\n| `DISTINCT` | [L1](#/lesson/01-filtering-and-null) | [D: SELECT & functions](#/drill/00-select-and-functions) |\n| **Filtering** | | |\n| `=`, `<>`, `IN`, `BETWEEN`, `LIKE`; `AND`/`OR` precedence | [L1](#/lesson/01-filtering-and-null) | [D: WHERE & NULL](#/drill/01-filtering-and-null) |\n| NULL: three-valued logic, `IS NULL`, `<>`/`NOT`/`NOT IN` traps | [L1](#/lesson/01-filtering-and-null) | [D: WHERE & NULL](#/drill/01-filtering-and-null) |\n| `CASE WHEN` (including NULL branches) | [L1](#/lesson/01-filtering-and-null) | [D: WHERE & NULL](#/drill/01-filtering-and-null), P: t1-06 |\n| `COALESCE`, `NULLIF` | [L1](#/lesson/01-filtering-and-null), [L2](#/lesson/02-aggregation-and-grain) | [D: WHERE & NULL](#/drill/01-filtering-and-null), [D: aggregation](#/drill/02-aggregation) |\n| **Aggregation** | | |\n| `COUNT(*)` / `COUNT(col)` / `COUNT(DISTINCT)`; aggregates skip NULLs | [L2](#/lesson/02-aggregation-and-grain) | [D: aggregation](#/drill/02-aggregation) |\n| `GROUP BY` (several columns, expressions), `HAVING` | [L2](#/lesson/02-aggregation-and-grain) | [D: aggregation](#/drill/02-aggregation), P: t1-07 |\n| Conditional aggregation, pivots, rates; integer division | [L2](#/lesson/02-aggregation-and-grain) | [D: aggregation](#/drill/02-aggregation), [D: patterns](#/drill/07-classic-patterns), P: t1-08, t2-02 |\n| Median / percentiles | [D: aggregation](#/drill/02-aggregation) | |\n| **Joins** | | |\n| `INNER` / `LEFT` join syntax, aliases, multi-table | [L3](#/lesson/03-joins) | [D: joins](#/drill/03-joins), P: t2-03 |\n| `ON` vs `WHERE` with LEFT JOIN; counting after LEFT JOIN | [L3](#/lesson/03-joins) | [D: joins](#/drill/03-joins) |\n| Composite keys; NULL keys | [L3](#/lesson/03-joins) | [D: joins](#/drill/03-joins), P: t2-10 |\n| Fan-out; aggregate-then-join | [L4](#/lesson/04-fan-out-and-anti-joins) | [D: joins](#/drill/03-joins), P: t2-06 |\n| Semi / anti joins (`EXISTS`, `NOT EXISTS`, `LEFT JOIN ... IS NULL`) | [L4](#/lesson/04-fan-out-and-anti-joins) | [D: joins](#/drill/03-joins), P: t2-04 |\n| Self joins, range (non-equi) joins, `CROSS JOIN`, `FULL OUTER JOIN` | [D: joins](#/drill/03-joins) | |\n| **Query structure** | | |\n| CTEs; subqueries (scalar, `IN`, correlated, in `FROM`) | [L5](#/lesson/05-ctes-and-subqueries) | [D: subqueries](#/drill/04-subqueries-and-sets), P: t2-05, t2-08 |\n| `UNION ALL` / `UNION` / `INTERSECT` / `EXCEPT` | [L5](#/lesson/05-ctes-and-subqueries) | [D: subqueries](#/drill/04-subqueries-and-sets) |\n| **Window functions** | | |\n| `PARTITION BY` / `ORDER BY`; windows vs `GROUP BY`; run order | [L6](#/lesson/06-window-functions-ranking) | [D: windows](#/drill/05-window-functions) |\n| `ROW_NUMBER` / `RANK` / `DENSE_RANK`; top-N; dedup; first/latest | [L6](#/lesson/06-window-functions-ranking) | [D: windows](#/drill/05-window-functions), P: t3-01, t3-05 |\n| `LAG` / `LEAD`; running totals; moving averages; frames | [L7](#/lesson/07-window-functions-sequences) | [D: windows](#/drill/05-window-functions), P: t3-02, t3-03 |\n| `NTILE`, `FIRST_VALUE`, `LAST_VALUE`, share of group | [D: windows](#/drill/05-window-functions) | |\n| **Dates** | | |\n| DATE vs TIMESTAMP; half-open ranges | [L8](#/lesson/08-dates-and-time) | [D: dates](#/drill/06-dates) |\n| `EXTRACT`, `DATE_TRUNC` (month/quarter/week), intervals, date differences | [L8](#/lesson/08-dates-and-time) | [D: dates](#/drill/06-dates), P: t3-06, t3-08 |\n| Month ends, months-between, calendars for missing periods | [L8](#/lesson/08-dates-and-time) | [D: dates](#/drill/06-dates), [D: joins](#/drill/03-joins) |\n| **Patterns** | | |\n| Nth event, A-then-B, retention cohorts, streaks (gaps & islands), consecutive events, YoY, histograms, Pareto | [D: patterns](#/drill/07-classic-patterns) | P: t3-04, t3-09, t4-06 |\n| Lending metrics: approval, DPD, vintages, FPD, loss rates | [L9](#/lesson/09-lending-metrics) | P: Tier 4 |\n| **Interview craft** | | |\n| Clarify, plan the grain, build in steps, verify, explain | [L10](#/lesson/10-interview-craft) | [Mock screens](#/mock) |\n| Dialect differences | [Dialect sheet](#/ref/03-dialects) | |"}]}]};
